window.TOOLKIT = {"plugin": "moons2star-consulting", "packs": [{"id": "frame", "name": "Frame the problem", "tagline": "Get the question right before anyone builds a slide."}, {"id": "evidence", "name": "Gather evidence", "tagline": "Find, test and combine facts you can stand behind."}, {"id": "market", "name": "Understand the market", "tagline": "Size it, segment it, and see who wins and why."}, {"id": "strategy", "name": "Shape the strategy", "tagline": "Turn analysis into real choices with clear trade-offs."}, {"id": "numbers", "name": "Run the numbers", "tagline": "Business cases, models and metrics a CFO will trust."}, {"id": "story", "name": "Tell the story", "tagline": "Storylines, slides and memos that get to a decision."}, {"id": "deliver", "name": "Deliver and change", "tagline": "Plans, roles, risks and the people side of change."}, {"id": "board", "name": "Boardroom and governance", "tagline": "Board papers, oversight questions, risk, ESG and AI governance."}, {"id": "ai", "name": "AI transformation", "tagline": "Find, prove and scale AI use cases responsibly."}, {"id": "leader", "name": "Leader's desk", "tagline": "Everyday tools for meetings, negotiations and messages."}, {"id": "playbooks", "name": "Playbooks", "tagline": "End-to-end flows that chain several skills together."}], "skills": [{"id": "problem-statement", "pack": "frame", "title": "Problem Statement", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Turn a vague ask into one sharp, decision-focused question with scope and success criteria.", "tryp": "Our CEO says 'we need to fix customer churn'. Help me frame the real problem. We are a B2B SaaS firm, ~2,000 customers, churn went from 8% to 13% in a year.", "when": ["A project or request is starting and the ask is fuzzy ('look into pricing', 'fix churn')", "A team keeps debating solutions before agreeing on the problem", "You need a one-paragraph brief to align a sponsor before work begins"], "inputs": ["The request in the sponsor's own words", "Who will make the decision, and by when", "What is in and out of scope; known constraints (budget, policy, time)", "Any numbers that show the problem today"], "pairs": ["audience-brief", "issue-tree", "hypothesis-tree", "engagement-workplan"], "body": "# Problem Statement\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a one-page problem definition a sponsor can approve in two minutes. It focuses all later work on a decision and stops the project drifting into \"everything about customers\".\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **A problem worth solving is a decision someone must take.** No decision-maker and no deadline means a topic, not a problem.\n2. **Outcome, not activity.** Frame the result in measurable business terms (revenue, cost, risk, customer, time), not in deliverables (\"a report on…\").\n3. **Symptoms vs root problem.** \"Churn rose\" is a symptom. The problem might be onboarding, price, product gaps or service. Frame the question so the cause can be found, not presumed.\n4. **Solution-neutral, unless the solution is already decided.** \"How do we build an app to…\" presupposes the answer. If the sponsor has decided, say so and reframe the question as \"how to make it succeed\".\n5. **Scope is a promise.** Explicit out-of-scope and parked items protect delivery and relationships.\n6. **The right altitude.** Big enough to matter, small enough to answer in the time available.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Who asked, in their own words, and why now (the trigger)?\n- Who decides, by when, and what will they do with the answer?\n- What has been tried before, and what happened?\n- What constraints are fixed (budget, policy, people, technology, regulation)?\n- What data or evidence exists today?\n\n## Method\n1. **Capture the ask verbatim,** plus the trigger and the history.\n2. **Ask \"so that…?\" repeatedly** until you reach a business outcome. Ask \"why does this matter?\" until you reach money, risk, customers or mission.\n3. **Separate symptoms from potential root causes;** list both. Keep the question open to causes.\n4. **Write the question:** \"What must [decision-maker] do to achieve [measurable outcome] by [date], given [constraints]?\"\n5. **Success criteria:** 2-4 measurable tests of a solved problem. Include a guardrail (what must not get worse).\n6. **Scope:** in, out, and parked (related but later); include geographies, segments, processes and time period.\n7. **Stakeholders:** who must accept the answer and what each needs to believe; known positions.\n8. **Unknowns and hypotheses:** the 3-5 biggest unknowns; initial hypotheses to test (feeds the issue or hypothesis tree).\n9. **Stress test:**\n   - **Too broad?** Can it be answered in the time? If not, narrow it.\n   - **Too narrow?** Does it presuppose a cause or solution?\n   - **Solution in disguise?** Does it contain \"implement\", \"build\" or \"launch\"?\n   - **Measurable?**\n   - **Owned?**\n10. **Confirm** with the sponsor and record the agreed version.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **When the sponsor insists on a solution,** don't fight it. Frame a two-part question: will the chosen solution solve the underlying problem, and how should it be executed? Make the underlying problem explicit.\n- **Political subtext** matters. The stated question may hide the real one, such as justifying a reorganisation or a budget. Ask privately what decision will actually be made.\n- **Multiple decision-makers** need one accountable owner; others are stakeholders.\n- **Short timelines need narrower questions.** A 4-week project can't answer a 5-year strategy question well.\n\n## Red flags\n- No named decision-maker or date.\n- An outcome described as a deliverable (\"a report\", \"a dashboard\").\n- A question that names the solution.\n- No out-of-scope list.\n- Success criteria that are adjectives (\"better\", \"improved\").\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board ask:** frame around the board's decision and its oversight duties; include risk appetite and regulatory context.\n- **Transformation:** a problem statement per workstream, plus an overarching one; link to the business case.\n- **AI initiative:** frame the business problem first; AI is a candidate means, not the goal.\n- **Crisis:** narrow, time-boxed question (\"What must we decide in the next 48 hours to…?\").\n- **Public sector:** include citizen outcomes, equity, and statutory duties.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Problem Statement: [title]\n**The question:** What must [decision-maker] do to [measurable outcome] by [date], given [constraints]?\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Ask (verbatim) and trigger | |\n| Decision-maker / deadline | |\n| Symptoms observed | |\n| Possible root causes (to test) | |\n| Success criteria (incl. guardrail) | 1. ... 2. ... 3. ... |\n| In scope | |\n| Out of scope | |\n| Parked (later) | |\n| Constraints | |\n| Stakeholders: what they must believe | |\n| Biggest unknowns / initial hypotheses | |\n\n**Stress test:** breadth ✓ | solution-neutral ✓ | measurable ✓ | owned ✓\n**Agreed with sponsor on:** [date]\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Decision-maker, measurable outcome and date are in the question.\n- [ ] Symptoms are separated from causes; the question stays open to causes.\n- [ ] No solution embedded, unless explicitly decided (then reframed).\n- [ ] Success criteria are measurable, with a guardrail.\n- [ ] In, out and parked scope; constraints; stakeholders.\n- [ ] Fits the time and resources available.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Framing reflects only the context you provide. Confirm the problem definition with your sponsor and stakeholders.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `audience-brief`: Profile who you are persuading: what they care about, what they already believe, and what would change their mind.\n- `issue-tree`: Break a big question into a logical tree of smaller questions you can actually answer.\n- `hypothesis-tree`: Start from a likely answer, then list exactly what must be true and how you would prove or kill it.\n- `engagement-workplan`: Turn the issue tree into a week-by-week plan: analyses, owners, data, meetings and deliverables.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Structured problem solving:** defining a specific, measurable, action-oriented problem statement before analysis is a core step taught in management-consulting and business-school problem-solving courses.\n- **SMART criteria** (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound) applied to problem and objective definition.\n- **Root-cause thinking:** \"5 whys\" and cause-and-effect analysis from quality-management practice, used here to separate symptoms from causes.\n- **Project chartering:** scope, out-of-scope and stakeholder definition as in professional project-management standards."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional B2B SaaS company, ~2,000 customers)\n\n**Ask (verbatim, from the CEO):** \"We need to fix customer churn. Look into it and come back with a plan.\"\n**Trigger:** annual logo churn rose from 8% to 13% in 12 months. The investor board flagged it as a risk to the next funding round.\n\n**First draft (rejected):** \"Build a customer-success program to reduce churn.\" It names a solution.\n\n**Agreed question:** what must the Chief Customer Officer change in onboarding, pricing, product adoption and account coverage to bring annual logo churn back below 9%, and net revenue retention above 105%, within 12 months, without adding net headcount?\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Decision-maker / deadline | CCO, with CEO approval; plan by 15 March for the Q2 budget |\n| Symptoms | Churn up 5 pts; 60% of churn among customers under 18 months old; support tickets per account +30% |\n| Possible root causes (to test) | Onboarding takes too long (data migration); price rise of 2025 hit small accounts; key features under-adopted; no coverage for mid-market accounts after the sales handover |\n| Success criteria | 1. Logo churn ≤9% (rolling 12 months) 2. NRR ≥105% 3. Time-to-first-value ≤30 days. **Guardrail:** CSAT does not fall below 4.2 |\n| In scope | Mid-market and SMB segments in India and the Middle East; onboarding, pricing, adoption, coverage model |\n| Out of scope | Enterprise segment (separate team, low churn); product roadmap re-prioritisation |\n| Parked | A new pricing model for 2028 (revisit after the analysis) |\n| Constraints | No net headcount; existing CRM; pricing changes need board approval |\n| Stakeholders | CFO (must believe the fix is cost-neutral); CPO (must accept that adoption issues are partly product); Sales (must accept a new handover) |\n| Unknowns / hypotheses | H1: Onboarding duration drives early churn. H2: The 2025 price rise caused SMB churn. H3: Accounts without a named success manager churn 2x more |\n\n**Stress test:** answerable in 8 weeks with the existing data ✓ | solution-neutral ✓ | measurable ✓ | owned by the CCO ✓"}]}, {"id": "audience-brief", "pack": "frame", "title": "Audience Brief", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Profile who you are persuading: what they care about, what they already believe, and what would change their mind.", "tryp": "I'm presenting a cloud migration proposal to our CFO and the audit committee chair next week. Build an audience brief.", "when": ["Before writing any deck, memo or speech that must persuade", "When presenting to a board, investors, a regulator or a new executive", "When a past presentation 'landed badly' and you need to know why"], "inputs": ["Who the audience is (names, roles, or type)", "What you want them to decide or do", "What you know about their priorities, history with the topic and style", "The format and time you will get"], "pairs": ["scqa-storyline", "executive-summary", "stakeholder-map", "meeting-brief"], "body": "# Audience Brief\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear picture of who you must convince, what they currently believe, what will move them, and how to pre-wire the room, so the content is built for the reader rather than the author.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Decisions are social.** The formal decision-maker relies on advisers, so map the influence network, not just the org chart.\n2. **Start from their beliefs.** Persuasion moves people from what they believe now. Write down both ends of the shift.\n3. **Objections are information.** Anticipate them in the audience's words and prepare the evidence each needs. The best decks answer the first question before it's asked.\n4. **Altitude and language fit the reader:** a CFO wants cash and risk; a CTO wants architecture and feasibility; a board wants strategy, risk and oversight.\n5. **No surprises in the room.** Pre-wire key people, especially potential blockers, before the meeting.\n6. **One ask.** A clear decision, phrased so \"yes\" is easy.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- What decision do you need, and by when?\n- Who attends, who influences from outside the room, and who can block?\n- What happened the last time this topic or team came to this audience?\n- Format and time: pre-read or live? 10 minutes or 60? In person or virtual?\n\n## Method\n1. **List the audience by role:** decision-maker; influencers (trusted advisers, committee chairs); blockers (budget owners, risk or compliance, affected units); gatekeepers (chief of staff, secretariat); implementers.\n2. **Profile each:**\n   - what they are measured on (KPIs, incentives);\n   - their current priorities and worries;\n   - their history with the topic;\n   - their preferred style (data-first or story-first; detail or headline);\n   - their likely first question.\n3. **Beliefs shift:** \"Now they think … → after, they need to think …\". Keep it to one or two shifts per person.\n4. **Objections and evidence:** the top 5 objections (in their words), with the evidence or concession that addresses each.\n5. **Altitude and format:** the level of detail; numbers in the body vs the appendix; vocabulary to use or avoid; length; pre-read vs presentation; visual vs narrative.\n6. **Politics:** alliances, rivalries, sensitivities (e.g. a proposal that reduces someone's remit); who should present which part.\n7. **Pre-wiring plan:** who to meet before the meeting, in what order, with what message, and what you'll ask them.\n8. **The ask:** one sentence, a decision with a date (\"Approve X by Y\"); plus fallback asks (e.g. approve a pilot).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Influencers can matter more than the decider.** A CFO's analyst or a board member's trusted adviser may shape the decision long before the meeting.\n- **Concede early on small points** to build credibility for the big one.\n- **Adjust messengers.** Sometimes a peer of the blocker, or a customer, should carry the argument.\n- **Read the room's appetite for risk.** The same proposal framed as a \"pilot\" vs a \"transformation\" lands very differently.\n- **Respect, don't manipulate.** Persuade with evidence and genuine benefits. Pre-wiring is about listening and adjusting, not lobbying for an ambush.\n\n## Red flags\n- An audience treated as one homogeneous group.\n- Objections written in the presenter's language, not the audience's.\n- No pre-wiring of people who can block.\n- The ask buried, or \"for information\" when a decision is needed.\n- Detail level set by what the team knows rather than what the audience needs.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board:** fiduciary lens; committee chairs as key influencers; papers circulated in advance; Chair and Company Secretary as gatekeepers.\n- **Investors:** return, risk and growth; consistency with past guidance; credibility of management.\n- **Regulators:** compliance, customer protection, systemic risk; a formal, evidence-heavy tone.\n- **Internal ExCo:** resource trade-offs between peers; alignment with the CEO's agenda.\n- **Clients:** business outcomes, risk of change, proof from similar clients, commercial terms.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Audience Brief: [meeting / document], [date]\n**The ask (one sentence):** ...  |  **Fallback ask:** ...\n\n| Person / role | Decision role | Measured on / worries | Believes now | Needs to believe | Likely first question | Style |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Objections → evidence / concession**\n1. \"...\" → ...\n\n**Altitude and format:** ...\n**Political dynamics:** ...\n**Pre-wiring plan:**\n| Who | When | Message | What we ask / listen for | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Decider, influencers and blockers are identified.\n- [ ] Each person has a belief shift and a likely first question.\n- [ ] Objections are in the audience's words, with evidence.\n- [ ] Format and altitude fit the audience.\n- [ ] A pre-wiring plan for anyone who can block.\n- [ ] One clear decision ask, plus a fallback.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Framing reflects only the context you provide. Confirm the problem definition with your sponsor and stakeholders.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `scqa-storyline`: Build the storyline every executive document needs: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n- `stakeholder-map`: Map who matters, where they stand, and the plan to move each of them.\n- `meeting-brief`: A one-page brief before any important meeting: objective, people, positions, your asks and your lines.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Stakeholder analysis and influence mapping:** power-interest grids and decision-role mapping, from project-management and change-management practice.\n- **Persuasion and audience analysis:** classical rhetoric (credibility, logic, emotion) and modern business-communication curricula on audience-centred writing.\n- **Pre-wiring (\"nemawashi\"-style consensus building):** a long-standing practice of informal consultation before formal decisions.\n- **Board communication practice:** company-secretarial guidance on board papers and pre-reads (see the `board-paper` skill)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional manufacturer, proposal to migrate 4 data centres to hybrid cloud)\n\n**The ask:** approve ₹31 Cr over 24 months for hybrid-cloud migration, replacing a ₹48 Cr hardware refresh. Decision at the Audit and Risk Committee on 12 May, then the board.\n**Fallback ask:** approve Phase 1 (₹9 Cr: non-critical workloads) and a cost review after 6 months.\n\n| Person | Role | Measured on / worries | Believes now | Needs to believe | Likely first question | Style |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CFO | Influencer (recommends to the board) | Free cash flow; opex creep | \"Cloud always costs more over time\" | \"3-year TCO is 12% lower; opex controls exist\" | \"What happens to run-rate in year 3?\" | Numbers first |\n| Audit and Risk Committee Chair | Decision gate | Cyber, data residency, vendor concentration | \"Cloud = loss of control\" | \"Controls are stronger than today; exit plan exists\" | \"Where is our data, and who can access it?\" | Risk-focused, formal |\n| COO | Blocker (plants depend on uptime) | Plant uptime | \"Migration will disrupt production\" | \"Plant systems stay on-site; phased cutover\" | \"What's the downtime plan?\" | Practical |\n| Independent director (ex-tech) | Influencer | Architecture quality | Neutral, sceptical of vendors | \"Multi-cloud-ready design; no lock-in\" | \"How portable is this?\" | Technical |\n| CEO | Decision-maker (with board) | Strategy, cost, speed | Supportive if the board is comfortable | \"Board risks are addressed\" | \"Are the CFO and COO aligned?\" | Headline |\n\n**Objections → evidence / concession**\n1. \"Costs will creep after year 2.\" → A 5-year TCO with reserved-capacity pricing, monthly cost governance and tagging, and a cost-overrun trigger to the CFO.\n2. \"Data residency and DPDP compliance.\" → Data stays in India regions; a processor contract mapped to DPDP obligations; DPO sign-off.\n3. \"Plant downtime.\" → **Concession:** plant OT systems stay on-premise; only IT workloads move.\n4. \"Vendor lock-in.\" → Containerised workloads; an exit plan with tested data export.\n\n**Altitude and format:**\n- 6-page paper plus a 10-slide summary.\n- TCO table on page 2.\n- Architecture detail in the appendix.\n- Avoid acronyms; define \"hybrid cloud\" in one line.\n\n**Pre-wiring:**\n| Who | When | Message | Ask / listen for | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CFO | 2 weeks before | TCO and cost controls | Agree assumptions; invite challenge | CIO |\n| COO | 2 weeks before | OT stays on-site | Confirm the cutover windows | CIO + Plant IT |\n| ARC Chair | 10 days before | Risk and controls summary | Which assurance she wants (e.g. internal audit review) | CIO + CRO |\n| Independent director | 1 week before | Architecture walkthrough | Concerns on portability | Chief Architect |"}]}, {"id": "issue-tree", "pack": "frame", "title": "Issue Tree", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Break a big question into a logical tree of smaller questions you can actually answer.", "tryp": "Build an issue tree for: why has our hospital's operating margin fallen from 6% to 1% in two years?", "when": ["After the problem statement is agreed and before analysis starts", "To split work across a team without overlap", "When a discussion goes in circles and needs structure"], "inputs": ["The key question (ideally from the Problem Statement skill)", "Whether you need a 'why' tree (diagnose a cause) or a 'how' tree (find solutions)", "Any data you already have"], "pairs": ["mece-check", "hypothesis-tree", "engagement-workplan", "kpi-driver-tree"], "body": "# Issue Tree\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a map of the problem that ensures nothing important is missed and nothing is done twice. Every branch ends in a question that a specific analysis can answer, and the team knows which branches to work on first.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **One question at the top,** taken from the agreed problem statement.\n2. **Choose the tree type deliberately.** A **diagnostic tree** (\"Why is margin falling?\") finds causes. A **solution tree** (\"How could we raise margin by 3 points?\") generates options. Don't mix them in one tree.\n3. **Splitting logic quality determines tree quality.** Best to worst:\n   - **mathematical** (profit = revenue − cost; revenue = volume × price);\n   - **process or value chain** (acquire → onboard → serve → renew);\n   - **segment** (by customer, product, geography, channel);\n   - **stakeholder** (customers, employees, suppliers, regulators);\n   - **framework-based** (internal vs external; the five forces).\n   Avoid loose lists.\n4. **MECE at every level.** No overlaps, so work isn't duplicated; no gaps, so the cause isn't missed.\n5. **Leaves are answerable questions** linked to a specific analysis and data source, not topics.\n6. **Prioritise and prune.** Work the leaves with the highest expected impact and uncertainty first. Explicitly drop branches that can't change the decision.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The key question (from `problem-statement`) and the decision it serves.\n- Diagnostic or solution orientation.\n- Available data and time; team size (the tree becomes the work split).\n\n## Method\n1. **Write the key question** at the root.\n2. **First split:** pick the strongest logic available. Write 2-5 branches. Test MECE: could any example sit in two branches (overlap)? Is there a case that fits none (gap)?\n3. **Decompose each branch** 2-4 more levels, preferably using different logics at different levels (e.g. mathematical at level 1, segment at level 2, process at level 3).\n4. **Turn leaves into questions:** \"Did X change?\", \"Is Y above benchmark?\", \"Could Z deliver ≥₹10 Cr?\"\n5. **Annotate each leaf:** the analysis (e.g. price-volume-mix bridge, cohort analysis, benchmark), the data source, owner, effort (S/M/L), and priority (impact × likelihood × uncertainty).\n6. **Prune:** mark leaves that are unlikely or immaterial, and say why.\n7. **Sequence:** order the high-priority leaves; note dependencies.\n8. **Visualise:** an indented outline (and optionally a simple text diagram).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **When no mathematical split exists,** use process or segment splits and state the assumption that makes them complete.\n- **\"Other\" is allowed** at a level, if it is small and checked for size.\n- **For diagnostic trees,** quantify each branch early (e.g. a revenue bridge) to drop small branches fast.\n- **Revisit the tree** as evidence arrives. It's a working tool, not a deliverable.\n\n## Red flags\n- Branches that overlap (\"cost\" and \"efficiency\"; \"digital\" and \"marketing\").\n- Leaves that are topics (\"IT\", \"people\").\n- Levels mixing dimensions (product lines alongside regions).\n- No priorities; every branch worked equally.\n- A tree that mixes why and how.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Profitability problems:** profit = (price − unit cost) × volume − fixed costs; then segment and process.\n- **Growth questions:** revenue = customers × frequency × spend; or the product-market matrix.\n- **Operational problems:** process steps × failure modes; capacity = resources × productivity × availability.\n- **People and organisation:** attraction → selection → onboarding → development → retention.\n- **Risk or incident analysis:** cause categories (people, process, technology, external) at each process step.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Issue Tree: [key question]\n**Type:** Diagnostic (why) / Solution (how)  |  **Level-1 logic:** ...\n\n1. Branch A\n   1.1 Question\n       - Analysis: ... | Data: ... | Owner: ... | Effort: S/M/L | Priority: H/M/L\n   1.2 ...\n2. Branch B\n   ...\n\n**MECE check:** level 1 ✓ (logic: ...), level 2 ✓ ...\n**Work first (top 3-5 leaves):** ...\n**Pruned (and why):** ...\n**Sequence and dependencies:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] A single root question; the tree type is stated and not mixed.\n- [ ] The splitting logic is stated per level; MECE checked at each level.\n- [ ] Leaves are answerable questions with an analysis, data source and owner.\n- [ ] Priorities and pruning are explicit.\n- [ ] Early quantification is used to focus (diagnostic trees).\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Framing reflects only the context you provide. Confirm the problem definition with your sponsor and stakeholders.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `mece-check`: Audit any list, structure or storyline for overlaps and gaps, then fix it.\n- `hypothesis-tree`: Start from a likely answer, then list exactly what must be true and how you would prove or kill it.\n- `engagement-workplan`: Turn the issue tree into a week-by-week plan: analyses, owners, data, meetings and deliverables.\n- `kpi-driver-tree`: Break a top metric into the drivers people can act on, with owners and targets.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Issue trees / logic trees and the MECE principle:** standard structured problem-solving techniques taught in business schools and consulting training.\n- **Driver decomposition:** profit and return trees from management accounting.\n- **Root-cause analysis:** cause-and-effect (fishbone) and \"5 whys\" from quality management.\n- **Hypothesis-driven problem solving:** using trees to prioritise analysis (see the `hypothesis-tree` skill)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional 400-bed hospital, operating margin fell from 6% to 1% in two years)\n\n**Key question:** why has operating margin fallen from 6% to 1% between FY24 and FY26?\n**Type:** diagnostic. **Level-1 logic:** margin = (revenue − operating cost) ÷ revenue; revenue = inpatient + outpatient + other; cost = staff + medical consumables + other operating.\n\n**Early quantification** (bridge, ₹ Cr):\n- Revenue +₹48 Cr; costs +₹78 Cr.\n- The cost increase is driven by staff (+₹41 Cr) and consumables (+₹22 Cr).\n- So the cost branches get priority.\n\n1. **Did revenue per unit of activity fall?**\n   1.1 Did the payer mix shift towards lower-tariff payers (government schemes vs private insurance vs cash)?\n       - Analysis: revenue and volume by payer, FY24-26 | Data: billing | Owner: Finance | Effort: S | **Priority: H**\n   1.2 Did package rates or insurer discounts change?\n       - Analysis: realised rate per package, top 30 procedures | Data: contracts, billing | Owner: RCM | M | M\n   1.3 Did the case mix shift towards less complex, lower-revenue cases?\n       - Analysis: case-mix index trend | Data: medical records | Owner: Medical Admin | M | M\n   1.4 Did occupancy or OT utilisation fall?\n       - Analysis: occupancy and OT minutes by specialty | Data: HIS | Owner: Operations | S | L (both are stable)\n2. **Did cost per unit of activity rise?**\n   2.1 Staff cost\n       2.1.1 Did agency or contract nurse use rise? (rate × hours) | Data: payroll, vendor invoices | HR | S | **H**\n       2.1.2 Did consultant payouts (fee-share %) change? | Data: contracts | Finance | S | **H**\n       2.1.3 Did the staffing ratio per occupied bed rise? | Data: rosters, HIS | Nursing | M | M\n   2.2 Consumables cost\n       2.2.1 Did prices rise (vs CPI and the contracts)? | Data: purchase data | Procurement | S | **H**\n       2.2.2 Did usage per case rise (e.g. implants, high-value consumables)? | Data: issue records by case | Pharmacy / OT | M | **H**\n       2.2.3 Did wastage or expiry increase? | Data: inventory write-offs | Stores | S | M\n   2.3 Length of stay: did ALOS rise for the same case mix? | Data: HIS | Medical Director | M | M\n   2.4 Other operating costs (utilities, maintenance, IT) | Data: GL | Finance | S | L (+₹15 Cr; mostly a known energy tariff increase)\n3. **Were there one-offs or accounting changes?**\n   3.1 Provisions, write-offs (e.g. receivables from government schemes) | Data: GL notes | Finance | S | **H**\n   3.2 Classification changes between years | Data: auditor notes | Finance | S | M\n\n**MECE check:** level 1 is a revenue/cost/one-off identity; level 2 for cost uses categories that sum to the total.\n\n**Work first:**\n- 2.1.1 agency nurses;\n- 2.2.2 consumables per case;\n- 2.1.2 consultant payouts;\n- 1.1 payer mix;\n- 3.1 receivable provisions.\n\n**Pruned:** 1.4 (occupancy is stable at 79-81%); 2.4 (explained by a known tariff increase)."}]}, {"id": "hypothesis-tree", "pack": "frame", "title": "Hypothesis Tree", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Start from a likely answer, then list exactly what must be true and how you would prove or kill it.", "tryp": "Hypothesis: we should exit our retail banking business in Country X. Build the hypothesis tree and the tests.", "when": ["You have an informed 'day-one answer' and want to test it fast", "Time is short and you cannot analyse everything", "You need to show a sponsor what evidence would change the recommendation"], "inputs": ["The question and your best current answer", "What is known already, and what data is available", "Time available"], "pairs": ["issue-tree", "research-plan", "red-team-review", "pre-mortem"], "body": "# Hypothesis Tree\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** speed with discipline. You start from your best answer, then try hard to prove it wrong, so you converge on the truth faster and can show exactly what the recommendation rests on.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **State a complete answer early,** as a recommendation with a reason, not a topic. \"We should exit retail banking in Country X because returns won't exceed the cost of equity\" is a hypothesis; \"retail banking performance\" is not.\n2. **Claims must be necessary and sufficient.** Each supporting claim is necessary (if it fails, the hypothesis weakens materially), and together they suffice.\n3. **Pre-commit to thresholds.** Write what number confirms and what number kills each claim before you look.\n4. **Seek disconfirmation.** At least one test per key claim should be designed to break it. Confirmation bias is the biggest risk in this method.\n5. **Keep a real alternative alive.** Name the alternative hypothesis and the evidence that would favour it.\n6. **Update visibly.** Mark claims confirmed, killed or reshaped as evidence arrives, and change the answer if needed.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The key question and decision; the time available.\n- What is known already; expert intuition; available data.\n- Stakeholder positions (where bias may come from).\n\n## Method\n1. **Governing hypothesis:** answer the key question in one sentence, with \"because\".\n2. **Supporting claims (3-5):** what must be true. Typical structure:\n   - **Desirability:** is it worth it?\n   - **Feasibility:** can we do it?\n   - **Viability:** does it pay?\n   - **Risk:** is it within appetite?\n   - **Alternatives:** is it better than the options?\n3. **For each claim:**\n   - the test (analysis);\n   - data needed and source;\n   - confirmation threshold;\n   - kill criterion;\n   - owner and time.\n4. **Prioritise:** test first the claims that are most likely to be wrong and most damaging if wrong (fragility × consequence).\n5. **Design disconfirmation:** for the top claims, specify the evidence that would prove them false, and actively look for it (e.g. interview lost customers, not just happy ones).\n6. **Alternative hypothesis:** write it in full; note the evidence that would favour it.\n7. **\"What would change our mind\":** a short list for the sponsor.\n8. **Evidence log:** per claim, results, source, date, and status (confirmed / killed / reshaped).\n9. **Update the governing hypothesis** as needed; communicate changes promptly.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Hypotheses are tools, not positions.** Teams that \"own\" a hypothesis defend it. Rotate a devil's advocate.\n- **Early answers can anchor.** If the evidence is thin, generate 2-3 competing hypotheses and test their key distinguishing claims first.\n- **Good-enough evidence:** set the precision needed for the decision; don't over-research claims that can't flip the answer.\n- **Qualitative claims** (e.g. \"the team can execute\") still need tests: references, track record, pilot results.\n\n## Red flags\n- A hypothesis that is a topic or a question.\n- Claims that are interesting but not necessary.\n- No kill criteria; thresholds set after seeing the data.\n- Only confirming evidence sought.\n- An alternative hypothesis that is a straw man.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Investment / M&A due diligence:** claims on the market, competitive position, management, synergies, valuation and risks; kill criteria tied to price.\n- **Market entry:** desirability (demand), feasibility (licences, partners), viability (unit economics), risk (regulation).\n- **Turnaround:** a root-cause hypothesis, then a fix hypothesis with measurable milestones.\n- **Product / AI pilot:** a value hypothesis with pilot metrics as tests (see `ai-pilot-design`).\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Hypothesis Tree: [key question]\n**Governing hypothesis:** We should ... because ...\n\n| # | Supporting claim (must be true) | Test / analysis | Data / source | Confirms if | Kills if | Fragility × consequence | Owner / date | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Disconfirmation tests:** ...\n**Alternative hypothesis:** ... (evidence that would favour it: ...)\n**What would change our mind:** ...\n**Evidence log:**\n| Date | Claim | Finding | Source | Status change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The governing hypothesis is a full answer with \"because\".\n- [ ] Claims are necessary and together sufficient.\n- [ ] Every claim has thresholds and a kill criterion, set in advance.\n- [ ] Testing is prioritised by fragility × consequence.\n- [ ] Explicit disconfirmation tests and a real alternative hypothesis.\n- [ ] An evidence log, and a process for updating the answer.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Framing reflects only the context you provide. Confirm the problem definition with your sponsor and stakeholders.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `issue-tree`: Break a big question into a logical tree of smaller questions you can actually answer.\n- `research-plan`: Plan exactly what to find out, from which sources, in what order, before searching anything.\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n- `pre-mortem`: Imagine the project has failed, explain why, and fix those causes now.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Hypothesis-driven problem solving:** a standard approach in strategy consulting and business-school problem-solving courses (state an answer, define what must be true, test the critical claims first).\n- **Falsification:** the scientific principle that hypotheses should be framed so that evidence can disprove them.\n- **Cognitive biases:** confirmation bias, anchoring and motivated reasoning in decision-making; debiasing with pre-set criteria, devil's advocates and red teams.\n- **\"What would have to be true\":** a strategy-practice technique for making assumptions explicit and testable.\n- **Desirability, feasibility, viability:** a common lens from innovation and design practice."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional regional bank with a sub-scale retail-banking operation in a neighbouring country)\n\n**Key question:** what should we do with our retail-banking business in Country X (42 branches, 3% market share, ROE 6% vs 13% cost of equity)?\n\n**Governing hypothesis:** we should exit retail banking in Country X via a sale to a local player within 18 months, because a credible plan cannot lift ROE above the cost of equity within 3 years, and a buyer will pay at least book value.\n\n| # | Claim | Test | Data | Confirms if | Kills if | F×C | Owner | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Standalone ROE stays < 13% through FY29 even with a cost plan | Bottom-up turnaround model (cost, pricing, digital migration) | Finance, benchmarks | Best credible case ROE < 11% | Credible plan reaches ≥13% by FY29 | High × High → **first** | CFO | Testing |\n| 2 | A buyer will pay ≥1.0x book | Confidential soundings with 4-6 potential buyers; transaction comparables | Advisers, deal data | ≥2 indications ≥1.0x | All indications < 0.8x | High × High | Strategy | Not started |\n| 3 | Exit doesn't damage group strategy (corporate clients, trade finance) | Revenue linkage analysis; client interviews | CRM, interviews | <5% of group revenue at risk | >10% at risk | Medium × High | Head Corporate | Not started |\n| 4 | Regulatory approval is obtainable within 12 months | Counsel view; regulator precedent | Legal | Clear precedent | Material conditions or delay > 18 months | Medium × Medium | General Counsel | Not started |\n| 5 | Separation costs are < 15% of proceeds | IT and operations separation estimate | IT, Operations | < 15% | > 25% | Medium × Medium | COO | Not started |\n\n**Disconfirmation tests:**\n- For claim 1, the turnaround is modelled by a team **not** involved in the exit recommendation, and the best-performing peer is benchmarked to see what's possible.\n- For claim 3, interview the 20 largest corporate clients specifically about the risk of losing retail services.\n\n**Alternative hypothesis:** keep the business and refocus on affluent customers and digital channels (close 25 branches), because the affluent segment earns 18% ROE.\n*Evidence that would favour it:* segment-level ROE confirming ≥15% for affluent customers, and digital adoption ≥40%.\n\n**What would change our mind:**\n- A credible plan to ≥13% ROE.\n- Buyer indications < 0.8x book.\n- A strategic dependency of corporate clients on retail.\n\n**Evidence log (week 3):**\n| Date | Claim | Finding | Source | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 18 Mar | 1 | Best credible case ROE 10.5% by FY29; affluent segment ROE 16% | Turnaround model v2 | Confirmed overall; **alternative partly supported** |\n| 22 Mar | 3 | 3 of 20 corporate clients say retail access matters (~₹40 Cr revenue) | Interviews | Reshaped: need a referral agreement with the buyer |"}]}, {"id": "mece-check", "pack": "frame", "title": "MECE Check", "time": "5 min", "summary": "Audit any list, structure or storyline for overlaps and gaps, then fix it.", "tryp": "Check this for MECE: our growth levers are 1) new customers 2) digital 3) pricing 4) upselling 5) Asia expansion 6) partnerships.", "when": ["Reviewing a structure, workstream list, agenda or set of options", "A slide lists 'key reasons' or 'key drivers' that feel messy", "Before you assign work to people (overlap = duplicated effort)"], "inputs": ["The list or structure to check", "What it is meant to cover (the 'whole')"], "pairs": ["issue-tree", "pyramid-structure", "customer-segmentation", "deck-quality-check"], "body": "# MECE Check\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a structure where every item has exactly one home and nothing is missing. That means no duplicated work, no blind spots, and an argument that holds together.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Define the \"whole\" first.** You can't test exhaustiveness without knowing what the list is meant to cover (e.g. \"all sources of revenue growth in FY27\", \"all costs in the P&L\").\n2. **One logic per level.** Most MECE failures come from mixing dimensions (channel vs region vs customer type) in one list.\n3. **Test with real examples.** Push 10-20 real cases through the structure: each should land in exactly one bucket.\n4. **Exhaustive within materiality.** A small \"other\" bucket is fine if it is sized and immaterial. A large \"other\" is a design failure.\n5. **Same level of abstraction.** Siblings should be comparable (\"Asia\" next to \"Mumbai\" is a level error).\n6. **MECE serves usefulness.** A perfectly MECE structure that doesn't help the decision is still wrong. Pick the logic that best supports the decision.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- What is the structure for (analysis, work allocation, a storyline, an org design)?\n- What is the whole?\n- What real cases or data can be used to test it?\n\n## Method\n1. **State the whole** and its boundary (time, scope).\n2. **Identify the logic** of the current items (formula, process, segment, stakeholder, time, other). Flag mixed logics.\n3. **Overlap test:** for each pair of items, can one real example belong to both? Record each overlap with an example.\n4. **Gap test:** brainstorm edge cases and push real data through. Record anything that fits nowhere, with a rough size.\n5. **Level test:** are siblings at the same level of detail?\n6. **Redesign:** choose the logic that best serves the purpose:\n   - **mathematical** (adds or multiplies to the whole);\n   - **process** (sequential stages);\n   - **segment** (non-overlapping definitions with explicit rules);\n   - **binary** (A / not-A);\n   - **framework** (with stated assumptions).\n   Write explicit inclusion rules for segments.\n7. **Map** every original item to its new home; flag items that were really cross-cutting themes (handle these as enablers or lenses, not buckets).\n8. **Re-test** the new structure with the same cases.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Cross-cutting themes** (digital, AI, sustainability) often break MECE when listed alongside functional buckets. Treat them as lenses applied across buckets, or as enablers.\n- **Organisational designs can't always be MECE** (matrix structures). Make overlaps explicit with decision rights (see `raci-builder`).\n- **Storylines:** supporting arguments should be MECE relative to the claim. Overlapping reasons weaken credibility.\n- **Don't let MECE purity paralyse.** For brainstorming, generate freely first; structure afterwards.\n\n## Red flags\n- No defined whole.\n- Lists mixing dimensions (\"new customers, digital, pricing, Asia, partnerships\").\n- A large \"other\" bucket.\n- Siblings at different levels.\n- Segment definitions without inclusion rules (e.g. \"SME\" with no size threshold).\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Workstreams:** test with real activities. Each activity has exactly one owner workstream; cross-cutting enablers are listed separately.\n- **Customer segmentation:** rules-based, non-overlapping definitions, plus a test on the customer file.\n- **Agendas:** each topic once; decisions vs updates separated.\n- **Option sets:** options should be mutually exclusive choices; if they can be combined, say so and present combinations explicitly.\n- **Risk categories:** align to a standard taxonomy; map overlaps (e.g. cyber vs operational) with a primary-category rule.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## MECE Check: [structure]\n**The whole:** ...  |  **Purpose:** ...  |  **Verdict:** Pass / Needs work - why\n\n| Issue | Type (overlap / gap / mixed logic / level) | Example | Size / impact |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Proposed structure (logic: ...)**\n1. ... (inclusion rule: ...)\n2. ...\nCross-cutting lenses / enablers: ...\n\n**Mapping:** original item → new home\n**Re-test with cases:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The whole and the purpose are defined.\n- [ ] Overlaps and gaps are shown with real examples and sizes.\n- [ ] Mixed logic and level issues are diagnosed.\n- [ ] The new structure uses one logic per level, with inclusion rules.\n- [ ] Every original item is mapped; cross-cutting themes are handled as lenses.\n- [ ] Re-tested with cases.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Framing reflects only the context you provide. Confirm the problem definition with your sponsor and stakeholders.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `issue-tree`: Break a big question into a logical tree of smaller questions you can actually answer.\n- `pyramid-structure`: Structure any argument top-down: answer first, grouped support, evidence below.\n- `customer-segmentation`: Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.\n- `deck-quality-check`: A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **MECE principle:** a core structured-thinking principle in business problem solving and executive communication.\n- **Classification and taxonomy design:** mutually exclusive categories with explicit inclusion rules, from information science and statistics (e.g. industry classification systems).\n- **Revenue and profit decomposition:** management-accounting identities used as the most robust MECE logic.\n- **Risk taxonomies:** standard enterprise-risk category structures (e.g. per ISO 31000-aligned frameworks) with primary-category rules."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional consumer-durables company, FY27 growth levers)\n\n**Original list:**\n1. New customers\n2. Digital\n3. Pricing\n4. Upselling\n5. South-East Asia expansion\n6. Partnerships\n7. Premium range\n\n**The whole:** all sources of incremental revenue in FY27 vs FY26.\n**Purpose:** assign growth workstreams with targets.\n**Verdict:** Needs work. Mixed logic (customer type, channel, price, geography, route, product), with overlaps.\n\n| Issue | Type | Example | Size / impact |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"Digital\" overlaps \"New customers\" and \"Upselling\" | Overlap | An online first-time buyer counts in both | Double-counted targets |\n| \"Partnerships\" is a route, not a source | Mixed logic | A retail partner could drive new customers in SE Asia | — |\n| \"Premium range\" overlaps \"Upselling\" and \"Pricing\" | Overlap | Existing customer trades up to premium | — |\n| Retention of existing customers missing | Gap | Replacement purchases by lapsed customers (~25% of revenue) | Large |\n| Price/mix vs volume not separated | Mixed logic | — | Targets not additive |\n\n**Proposed structure (logic: revenue = customers × purchases per customer × revenue per purchase, by market):**\n1. **Win new customers** (rule: first purchase in the last 24 months), in India and new markets.\n2. **Retain and reactivate** (rule: purchased 2-5 years ago; replacement cycle).\n3. **Increase revenue per purchase:**\n   3a. Price.\n   3b. Mix / premium.\n   3c. Attach (accessories, extended warranties).\n\n**Markets as a second dimension:** India (existing), SE Asia (new). Targets are set in a lever × market grid.\n**Cross-cutting enablers (lenses, not buckets):** digital channels, partnerships, the AI-driven personalisation engine.\n\n**Mapping:**\n- New customers → 1.\n- Digital → enabler (applies to 1, 2, 3c).\n- Pricing → 3a.\n- Upselling → 3b/3c.\n- SE Asia → market dimension.\n- Partnerships → enabler.\n- Premium range → 3b.\n\n**Re-test:** 20 sample transactions each land in exactly one lever × market cell. The lever totals add up to the revenue bridge."}]}, {"id": "stakeholder-map", "pack": "frame", "title": "Stakeholder Map", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Map who matters, where they stand, and the plan to move each of them.", "tryp": "Map stakeholders for rolling out a shared-services finance centre across 6 business units. The BU CFOs are sceptical.", "when": ["At project start, or when a decision needs buy-in from many people", "When an initiative stalls and you suspect politics", "Before a board or SteerCo where someone may block"], "inputs": ["The initiative and the decision needed", "Names or roles of people involved", "What you know of each person's position"], "pairs": ["change-plan", "audience-brief", "raci-builder", "negotiation-prep"], "body": "# Stakeholder Map\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear map of who can make or break the initiative, what they care about, where they stand today, and a specific plan to move the important ones, owned by named people and reviewed as things change.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Influence ≠ seniority.** Informal influencers (respected experts, union leaders, long-tenured managers, the CEO's chief of staff) often shape outcomes more than titles.\n2. **Interests explain positions.** Understand what each person gains or loses (remit, budget, status, workload, job security, bonus) before trying to persuade them.\n3. **Stances are dynamic.** People move as information, politics and personal impact change. Map current and target stance, and review regularly.\n4. **Coalitions win.** Identify sponsors and champions early and give them roles. Plan how to convert, neutralise or work around blockers ethically.\n5. **The messenger matters as much as the message.** Choose who engages whom, based on relationships and credibility.\n6. **Respect and transparency.** Engagement means listening and adapting the plan, not just managing perceptions.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The initiative, its decision points and timeline.\n- Who is affected, positively and negatively (internal and external)?\n- Formal governance (approvers, committees) and informal networks.\n- History: past initiatives and how people reacted.\n\n## Method\n1. **Identify stakeholders** broadly:\n   - decision-makers;\n   - approvers (boards, committees, regulators);\n   - influencers;\n   - implementers;\n   - affected groups (employees, unions, customers, suppliers, communities);\n   - external parties (investors, media).\n2. **Assess each:**\n   - **influence** (H/M/L; formal and informal);\n   - **interest or impact** (H/M/L);\n   - **current stance:** champion / supporter / neutral / sceptic / blocker;\n   - **target stance;**\n   - **gains and losses;**\n   - **key concerns** (in their words);\n   - **what they need to see.**\n3. **Map relationships:** who listens to whom; trusted advisers; alliances and rivalries; influence paths to key decision-makers.\n4. **Prioritise:** the high-influence stakeholders whose current stance is below target come first.\n5. **Engagement plan per priority stakeholder:** objective; message framed in their interests; evidence; concessions or design changes you can offer; messenger; channel (1:1, workshop, steering role); timing; owner.\n6. **Coalition design:** roles for champions (co-presenting, leading pilots); a sponsor's visible actions; how sceptics are involved (design input, pilot evaluation).\n7. **Group-level plans** for large affected populations (see `change-plan`).\n8. **Review rhythm:** update stances after key meetings and milestones; track the movement.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Sceptics often have valid points.** Incorporate their concerns into the design; converted sceptics become credible advocates.\n- **Don't over-rely on the sponsor's authority.** Mandated compliance without buy-in tends to unravel after the sponsor moves on.\n- **Map losers honestly.** People losing remit or jobs deserve direct, early, respectful communication and support, not spin.\n- **Sensitive information:** keep stakeholder maps confidential and factual. They describe positions and interests, not personal judgements.\n\n## Red flags\n- Only senior names; no informal influencers or affected groups.\n- Stances assumed rather than tested in conversation.\n- One message for everyone.\n- No owner per relationship.\n- The map never updated after the kickoff.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **M&A integration:** both organisations' leaders, key talent, customers at risk, regulators, unions.\n- **Reorganisation / GCC set-up:** affected managers, onshore teams whose work moves, HR and legal, works councils where applicable.\n- **Policy / regulatory change:** regulators, industry bodies, consumer groups, media.\n- **Technology / AI roll-out:** frontline users, middle managers (adoption gatekeepers), IT security, risk and compliance, employee representatives.\n- **Board decisions:** individual directors' perspectives, committee chairs, major shareholders.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Stakeholder Map: [initiative], [date]\n\n| Stakeholder | Influence | Interest / impact | Stance now → target | Gains / loses | Key concern (their words) | Needs to see | Relationship owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Grid:** Manage closely (high influence, below target): ... | Leverage (high influence, supportive): ... | Keep informed: ... | Monitor: ...\n**Influence paths:** ...\n**Coalition:** sponsor actions ... | champion roles ... | sceptic involvement ...\n\n**Engagement plan (priority stakeholders)**\n| Who | Objective | Message (in their interests) | Evidence / concession | Messenger | Channel | When | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Review rhythm:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Identification is broad, including informal influencers and affected groups.\n- [ ] Interests (gains and losses) explain each stance.\n- [ ] Current and target stances; priorities are clear.\n- [ ] Tailored messages, messengers and channels, with owners.\n- [ ] Coalition roles for sponsors and champions; sceptics are involved.\n- [ ] A review rhythm; confidentiality noted.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Framing reflects only the context you provide. Confirm the problem definition with your sponsor and stakeholders.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n- `audience-brief`: Profile who you are persuading: what they care about, what they already believe, and what would change their mind.\n- `raci-builder`: Clarify who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each activity or decision.\n- `negotiation-prep`: Prepare a negotiation: interests, BATNA, ZOPA, trade-offs, opening position and concession plan.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts and public standards)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Stakeholder analysis:** power-interest (influence-impact) grids and salience concepts, from project-management standards and strategic-management curricula.\n- **ISO 21502:2020** (project, programme and portfolio management: guidance on project management), stakeholder engagement sections.\n- **Organisational network analysis:** identifying informal influence and brokers.\n- **Change-management practice:** sponsorship, coalition building and resistance management (see the `change-plan` skill).\n- **Employment and consultation law:** for affected employees, follow applicable labour law and consultation requirements (verify with HR and counsel)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional group consolidating finance operations from 6 business units into a shared-services centre)\n\n| Stakeholder | Influence | Impact | Stance → target | Gains / loses | Key concern | Needs to see | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Group CFO (sponsor) | H | H | Champion → Champion | Gains cost and control | \"Will BU CFOs really let go?\" | BU commitment | Programme director |\n| BU CFO, Retail | H | H | Sceptic → Neutral | Loses 14 FTE, direct control | \"Service quality will drop and my close will slip\" | SLA guarantees, opt-back clause | Group CFO |\n| BU CFO, Manufacturing | H | H | Neutral → Supporter | Loses 9 FTE; gains a standardised close | \"Plant accounting is too specialised\" | Plant-accounting expertise retained in the centre | Group Controller |\n| BU CFO, Services | M | M | Supporter → Champion | Gains scalability for growth | — | Early wave and a co-design role | Programme director |\n| Finance staff in BUs (60 people) | M (collectively) | H | Anxious / sceptic → Neutral | Job changes, relocation | \"Will I lose my job or have to move?\" | Clear options: redeployment, transfer, support | CHRO + BU heads |\n| Internal Audit Head | M | M | Neutral → Supporter | Gains standard controls | \"Segregation of duties during transition\" | Control design and transition testing | CRO |\n| CIO | H | M | Neutral → Supporter | More ERP work, no budget | \"Another unfunded mandate\" | Funded ERP harmonisation budget | Group CFO |\n| Employee representatives (2 plants) | M | H | Unknown → Neutral | — | Job security | Early consultation, redeployment commitments | CHRO |\n\n**Influence paths:**\n- The Retail BU CFO trusts the Group Controller (former colleague) more than the programme team.\n- The CEO listens closely to the Manufacturing BU head on operational risk.\n\n**Coalition:**\n- **The Group CFO announces personally** and chairs the monthly SteerCo.\n- **The Services BU CFO goes in wave 1** and presents the results at the leadership forum.\n- **The Retail CFO is invited to co-design the SLA framework.**\n\n**Engagement plan (extract):**\n| Who | Objective | Message | Evidence / concession | Messenger | Channel | When | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Retail BU CFO | Neutral by wave-2 decision | \"You keep control of decisions; we take the transactions, with guaranteed service\" | SLA with penalties; 6-month opt-back; Retail-dedicated team lead | Group Controller, then the Group CFO | 1:1, then the SLA workshop | Weeks 2-4 | Group CFO |\n| BU finance staff | Reduce anxiety; retain key talent | \"Clear options for everyone, decided with you\" | Options window; retention bonus for critical roles; reskilling | BU CFO + HR partner | Town halls, then 1:1s | Week 3 onward | CHRO |\n| CIO | Supporter | \"Funded ERP harmonisation that also fixes your data issues\" | ₹6 Cr ring-fenced budget | Group CFO | 1:1 | Week 1 | Group CFO |\n\n**Review:** stances updated after every SteerCo; the map is shared only with the core programme team."}]}, {"id": "engagement-workplan", "pack": "frame", "title": "Engagement Workplan", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Turn the issue tree into a week-by-week plan: analyses, owners, data, meetings and deliverables.", "tryp": "Build a 6-week workplan for a pricing review across 3 product lines, team of 4, SteerCo every 2 weeks.", "when": ["Kicking off a project, study or engagement", "Re-planning after scope change", "Showing a sponsor how the work will reach an answer on time"], "inputs": ["Key question and issue tree (or hypotheses)", "Duration, team size, roles", "Fixed dates: SteerCos, board, budget cycle"], "pairs": ["issue-tree", "research-plan", "ghost-deck", "steerco-update"], "body": "# Engagement Workplan\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a plan that reaches a defensible answer on a fixed date. Every analysis traces to the question, data is requested early, a draft storyline exists well before the end, and the sponsor is engaged at the right moments.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Plan backwards from the decision date.** Final deliverable → draft storyline → key analyses → data receipt → kick-off.\n2. **Every analysis must earn its place** by answering a leaf of the issue or hypothesis tree. Interesting-but-irrelevant analyses get cut.\n3. **Answer first.** A draft storyline (ghost deck) by roughly a quarter to a third of the timeline focuses the team and surfaces gaps early.\n4. **Data is the usual critical path.** Request on day 1-2, with owners and dates; have fallbacks (proxies, samples).\n5. **Sponsor touchpoints have a purpose:** align, test hypotheses, decide. No surprises at the final meeting.\n6. **Capacity is real.** Match workload to people, including client or business counterparts; keep a 10-20% buffer.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Problem statement, key question, decision date and audience.\n- The issue or hypothesis tree (or create one now).\n- Team (size, skills), business counterparts, availability.\n- Data availability and access lead times; fixed dates (board, budget, SteerCos, holidays).\n\n## Method\n1. **Workstreams:** group the tree's leaves into 3-5 workstreams, each with a lead and a counterpart.\n2. **Analysis plan per workstream:** analysis → question answered → input data → method → output exhibit → owner → effort → due date.\n3. **Data request list:** item, source system or owner, format, period, due date, status, fallback.\n4. **Milestones (working backwards):**\n   - **Final:** decision meeting and pre-reads (e.g. 7 days before).\n   - **Draft final storyline** about 2 weeks before.\n   - **Interim findings or hypothesis test** at the midpoint.\n   - **Ghost deck v1** at 25-33%.\n   - **Data received** in the first 20-25%.\n   - **Kick-off** in week 1.\n5. **Governance:** sponsor check-ins (weekly, 30 min), SteerCos (purpose and decisions per meeting), team rhythm (daily stand-up; weekly problem-solving session).\n6. **Critical path and buffers:** identify the dependencies (data, interviews, approvals); add a 10-20% buffer.\n7. **Risks to the plan:** data delays, stakeholder availability, scope creep, analytical dead-ends; mitigations and triggers.\n8. **Communication plan:** who gets what, when (status notes, pre-wiring before SteerCos).\n9. **Visual plan:** a week-by-week table by workstream.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Protect the end.** The last 20% of time is for synthesis, storyline and pre-wiring, not new analysis.\n- **Ruthless scoping.** New questions mid-stream go to a \"parked\" list unless the sponsor trades something off.\n- **Start interviews early;** calendars are the hidden critical path.\n- **Use the first SteerCo to test hypotheses,** not to present a methodology.\n\n## Red flags\n- Analyses not linked to the tree.\n- The storyline first drafted in the final week.\n- Data requested in week 3.\n- SteerCos without decisions to make.\n- No buffer; the team over-allocated.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Internal strategy studies:** include budget-cycle dates and functional reviewers; pre-wire ExCo members.\n- **Due diligence (2-4 weeks):** data room access is the critical path; daily client calls; red-flag reporting early.\n- **Transformation diagnostics:** site visits and interviews; quick wins identified in parallel.\n- **AI discovery sprints:** workshops, data-readiness checks, use-case scoring, pilot designs (see the AI pack).\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Workplan: [project], [start] - [decision date]\n**Key question:** ...  |  **Sponsor:** ...  |  **Decision meeting:** ...\n\n### Week-by-week\n| Week | WS1 | WS2 | WS3 | Milestones / meetings |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Analysis plan\n| Workstream | Analysis | Answers (tree leaf) | Data | Output exhibit | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Data request list\n| Item | Owner / source | Format / period | Due | Status | Fallback |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Governance and rhythm\n### Critical path, buffer and risks\n| Risk | Trigger | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Planned backwards from the decision date.\n- [ ] Every analysis maps to a tree leaf and produces an exhibit.\n- [ ] Ghost deck by about a third of the timeline; the final 20% protected.\n- [ ] Data requests in week 1, with owners and fallbacks.\n- [ ] Meetings have purposes and decisions; a buffer is included.\n- [ ] Plan risks and mitigations stated.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Framing reflects only the context you provide. Confirm the problem definition with your sponsor and stakeholders.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `issue-tree`: Break a big question into a logical tree of smaller questions you can actually answer.\n- `research-plan`: Plan exactly what to find out, from which sources, in what order, before searching anything.\n- `ghost-deck`: Draft the whole deck as titles and sketched exhibits before doing the analysis, so the work stays focused.\n- `steerco-update`: Write a crisp steering committee update: status, decisions needed, risks, and next steps.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts and public standards)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Hypothesis-driven workplanning:** linking analyses to issue-tree leaves and drafting the storyline early, as taught in structured problem-solving and consulting training.\n- **Project planning fundamentals:** work breakdown, critical path, buffers and milestone planning (e.g. ISO 21502:2020 guidance on project management; professional project-management bodies' standards).\n- **Governance practice:** steering-committee purpose and decision-focused meetings.\n- **Data-request management:** standard practice in due diligence and analytics projects (owners, formats, fallbacks)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; 6-week pricing review for 3 product lines, team of 4, SteerCos every 2 weeks)\n\n**Key question:** what pricing changes across product lines A, B and C will raise gross margin by ≥2 points within 12 months without losing more than 3% of volume?\n**Sponsor:** CCO. **Decision meeting:** ExCo, end of week 6 (pre-read due end of week 5).\n\n| Week | WS1: Price realisation and leakage | WS2: Customer value and willingness to pay | WS3: Competitor and market | Milestones |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Data request; transaction data profiling | Interview guide; book 15 customer interviews | Competitor price scan plan | Kick-off; data request issued (day 2) |\n| 2 | Price waterfall by segment | Interviews 1-8 | Price benchmarks A, B | **Ghost deck v1 (end of week 2)**; sponsor check-in |\n| 3 | Discount variance analysis | Interviews 9-15; value drivers | Benchmarks C; competitor moves | **SteerCo 1:** test hypotheses; agree price-test scope |\n| 4 | Leakage sizing; policy options | Willingness-to-pay synthesis | Elasticity estimates (historic) | Interim findings |\n| 5 | Impact model (margin, volume) | Segment price recommendations | Risk of competitor response | **SteerCo 2:** options and trade-offs; pre-read drafted |\n| 6 | Final numbers | Rollout and communication plan | — | Pre-wiring (CFO, Head Sales); **ExCo decision** |\n\n**Analysis plan (extract):**\n| WS | Analysis | Answers | Data | Exhibit | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Price waterfall (list → invoice → pocket price) | Where is margin leaking? | 24 months of invoice lines, rebates, freight | Waterfall by segment | Analyst 1 | Wk 2 |\n| 1 | Discount dispersion vs deal size | Is discounting disciplined? | Same + CRM | Scatter: discount % vs volume | Analyst 1 | Wk 3 |\n| 2 | Value-driver interviews | What do customers pay for? | 15 interviews | Value map by segment | Consultant | Wk 4 |\n| 3 | Historic elasticity | What volume loss follows price moves? | 3 years of price changes and volumes | Elasticity by line | Analyst 2 | Wk 4 |\n\n**Data request:**\n| Item | Owner | Due | Fallback |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Invoice-level transactions (24 months) | Finance systems | Day 5 | 12-month extract |\n| Rebate agreements | Sales ops | Day 7 | Top 50 customers only |\n| Freight cost by shipment | Logistics | Day 7 | Average freight per km |\n\n**Critical path:** invoice data by day 5 (owner: Finance systems lead; escalate to the CFO if late).\n**Buffer:** 2 days in week 5.\n\n**Risks:**\n| Risk | Trigger | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interview no-shows | <10 interviews booked by week 2 | Sales to escalate; add a short survey |\n| Elasticity data too noisy | No usable price changes | Use willingness-to-pay ranges plus a 2-region price test proposal |"}]}, {"id": "research-plan", "pack": "evidence", "title": "Research Plan", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Plan exactly what to find out, from which sources, in what order, before searching anything.", "tryp": "Plan the research to understand the Indian cold-chain logistics market for a possible investment. I have 2 weeks.", "when": ["Before desk research, due diligence or a briefing", "When research tends to sprawl and eat time", "To brief an analyst or junior team member"], "inputs": ["The question or hypothesis the research serves", "Time available", "Sources you have access to (databases, experts, internal data)"], "pairs": ["source-validation", "research-synthesis", "interview-guide", "market-sizing"], "body": "# Research Plan\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** research that answers the decision's questions in the time available, from sources you can trust, with every key number cross-checked and traceable, instead of hours of interesting-but-irrelevant reading.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **The decision defines the questions.** List the 5-12 questions whose answers would change the decision. Everything else is optional.\n2. **Classify each question.** *Facts* are findable; *estimates* need triangulation; *judgements* need expert or customer input. Each needs a different method.\n3. **Right source for the question.** Primary and official sources beat secondary summaries; recent beats old; independent beats self-interested.\n4. **Precision fit for purpose.** ±20% may be enough for a go or no-go; a price decision may need ±5%. Don't over-research.\n5. **Triangulate what matters.** Any number that drives the decision needs at least two independent sources, or a source plus a sanity check.\n6. **Time-box and sequence.** Attack the biggest unknowns first; stop when the answer is good enough.\n7. **Ethics and compliance.** Respect expert-network rules, confidentiality, data protection and copyright.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision, the deadline and the audience.\n- What is already known (internal data, past studies).\n- Access: databases, subscriptions, internal systems, expert networks, customers.\n- Constraints: confidentiality (can't reveal the client or intent), budget, language, geography.\n\n## Method\n1. **Decision and key questions:** derive them from the issue or hypothesis tree; tie each to the decision.\n2. **Classify** each as fact, estimate or judgement.\n3. **Source plan per question:**\n   - **Official and statistical:** census, national surveys, regulators, central bank, trade data.\n   - **Corporate:** annual reports, investor presentations, filings.\n   - **Industry:** associations, trade publications.\n   - **Academic and think-tank** research.\n   - **Internal:** CRM, finance, operations.\n   - **Experts:** former industry executives, academics.\n   - **Customers:** interviews, surveys.\n   - **Field:** store visits, mystery shopping.\n4. **Search strings and starting points:** specific queries and source categories for each question.\n5. **Precision needed** and **triangulation plan** (which 2+ sources per critical number).\n6. **Time boxes** and an **order of attack:** biggest uncertainties and longest lead times (expert calls, surveys, data requests) first.\n7. **Source log:** claim | value | source | date | method / definition | reliability grade | link or location.\n8. **Ethics and compliance:** no misrepresentation; expert-call compliance (no confidential or price-sensitive information); data protection for personal data; licence terms for paid content.\n9. **Stop rule:** when each critical question reaches the needed precision, or the time box ends; record residual uncertainty.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Secondary summaries are leads, not evidence.** Trace numbers to their primary source.\n- **Beware definitional drift** across sources (e.g. \"SME\" definitions, gross vs net figures). Record the definitions.\n- **When data doesn't exist,** design a quick primary method (5-10 expert calls, a small survey, a field visit) rather than relying on weak secondary estimates.\n- **Note what would change the answer,** and prioritise research there.\n\n## Red flags\n- Questions not linked to the decision.\n- A single source for the key number.\n- Undated sources; unknown definitions.\n- Expert calls planned without compliance guardrails.\n- No time boxes; research continues past the decision date.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Due diligence:** a data-room-first plan, plus management sessions, expert calls, customer references, red-flag checks.\n- **Market entry:** regulatory and licensing, market size, competitors, channels, local partners, costs.\n- **Board briefing:** authoritative sources, concise synthesis, dated facts.\n- **Policy or regulatory analysis:** primary legal texts, regulator consultations, enforcement actions.\n- **Technology scouting:** standards bodies, academic preprints, product documentation, pilot evidence.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Research Plan: [topic], for [decision], by [date]\n\n| # | Question | Type (fact / estimate / judgement) | Best sources | Starting searches | Precision needed | Triangulation | Time box | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Order of attack:** ...\n**Source log format:** claim | value | source | date | definition | grade | link\n**Ethics and compliance rules:** ...\n**Stop rule:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Every question links to the decision; types are classified.\n- [ ] Sources fit each question; primary and official sources prioritised.\n- [ ] Precision targets and triangulation for critical numbers.\n- [ ] Time boxes and sequencing; long-lead items first.\n- [ ] Source log and ethics rules defined; a stop rule.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Research quality depends on the sources available to Claude in your session. Verify key facts against primary sources.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `source-validation`: Grade every source and claim for reliability so you never put a weak number in front of a board.\n- `research-synthesis`: Turn piles of notes, reports and links into a few clear findings with evidence and implications.\n- `interview-guide`: Write a crisp guide for expert, customer or employee interviews that gets real answers, not opinions.\n- `market-sizing`: Size a market top-down and bottom-up, reconcile the two, and show every assumption.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public data types and widely taught practice)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Research design:** linking research questions to decisions; triangulation; primary vs secondary research, as taught in business-research-methods curricula.\n- **Official data (India; check the latest):** national statistics office releases; ministry dashboards; regulators (RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, TRAI and others); Ministry of Corporate Affairs filings; trade statistics.\n- **International:** national statistics agencies; multilateral databases.\n- **Expert-network compliance:** standard practice to avoid material non-public and confidential information; insider-trading regulations (e.g. SEBI PIT Regulations).\n- **Data protection and copyright:** DPDP Act 2023 for personal data; licence terms for subscription content."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; screening an investment in Indian cold-chain logistics, 2 weeks)\n\n**Decision:** go or no-go on a detailed diligence of 3 cold-chain operators (investment committee in 2 weeks).\n\n| # | Question | Type | Best sources | Starting searches | Precision | Triangulation | Time | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Cold-storage capacity and utilisation by region | Fact / estimate | Agriculture-ministry and horticulture-board statistics; warehousing regulator data; operator filings | \"cold storage capacity state-wise [year]\"; \"cold chain utilisation India\" | ±20% | Govt data + sum of top-10 operator capacity | 2 days | Analyst 1 |\n| 2 | Demand growth: pharma, dairy, quick commerce, seafood exports | Estimate | Sector-ministry data; export statistics; listed companies' disclosures | \"vaccine cold chain capacity India\"; \"dairy processing growth\" | ±25% | 2 sectors bottom-up + industry-body estimate | 3 days | Analyst 2 |\n| 3 | Unit economics: revenue per pallet, utilisation, energy cost share | Fact / estimate | Operator filings; 4 expert calls (former operations heads) | — | ±15% | Filings + experts | 3 days | Lead |\n| 4 | Regulatory incentives and standards (subsidies, energy, food-safety rules) | Fact | Official scheme guidelines; food-safety regulator | \"cold chain subsidy scheme guidelines [year]\" | Exact | Official text only | 1 day | Analyst 1 |\n| 5 | Competitive intensity and consolidation | Judgement | Experts; filings; deal databases | — | Qualitative | 4 experts + deal history | 2 days | Lead |\n| 6 | Key risks (power reliability, technology obsolescence, customer concentration) | Judgement | Experts; operator risk disclosures | — | Qualitative | ≥3 independent views | 1 day | Lead |\n\n**Order of attack:** book expert calls on day 1 (longest lead); Q4 official texts; Q1 and Q2 data; Q3 after the first expert calls.\n\n**Source log format:** claim | value | source | date | definition (e.g. \"capacity in million tonnes, multi-commodity\") | grade A/B/C | link.\n\n**Ethics:**\n- Expert calls through a compliant network.\n- No questions about current employers' confidential information.\n- Don't reveal the target names.\n\n**Stop rule:** an IC memo with Q1-Q3 at the stated precision by day 9; days 10-11 for synthesis."}]}, {"id": "source-validation", "pack": "evidence", "title": "Source Validation", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Grade every source and claim for reliability so you never put a weak number in front of a board.", "tryp": "Validate these claims for our deck: 1) the global AI market will be $1.8T by 2030 (from a blog) 2) 70% of transformations fail 3) our churn is 2x industry average.", "when": ["Before key facts go into a recommendation", "When sources disagree", "When a statistic sounds too neat (70% of X fail...)"], "inputs": ["The claims or numbers", "Where each came from (links or document names)"], "pairs": ["research-synthesis", "deck-quality-check", "board-paper", "red-team-review"], "body": "# Source Validation\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** protection from the embarrassing statistic. Every claim you present is traced to where it came from, judged on its merits, and worded to match what the evidence actually supports.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Trace to the original.** A number quoted in an article, report or deck is a lead. Find the primary source (study, filing, official dataset, regulation). If you can't, say so.\n2. **Judge the method, not the brand.** A famous publisher can still use a weak sample or a self-serving definition. Check the sample size, method, geography, period and who funded it.\n3. **Definitions must match your use.** \"AI market\", \"SME\", \"digital user\" and \"active customer\" vary widely between sources.\n4. **Recency matters by domain.** Technology and market figures age fast; demographic data ages slowly. Flag stale data.\n5. **Independence.** Vendor-funded surveys and advocacy reports need corroboration.\n6. **Calibrated wording.** Match verbs to confidence: \"shows\" (strong), \"suggests\" (moderate), \"one survey reports\" (weak).\n7. **Never fabricate.** If verification is impossible in this session, mark the claim unverified and say what to check.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The claims to validate (exact wording and the number).\n- Where each came from (link, document, person).\n- How the claims will be used (board decision, external publication, internal discussion). Higher stakes need a higher bar.\n\n## Method\n1. **List claims** verbatim, with their current source.\n2. **Trace to the original:** follow citations to the primary source; record the chain. Note misquotations or context loss (e.g. a \"70% of projects\" finding that came from a small, non-random sample).\n3. **Assess the source:**\n   - **Authority:** official statistics, audited filings, peer-reviewed work > industry surveys > vendor marketing and blogs.\n   - **Method:** sample size and selection, response rate, measurement, period, geography.\n   - **Definitions:** what exactly was measured.\n   - **Recency:** publication and data dates.\n   - **Independence:** funding and interests.\n4. **Corroborate:** find at least one independent source or sanity check (order-of-magnitude logic, triangulation).\n5. **Grade:**\n   - **A (strong):** primary, robust method, matching definition, corroborated.\n   - **B (usable with caveat):** reasonable but limited; state the caveat.\n   - **C (do not use as fact):** untraceable, weak method, conflicted, or contradicted.\n6. **Safe wording** per claim, matched to its grade; propose alternatives for grade C claims (another source, or reframing).\n7. **Log:** claim | original source | date | method notes | corroboration | grade | safe wording | to-verify actions.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Widely repeated does not mean true.** Many famous business statistics come from small or unclear studies.\n- **For contested topics,** present the range of credible estimates and why they differ.\n- **For legal or regulatory claims,** only official texts or authoritative legal commentary count. Note the date and status (draft vs notified).\n- **For internal numbers,** validate against the system of record and the owner (Finance for financials).\n\n## Red flags\n- Statistics with no traceable original.\n- Rounded \"magic numbers\" (70%, 80/20, 10x) with no method.\n- Definitions that don't match the use.\n- Vendor-funded studies presented as independent.\n- Old data presented as current.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board papers:** grade A or B only; caveats explicit; dates on all facts.\n- **External publications:** stricter standard; permissions for quotes and data; citations.\n- **Investment cases:** reconcile with audited data; disclose estimates.\n- **AI-generated content:** verify every factual claim and citation independently. AI can produce plausible but false references.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Source Validation: [document / topic]\n\n| # | Claim (verbatim) | Original source (chain) | Date | Method / definition notes | Corroboration | Grade | Safe wording |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Do not use (grade C):** ... → alternatives\n**Use with caveat (grade B):** ...\n**Actions to verify:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Every claim is traced to an original source, or marked \"original not found\".\n- [ ] Method, definitions, recency and independence assessed.\n- [ ] Corroboration attempted for decision-critical claims.\n- [ ] Grades assigned; grade C claims not used as fact.\n- [ ] Safe wording matches confidence; no invented sources.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Research quality depends on the sources available to Claude in your session. Verify key facts against primary sources.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `research-synthesis`: Turn piles of notes, reports and links into a few clear findings with evidence and implications.\n- `deck-quality-check`: A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.\n- `board-paper`: Write a board paper directors can read in 10 minutes: purpose, recommendation, analysis, risks and the resolution.\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Source evaluation:** information-literacy criteria (currency, relevance, authority, accuracy, purpose) taught in research curricula.\n- **Evidence hierarchies:** primary and official data, audited filings and peer-reviewed research ranked above secondary summaries and opinion.\n- **Statistical literacy:** sample size and selection, response bias, definitions, base rates.\n- **Fact-checking practice:** tracing claims to their origin; lateral reading (checking what other independent sources say).\n- **AI-generated content:** verify citations independently; generative models can produce plausible but non-existent references."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; claims proposed for a board deck on AI investment)\n\n| # | Claim | Original source (chain) | Date | Notes | Corroboration | Grade | Safe wording |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | \"The global AI market will be $1.8 trillion by 2030\" | Blog → press release from a market-research vendor; methodology paywalled | 2023 | Definition unclear (hardware, software, services?); vendor-funded | Other estimates range from $0.8-2.0T with different definitions | **C** | Don't use. Alternative: \"Estimates of the AI market vary widely by definition. Our case does not depend on market size.\" |\n| 2 | \"70% of transformations fail\" | Repeated in articles → traced to a 1990s management-book estimate, not a systematic study | 1990s | No defined sample or definition of failure | Later studies report a wide range | **C** | \"Many large change programmes fall short of their goals. Published estimates vary widely.\" |\n| 3 | \"Our customer churn is twice the industry average\" | Internal deck → CRM churn 13% vs an \"industry average\" of 6.5% from a trade-association survey | 2026 / 2025 | Survey covers enterprise SaaS (we are mid-market); voluntary response | No other benchmark found | **B** | \"Our 13% churn is above the 6-8% reported in an industry survey of mainly enterprise vendors; mid-market benchmarks are not available.\" |\n| 4 | \"DPDP Rules apply from May 2027\" | Official gazette notification of the DPDP Rules (November 2025) → phased commencement | 2025 | Some provisions effective immediately, others at 12 and 18 months | Legal commentaries consistent | **A** (verify the dates against the gazette text) | \"Most data-fiduciary obligations under the DPDP Rules apply 18 months after notification, around May 2027; some provisions apply earlier.\" |\n| 5 | \"GenAI improves productivity by 40%\" | Consultancy blog → a field study of one task type | 2023 | Task-specific; quality dropped on tasks outside AI's competence | Other studies show 14-55% on specific tasks | **B** | \"Field studies report productivity gains from about 14% to over 50% on specific tasks, with quality risks on complex work. Our pilot measured 39% on standard contracts.\" |\n\n**Actions to verify:** for claim 3, ask the trade association for its mid-market cut. For claim 5, replace external figures with our pilot data wherever possible."}]}, {"id": "research-synthesis", "pack": "evidence", "title": "Research Synthesis", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Turn piles of notes, reports and links into a few clear findings with evidence and implications.", "tryp": "Synthesise these 6 analyst reports and my notes on the EV charging market into key findings for our investment committee.", "when": ["After desk research or document review", "When a team has lots of material but no clear message", "To brief a busy executive on a complex topic"], "inputs": ["The material (paste, attach or summarise)", "The question it should answer", "The audience"], "pairs": ["insight-synthesis", "executive-summary", "source-validation", "scqa-storyline"], "body": "# Research Synthesis\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a few clear, sourced findings and a bottom line that answer the question, with the conflicts and gaps shown honestly, instead of a document-by-document summary.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Question first.** Read everything through the lens of the decision's questions. Material that doesn't bear on them is parked.\n2. **Atomic facts with sources.** Extract one claim per line, with its source, date and definition. Synthesis is built from these, not from memory.\n3. **Synthesis, not summary.** Findings combine evidence across sources into insights. Summarising each document in turn is not synthesis.\n4. **Findings are sentences with a point of view:** \"Returns depend on utilisation above 15%, which only highway sites achieve\", not \"Utilisation\".\n5. **Strength is explicit:** the number of independent sources, their quality and their agreement.\n6. **Contradictions are information.** Show them and explain the likely reasons (definitions, time periods, methods, interests).\n7. **Bottom line and implications:** what this means for the decision, and what remains uncertain.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The questions and the decision; the audience.\n- The material: types, volume, quality; any already-validated sources (see `source-validation`).\n- Time available; the required format.\n\n## Method\n1. **Restate the questions** (3-8).\n2. **Extract atomic facts:** claim | source | date | definition | strength; tag each to a question.\n3. **Cluster** the facts by question and theme; look for patterns, mechanisms and causes.\n4. **Write findings** (3-7): full sentences answering the questions; quantified where possible.\n5. **Grade strength** for each finding: strong (multiple independent, high-quality sources agree), moderate, weak (single or low-quality source).\n6. **Contradictions:** list conflicting evidence; explain the likely reasons; state which view you lean towards and why.\n7. **Implications:** for each finding, the \"so what\" for the decision.\n8. **Open questions:** what's unknown, why it matters, and how to close it (data, experts, a test).\n9. **Bottom line:** 2-3 sentences answering the overall question, with the confidence level.\n10. **Traceability:** a finding-to-source map, or footnotes.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Weight evidence, don't count it.** One rigorous primary study can outweigh five opinion pieces.\n- **Recency vs depth:** recent anecdotes vs older rigorous data. Explain how you balanced them.\n- **Surprises deserve scrutiny.** If a finding contradicts expectations, check it twice before presenting it.\n- **Separate facts from interpretation** in the write-up.\n\n## Red flags\n- Document-by-document summaries.\n- Findings that are topic labels.\n- No sources or dates; no strength grading.\n- Contradictions hidden or averaged away.\n- No bottom line, or no implications.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Due diligence:** findings grouped by diligence area, with red flags first; a link to valuation implications.\n- **Literature review:** evidence quality grading; consensus vs debate; gaps in research.\n- **Market study:** size, growth, segments, competition, customer needs, regulation.\n- **Policy or regulatory review:** the current law, proposed changes, enforcement trends and implications.\n- **Board briefing:** a one-page synthesis with a bottom line and 3-5 findings.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Research Synthesis: [question]\n**Bottom line (2-3 sentences, confidence):** ...\n\n### Finding 1: [full-sentence insight, quantified]\n- Evidence: fact (source, date); fact (source, date)\n- Strength: Strong / Moderate / Weak\n- Contradictions: ... (likely reason)\n- So what: ...\n\n### Finding 2 ...\n\n**Open questions and how to close them:** ...\n**Parked material (not relevant to the decision):** ...\n**Sources reviewed:** list with dates\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Findings answer the stated questions; full sentences, quantified.\n- [ ] Every finding is traceable to dated sources.\n- [ ] Strength is graded; contradictions shown and explained.\n- [ ] Implications and open questions with closing actions.\n- [ ] A clear bottom line with a confidence level.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Research quality depends on the sources available to Claude in your session. Verify key facts against primary sources.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `insight-synthesis`: Turn analysis outputs into true insights: what it means, why it matters, what to do.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n- `source-validation`: Grade every source and claim for reliability so you never put a weak number in front of a board.\n- `scqa-storyline`: Build the storyline every executive document needs: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Evidence synthesis:** narrative and systematic review methods (question framing, extraction, quality appraisal, synthesis), as taught in research-methods curricula and evidence-based-management practice.\n- **Grading evidence strength:** approaches that weigh study quality, consistency and independence (adapted from evidence-grading systems used in health and policy research).\n- **Triangulation:** combining multiple sources and methods to corroborate findings.\n- **Source validation:** see the `source-validation` skill."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; EV charging in India for an investment committee: 6 reports, 4 filings, 5 expert calls)\n\n**Bottom line:**\n- Public EV charging returns are driven by utilisation, not network size.\n- Only highway, fleet-depot and dense urban-hub sites reach the ~15% utilisation needed for attractive returns today.\n- A focused fleet-and-highway operator is investable; a broad urban-network play is not, yet.\n- **Confidence:** moderate.\n\n### Finding 1: Site utilisation above ~15% is the threshold for mid-teens project IRR; most urban public chargers run at 5-8%\n- **Evidence:**\n  - Two listed operators' disclosures (FY26): network-average utilisation 6-9%.\n  - Operator investor presentation: highway sites 18-24%.\n  - 3 expert calls: a break-even of ~11-13% on fast chargers (est.).\n- **Strength:** moderate (disclosures + experts; definitions of utilisation vary).\n- **Contradictions:** one industry report claims 20% average utilisation. It defines utilisation as hours with any session, not energy delivered. That definitional difference explains the gap.\n- **So what:** screen targets on site mix and utilisation by site type, not on charger count.\n\n### Finding 2: Fleet electrification (ride-hailing, last-mile delivery) provides anchor demand that public consumer demand doesn't yet\n- **Evidence:** fleet-operator EV adoption targets; e-3W and e-4W fleet registrations growing faster than private cars (vehicle-registration data); 2 depot-charging operators' contracts.\n- **Strength:** strong.\n- **So what:** prefer operators with long-term fleet contracts (take-or-pay).\n\n### Finding 3: Grid connection costs and timelines are the main execution risk\n- **Evidence:** 4 of 5 experts cited 6-14 month delays for high-capacity connections; state regulatory orders on connection charges vary.\n- **Strength:** moderate.\n- **So what:** diligence should test each target's pipeline for secured grid connections.\n\n### Finding 4: Policy support is shifting from vehicle purchase subsidies to infrastructure incentives\n- **Evidence:** central scheme notifications (verify the current scheme), and state EV policies.\n- **Strength:** strong (official texts), but the incentive amounts change frequently.\n- **So what:** model cases without incentives, and treat incentives as upside.\n\n**Open questions:**\n- Real utilisation by site type for the 3 targets → data request in diligence.\n- Battery-swapping substitution risk for 2- and 3-wheelers → 2 more expert calls.\n\n**Parked:** consumer-sentiment surveys on range anxiety (not decision-critical for a fleet-led thesis)."}]}, {"id": "interview-guide", "pack": "evidence", "title": "Interview Guide", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Write a crisp guide for expert, customer or employee interviews that gets real answers, not opinions.", "tryp": "Write a 45-minute interview guide for talking to hospital procurement heads about buying AI radiology tools.", "when": ["Expert network calls, customer discovery, employee listening tours", "Due diligence reference calls", "Any interview where you have limited time with a senior person"], "inputs": ["Purpose and hypotheses to test", "Who is being interviewed", "Length of the interview", "Any compliance rules (e.g. no confidential info from experts)"], "pairs": ["interview-synthesis", "research-plan", "jobs-to-be-done", "survey-design"], "body": "# Interview Guide\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** interviews that produce evidence you can use: real behaviour, real numbers and honest views, in a consistent format you can synthesise, instead of polite chats that confirm what you already believed.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Objectives first.** 3-5 learning objectives tied to the decision. Every question serves one of them; anything else is cut.\n2. **The right people, enough of them.** Cover the variety that matters (segments, roles, regions, users vs buyers, fans vs churned). Typically 5-8 per segment for patterns; 12-20 overall for a study.\n3. **Past behaviour beats opinion.** \"Tell me about the last time you…\" is evidence; \"Would you use…?\" is speculation. People are poor predictors of their own future behaviour and politely over-positive.\n4. **Funnel structure.** Start broad and open, then narrow to specifics and quantification; test your hypotheses last, so you don't contaminate earlier answers.\n5. **Neutral wording.** No leading, double-barrelled or loaded questions. Don't pitch the idea.\n6. **Quantify where possible:** frequency, volumes, spend, time, switching costs, ranges.\n7. **Ethics and compliance:** informed consent, confidentiality, data protection, no material non-public or confidential information from experts.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision and hypotheses; what you already know.\n- Interview type: expert, customer, employee, executive, partner, reference.\n- Who: segments, roles, how many; recruitment route (own customers, panel, expert network, referrals).\n- Constraints: time per call (30-60 min), language, recording permission, incentives.\n\n## Method\n1. **Learning objectives** (3-5), each linked to the decision.\n2. **Sample design:** segments × number; include critics, lost customers and non-users, not only fans.\n3. **Screener:** 4-6 qualifying questions (role, responsibility, recency of experience, company size, conflicts of interest).\n4. **Opening (3-5 min):** purpose (without revealing confidential intent), consent and recording, confidentiality, \"no right answers\", time check.\n5. **Context (5-10 min):** role, responsibilities, the environment.\n6. **Core sections (one per objective):**\n   - Open question → probes (what, how, why, example, numbers) → the specific detail.\n   - Behaviour-based: \"Walk me through the last time…\"\n   - Quantification: \"Roughly how often / how much / how long?\"\n   - Pain and workaround: \"What's hardest? What have you tried? What did it cost?\"\n7. **Hypothesis testing (late):** concept or claim reactions, with neutral framing; ask what would make them pay or switch, and what would stop them.\n8. **Close (3-5 min):** \"What should I have asked?\"; referrals; thanks; next steps.\n9. **Time plan:** minutes per section; mark which questions to drop if short of time.\n10. **Note template:** respondent ID | segment | key quotes (verbatim) | facts and numbers | behaviours | pains | surprises | hypothesis evidence (for / against).\n11. **Compliance (expert calls):** a pre-call disclaimer; no current-employer confidential information; no price-sensitive information; stop if a respondent strays.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Expert vs customer:** experts give an industry view and estimates (verify them); customers give needs and behaviour. Use both.\n- **The interviewer's opinion is invisible.** Don't react to answers; probe neutrally (\"Say more about that\").\n- **Silence works.** Pause after answers; respondents often add the most useful detail.\n- **Pivot when the pattern is clear.** After 5-6 interviews in a segment, adjust the guide to probe new themes.\n- **Senior executives:** fewer questions, more strategic; prepare tightly; respect time.\n\n## Red flags\n- \"Would you buy…?\" as the main evidence.\n- Leading or double-barrelled questions.\n- Only friendly customers or internal champions interviewed.\n- No quantification; no note template.\n- Expert calls without compliance guardrails.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Customer discovery / jobs-to-be-done:** last-purchase or switch timeline (first thought → search → choice → use).\n- **Win-loss:** decision process, alternatives considered, decisive factors, price perception.\n- **Employee listening:** psychological safety; confidentiality and anonymised reporting; avoid manager presence.\n- **Diligence reference calls:** relationship, satisfaction, risks, switching intent, share of wallet.\n- **Stakeholder interviews (change or strategy):** priorities, concerns, success measures, influence map.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Interview Guide: [study], for [decision]\n**Objectives:** 1. ... 2. ... 3. ...\n**Sample:** [segments × n], incl. critics / lost / non-users\n**Screener:** ...\n\n| Time | Section | Questions (open → probes) | Objective |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0-5 | Opening | consent, confidentiality | — |\n\n**Drop if short of time:** ...\n**Note template:** ...\n**Compliance guardrails:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Objectives tied to the decision; every question maps to one.\n- [ ] Sample covers critics and non-users; a screener defined.\n- [ ] Behaviour-based, neutral, quantifying questions in a funnel; hypotheses tested last.\n- [ ] Time plan, drop list and note template.\n- [ ] Consent, confidentiality and compliance guardrails.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Research quality depends on the sources available to Claude in your session. Verify key facts against primary sources.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `interview-synthesis`: Turn many interview notes into patterns, quotes and numbers, with how strongly each is supported.\n- `research-plan`: Plan exactly what to find out, from which sources, in what order, before searching anything.\n- `jobs-to-be-done`: Understand the real 'job' customers hire you for, and the outcomes they measure you on.\n- `survey-design`: Design a short, unbiased survey that produces numbers you can actually use.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Qualitative interviewing:** semi-structured interview design, funnel questioning, neutral probing, as taught in social-science and market-research methods curricula.\n- **Behaviour over opinion:** customer-discovery practice of asking about specific past events rather than hypothetical intentions (stated vs revealed preference).\n- **Sample size for qualitative work:** saturation concepts (themes typically stabilise after a modest number of interviews per homogeneous segment).\n- **Ethics:** informed consent, confidentiality and anonymisation; market-research codes of conduct; data protection (DPDP Act 2023 in India).\n- **Expert-call compliance:** avoiding confidential and material non-public information; insider-trading rules (e.g. SEBI PIT Regulations)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; B2B payroll software, understanding why mid-sized customers churn, 45-minute calls)\n\n**Decision:** whether to invest in a dedicated onboarding team vs product fixes to cut churn.\n\n**Objectives:**\n1. Understand the moments and reasons customers decide to leave.\n2. Size the cost of the pain (time, errors, money).\n3. Learn what they switched to and why.\n4. Test whether early onboarding experience predicts churn.\n\n**Sample (16 interviews):**\n- 6 churned in the last 6 months.\n- 4 at-risk (low usage).\n- 4 healthy long-term customers.\n- 2 lost deals (chose a competitor).\n- Respondents: payroll or HR heads; include 3 finance heads (budget owners).\n\n**Screener:** responsible for payroll decisions; involved in the selection or the exit; tenure ≥6 months in role; no current employment with a competitor.\n\n| Time | Section | Questions | Objective |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0-4 | Opening | Purpose: \"improving payroll software for companies like yours\"; consent to record; confidentiality; no right answers | — |\n| 4-9 | Context | Your role; payroll size and complexity (headcount, states, pay cycles); who else is involved | — |\n| 9-20 | Onboarding | \"Walk me through your first 60 days with us.\" What went well or badly? How long until your first correct payroll? How many issues needed support? | 4 |\n| 20-32 | The exit (churned) or the doubt (at-risk) | \"When did you first think about leaving? What happened that week?\" Then: who decided, what alternatives, what it cost to switch | 1, 3 |\n| 32-38 | Cost of pain | \"Roughly how many hours a month on fixes or workarounds? Any penalties or employee complaints? Approximate cost?\" | 2 |\n| 38-42 | Hypothesis | \"If you'd had a named onboarding specialist for 60 days, would that have changed anything? What specifically?\" (neutral; also ask what *wouldn't* have helped) | 4 |\n| 42-45 | Close | \"What should I have asked?\"; anyone else to speak to; thanks | — |\n\n**Drop if short of time:** the context detail on pay cycles; the referral question.\n\n**Note template:** ID | segment | first-correct-payroll days | support tickets in the first 60 days | trigger event | alternatives | switching cost | hours per month on fixes | key verbatim quotes | evidence for / against the onboarding hypothesis.\n\n**Compliance:**\n- Respondent data handled under data-protection obligations.\n- Quotes anonymised in reporting.\n- Incentive offered within ethical norms."}]}, {"id": "interview-synthesis", "pack": "evidence", "title": "Interview Synthesis", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Turn many interview notes into patterns, quotes and numbers, with how strongly each is supported.", "tryp": "Here are notes from 12 customer interviews about our onboarding. Synthesise the findings.", "when": ["After a round of interviews or calls", "When you need quotes and patterns for a deck", "To compare views across groups (customers vs staff, region A vs B)"], "inputs": ["Interview notes or transcripts", "The objectives or hypotheses", "Interviewee profiles (role, segment)"], "pairs": ["interview-guide", "insight-synthesis", "survey-design", "customer-segmentation"], "body": "# Interview Synthesis\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** an honest answer to \"what did we learn?\": themes you can count, segment differences you can act on, and quotes that represent the pattern, not just the most memorable person.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Breadth over vividness.** A theme heard from 11 of 16 respondents across segments beats one dramatic quote. Always state \"n of N\".\n2. **Segment differences are often the insight.** Churned vs healthy customers, buyers vs users, small vs large.\n3. **Code systematically.** Start from the learning objectives (deductive codes), add emerging codes (inductive), and apply them consistently.\n4. **Separate observation from interpretation.** What people said and did vs what you infer.\n5. **Quotes illustrate; they don't prove.** Choose typical quotes, label the respondent type, and anonymise.\n6. **Contradictions and outliers are reported.** They may signal a sub-segment or a flaw in the hypothesis.\n7. **Interviews are small samples.** Use them for direction and mechanisms; validate magnitudes with quantitative data or surveys where it matters.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The objectives and decision; the interview guide.\n- Notes or transcripts: number, segments, quality, interviewer differences.\n- Confidentiality rules for quotes.\n\n## Method\n1. **Coding frame:** codes per objective plus emerging codes; define each in one line.\n2. **Code every interview:** tag passages; extract facts and numbers (hours, costs, frequencies, alternatives).\n3. **Evidence matrix:** respondent × theme (✓, ✗ (contradicts), blank (not discussed)), with a segment column.\n4. **Theme strength:** n/N overall and by segment; note intensity (how much it matters to them) separately from frequency.\n5. **Write themes** as full sentences, with mechanisms (\"Churn is triggered by the first payroll error that reaches employees, not by price\").\n6. **Segment comparison:** where themes differ by segment.\n7. **Quantitative extracts:** ranges and medians of the numbers respondents gave (with n); treat them as indicative.\n8. **Quotes:** 1-2 per theme; typical; labelled by segment; anonymised.\n9. **Contradictions and outliers:** what doesn't fit, and the possible explanation.\n10. **Implications:** what this means for the decision and hypotheses (supported, refuted, nuanced).\n11. **Confidence and gaps:** sample limits, missing segments, interviewer bias; what to validate next.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Saturation:** if new interviews stop adding themes, the sample is probably enough for direction.\n- **Weight respondents appropriately.** A decision-maker's view on why they left outweighs a junior user's guess.\n- **Beware of confirmation:** actively look for evidence against your hypothesis; report it.\n- **Interviewer effects:** if interviewers differ, check whether themes cluster by interviewer.\n- **Numbers from interviews** are self-reported and small-n. Present medians and ranges, not precise averages.\n\n## Red flags\n- Themes without n/N.\n- Only supportive quotes.\n- No segment comparison.\n- Single dramatic anecdotes driving conclusions.\n- Precise percentages from 12 interviews.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Win-loss:** decisive factors by won vs lost; price vs value perception; sales-process issues.\n- **Employee listening:** themes by function and level; strict anonymity (suppress small groups).\n- **Expert calls:** consensus vs divergent views; estimates with ranges; experts' biases.\n- **Stakeholder interviews:** alignment map; priorities and concerns by stakeholder group.\n- **Diligence references:** red flags first; customer satisfaction and switching risk.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Interview Synthesis: [study] (N = __; segments: ...)\n**Bottom line:** ...\n\n| Theme (full sentence) | Overall n/N | Seg A | Seg B | Intensity | Typical quote (segment) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Segment differences:** ...\n**Numbers from interviews (indicative):** metric: median, range (n)\n**Contradictions and outliers:** ...\n**Hypotheses:** supported / refuted / nuanced\n**Implications for the decision:** ...\n**Confidence and gaps; validate next:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Systematic coding; an evidence matrix built.\n- [ ] Every theme has n/N and segment splits; intensity noted.\n- [ ] Typical, labelled, anonymised quotes.\n- [ ] Contradictions and outliers reported; hypotheses assessed.\n- [ ] Implications, confidence, gaps and validation steps.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Research quality depends on the sources available to Claude in your session. Verify key facts against primary sources.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `interview-guide`: Write a crisp guide for expert, customer or employee interviews that gets real answers, not opinions.\n- `insight-synthesis`: Turn analysis outputs into true insights: what it means, why it matters, what to do.\n- `survey-design`: Design a short, unbiased survey that produces numbers you can actually use.\n- `customer-segmentation`: Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Qualitative analysis:** thematic analysis (coding, theme development, review); deductive and inductive coding; framework matrices, as taught in qualitative-research methods curricula.\n- **Saturation:** the point where new data adds no new themes.\n- **Rigour:** reporting frequency across respondents, negative cases, reflexivity about researcher bias.\n- **Ethics:** anonymisation; small-group suppression in employee research; data protection (DPDP Act 2023 in India)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; payroll software churn study, N = 16: 6 churned, 4 at-risk, 4 healthy, 2 lost deals)\n\n**Bottom line:**\n- Churn is driven by a bad first 60 days, specifically the first payroll error that reaches employees, more than by price or features.\n- The evidence favours investing in onboarding, plus 2 product fixes, over feature development.\n- **Confidence:** moderate (small sample; validate with ticket data).\n\n| Theme | Overall | Churned (6) | At-risk (4) | Healthy (4) | Lost (2) | Intensity | Typical quote |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| The first employee-visible payroll error triggered the decision to leave or doubt us | 9/14* | 5/6 | 3/4 | 1/4 | n/a | Very high | \"Once salaries went wrong, the CFO stopped trusting the system.\" (churned, finance head) |\n| Onboarding took longer than promised; no single point of contact | 10/14* | 6/6 | 3/4 | 1/4 | n/a | High | \"We were passed between four people.\" (at-risk, HR head) |\n| Multi-state statutory set-up was confusing | 8/16 | 4/6 | 2/4 | 1/4 | 1/2 | High | \"Each state's deductions took us weeks to get right.\" (churned) |\n| Price was a reason to leave | 3/16 | 2/6 | 0/4 | 0/4 | 1/2 | Medium | — |\n| Healthy customers had an internal payroll expert who learned the product deeply | 4/4 healthy vs 1/6 churned | 1/6 | 1/4 | 4/4 | n/a | — | \"Our payroll lead became the in-house champion.\" (healthy) |\n\n*Excludes the 2 lost deals (never onboarded).\n\n**Numbers from interviews (indicative):**\n- Days to first correct payroll: churned median 52 (range 35-80, n=6); healthy median 24 (range 18-30, n=4).\n- Hours per month on fixes: churned median 18 (n=5).\n\n**Contradictions and outliers:**\n- One healthy customer had a poor onboarding but stayed. Their payroll head had used us at a previous employer, a sign that an expert champion can compensate.\n- One churned customer left after an acquisition (a parent-company mandate). This is a non-addressable churn reason.\n\n**Hypotheses:**\n- Onboarding predicts churn: **supported** (strong pattern across segments).\n- Price drives churn: **refuted** for most respondents.\n- Multi-state complexity: **nuanced** (matters most for companies in 3+ states).\n\n**Implications:**\n- Dedicated onboarding specialist for the first 60 days, with a target of the first correct payroll in ≤30 days.\n- Product fixes: a guided multi-state statutory set-up; a payroll pre-check that catches errors before payslips are issued.\n- Champion programme: train one customer-side payroll expert.\n\n**Confidence and gaps:**\n- Small sample; no respondents under 100 employees.\n- **Validate:** correlate days-to-first-correct-payroll and first-60-day tickets with churn across all customers (CRM and support data)."}]}, {"id": "survey-design", "pack": "evidence", "title": "Survey Design", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Design a short, unbiased survey that produces numbers you can actually use.", "tryp": "Design a 10-minute survey for SME owners in India about willingness to pay for an AI bookkeeping assistant.", "when": ["Customer, employee, pulse or market surveys", "Pricing or feature-priority research", "When you need numbers from many people, not depth from a few"], "inputs": ["Objectives and decisions the survey serves", "Target respondents and how you reach them", "Length limit", "Any must-ask questions"], "pairs": ["survey-analysis", "pricing-strategy", "customer-segmentation", "interview-guide"], "body": "# Survey Design\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a survey that answers the decision's questions with known precision, from the right people, with questions that measure what you think they measure, and an analysis plan agreed before the data arrives.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Analysis plan first.** Write the tables and charts you'll produce, and the decision each informs, before writing questions. Any question that feeds no table is cut.\n2. **Who, precisely.** Define the population and sampling frame; know who is missing (non-customers? lapsed users? offline segments?).\n3. **Sample size from precision.** About ±3 points needs roughly 1,000 responses; ±5 points needs about 400. Every subgroup you'll compare needs its own adequate n (≥100 to read; ~300+ to detect modest differences).\n4. **One concept per question; neutral words.** No double-barrelled, leading or loaded questions; define terms; specify the time frame.\n5. **Balanced scales, consistently applied.** The same scale direction throughout; a midpoint where a neutral answer is legitimate; \"don't know\" or \"not applicable\" where real.\n6. **Short and mobile-first.** Under ~10 minutes; fatigue degrades quality in later questions.\n7. **Pilot it.** 5-10 cognitive interviews or a soft launch catches confusing questions before they cost you the study.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision; key questions; the hypotheses to test.\n- Population and frame (CRM list, panel, employees, website visitors); expected response rate.\n- Subgroups to compare (segment, region, tenure, role).\n- Mode (online, phone, in-person), language(s), incentives, timing.\n- Constraints: consent, data protection, anonymity (especially for employees).\n\n## Method\n1. **Objectives and analysis plan:** the decision → questions → tables / charts → the survey questions needed.\n2. **Population, frame and sampling:** random or stratified from the frame; quotas for key subgroups; plan weighting if the sample will differ from the population.\n3. **Sample size:** the margin of error at 95% ≈ 1.96 × √(p(1−p)/n). Plan for subgroup needs and expected response rates (invites = required n ÷ response rate).\n4. **Question design by measurement type:**\n   - Behaviour (frequency, recency, spend): specific time frames.\n   - Attitude or satisfaction: balanced 5- or 7-point scales.\n   - Importance: forced trade-offs (ranking, max-diff) rather than \"rate everything important\".\n   - Willingness to pay: methods designed for price research; treat stated intent cautiously.\n   - Demographics and firmographics: at the end, unless they are screeners.\n5. **Wording:** simple, specific, neutral; avoid jargon, negatives and assumptions; balanced response options; mutually exclusive, exhaustive categories.\n6. **Flow:** screeners → warm-up → core topics (general → specific) → sensitive items → demographics → thanks. Use skip logic; randomise option order where order bias is likely.\n7. **Bias controls:**\n   - Order effects: randomise.\n   - Acquiescence: avoid agree/disagree batteries where possible.\n   - Social desirability: anonymity, indirect questions.\n   - Recall: short time frames.\n8. **Quality controls:** attention checks; speeder and straight-liner flags; open-text sense checks; duplicate checks.\n9. **Pilot:** cognitive interviews plus a soft launch (5-10% of sample); fix and finalise.\n10. **Consent and data protection:** purpose, use, retention, anonymity statement; minimal personal data.\n11. **Field plan:** timing, reminders, incentives, monitoring of quotas and response rates.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Stated vs revealed:** surveys over-state intent (\"likely to buy\"). Discount purchase intent; use behavioural data where available.\n- **Net scores** (e.g. promoters minus detractors) are simple but lose information. Report the full distribution too.\n- **Open questions are expensive** to analyse; use 1-3 well-placed ones (\"Why did you give that score?\").\n- **Employee surveys:** a minimum reporting group size (e.g. ≥10) to protect anonymity; communicate actions afterwards, or participation falls.\n- **Longitudinal tracking:** keep wording and scales identical between waves, or comparisons break.\n\n## Red flags\n- No analysis plan; questions \"nice to know\".\n- Subgroups too small to compare.\n- Leading or double-barrelled wording; unbalanced scales.\n- 25-minute surveys; no mobile testing.\n- No pilot; no quality checks; consent missing.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Customer satisfaction / loyalty:** relationship vs transactional; driver analysis needs consistent attribute ratings.\n- **Market sizing / needs:** a sampling frame beyond your own customers; screening for category users.\n- **Pricing:** structured price-research methods; careful framing; caution on stated willingness to pay.\n- **Employee engagement:** anonymity, validated item sets, action-planning follow-through.\n- **Concept testing:** monadic design (each respondent sees one concept) to avoid comparison bias.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Survey Design: [study], for [decision]\n**Objectives and analysis plan:**\n| Decision question | Table / chart | Survey questions |\n|---|---|---|\n\n**Population and frame:** ... **Sampling:** ... **Target n:** __ (±__ pts); subgroups: ...; invites needed: __ at __% response\n**Questionnaire:** numbered questions with response options, skip logic and randomisation notes\n**Quality controls:** ...\n**Pilot plan:** ...\n**Consent and data protection statement:** ...\n**Length:** __ min (mobile tested)\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Every question maps to an analysis-plan table and a decision.\n- [ ] Population, frame and sample size with precision; subgroups adequately sized.\n- [ ] Neutral, single-concept wording; balanced scales; logical flow.\n- [ ] Bias and quality controls; pilot planned.\n- [ ] Consent and data protection; length within ~10 minutes.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Research quality depends on the sources available to Claude in your session. Verify key facts against primary sources.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `survey-analysis`: Turn survey results into a few significant, decision-relevant insights with honest caveats.\n- `pricing-strategy`: Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.\n- `customer-segmentation`: Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.\n- `interview-guide`: Write a crisp guide for expert, customer or employee interviews that gets real answers, not opinions.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Questionnaire design:** principles for neutral wording, balanced scales, question order and cognitive pretesting, as taught in survey-methodology curricula.\n- **Sampling and precision:** margin of error for proportions (1.96 × √(p(1−p)/n) at 95%); stratified sampling; quotas; weighting.\n- **Survey error sources:** coverage, sampling, non-response and measurement error (the total-survey-error framework).\n- **Standards:** ISO 20252 (market, opinion and social research); market-research codes of conduct.\n- **Data protection:** DPDP Act 2023 (India) and equivalent laws elsewhere; consent and purpose limitation."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; customer survey for a B2B logistics software firm deciding where to invest next year)\n\n**Decision:** prioritise investment among onboarding, support, a mobile app and pricing changes.\n\n| Decision question | Table / chart | Survey questions |\n|---|---|---|\n| Which experiences drive overall satisfaction? | Driver chart (attribute ratings vs overall) | Q3 overall satisfaction; Q5a-f attribute ratings |\n| Do SMEs and enterprises differ? | Every table split by segment | S2 company size (screener / quota) |\n| How big is mobile demand among field users? | Share using phones at work; feature ranking | Q7, Q8 |\n| Is price a churn risk? | Value-for-money by segment; switching consideration | Q9, Q10 |\n\n**Population and frame:** active customer accounts (CRM), one decision-maker and one daily user per account.\n\n**Sampling:** stratified by segment (SME, enterprise) with quotas.\n\n**Target n = 1,000**, so about ±3.1 points overall:\n- Each segment needs ≥300 (±5.7 points).\n- At an expected 12% response rate, invite about 8,400 contacts.\n- Weight to the true segment mix at analysis.\n\n**Questionnaire (excerpt):**\n- S1. Do you use [product] at least monthly in your job? (Yes / No → end)\n- S2. How many employees does your company have? (<50 / 50-249 / 250-999 / 1,000+)\n- Q3. Overall, how satisfied are you with [product]? (Very dissatisfied … Very satisfied, 5 points)\n- Q4. Why did you give that rating? (open)\n- Q5. How satisfied are you with each of the following? (randomised; 5 points + \"not used\"): a) setting up / onboarding, b) day-to-day ease of use, c) reporting, d) support response time, e) support quality, f) integrations.\n- Q6. Which ONE of these would most improve your experience? (randomised list + other)\n- Q7. In the last month, how often did you need [product] while away from a desk? (Never / 1-3 times / weekly / daily)\n- Q9. Considering what you pay, how would you rate the value for money? (Very poor … Very good)\n- Q10. In the last 6 months, have you seriously considered switching to another provider? (Yes / No / Don't know)\n\n**Fixes made after review:**\n- An earlier draft asked \"How satisfied are you with our fast and friendly support?\" This was leading and double-barrelled, so it was split into Q5d and Q5e with neutral wording.\n- \"Would you pay more for a mobile app?\" was replaced by the behaviour question Q7. Price sensitivity is handled separately.\n\n**Quality controls:** one attention check; flags for responses under 3 minutes and straight-lining in Q5; duplicate-email check.\n\n**Pilot:** 8 cognitive interviews (4 SME, 4 enterprise), then a 5% soft launch.\n\n**Consent:** the purpose, that responses are reported in aggregate only, retention of 12 months, and a contact for data requests.\n\n**Length:** about 8 minutes, tested on mobile."}]}, {"id": "survey-analysis", "pack": "evidence", "title": "Survey Analysis", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Turn survey results into a few significant, decision-relevant insights with honest caveats.", "tryp": "Here's our employee engagement survey export (CSV, 1,400 responses). What are the real insights?", "when": ["After an employee, customer or market survey", "When a team has charts but no clear message", "Comparing segments, regions or time periods"], "inputs": ["The data (file or table) and the questionnaire", "Objectives", "Segments to compare"], "pairs": ["survey-design", "exhibit-builder", "insight-synthesis", "chart-selection"], "body": "# Survey Analysis\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** findings you can defend: which differences are real, which are noise, how sure you can be, and what the results mean for the decision, without overclaiming from small subgroups or skewed samples.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Clean before you count.** Remove speeders, straight-liners, failed attention checks and duplicates. Document the rules and how many responses were removed.\n2. **Know who answered.** Response rate and profile vs the population. If the sample skews (e.g. too many happy customers), weight it and say so.\n3. **Base sizes everywhere.** Every percentage shows its n. Subgroups under ~100 are indicative only; under ~30, don't report percentages.\n4. **Uncertainty is part of the answer.** Margins of error and significance tests (or confidence intervals) on key comparisons. \"Higher\" means statistically and practically higher.\n5. **Distributions, not just averages.** A 3.5 average can hide polarisation. Show the full spread and top-2 / bottom-2 box results.\n6. **Correlation is not causation.** Driver analysis shows association; confirm causes with behavioural data or experiments.\n7. **Findings drive decisions:** each finding gets a so-what and a confidence level.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The analysis plan and decision; the questionnaire; the population and sampling design.\n- The raw data file and field report (invites, completes, dates, mode).\n- Known population figures for weighting (segment mix, region, size).\n\n## Method\n1. **Cleaning:** apply the quality rules; report the before and after counts.\n2. **Response and representativeness:** response rate; sample vs population profile; non-response concerns.\n3. **Weighting (if needed):** weight to known population margins; report the effective sample size ≈ (Σw)² ÷ Σw², and use it for margins of error.\n4. **Toplines:** full distributions for key questions; top-2 box and bottom-2 box; net scores with their components.\n5. **Cross-tabs:** the planned subgroups only (avoid fishing); base sizes shown.\n6. **Significance:** a two-proportion z-test or chi-square for proportions; t-tests for means; flag differences significant at 95%; adjust or be cautious with many comparisons.\n7. **Driver analysis:** correlation or regression of attributes against the overall outcome; combine with stated importance; plot importance vs performance; caveat as association.\n8. **Open text:** code into themes (n per theme); select typical quotes.\n9. **Findings:** full-sentence insights with numbers, n, significance and a so-what; confidence high / medium / low.\n10. **Limitations:** coverage gaps, non-response, self-report, timing.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Statistically significant but trivial** (e.g. 1 point on n = 20,000) is not a finding; focus on differences that matter for the decision.\n- **Non-significant isn't \"no difference\".** It may be an underpowered comparison; say \"not detectable with this sample\".\n- **Net scores:** small shifts are often within noise; the margin of error on a net score is larger than on a single proportion.\n- **Multiple comparisons:** with 40 cross-tabs, about 2 will be \"significant\" by chance at 95%; pre-specify key tests.\n- **Stated intent:** discount purchase or switching intentions; validate with behaviour (churn data).\n\n## Red flags\n- Percentages without base sizes.\n- \"Segment A is higher\" with overlapping intervals.\n- Averages only; polarised distributions hidden.\n- No cleaning or weighting notes.\n- Driver correlations presented as causes.\n- Cherry-picked cross-tabs.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Customer satisfaction / loyalty:** link to retention data; driver analysis; verbatim themes.\n- **Employee surveys:** minimum group sizes for anonymity; benchmark to previous waves (same wording); action priorities.\n- **Market studies:** weight to population; size estimates with ranges; screen-out rates as incidence.\n- **Concept tests:** monadic comparisons with significance; discount top-box intent.\n- **Tracking studies:** wave-on-wave changes with significance; seasonality.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Survey Analysis: [study] (n = __ clean, of __ completes; response rate __%; fieldwork dates)\n**Bottom line:** ...\n\n**Cleaning and weighting:** removed __ (rules); weighted to [margins]; effective n __; MOE ±__ pts\n\n| Finding (full sentence) | Evidence (%, n) | Significant? | Confidence | So what |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Key distributions:** ...\n**Driver analysis (association, not causation):** ...\n**Open-text themes (n):** ...\n**Limitations:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Cleaning rules and counts; response rate and representativeness reviewed.\n- [ ] Weighting and effective n reported where used.\n- [ ] Base sizes on all figures; small bases flagged.\n- [ ] Margins of error and significance on key comparisons.\n- [ ] Distributions shown; drivers caveated; findings have a so-what and confidence.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Research quality depends on the sources available to Claude in your session. Verify key facts against primary sources.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `survey-design`: Design a short, unbiased survey that produces numbers you can actually use.\n- `exhibit-builder`: Design a complete, board-ready exhibit: title, chart, callouts, source, and the code to draw it.\n- `insight-synthesis`: Turn analysis outputs into true insights: what it means, why it matters, what to do.\n- `chart-selection`: Pick the right chart for the message, and avoid charts that confuse or mislead.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Inference for proportions:** margin of error, two-proportion z-tests, chi-square tests, confidence intervals, as taught in introductory statistics.\n- **Weighting:** post-stratification and raking to known population margins; effective sample size (Kish approximation (Σw)² ÷ Σw²).\n- **Data quality:** speeders, straight-lining, attention checks; the total-survey-error framework.\n- **Multiple comparisons:** the false-discovery risk when running many tests; pre-specification.\n- **Driver analysis:** correlation and regression; importance-performance analysis; association vs causation.\n- **Standards:** ISO 20252; market-research codes of conduct; data protection laws (DPDP Act 2023 in India)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; the B2B logistics software customer survey)\n\n**Field:**\n- 9,600 contacts invited; 1,262 completes.\n- 62 removed (31 speeders, 19 straight-liners, 12 failed attention checks), leaving **n = 1,200 clean**.\n- Response rate 12.5% on clean completes.\n\n**Representativeness:** the sample is 70% SME (840) and 30% enterprise (360), but the customer base is 85% SME / 15% enterprise. Results are weighted to the true mix (SME weight 1.21, enterprise weight 0.50).\n- **Effective n ≈ 1,084**, so the margin of error is about **±3.0 points** overall.\n- Per segment: SME ±3.4, enterprise ±5.2 (at 50%).\n\n**Bottom line:**\n- Satisfaction is moderate (60% satisfied, weighted).\n- Enterprise customers are markedly less satisfied, driven by onboarding and support response time.\n- Investment should go to onboarding and support capacity before a mobile app.\n- **Confidence:** high on the direction; medium on driver magnitudes.\n\n| Finding | Evidence | Significant? | Confidence | So what |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 60% of customers are satisfied (top-2 box), weighted; the unweighted figure would show 58% | SME 62% (n=840); enterprise 48% (n=360) | — | High | Report the weighted figure; the unweighted sample understates SMEs |\n| Enterprise satisfaction is 14 points below SME | 48% vs 62% | Yes (z ≈ 4.5) | High | Enterprise retention plan needed; these are the highest-value accounts |\n| North-region customers are more satisfied than South | 48% (n=400) vs 38% (n=500) | Yes (z ≈ 3.0) | Medium | Investigate South support coverage (local language, hours) |\n| Mid-size (250-999) vs large (1,000+) enterprise satisfaction differs | 31% vs 26% (n=150 each) | **No** (z ≈ 0.96) | Low | Not detectable with this sample; don't act on it |\n| Field users need mobile access, but it's not a top driver | 41% use the product away from a desk weekly+; mobile ranked 4th of 6 as the \"one improvement\" | — | Medium | Phase the mobile app after onboarding and support fixes |\n\n**Key distribution:** value for money is polarised in enterprise (34% good or very good, 38% poor or very poor) but not in SME. The average alone (3.0) hides this.\n\n**Driver analysis (correlation with overall satisfaction; association, not causation):**\n- Onboarding r = 0.52; support response time r = 0.47; ease of use r = 0.44; reporting r = 0.31; integrations r = 0.28; price r = 0.21.\n- **Performance is lowest on** onboarding (41% satisfied) and support response time (44%). These are high-association, low-performance, so they are priorities.\n\n**Open-text themes (Q4, n = 1,036 answered):**\n- Slow support replies: 212.\n- Hard initial set-up: 174.\n- Missing reports: 96.\n- Price: 71.\n\n**Limitations:**\n- Customers only (no lost customers).\n- Self-reported satisfaction.\n- Fieldwork in the quarter-end peak may depress support scores.\n- **Validate:** link responses to churn data next quarter."}]}, {"id": "market-sizing", "pack": "market", "title": "Market Sizing", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Size a market top-down and bottom-up, reconcile the two, and show every assumption.", "tryp": "Size the market for AI-powered invoice processing for mid-size companies in India.", "when": ["Market entry or investment decisions", "Product launch business cases", "Checking a market number someone quoted"], "inputs": ["The product or service and the geography", "The customer definition", "Any known data points (prices, customer counts, reports)"], "pairs": ["customer-segmentation", "competitor-benchmark", "business-case", "source-validation"], "body": "# Market Sizing\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a market size you can defend under questioning. It is precisely defined, built two independent ways, reconciled, expressed as a range, and grounded in realistic share assumptions.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Definition is half the answer.** Product scope, customer type, geography, year, value vs volume, and the price point (manufacturer, retail or end-user spend). Most disagreements are definitional.\n2. **Two independent methods at least.** Top-down (from a known total, narrowed) and bottom-up (units × adoption × price). A third (supply-side: sum of player revenues; or analogue markets) when possible.\n3. **Reconcile, don't average.** If the methods differ by more than ~25-30%, find out why: definition, double counting, price-point mismatch.\n4. **TAM, SAM, SOM honestly:**\n   - **TAM:** the total demand for the need.\n   - **SAM:** the portion you can serve with your model, channels and geography.\n   - **SOM:** what you can realistically win in 3-5 years, benchmarked to what comparable entrants achieved.\n5. **Ranges, not false precision.** Give low / base / high, and name the swing assumption.\n6. **Every number is labelled:** sourced (with date) or estimated (with logic). Never present an estimate as a fact.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision (enter? invest? price?), the precision needed (±20% is often enough to screen).\n- The offering and its target customers; the geography and the year.\n- Available data: government statistics, industry bodies, company filings, surveys, internal data.\n\n## Method\n1. **Define** the market in one precise sentence; list inclusions and exclusions.\n2. **Top-down:** start from a published total (sector output, household consumption, spend category). Apply filters (segment share, relevant product share, price-point adjustments). Cite each.\n3. **Bottom-up:** number of target customers (by segment) × adoption or penetration × units or usage per customer × price. Build each driver from evidence (census, registries, surveys, pilot data).\n4. **Supply-side check (if possible):** sum the revenues of the main players (from filings) plus an estimate for the long tail.\n5. **Reconcile:** compare; explain differences; choose a base and justify it.\n6. **TAM / SAM / SOM:** apply serviceability filters for SAM. For SOM, use the share trajectories of comparable entrants and your capacity, channels and budget.\n7. **Range and sensitivity:** low / base / high; identify the most sensitive driver (compute with code).\n8. **Growth:** historic CAGR and forecast drivers (demographics, income, penetration, price, regulation, technology). Check the forecast's consistency with the drivers.\n9. **Source and estimate log:** each number with its source, date, or estimation logic.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Analyst reports disagree.** Use them as one input, check their definitions, and never rely on a single report for the key number.\n- **New categories** have no history. Use analogues (adoption curves of similar products) and willingness-to-pay data, and widen the ranges.\n- **Value vs volume:** price deflation can shrink value while volume grows. Show both if relevant.\n- **SOM ambition vs evidence:** if the plan needs 20% share in year 3 where comparable entrants reached 3-5%, flag it.\n\n## Red flags\n- A single TAM number from a press release.\n- Mixing retail and manufacturer prices.\n- SOM = 10% of TAM \"because it's conservative\".\n- No date or source on key drivers.\n- A forecast CAGR inconsistent with the stated drivers.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **B2B:** firms by size and industry (business registries, census of establishments) × adoption × contract value.\n- **Consumer:** households or individuals by income and geography × penetration × frequency × price.\n- **Healthcare:** disease prevalence × diagnosis rate × treatment rate × compliance × price.\n- **Financial services:** customers × product penetration × balances or premiums × margin.\n- **Software / SaaS:** firms × seats or usage × price per seat; land-and-expand dynamics.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Market Size: [market], [geography], [year], [value/volume at price point]\n**Definition:** ... (includes ...; excludes ...)\n\n### Bottom-up\n| Driver | Value | Source / basis |\n|---|---|---|\n### Top-down\n[start figure] × [filter] × [filter] = ... (sources)\n### Supply-side check (if available)\n### Reconciliation\n### TAM / SAM / SOM\n| | Low | Base | High |\n|---|---|---|---|\n**SOM basis (comparable entrants):** ...\n**Most sensitive assumption:** ...\n**Growth:** historic ...; forecast ... driven by ...\n**Source and estimate log:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Precise definition, including the price point and year.\n- [ ] At least two independent methods; reconciled, with reasons.\n- [ ] TAM, SAM and SOM with a benchmarked SOM.\n- [ ] Ranges and the most sensitive driver, computed with code.\n- [ ] Each number labelled as sourced (with date) or estimated (with logic).\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `customer-segmentation`: Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.\n- `competitor-benchmark`: Compare competitors on the factors that matter to customers and economics, and spot where to win.\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `source-validation`: Grade every source and claim for reliability so you never put a weak number in front of a board.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public data types and widely taught methods; verify current data)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Methods:** top-down, bottom-up and supply-side triangulation; TAM, SAM and SOM, as taught in strategy and entrepreneurship curricula.\n- **Public data sources (India; check the latest releases):** national statistics office surveys (e.g. household consumption expenditure, annual survey of industries, periodic labour force survey); Ministry of Corporate Affairs company data; RBI and sector-regulator statistics; GST and e-invoicing statistics; industry-ministry reports.\n- **International:** national statistics agencies; World Bank and IMF databases; UN trade data.\n- **Company filings:** annual reports and investor presentations of listed players for supply-side checks.\n- **Adoption analogues:** diffusion-of-innovation curves for comparable products and markets."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; AI-powered accounts-payable automation for mid-size companies in India, 2030, annual software and service spend at end-customer prices)\n\n**Definition:** annual spend by Indian companies with ₹100-1,000 Cr revenue on AP automation software and related services (invoice capture, matching, workflow, AI exception handling). Excludes ERP licences, BPO labour and large enterprises.\n\n## Bottom-up (base)\n| Driver | Value | Source / basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Target companies (₹100-1,000 Cr revenue) | ~38,000 | Company-registry filings analysis (est., 2026) |\n| Adoption by 2030 | 25% | Analogue: cloud-HRMS adoption in the same segment reached ~30% in 6 years (est.) |\n| Average annual spend per adopter | ₹6 lakh | Vendor list prices for 10-30k invoices/yr, with a 20% discount (est.) |\n| **Market 2030** | **₹570 Cr** | 38,000 × 25% × ₹6 L |\n\n## Top-down\n- Mid-market enterprise software spend 2030 (est. ₹19,000 Cr; industry body projection, verify) × finance-application share (~12%) × AP-automation share of finance apps (~25%).\n- = **₹570 Cr**, which coincides with the bottom-up; check for circularity (independent sources ✓).\n\n## Supply-side check\nTop 6 vendors' India mid-market revenue (filings and estimates) ≈ ₹140 Cr in 2026. At a ~38% CAGR this gives ~₹510 Cr in 2030, consistent.\n\n## TAM / SAM / SOM (2030, ₹ Cr)\n| | Low | Base | High |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| TAM (all target firms adopt) | 1,900 | 2,280 | 2,700 |\n| SAM (our ERP integrations cover 70% of firms; English plus 2 Indian languages) | 330 | 400 | 480 |\n| SOM (share 8-15% of SAM) | 26 | 44 | 72 |\n\n**SOM basis:** comparable SaaS entrants in Indian mid-market finance software reached 5-15% share of their serviceable segment within 4 years (est. from filings).\n**Most sensitive assumption:** adoption rate (15-35% moves the base market from ₹340 Cr to ₹800 Cr).\n**Growth:** e-invoicing mandates and GST compliance push adoption. AI reduces the cost per invoice, which may compress price per adopter (watch value vs volume)."}]}, {"id": "customer-segmentation", "pack": "market", "title": "Customer Segmentation", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.", "tryp": "Segment our B2B customers (logistics software) — we have revenue, industry, size, products used and support tickets for 800 accounts.", "when": ["Deciding which customers to focus on", "Designing offers, pricing tiers or sales coverage", "When 'one size fits all' is failing"], "inputs": ["Customer data or a description of the customer base", "The decision the segmentation serves", "Constraints (sales channels, products)"], "pairs": ["jobs-to-be-done", "pricing-strategy", "market-sizing", "unit-economics"], "body": "# Customer Segmentation\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a segmentation the business can actually use: segments that differ in what they need and what they're worth, can be identified by sales and systems, and come with clear choices about where to focus and how to serve each.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Decision first.** Segments exist to drive different actions (offers, pricing, channels, service levels). If two segments would be treated the same, merge them.\n2. **Needs and behaviours drive; descriptors identify.** Segment on why and how customers buy, then find observable traits (size, industry, geography, transaction patterns) to assign customers in practice.\n3. **Value and cost-to-serve.** Profitability by segment, not just revenue, determines priority.\n4. **Actionable rules.** Simple, explainable assignment rules beat black-box clusters that sales can't apply.\n5. **Few segments.** 4-7 is usually right. Too many dilute focus.\n6. **Validate and track.** Test segments with real behaviour; monitor migration between segments over time.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision: focus, pricing, channel design, product roadmap, service model.\n- Data available: transactions, product holdings, revenue and margin, service interactions, survey and interview data.\n- Constraints: channel capacity, product range, regulation (e.g. fair treatment).\n\n## Method\n1. **Hypothesise segmentation bases** from interviews and experience: needs (outcomes sought), behaviours (purchase frequency, channel use, price sensitivity), value (revenue, margin, potential), and firmographics or demographics.\n2. **Analyse data:** profile customers on the candidate bases. Use simple rules or clustering where data allows; check stability.\n3. **Form 4-7 segments.** Test each: sizeable, reachable or identifiable, different in needs or economics, stable, actionable.\n4. **Profile each:** name; size (count, revenue, margin); growth; needs and pains; buying process and decision-makers; channel preferences; price sensitivity; cost-to-serve; share of wallet and our position.\n5. **Assignment rules:** a decision tree or scoring rules using available data; coverage test (what % of customers are assigned unambiguously).\n6. **Prioritise:** attractiveness (size, growth, margin, fit) × ability to win (current share, capabilities, competition).\n7. **What changes per segment:** value proposition, product or bundle, pricing, channel and coverage model, service level, marketing message.\n8. **Validate:** test with sales teams and a sample of customers; pilot differentiated actions.\n9. **Track:** segment KPIs and migration; refresh annually.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Avoid demographic-only segments** (e.g. \"SMEs\") when needs vary widely within them.\n- **Use value and potential** to differentiate service intensity, but avoid unfair treatment where regulations require it (e.g. vulnerable customers in financial services).\n- **Segment migration** is often the real opportunity (moving customers into higher-value segments).\n- **Keep a \"low-touch\" segment** with a digital-first, efficient model. Not every customer needs high service.\n\n## Red flags\n- Segments that would receive identical treatment.\n- No assignment rules; sales can't identify the segments.\n- Segments sized by count only, with no value or margin.\n- 12 segments with tiny sizes.\n- A segmentation done once and never used.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **B2B:** firmographics plus needs (e.g. compliance-driven vs growth-driven), buying centre, contract size, cost-to-serve.\n- **Consumer:** life stage, needs, behaviours, channel, price sensitivity; privacy constraints on data use.\n- **Banking and insurance:** needs-based segments with fairness and suitability rules; value tiers for service.\n- **Healthcare:** patient segments by condition, payer and engagement; referral-source segments.\n- **SaaS:** usage-based segments, expansion potential, product-led vs sales-led.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Customer Segmentation: [business], for [decision]\n**Basis:** primary ... | secondary ... | descriptors for assignment ...\n\n| Segment | Size (#, revenue, margin) | Growth | Defining needs | Buying behaviour | Cost-to-serve | Our position | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Profiles:** short paragraph per segment\n**Assignment rules:** decision tree / scoring; coverage ...%\n**What changes by segment:** proposition | product | price | channel | service\n**Validation and tracking:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Linked to a decision; segments drive different actions.\n- [ ] Needs and behaviours are the basis; descriptors make segments assignable.\n- [ ] Size, value, margin and cost-to-serve per segment.\n- [ ] Clear assignment rules with coverage.\n- [ ] Priorities, and what changes per segment.\n- [ ] A validation and tracking plan; fairness considered where regulated.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `jobs-to-be-done`: Understand the real 'job' customers hire you for, and the outcomes they measure you on.\n- `pricing-strategy`: Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.\n- `market-sizing`: Size a market top-down and bottom-up, reconcile the two, and show every assumption.\n- `unit-economics`: Work out whether each customer, order or unit makes money, and which levers fix it.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts; verify regulatory specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Market segmentation:** needs-based, behavioural, value-based and descriptive bases, as taught in marketing-strategy curricula; segment-attractiveness criteria (measurable, accessible, substantial, differentiable, actionable).\n- **Customer profitability and cost-to-serve analysis:** management-accounting practice.\n- **Clustering and rules-based assignment:** standard analytics methods (k-means, decision trees). Explainability matters for adoption.\n- **Fair treatment:** consumer-protection and sector rules (e.g. financial-services conduct expectations) and data-protection law (DPDP Act 2023) on using personal data for segmentation."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional logistics-software provider, 800 B2B customers)\n\n**Decision:** redesign sales coverage and customer-success models for next year.\n**Basis:** needs (scale and integration complexity, growth stage) + value (annual contract value, margin) + behaviour (product usage, support load).\n\n| Segment | Customers | Revenue / margin | Growth | Needs | Behaviour | Cost-to-serve | Position | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Scaling shippers | 112 (14%) | ₹48 Cr (31%) / 62% | +22% | Multi-warehouse, APIs, carrier integrations | Heavy API use; frequent change requests | High | Strong | **1: invest** |\n| Stable mid-market | 260 (33%) | ₹52 Cr (34%) / 68% | +6% | Reliability, compliance reports | Steady usage; low support | Medium | Strong | 2: retain and upsell |\n| Small digital-first | 330 (41%) | ₹21 Cr (14%) / 55% | +15% | Self-serve, low price | Low usage depth; churn 18% | Low (digital) | Medium | 3: automate |\n| Enterprise pilots | 18 (2%) | ₹19 Cr (12%) / 40% | +40% | Custom workflows, security reviews | Long cycles; bespoke | Very high | Weak | 4: selective |\n| Dormant / at-risk | 80 (10%) | ₹14 Cr (9%) / 50% | −20% | — | Usage down >50% over 6 months | Medium | — | Win-back or exit |\n\n**Assignment rules:**\n1. ACV ≥ ₹1 Cr and security review → Enterprise.\n2. Else, ≥3 warehouses or API calls ≥50k/month → Scaling.\n3. Else, ACV ≥ ₹10 L → Stable mid-market.\n4. Else → Small digital-first.\n5. Override: usage −50% over 6 months → At-risk.\n\nCoverage: 97% assigned automatically; 3% by manual review.\n\n**What changes:**\n- **Scaling:** a named solution architect and CSM; an integration roadmap council.\n- **Stable:** pooled CSMs; a compliance-reporting add-on.\n- **Small:** onboarding automation; in-app help; monthly plans.\n- **Enterprise:** pursue only deals with a ≥50% product fit; price the professional services fully.\n- **At-risk:** a 60-day win-back playbook.\n\n**Validation:** reviewed with 6 sales leads and 20 customers; a pilot of the new coverage model in 2 regions for Q1."}]}, {"id": "competitor-benchmark", "pack": "market", "title": "Competitor Benchmark", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Compare competitors on the factors that matter to customers and economics, and spot where to win.", "tryp": "Benchmark the top 5 competitors of a mid-size Indian private bank on digital, cost-to-income, CASA ratio and SME lending.", "when": ["Strategy reviews and market entry", "Pricing and product decisions", "Board or investor briefings on competitive position"], "inputs": ["Your company and market", "Competitors you know of", "Criteria that matter (or let the skill propose them)", "Any data you have"], "pairs": ["five-forces", "swot-tows", "pricing-strategy", "source-validation"], "body": "# Competitor Benchmark\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a fair, evidence-based picture of where you stand against the alternatives customers actually consider, and what competitors are likely to do next, translated into specific implications.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **The competitive set is defined by customers,** not by industry codes. Include substitutes and new business models (including AI-native entrants).\n2. **Criteria come from choice drivers.** What do customers choose on (price, quality, speed, service, breadth, trust, integration)? Pair them with economics (growth, margin, cost ratios) and capabilities.\n3. **Like for like.** Normalise definitions, fiscal years, currencies and segments. Label estimates.\n4. **Look forward.** Strategy, investments, hiring, partnerships and pricing moves signal future positions.\n5. **So what.** End with implications: where to defend, attack, differentiate, or avoid.\n6. **Ethical intelligence only.** Public sources, customer and expert interviews conducted properly, and your own data. No confidential information obtained improperly; comply with competition law (no sharing of competitively sensitive information).\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision (pricing, product, entry, investment) and the segment or geography.\n- The customer choice drivers (from research, win/loss analysis, reviews).\n- Available data: filings, investor materials, websites, reviews, mystery shopping, expert calls, win/loss data.\n\n## Method\n1. **Define the set:** 4-8 competitors across three rings (direct, adjacent, substitutes or new entrants).\n2. **Select criteria (8-12):** customer-facing (price, quality, features, service levels, speed, coverage, brand trust), economic (revenue growth, margins, cost ratios, scale), and capability (technology, data, talent, distribution, partnerships).\n3. **Collect data:** cite sources and dates; mark estimates; normalise.\n4. **Benchmark table:** us vs each competitor; highlight gaps (lead / parity / lag).\n5. **Positioning map:** pick the two dimensions customers value most; plot competitors; identify clusters and white space.\n6. **Competitor profiles:** strategy, recent moves, strengths, vulnerabilities, likely next moves and triggers.\n7. **Win/loss insight** (if data exists): why we win and lose against each.\n8. **Implications:** where we lead and must defend; where we lag and must fix or avoid; white space to attack; competitor moves to pre-empt.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Benchmarks against the best competitor in each dimension** (not one average) reveal achievable targets.\n- **Small, fast entrants** often matter more than large incumbents. Track their customer traction, not their size.\n- **Beware vanity metrics** (feature counts). Weight criteria by customer importance.\n- **Public filings lag.** Combine them with timely signals (job postings, product releases, pricing pages, customer reviews).\n\n## Red flags\n- Only the familiar rival is analysed.\n- Mixed periods or definitions.\n- Feature checklists with no customer weighting.\n- No implications.\n- Information from questionable sources (former employees sharing confidential data).\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Pricing decisions:** price ladders, discount practices, value-for-money positioning.\n- **Investment / M&A:** financial benchmarks (growth, margins, returns, valuation multiples).\n- **Banking and insurance:** cost-to-income, NIM or combined ratio, digital adoption, NPS, regulatory ratios.\n- **Technology / SaaS:** product capability, integrations, implementation time, net revenue retention, pricing model.\n- **Operations:** cost per unit, productivity, quality, delivery performance.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Competitor Benchmark: [market / segment], [period]\n**Competitive set:** direct ... | adjacent ... | substitutes / new entrants ...\n**Choice drivers (weights):** ...\n\n| Criterion (weight) | Us | Comp A | Comp B | Comp C | Best-in-class | Source / date |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Positioning map:** x = ..., y = ... (positions and white space)\n**Profiles and likely next moves:** ...\n**Win/loss insights:** ...\n**Implications:** lead / lag / white space / pre-empt\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The competitive set includes substitutes and new entrants.\n- [ ] Criteria are weighted by customer choice drivers, plus economics and capabilities.\n- [ ] Normalised data with sources and dates; estimates labelled.\n- [ ] A positioning map and forward-looking profiles.\n- [ ] Clear implications; ethical and competition-law-compliant sourcing.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `five-forces`: Judge how attractive an industry is and what drives profits, using the five forces framework with evidence.\n- `swot-tows`: Do SWOT properly: evidence-based factors, then turn them into concrete strategic moves (TOWS).\n- `pricing-strategy`: Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.\n- `source-validation`: Grade every source and claim for reliability so you never put a weak number in front of a board.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public data types and laws; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Competitive analysis methods:** competitor profiling, positioning maps and win/loss analysis, from strategy and marketing curricula.\n- **Public information:** annual reports, investor presentations, regulatory disclosures (e.g. bank Basel III Pillar 3 disclosures), websites, pricing pages, job postings, product release notes, customer reviews.\n- **Competitive-intelligence ethics:** professional codes requiring lawful, ethical collection and the avoidance of misrepresentation.\n- **Competition law:** India's Competition Act, 2002 (anti-competitive agreements; information exchange among competitors) and equivalents elsewhere. Avoid exchanging competitively sensitive information with rivals."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional mid-size Indian private bank vs 4 peers, MSME and retail focus, FY26)\n\n**Competitive set:**\n- **Direct:** Banks A, B, C (mid-size private).\n- **Adjacent:** small finance bank D.\n- **Substitutes / new entrants:** fintech lenders (digital MSME credit), represented by a composite.\n\n**Choice drivers (MSME customers, from 60 interviews):** speed of credit decision (30%), relationship-manager quality (20%), pricing (20%), digital banking (15%), product breadth (15%).\n\n| Criterion | Us | Bank A | Bank B | Bank C | SFB D | Fintech composite | Source / date |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| MSME loan decision time (≤₹50 L) | 9 days | 7 | 12 | 6 | 4 | 2 days | Mystery shopping, Q1 FY27 |\n| Cost-to-income | 54% | 47% | 51% | 45% | 58% | — | Annual reports FY26 |\n| CASA ratio | 34% | 38% | 31% | 41% | 22% | — | Annual reports FY26 |\n| Digital MSME onboarding (fully online) | No | Partial | No | Yes | Yes | Yes | Websites, Q1 FY27 |\n| Gross NPA (MSME) | 3.9% | 3.1% | 4.6% | 2.8% | 4.2% | n/a | Disclosures FY26 |\n| Customer NPS (MSME) | 31 | 35 | 22 | 38 | 29 | 44 | Survey, n=600 (est.) |\n\n**Positioning map** (speed of credit vs relationship depth):\n- Fintechs: fast and thin.\n- Banks A and C: fast-ish and deep.\n- **We sit mid-slow and deep.**\n- White space: \"fast and deep\" for MSMEs needing ₹20 L-₹2 Cr.\n\n**Likely next moves:**\n- Bank C is expanding its digital MSME lending to Tier-2 cities (job postings, product launches).\n- Fintechs are partnering with banks for co-lending (regulatory filings).\n\n**Implications:**\n- **Lag:** decision speed and cost-to-income. Fix with straight-through processing for loans under ₹50 L, targeting ≤3 days.\n- **Lead:** relationship depth in Tier-2 clusters. Defend it with an RM-plus-digital model.\n- **White space:** fast and deep MSME credit of ₹20 L-₹2 Cr.\n- **Pre-empt:** consider co-lending with a fintech to match speed while using our balance sheet."}]}, {"id": "five-forces", "pack": "market", "title": "Five Forces", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Judge how attractive an industry is and what drives profits, using the five forces framework with evidence.", "tryp": "Five forces analysis for the Indian quick-commerce grocery industry.", "when": ["Market entry or exit", "Investment or M&A screening", "Explaining why margins are moving"], "inputs": ["Industry and geography", "Whether you are an incumbent, entrant or investor", "Any data you have"], "pairs": ["pestel-scan", "competitor-benchmark", "value-chain", "playbook-market-entry"], "body": "# Five Forces\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear explanation of why an industry is as profitable as it is, how that will change, and what a specific player should do about it: where to compete, how to position, and which forces to try to influence.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Define the industry precisely:** product scope and geography. \"Indian cement\" behaves very differently from \"cement in South India\"; regional markets have different rivalry.\n2. **Drivers, not adjectives.** Each force is rated from structural evidence (concentration, switching costs, scale economies, capital requirements, differentiation, buyer concentration, input substitutability).\n3. **Cross-check with profits.** Compare the conclusion with actual industry margins and returns on capital over a cycle. If the forces say \"unattractive\" but returns are high, re-examine.\n4. **Regulation, complements and technology** are not forces, but they shape forces. Include them explicitly.\n5. **Dynamics matter most.** Strategy depends on how forces will shift (e.g. AI lowering entry barriers, consolidation raising buyer power).\n6. **Implications for a specific player.** The analysis should end in positioning and actions, not a generic description.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The industry definition and the player whose perspective matters (incumbent, entrant, investor, supplier).\n- The horizon (3-5 years typical).\n- Available data: market shares, margins, capacity, cost structures, switching costs, regulatory changes.\n\n## Method\n1. **Define the industry** (product and geography) and its value chain position.\n2. **For each force, list drivers and evidence:**\n   - **Rivalry:** number and balance of competitors, industry growth, fixed costs and capacity utilisation, product differentiation, exit barriers, price competition patterns.\n   - **Threat of new entrants:** scale economies, capital requirements, access to distribution, regulatory barriers and licences, brand and switching costs, incumbent retaliation, technology shifts lowering barriers.\n   - **Buyer power:** buyer concentration, volume share, switching costs, price sensitivity, backward-integration threat, information availability.\n   - **Supplier power:** supplier concentration, uniqueness of inputs, switching costs, forward-integration threat, importance of the industry to suppliers.\n   - **Substitutes:** price-performance of alternatives, switching costs, buyer propensity to switch.\n3. **Rate each force** Low / Medium / High, with a one-line justification.\n4. **Profitability cross-check:** industry margins and ROCE over a cycle; reconcile with the ratings.\n5. **Shaping factors:** regulation, complements, technology (including AI and digital platforms), ESG requirements.\n6. **Trends:** how each force will change over the horizon, and why; signposts.\n7. **Implications:** where profit pools are and will be; positioning (cost leadership, differentiation, focus); actions to shape forces (consolidation, switching costs, partnerships, vertical moves); go or no-go for entrants and investors.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Segment-level analysis** often reveals attractive niches in unattractive industries.\n- **Platforms and ecosystems** change the forces (network effects raise entry barriers; aggregators raise buyer power). Analyse the platform's role.\n- **Government as a buyer or price-setter** (e.g. healthcare tariffs, public procurement) is a powerful buyer force.\n- **Don't confuse growth with attractiveness.** Fast-growing markets can have intense rivalry and low returns.\n\n## Red flags\n- An industry defined too broadly.\n- Ratings without evidence; no profitability cross-check.\n- No trends; a static snapshot.\n- Generic implications (\"compete on quality\").\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Investor screening:** focus on durability of returns and on threats to margins.\n- **Entrant:** focus on entry barriers, incumbent retaliation and niche entry points.\n- **Incumbent:** focus on shaping forces and defending the profit pool.\n- **Regulated sectors:** regulator as a quasi-force (tariffs, licences, mandates).\n- **Digital markets:** network effects, data advantages, multi-homing costs.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Five Forces: [industry, geography], perspective of [player], horizon [..]\n\n| Force | Rating | Key drivers and evidence | Trend (direction, why) | Signposts |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Rivalry | | | | |\n| New entrants | | | | |\n| Buyer power | | | | |\n| Supplier power | | | | |\n| Substitutes | | | | |\n\n**Shaping factors (regulation, complements, technology):** ...\n**Profitability cross-check:** industry margins/ROCE ... → consistent / explained by ...\n**Profit pools now and in future:** ...\n**Implications for [player]:** positioning ... | actions to shape forces ... | go / no-go ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Precise industry definition and perspective.\n- [ ] Each force rated with structural evidence.\n- [ ] A profitability cross-check.\n- [ ] Shaping factors and trends with signposts.\n- [ ] Specific implications for the player.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `pestel-scan`: Scan political, economic, social, technological, environmental and legal shifts, and rank what really matters.\n- `competitor-benchmark`: Compare competitors on the factors that matter to customers and economics, and spot where to win.\n- `value-chain`: Map where value and cost sit across the chain, and find where to compete, cut or integrate.\n- `playbook-market-entry`: Decide whether, where and how to enter a new market, from attractiveness to entry mode and launch plan.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts and public data)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Five forces framework of industry analysis:** rivalry, entry, buyer power, supplier power, substitutes. A core strategy-curriculum framework, with later extensions on complements and the role of government.\n- **Industry profitability data:** company filings; industry-association reports; capital-market data on returns by sector.\n- **Platform economics:** network effects, multi-homing, aggregator power (strategy and economics curricula).\n- **Competition and sector regulation:** Competition Act, 2002 (India) and sector regulators, as shaping factors."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; quick-commerce grocery in India's top 8 metros, perspective of an investor considering a growth-stage player, horizon 2026-2030)\n\n| Force | Rating | Drivers and evidence | Trend | Signposts |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Rivalry | **High** | 4-5 well-funded players; heavy discounting; high fixed costs of dark-store networks; low differentiation on core grocery | Easing slightly as funding tightens and players focus on contribution margin; consolidation possible | Discount depth; store-opening pace; funding rounds |\n| New entrants | **Medium** | High capital needs and density economics deter small entrants; but large e-commerce and retail groups can enter with existing supply chains | Stable; large incumbents' entry is the main risk | Launch announcements by large retailers |\n| Buyer power (consumers) | **Medium-High** | Zero switching costs; multi-app usage; price comparison is easy | Stable; loyalty programmes may raise switching costs slightly | Membership uptake; multi-homing rates |\n| Supplier power | **Low-Medium** | FMCG brands need the channel for impulse and urban reach; some large brands negotiate hard; private labels growing | Supplier power falls as quick commerce becomes a key channel | Ad-revenue share; private-label share |\n| Substitutes | **Medium** | Neighbourhood stores (fast, credit, relationships); scheduled e-grocery; modern trade | Neighbourhood stores digitising, so substitute strength rises | Neighbourhood-store app adoption |\n\n**Shaping factors:**\n- Regulation on dark stores (local zoning) and foreign-investment rules for inventory models.\n- Retail-media advertising as a complement and profit source.\n- AI-driven demand forecasting lowering waste.\n\n**Profitability cross-check:** most players report negative EBITDA, though improving contribution margins in mature cities. This is consistent with high rivalry and buyer power. Mature dark stores reportedly reach positive contribution (company disclosures; verify).\n\n**Profit pools:** advertising and private labels are more profitable than delivery fees; dense metro catchments beat expansion cities.\n\n**Implications for the investor:**\n- Back players with leading density in the top 3-4 metros, rising advertising and private-label share, and discipline on new-city expansion.\n- Watch for the entry of large retail groups.\n- **Go**, with conditions on unit-economics milestones (contribution per order positive in ≥70% of stores)."}]}, {"id": "pestel-scan", "pack": "market", "title": "PESTEL Scan", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Scan political, economic, social, technological, environmental and legal shifts, and rank what really matters.", "tryp": "PESTEL scan for a European pharma company setting up a manufacturing plant in Telangana.", "when": ["Strategy refresh or market entry", "Input to scenarios or risk registers", "Board horizon scanning"], "inputs": ["Business, market and geography", "Time horizon"], "pairs": ["scenario-planning", "five-forces", "risk-assessment", "esg-materiality"], "body": "# PESTEL Scan\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a focused view of the external shifts that will actually move this business. Each factor comes with evidence, a sense of how big and how certain it is, what to do about it, and what to watch.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Specific, not generic.** \"Interest rates\" belongs only if they materially affect this business (e.g. financing for customers, the cost of debt). Each factor names the mechanism of impact.\n2. **Evidence with dates.** Laws, rates, incentives and policies change. Every factor cites its source and date, and time-sensitive ones are flagged for live checks.\n3. **Impact × certainty.** High-impact, high-certainty factors go into the base plan; high-impact, low-certainty factors become scenario axes or risks.\n4. **Both directions.** Each factor can create opportunity or threat; often both for different segments.\n5. **From scan to action.** The top 5-8 factors get implications, owners and signposts.\n6. **Horizon-appropriate.** Short horizons emphasise policy and economic cycles; long horizons emphasise demographics, technology and climate.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The business, market, geography and horizon.\n- The decision the scan informs (entry, investment, strategy).\n- Existing risk registers and scenario work.\n\n## Method\n1. **Brainstorm 4-8 factors per category**, specific to the business:\n   - **Political:** government priorities, trade policy, industrial incentives, stability, public procurement.\n   - **Economic:** growth, inflation, rates, FX, commodity prices, consumer demand, credit availability.\n   - **Social:** demographics, urbanisation, income distribution, preferences, workforce skills, trust.\n   - **Technological:** AI and automation, digital public infrastructure, platform shifts, cost curves, cybersecurity.\n   - **Environmental:** climate physical risks, transition policy, resource scarcity, circularity, disclosure requirements.\n   - **Legal:** sector regulation, data protection, consumer protection, labour codes, competition, tax.\n2. **For each factor:** description of the trend; evidence and date; mechanism of impact on the business; impact (H/M/L); certainty (H/M/L); opportunity or threat; time-sensitivity flag.\n3. **Prioritise:** plot impact vs certainty; select the top 5-8.\n4. **Implications for each top factor:** what it means for strategy, operations and finances; actions (exploit, prepare, hedge, monitor); owner.\n5. **Signposts:** observable indicators with thresholds that tell you the factor is materialising.\n6. **Handoffs:** high-impact, low-certainty factors → `scenario-planning`; threats → `risk-assessment`; ESG items → `esg-materiality`.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Avoid laundry lists.** 30 factors with no prioritisation are less useful than 8 well-evidenced ones.\n- **Distinguish announced from enacted.** Draft rules and proposals carry different certainty from notified law.\n- **Local beats national.** State-level policies, incentives and labour rules often matter more for site decisions.\n- **Second-order effects:** e.g. AI adoption → skill shifts → wage changes → location economics.\n\n## Red flags\n- Generic factors with no mechanism of impact.\n- Undated regulatory claims stated as current.\n- No prioritisation; no implications or signposts.\n- Opportunities ignored.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Market entry:** emphasise political stability, regulation and licensing, incentives, tax, FX, labour and land.\n- **Manufacturing location:** incentives, infrastructure, power costs and reliability, labour availability, environmental clearances.\n- **Financial services:** monetary policy, credit cycle, digital public infrastructure, regulatory change, data protection.\n- **Healthcare:** public-funding schemes, pricing regulation, demographics and disease burden, technology adoption.\n- **Technology:** AI regulation, data localisation, talent supply, export controls.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## PESTEL Scan: [business / market], [geography], horizon [..]\n\n| Category | Factor | Trend and evidence (source, date) | Mechanism of impact | Impact | Certainty | Opp / Threat | Verify (time-sensitive) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Priority grid:** high impact / high certainty: ... | high impact / low certainty: ...\n**Top factors → implications → actions → owner → signpost (threshold)**\n1. ...\n**Handoffs:** scenarios ... | risk register ... | ESG ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Factors are specific, with a mechanism of impact.\n- [ ] Evidence is dated; time-sensitive items flagged for verification.\n- [ ] Impact and certainty rated; the top 5-8 prioritised.\n- [ ] Implications, actions, owners and signposts for the top factors.\n- [ ] Opportunities included; handoffs to scenarios and risks.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `scenario-planning`: Build 3-4 plausible futures from key uncertainties, and find moves that work across them.\n- `five-forces`: Judge how attractive an industry is and what drives profits, using the five forces framework with evidence.\n- `risk-assessment`: Identify, score and plan responses to the risks of a project, decision or business.\n- `esg-materiality`: Identify and prioritise the sustainability topics that matter most, using double materiality.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public data types; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **PESTEL / PEST analysis:** a standard environmental-scanning framework in strategy curricula.\n- **Official sources (India; check the latest):** Gazette notifications; ministry and regulator websites (e.g. pharmaceuticals, environment, labour, finance); state industrial policies and pollution-control board conditions; RBI publications; national statistics releases.\n- **International:** central-bank and statistics-agency data; multilateral organisations' outlooks; trade-policy announcements.\n- **Climate and environment:** national and state climate action plans; climate scenario sets used for disclosure (IFRS S2).\n- **Horizon scanning practice:** government foresight toolkits; signposts and indicators."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; European pharma company planning an API manufacturing plant in Telangana, horizon 2027-2032)\n\n| Cat. | Factor | Trend and evidence | Mechanism | Impact | Certainty | O/T | Verify |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P | Production-linked incentive (PLI) support for key starting materials and APIs | Scheme notified with application windows and eligible products (check the current notification and remaining windows) | Capex and per-unit incentives improve IRR | H | M | O | **Yes** |\n| P | Supply-chain diversification by Western buyers (\"China+1\") | Buyer sourcing policies and trade measures | Demand for non-China API capacity | H | M-H | O | Yes |\n| E | INR/EUR volatility | Historic volatility ~5-8% a year | Margin on export contracts; capex in EUR | M | M | T | — |\n| E | Solvent and energy price inflation | Commodity trends | Conversion cost | M | M | T | — |\n| S | Availability of skilled chemists and QA staff in the Hyderabad cluster | Large pharma cluster; rising wages and attrition | Ramp-up speed; quality compliance | H | H | O/T | — |\n| T | Continuous-flow manufacturing and process analytics | Regulatory encouragement of advanced manufacturing | Lower cost and better quality; needs capability | M | M | O | — |\n| T | AI in quality and batch-record review | Rapid vendor adoption | Faster release; data-integrity controls needed | M | M | O | — |\n| En | Effluent norms and zero-liquid-discharge expectations | State pollution-control board conditions | Capex for ZLD; licence-to-operate risk | H | H | T | **Yes** |\n| En | Water stress in the region | Groundwater data | Operating risk; community relations | M | M | T | — |\n| L | Revised Schedule M (Good Manufacturing Practice) compliance for Indian manufacturers | Notified with phased deadlines | Upgrades to the quality system; inspection readiness | H | H | T/O | **Yes** |\n| L | Export-market inspections (e.g. USFDA, EU GMP) | Inspection frequency rising | Warning-letter risk affects all exports | H | H | T | — |\n| L | DPDP Act (employee and patient data in trials) | Most obligations from May 2027 | Data processes | L-M | H | T | Yes |\n\n**Top factors → implications:**\n1. **Effluent and ZLD:** design ZLD from day 1 (₹45-60 Cr capex est.) — EHS Head. Signpost: consent-to-establish conditions.\n2. **GMP (Schedule M and export inspections):** build the quality system to the strictest export standard; a data-integrity programme — Quality Head. Signpost: inspection observations in the cluster.\n3. **PLI eligibility:** apply in the current window; align the product list — Strategy. Signpost: approval letter.\n4. **Talent:** an early hiring and training academy; retention plans — CHRO. Signpost: QA attrition >15%.\n5. **China+1 demand:** secure 2 anchor customer agreements before commissioning — Commercial. Signpost: signed agreements.\n\n**Handoffs:**\n- **Scenarios:** buyer diversification speed × incentive continuity.\n- **Risk register:** effluent, inspection outcomes, FX.\n- **ESG:** water and effluent."}]}, {"id": "value-chain", "pack": "market", "title": "Value Chain Analysis", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Map where value and cost sit across the chain, and find where to compete, cut or integrate.", "tryp": "Map the value chain for branded generics pharma in India and show where the profit pools are.", "when": ["Cost reduction and outsourcing questions", "Vertical integration or partnership decisions", "Understanding where disruption will hit"], "inputs": ["Company or industry", "Cost or margin data if available", "Question to answer"], "pairs": ["cost-takeout", "build-buy-partner", "operating-model", "five-forces"], "body": "# Value Chain Analysis\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a picture of where value and profit are created and captured, where you are strong or weak, and where the chain is shifting, so you can decide where to compete, what to own, and what to change.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Two lenses, chosen deliberately.** A *company* value chain (your activities) for cost and capability questions; an *industry* chain (inputs → manufacturing → distribution → retail → end user → after-sales) for profit-pool and integration questions.\n2. **Numbers at each step:** cost share, margin, capital employed, returns, and the number and concentration of players. Without numbers, it's a diagram, not an analysis.\n3. **Profit pools are not revenue pools.** The largest revenue step is often not the most profitable.\n4. **Advantage is located.** Identify the specific activities where you outperform (or underperform) competitors, with evidence (cost per unit, quality, speed).\n5. **Value migrates.** Technology, regulation and customer behaviour shift value between steps (e.g. to platforms, data owners, software layers, after-sales).\n6. **Options follow the map:** improve, automate, outsource, integrate, partner, exit, or reposition along the chain.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The question: cost, sourcing, integration, disruption or entry.\n- The company's or industry's chain and the data available (cost breakdowns, margins by step, peer data).\n- Technology and regulatory changes affecting the chain.\n\n## Method\n1. **Map the chain:**\n   - **Company:** primary activities (inbound logistics → operations → outbound → sales and marketing → service) plus support (procurement, technology, HR, infrastructure).\n   - **Industry:** steps from raw inputs to end customer, plus after-sales.\n2. **For each step:** activities; who performs them (us, suppliers, partners); cost share; margin; capital intensity; number and concentration of players; key success factors.\n3. **Profit pools:** estimate the profit at each industry step (revenue × margin); visualise as a width (revenue) × height (margin) chart described in text.\n4. **Our position:** performance vs peers at each step (cost, quality, speed); sources of advantage and disadvantage.\n5. **Value shifts:** technology (automation, AI, digital platforms), regulation, customer behaviour; which steps gain or lose.\n6. **Options:** for each step: improve (lean, AI), outsource (if non-differentiating), integrate (if margins or control justify it), partner, exit or reposition. Size the impact roughly.\n7. **Recommendations,** with the trade-offs and the next analyses needed.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Integration is tempting but costly.** Integrate when the step is critical to quality or supply security, when margins are attractive and sustainable, or when coordination failures are costly. Not just because the margin is high.\n- **Outsourcing non-core steps** frees capital, but can hollow out capabilities. Keep the know-how to manage suppliers.\n- **After-sales and data** are often under-appreciated profit pools.\n- **Use cost-to-serve** for downstream steps; it often reveals unprofitable channels or customers.\n\n## Red flags\n- An activity diagram with no numbers.\n- Revenue pools confused with profit pools.\n- Advantage asserted without peer evidence.\n- No view of how value will shift.\n- Integration recommended without capability or capital analysis.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Manufacturing:** materials, conversion, logistics, distribution margins, after-market.\n- **Pharma:** API → formulation → distribution → retail pharmacy → prescriber influence; trade margins.\n- **Financial services:** origination → underwriting → servicing → collections → funding; fintech unbundling.\n- **Retail and consumer:** sourcing → brand → distribution → retail → returns; marketplaces and quick commerce.\n- **Technology and AI:** chips → cloud → models → applications → services; where margins are moving.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Value Chain: [company / industry], [geography], [year]\n\n| Step | Activities | Who performs | Cost share | Margin | Capital intensity | Players / concentration | Our position (evidence) | Shift underway |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Profit pools (now → future):** ...\n**Sources of advantage / disadvantage:** ...\n**Options by step:** improve / automate / outsource / integrate / partner / exit - rough impact\n**Recommendations and trade-offs:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The right lens (company vs industry) for the question.\n- [ ] Numbers per step: cost share, margin, capital, players.\n- [ ] Profit pools distinguished from revenue; future shifts identified.\n- [ ] Advantage located with peer evidence.\n- [ ] Options sized, with trade-offs.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `cost-takeout`: Build a structured cost-reduction plan: baseline, levers, sizing, phasing and risks to service.\n- `build-buy-partner`: Decide whether to build a capability, acquire it, or partner, with a clear rationale.\n- `operating-model`: Design how the organisation will work: structure, processes, people, technology, governance and locations.\n- `five-forces`: Judge how attractive an industry is and what drives profits, using the five forces framework with evidence.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts and public data)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Value chain analysis:** primary and support activities as sources of competitive advantage; industry value-system analysis. Core strategy-curriculum concepts.\n- **Profit-pool mapping:** revenue × margin by value-chain step to locate where profits are earned.\n- **Make-or-buy and vertical integration:** transaction-cost and capability considerations (see `build-buy-partner`).\n- **Sector data (India; verify current rules):** trade-margin regulation and price controls for pharmaceuticals (national pharmaceutical pricing authority orders); company filings; industry-association statistics."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; branded generics pharma in India, industry lens, % of maximum retail price (MRP))\n\n| Step | Activities | Who | Share of MRP | Margin (on own revenue) | Capital | Concentration | Our position | Shift |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| API / key starting materials | Chemical synthesis | API makers (domestic + imports) | ~10-12% | 12-18% EBITDA | High | Moderate; import dependence for some | We buy 70% externally | Incentives for domestic APIs; China+1 |\n| Formulation manufacturing | Tablets, capsules, injectables | Branded companies + contract manufacturers | ~15-18% (cost) | — | Medium-high | Fragmented contract manufacturing | Own 3 plants; cost per unit ~8% above best peer | Tighter GMP rules raise compliance costs |\n| Brand and marketing | Medical representatives, brand building | Branded companies | ~20-25% (incl. field force) | Brand EBITDA ~20-25% (est.) | Low | Top 10 hold ~45% share | Strong in 2 therapies (cardiac, diabetes) | Digital detailing; trade-generics growth |\n| Distribution | Stockists, distributors | Wholesale network | ~8-10% (trade margin) | 2-4% | Working capital | Consolidating | Standard terms | Digital B2B distribution platforms |\n| Retail pharmacy | Dispensing | Chemists, chains, e-pharmacy | ~16-20% (trade margin) | 4-8% | Medium | Fragmented; chains growing | — | Chains and e-pharmacy gain bargaining power |\n\n(Shares are illustrative; verify against current trade-margin rules and company data.)\n\n**Profit pools:** brand ownership (marketing-led) earns the highest returns on capital. Distribution is high-revenue but low-margin. API is attractive under incentives but capital-heavy.\n\n**Our advantage and disadvantage:**\n- **Advantage:** prescriber relationships in cardiac and diabetes (market share 2x our overall average).\n- **Disadvantage:** manufacturing cost and field-force productivity (prescriptions per medical rep 15% below peers).\n\n**Options:**\n- **Improve:** field-force productivity via AI-assisted targeting and digital detailing (+₹30-40 Cr EBITDA est.).\n- **Outsource:** move low-volume SKUs to contract manufacturers (capex avoided ₹60 Cr).\n- **Integrate (selective):** backward integration into 2 critical APIs under incentives (supply security for top brands).\n- **Partner:** with retail chains and e-pharmacy for adherence programmes in chronic therapies.\n\n**Trade-off:** API integration adds capital and complexity. Pursue it only for molecules where supply risk threatens top brands."}]}, {"id": "jobs-to-be-done", "pack": "market", "title": "Jobs To Be Done", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Understand the real 'job' customers hire you for, and the outcomes they measure you on.", "tryp": "What jobs do CFOs hire an FP&A software for? Build a jobs-to-be-done map.", "when": ["Product or service design", "Positioning and messaging", "Finding innovation white space"], "inputs": ["Customer type and context", "Any interview notes or research", "Your product (optional)"], "pairs": ["customer-segmentation", "interview-guide", "pricing-strategy", "ai-opportunity-scan"], "body": "# Jobs To Be Done\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear understanding of the progress customers are trying to make, where today's solutions fail them, and what would make them switch, turned into opportunities your teams can build and sell.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Jobs are stable; solutions change.** Define the job independently of any product (\"get paid on time by customers\", not \"use invoicing software\").\n2. **Functional, emotional and social.** Executive buyers hire solutions to look competent, reduce personal risk and gain control, not just to complete tasks.\n3. **Outcomes are measurable.** Phrase desired outcomes as direction + metric + object + context (\"minimise the time to reconcile bank statements at month-end\").\n4. **Find underserved outcomes.** Opportunity = high importance with low satisfaction. Overserved outcomes indicate room to simplify or reduce price.\n5. **Switching has forces.** The push of the current situation and the pull of the new solution must overcome the anxiety of the new and the habit of the present.\n6. **Evidence from real behaviour.** Interview recent switchers about the actual purchase story (timeline, triggers, alternatives considered), not hypothetical preferences.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The customer type (job executor, buyer, decision-makers) and the market.\n- Existing research (interviews, reviews, support tickets, win/loss).\n- The decision (product roadmap, positioning, a new venture).\n\n## Method\n1. **Define the job executor** and the **core functional job** (verb + object + context).\n2. **Related jobs** (adjacent tasks done alongside); **emotional jobs** (how they want to feel or avoid feeling); **social jobs** (how they want to be perceived).\n3. **Job map:** for each step (define, locate, prepare, confirm, execute, monitor, modify, conclude), describe what the executor is trying to accomplish.\n4. **Desired outcomes:** 3-8 per key step, as measurable statements.\n5. **Score outcomes:** importance and satisfaction (from a survey, or estimated from interviews). Opportunity score = importance + max(importance − satisfaction, 0); state the scale used.\n6. **Switching forces:** from switch interviews, capture push (problems with the current situation), pull (attraction of the new), anxiety (fears about the new), and habit (inertia), plus the triggering events.\n7. **Opportunities:** underserved outcomes → product features, services, business models; overserved → simplify or a lower-cost offer; switching insights → messaging, onboarding, risk reversal (trials, guarantees).\n8. **Validate:** test concepts or messages against the outcomes with real customers.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **The buyer isn't always the executor.** In B2B, map the jobs of users, buyers, approvers and IT separately.\n- **Don't over-engineer surveys.** Even qualitative ranking from 15-20 good interviews can reveal the main opportunities.\n- **Watch for non-consumption:** people who don't use any solution because existing ones are too costly or complex. This is often the biggest opportunity.\n- **AI changes job maps.** Steps like \"locate\" and \"prepare\" may be automated. Customers still need to \"confirm\" and \"monitor\", so design for trust.\n\n## Red flags\n- Jobs that name a product or feature.\n- Outcomes that are vague (\"better insights\").\n- Emotional and social jobs ignored for executive buyers.\n- Evidence from hypothetical questions only.\n- Opportunities not linked to specific outcomes.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **B2B software:** job maps for users vs buyers; risk-reduction and compliance outcomes.\n- **Financial services:** life-event triggers (new business, home purchase); anxiety reduction is critical.\n- **Healthcare:** patient, caregiver and clinician jobs; adherence and reassurance.\n- **Consumer goods:** situational jobs (time of day, occasion), social signalling.\n- **Internal services (IT, HR, finance):** jobs of internal customers; service design.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Jobs To Be Done: [executor], [market]\n**Core functional job:** ...\n**Related jobs:** ... | **Emotional jobs:** ... | **Social jobs:** ...\n\n### Job map and desired outcomes\n| Step | What they're trying to do | Desired outcomes (measurable) | Importance | Satisfaction | Opportunity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Switching forces\nPush: ... | Pull: ... | Anxiety: ... | Habit: ... | Triggers: ...\n\n### Opportunities\n| Outcome (underserved / overserved) | Opportunity | Product / service / message idea | Validation test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The core job is solution-free; emotional and social jobs included.\n- [ ] A complete job map; outcomes are measurable.\n- [ ] Importance and satisfaction scored; underserved and overserved outcomes identified.\n- [ ] Switching forces from real purchase stories.\n- [ ] Opportunities linked to outcomes, with validation tests.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `customer-segmentation`: Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.\n- `interview-guide`: Write a crisp guide for expert, customer or employee interviews that gets real answers, not opinions.\n- `pricing-strategy`: Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.\n- `ai-opportunity-scan`: Find where AI can create real value in a function or business, grounded in processes and pain points.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Jobs-to-be-done theory:** customers \"hire\" products to make progress in specific circumstances. A widely taught innovation and marketing concept.\n- **Outcome-driven innovation:** job maps (define, locate, prepare, confirm, execute, monitor, modify, conclude), desired-outcome statements, and importance-satisfaction opportunity scoring.\n- **Switch interviews and the forces of progress:** push, pull, anxiety and habit as forces affecting switching decisions.\n- **Customer research methods:** in-depth interviews about real past behaviour (see `interview-guide`), surveys (see `survey-design`)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; CFOs of mid-size companies, FP&A, 18 interviews + a 120-respondent survey)\n\n**Core functional job:** \"Give the CEO and board a reliable view of next quarter's cash and profit, and explain the variances.\"\n**Related jobs:** prepare the budget; manage lender covenants; support pricing decisions.\n**Emotional jobs:** feel confident in the numbers; avoid surprises in board meetings.\n**Social jobs:** be seen as a strategic partner, not a scorekeeper.\n\n| Step | Trying to | Desired outcome | Imp. (1-10) | Sat. (1-10) | Opportunity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Locate | Gather actuals from systems | Minimise the time to consolidate actuals from multiple entities | 8.6 | 4.1 | **13.1** |\n| Prepare | Update drivers and assumptions | Minimise the effort to update driver assumptions with business owners | 7.9 | 4.8 | 11.0 |\n| Execute | Produce the forecast | Minimise the time to produce a reforecast | 8.1 | 5.5 | 10.7 |\n| Confirm | Check the numbers | Minimise the likelihood of formula and link errors | 8.8 | 5.2 | **12.4** |\n| Monitor | Explain variances | **Minimise the time to explain variance drivers to the CEO** | 9.1 | 3.6 | **14.6** |\n| Conclude | Communicate | Increase the clarity of the board narrative | 8.3 | 5.9 | 10.7 |\n| Execute | Scenario analysis | Increase the speed of what-if scenarios during meetings | 7.2 | 3.9 | 10.5 |\n\n(Opportunity = importance + max(importance − satisfaction, 0).)\n\n**Switching forces (from 9 recent switchers):**\n- **Push:** a board embarrassment over a variance nobody could explain; analyst turnover.\n- **Pull:** driver-based models; automated variance commentary.\n- **Anxiety:** \"Will the AI explanation be wrong in front of the board?\"; implementation effort.\n- **Habit:** a 10-year-old spreadsheet model that the CFO trusts.\n- **Triggers:** a new CEO or investor; an acquisition; a covenant scare.\n\n**Opportunities:**\n| Outcome | Opportunity | Idea | Validation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Explain variances fast (underserved) | Automated driver-based variance bridges, with AI-drafted commentary citing source transactions | \"Variance in 10 minutes\" feature; CFO review-and-approve flow | Prototype test with 8 CFOs on their last quarter's data |\n| Consolidate actuals (underserved) | Pre-built connectors for common ERPs and accounting packages | Connector library + a data-quality checker | Time-to-first-consolidation in a pilot |\n| Avoid errors (underserved) | Model-integrity checks | Automatic error and link checks before board packs | Error-detection test on real models |\n| Anxiety about AI | Trust features | Every sentence traceable to data; a \"confidence\" flag | Message test: \"every number traceable\" |"}]}, {"id": "pricing-strategy", "pack": "market", "title": "Pricing Strategy", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.", "tryp": "We sell HR software at a flat ₹500/user/month. Help us redesign pricing; margins are falling and big customers get 40% discounts.", "when": ["Launching a product or entering a market", "Margin pressure or uncontrolled discounting", "Moving to subscription, usage or tiered models"], "inputs": ["Product and customers", "Current prices, discounts, costs", "Competitor prices", "Goals (margin, volume, share)"], "pairs": ["customer-segmentation", "unit-economics", "survey-design", "sensitivity-analysis"], "body": "# Pricing Strategy\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** prices and a pricing system that capture the value you create, are defensible to customers and sales teams, and are controlled in execution, so that list-price gains don't leak away in discounts.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Value-based, cost-informed, competition-aware.** Customer value sets the ceiling; cost sets the floor; competitors anchor perceptions. Price in between, with intent.\n2. **Quantify value to the customer:** the savings, revenue or risk reduction your offer creates vs the next-best alternative, by segment.\n3. **The price metric matters as much as the level.** Charge per unit of value (per user, transaction, outcome, capacity) so revenue grows with customer benefit and feels fair.\n4. **Structure for segments.** Good-better-best tiers, bundles and add-ons let different segments self-select.\n5. **Realisation beats list price.** Analyse the pocket-price waterfall (list → invoice → pocket) to find leakage from discounts, rebates, freight and payment terms. Governance often adds more margin than list-price changes.\n6. **Evidence on willingness to pay.** Use transaction data, experiments, conjoint or price-sensitivity surveys, and win/loss. Stated intent overstates actual purchase.\n7. **Plan the change.** Grandfathering, notice periods, sales enablement and value communication decide whether a price change sticks.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Products, segments, current prices and discount practices; cost structure.\n- Objectives: margin, share, market entry, mix shift.\n- Competitor prices and positioning.\n- Data: transactions, win/loss, churn, surveys.\n- Legal and regulatory constraints (price controls, fair-dealing rules, competition law).\n\n## Method\n1. **Value analysis:** for each segment, the next-best alternative and its cost; the differentiation value (+ and −); economic value to the customer.\n2. **Cost floor:** variable and allocated costs; target margin; minimum acceptable price.\n3. **Competitive reference:** competitor prices normalised for features and service.\n4. **Price metric:** options (per seat, per transaction, per volume, per outcome, flat); test fairness, predictability, scalability and ease of measurement.\n5. **Structure:** tiers (each aimed at a segment); fences (features, usage, service levels); bundles and add-ons; minimums.\n6. **Realisation analysis:** the pocket-price waterfall by customer and deal; discount dispersion vs deal size; identify unjustified discounting.\n7. **Discount governance:** discount bands by deal size and segment; approval levels; deal desk; give-gets (discount in exchange for volume, term or prepayment).\n8. **Willingness-to-pay evidence:** existing data, a price-sensitivity survey, conjoint, or in-market tests.\n9. **Impact model:** revenue, margin, volume and churn by scenario (compute with code); break-even volume loss for a price increase.\n10. **Rollout:** new customers first; existing-customer grandfathering or a phased increase; value communication; sales training and incentives aligned to margin, not only volume.\n11. **Legal check:** competition law (no coordination with competitors); sector price controls; consumer-protection rules on price transparency.\n12. **KPIs:** price realisation, discount depth and dispersion, win rate, churn, margin.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Small price increases on high-margin products** often outperform volume pushes. Compute the break-even volume loss: for a price increase of p with contribution margin m, the volume can fall by p ÷ (m + p) before profit drops.\n- **Discount discipline is a culture change.** Sales incentives must reward margin.\n- **Outcome-based pricing** aligns incentives but needs measurable outcomes, trust and risk-sharing mechanisms.\n- **Penetration pricing** only works if scale economies or network effects follow; otherwise it trains customers to expect low prices.\n- **For AI products,** usage-based components protect margins as inference costs scale.\n\n## Red flags\n- Cost-plus pricing for differentiated products.\n- Discounts uncorrelated with deal size or strategic value.\n- Price changes announced without sales enablement.\n- Willingness to pay taken from a single \"how much would you pay?\" question.\n- No break-even analysis for price moves.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **B2B industrial:** value-in-use, contracts with index-linked escalators, rebates, service bundles.\n- **SaaS:** tiers, usage metrics, annual vs monthly, expansion pricing, discounting for multi-year terms.\n- **Consumer:** price ladders, pack-price architecture, promotions, channel pricing.\n- **Professional services:** value-based fees, fixed fees vs time-and-materials, outcome-based components.\n- **Regulated (pharma, utilities, financial services):** price caps, approvals, fairness obligations.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Pricing Strategy: [product / portfolio]\n**Objectives:** ...\n**Anchors:** value to customer ... | cost floor ... | competitor reference ...\n**Price metric:** ... because ...\n\n| Tier | Target segment | Includes / fences | Price | Value story |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Realisation:** pocket-price waterfall findings; leakage ₹...\n**Discount governance:** band - max % - approver - give-get\n**Willingness-to-pay evidence:** ...\n**Impact model:** scenario - revenue - margin - volume - churn; break-even volume loss ...\n**Rollout and communication:** new / existing customers; sales enablement\n**Legal checks:** ...\n**KPIs:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Value to the customer quantified by segment; floor and competitor anchors set.\n- [ ] The price metric is justified; tiers target segments.\n- [ ] Pocket-price leakage analysed; discount governance defined.\n- [ ] Willingness-to-pay evidence beyond stated intent.\n- [ ] Impact modelled with code, including break-even volume loss.\n- [ ] Rollout, legal checks and KPIs included.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Claude may not have current market data unless you provide it or it can search the web. Treat any market figures it suggests as estimates to verify.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `customer-segmentation`: Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.\n- `unit-economics`: Work out whether each customer, order or unit makes money, and which levers fix it.\n- `survey-design`: Design a short, unbiased survey that produces numbers you can actually use.\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts and laws; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Value-based pricing and economic value to the customer:** pricing-strategy curricula (next-best alternative plus differentiation value).\n- **Pocket-price waterfall and price-realisation analysis:** standard pricing-practice tools to identify leakage between list and net price.\n- **Willingness-to-pay methods:** the price sensitivity meter (four-question survey), conjoint analysis, discrete-choice experiments, and in-market tests. Stated intent overstates actual purchase.\n- **Price elasticity and break-even volume analysis:** managerial-economics fundamentals.\n- **Competition law:** Competition Act, 2002 (India) and equivalents. No price coordination or information exchange with competitors.\n- **Consumer protection:** price transparency and unfair-trade-practice rules (e.g. Consumer Protection Act, 2019 in India); sector price controls where applicable."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; HR software sold at a flat ₹500 per user per month, falling margins, large customers getting 40% discounts)\n\n**Objectives:** restore gross margin from 61% to 68% within 12 months; reduce discount dispersion; keep churn below 10%.\n\n**Anchors:**\n- **Value to customer:** payroll and compliance automation saves ~₹1,400 per employee per year in HR effort and avoids penalties (est. from 12 customer studies).\n- **Cost floor:** ₹170 per user per month (hosting, support, payroll-compliance updates).\n- **Competitors:** ₹350-₹700 per user per month, depending on modules.\n\n**Price metric:** per active employee per month, with module-based tiers, because value scales with headcount and module use.\n\n| Tier | Segment | Includes | Price (per employee per month) | Value story |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Essentials | Small firms (<200) | Payroll, compliance, self-service | ₹350 | \"Compliant payroll, zero penalties\" |\n| Professional | Mid-market (200-2,000) | + attendance, performance, analytics | ₹550 | \"One system for the employee life-cycle\" |\n| Enterprise | 2,000+ | + integrations, audit trails, dedicated success manager, SLAs | ₹700 list; volume bands | \"Control and scale\" |\n\n**Realisation analysis (last 12 months):**\n- Average discount 24%; dispersion is high.\n- 40% discounts were given on deals as small as 50 seats. Discount correlation with deal size is only 0.18.\n- Estimated leakage vs the policy proposed below: ₹6.2 Cr a year.\n\n**Discount governance:**\n| Deal size | Max discount | Approver | Give-get |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| <200 seats | 5% | Sales manager | Annual prepayment |\n| 200-2,000 | 15% | Regional head | 2-year term |\n| >2,000 | 25% | CFO (deal desk) | 3-year term + reference + case study |\n\n**Willingness to pay:**\n- Price-sensitivity survey (n=180): acceptable range ₹420-₹640 for the Professional tier.\n- Win/loss: price was the main loss reason in only 18% of losses.\n\n**Impact (computed with code; illustrative):**\n- Mix and discount changes raise average realised price from ₹380 to ₹445 (+17%).\n- Contribution margin is ~55% before the change.\n- Break-even volume loss = 0.17 ÷ (0.55 + 0.17) ≈ 24%. Expected churn impact is 3-5 points (est.).\n- Gross margin reaches ~67-69%.\n\n**Rollout:**\n- New pricing for new customers from 1 April.\n- Existing customers move at renewal, with a 12-month cap of +12%.\n- Value-review meetings for the top 100 accounts.\n- Sales incentives move to 60% revenue and 40% margin.\n\n**Legal:** no coordination with competitors; clear price disclosure in contracts.\n**KPIs:** realised price, discount dispersion, win rate by tier, net revenue retention, churn."}]}, {"id": "swot-tows", "pack": "strategy", "title": "SWOT to TOWS", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Do SWOT properly: evidence-based factors, then turn them into concrete strategic moves (TOWS).", "tryp": "Do a SWOT and TOWS for a regional hospital chain in Tier-2 Indian cities facing entry by national chains.", "when": ["Strategy offsites and annual reviews", "Quick strategic assessment of a business unit", "When someone asks for a 'SWOT' and you want it to lead somewhere"], "inputs": ["The organisation and its context", "Known facts: performance, market, competitors"], "pairs": ["strategic-options", "vrio-analysis", "pestel-scan", "competitor-benchmark"], "body": "# SWOT to TOWS\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a SWOT that survives challenge, because every item is evidenced and comparative, and that ends in a short list of strategic moves rather than four boxes of adjectives.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Anchor to a question.** \"SWOT for our company\" is too broad. \"SWOT for winning mid-market banking clients in the Gulf by 2028\" produces useful content.\n2. **Strengths and weaknesses are relative.** A strength is only a strength if it beats the alternatives customers have. \"Good people\" is not a strength unless it shows up in measurable outcomes better than competitors'.\n3. **Internal vs external, strictly.** S and W are about the organisation (capabilities, assets, costs, culture). O and T are external (customers, competitors, technology, regulation, macro). Plans are not opportunities.\n4. **Evidence or delete.** Each item cites a fact, metric, customer quote or benchmark.\n5. **Fewer, ranked items.** 3-6 per quadrant, ranked by importance to the question.\n6. **The value is in TOWS.** Combine quadrants to generate options, then prioritise.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The strategic question, the horizon and the decision-maker.\n- Evidence available: financials, market data, customer research, competitor benchmarks, capability assessments, employee surveys.\n- The relevant competitor set and customer segments.\n\n## Method\n1. **Frame** the question and the comparison set (whose alternatives do customers consider?).\n2. **External scan first** (it frames what strengths matter): opportunities and threats from customers, competitors, technology, regulation and macro (use `pestel-scan` and `five-forces` outputs if available). Rate each by impact and likelihood or timing.\n3. **Internal assessment relative to competitors:** capabilities, assets, cost position, brand, relationships, data, talent, culture, financial capacity. Cite evidence; use `vrio-analysis` to test claimed strengths.\n4. **Rank** within each quadrant; keep 3-6.\n5. **TOWS matrix:**\n   - **SO (offence):** use strengths to capture opportunities.\n   - **ST (defence via strength):** use strengths to blunt threats.\n   - **WO (fix to grow):** overcome weaknesses to capture opportunities (build, partner, buy).\n   - **WT (protect):** minimise weaknesses and avoid threats (retreat, restructure, hedge).\n   Generate 2-4 concrete options per cell: verb + object + measurable intent.\n6. **Prioritise options:** impact, feasibility (capabilities, capital), time to value and risk. Pick 3-6 to develop.\n7. **For each priority option:** what must be true (assumptions to test), first steps, owner.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **The same fact can be a strength and a weakness.** A large branch network is reach (S) and fixed cost (W). Name both sides honestly.\n- **Opportunities that everyone has aren't advantages.** Note which opportunities you are better placed to capture than rivals, and why.\n- **Threats deserve as much attention as opportunities.** Teams under-weight them. Use pre-mortem thinking.\n- **WT options are often the most valuable** and the least discussed (exit, simplify, shrink to profitable core).\n\n## Red flags\n- \"Strong brand\", \"great culture\", \"loyal customers\" with no evidence.\n- Internal plans listed as opportunities (\"launch app\").\n- 15 items per box.\n- No TOWS; the analysis stops at the SWOT.\n- Options that are vague (\"leverage synergies\").\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board strategy day:** focus on 2-3 strategic questions; pre-read SWOT; TOWS workshop.\n- **Investment thesis / due diligence:** a SWOT of the target vs peers; emphasise threats to the investment case.\n- **Personal or functional strategy** (e.g. IT or HR function): compare with internal customer expectations and external providers.\n- **Public sector:** opportunities from policy and funding; threats from fiscal constraints and public trust.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## SWOT to TOWS: [strategic question], [horizon]\n**Comparison set:** ...\n\n| Strengths (relative, evidenced) | Weaknesses (relative, evidenced) |\n|---|---|\n| 1. ... (evidence) | 1. ... (evidence) |\n| **Opportunities** (impact, timing) | **Threats** (impact, likelihood) |\n| 1. ... | 1. ... |\n\n### TOWS options\n| | Opportunities | Threats |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Strengths** | SO: ... | ST: ... |\n| **Weaknesses** | WO: ... | WT: ... |\n\n### Priority options\n| Option | Cell | Impact | Feasibility | Time | Risk | What must be true | First step / owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Anchored to a specific question and comparison set.\n- [ ] Every item is evidenced; strengths are relative to competitors.\n- [ ] Internal and external are correctly separated; no plans listed as opportunities.\n- [ ] 3-6 ranked items per quadrant.\n- [ ] TOWS options are concrete, then prioritised, with what must be true.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Strategic recommendations reflect only the information provided. Test them with people who know your market, organisation and constraints.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `vrio-analysis`: Test which resources and capabilities give a lasting advantage, and which are just table stakes.\n- `pestel-scan`: Scan political, economic, social, technological, environmental and legal shifts, and rank what really matters.\n- `competitor-benchmark`: Compare competitors on the factors that matter to customers and economics, and spot where to win.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **SWOT analysis:** a long-standing strategic-planning tool taught in strategy curricula; criticised when used without evidence or follow-through, which is why this skill insists on evidence and TOWS.\n- **TOWS matrix:** the extension that systematically pairs internal and external factors to generate strategies (SO, ST, WO, WT), widely taught in strategy courses.\n- **Resource-based view of the firm:** strengths should be valuable, rare and hard to imitate to be strategic (see the `vrio-analysis` skill).\n- **External analysis:** PESTEL and Five Forces (see the related skills) as inputs for opportunities and threats."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional regional hospital chain, 6 hospitals in 3 Tier-2 cities)\n\n**Question:** How do we defend and grow cardiac and oncology revenue in our 3 cities as two national chains open hospitals between 2027 and 2029?\n**Comparison set:** the national chains' new hospitals, 2 local trust hospitals, and patients travelling to metros.\n\n| Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Highest cardiac procedure volume in 2 of 3 districts (state insurance scheme data: 41% share) | 1. No PET-CT or radiation oncology; 30% of oncology patients referred out |\n| 2. 140 referring physicians with 10+ year relationships (CRM: 62% of IP admissions via referrals) | 2. ARPOB 18% below national chains (payer mix skewed to government schemes) |\n| 3. Cost per bed-day ~22% below national-chain benchmarks (published investor data) | 3. Senior consultant attrition 19% (2 key cardiologists left this year) |\n| 4. Trusted local brand: NPS 61 vs 48 for the trust hospital (own survey, n=1,200) | 4. Limited digital front door; 8% of appointments booked online |\n| **Opportunities** | **Threats** |\n| 1. Oncology demand growing ~12%/yr; current metro travel for radiation (high impact, now) | 1. National chain A opens 300 beds in City 1 in 2027 with a cardiac focus (high impact, likely) |\n| 2. Insurance penetration rising in Tier-2 cities (medium impact, 2-4 yrs) | 2. Poaching of cardiologists with 30-40% pay premiums (high, likely) |\n| 3. State scheme package-rate revisions for cardiac procedures (medium, 1-2 yrs; verify) | 3. Government scheme payment delays (avg 95 days) squeeze cash (medium, ongoing) |\n| 4. Tele-consult and remote monitoring for chronic cardiac patients (medium) | 4. Rising compliance and quality-accreditation costs (medium) |\n\n## TOWS options\n| | Opportunities | Threats |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S** | **SO1:** Build a cardiac centre of excellence with a referral-physician programme (joint clinics, tele-ECG) to grow volumes 20% before Chain A opens | **ST1:** Lock in key cardiologists with equity-like retention (revenue share + academic roles) |\n| **W** | **WO1:** Partner with an oncology specialist for radiation oncology and PET-CT under revenue share (avoids ₹60 Cr capex) | **WT1:** Reduce dependence on government schemes in City 3: renegotiate empanelment mix; factor receivables |\n| **W** | **WO2:** Digital front door: booking, reports, payments; target 35% online bookings | **WT2:** Exit low-margin general surgery in City 2 where Chain A will compete; redeploy beds to day care |\n\n## Priority options\n| Option | Impact | Feasibility | Time | Risk | What must be true | First step / owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SO1 cardiac CoE + referrals | High | High | 6-12 mo | Medium | Referrers value joint clinics; cath-lab capacity available | Referral programme design / COO |\n| ST1 cardiologist retention | High | Medium | 3 mo | High | Revenue-share model affordable at current ARPOB | Retention offers / CEO + CHRO |\n| WO1 oncology partnership | High | Medium | 12-18 mo | Medium | Partner accepts revenue share in Tier-2 volumes | Partner shortlist / Strategy |\n| WT1 receivables and payer mix | Medium | High | 3-6 mo | Low | Factoring cost < cost of delay | Payer review / CFO |"}]}, {"id": "vrio-analysis", "pack": "strategy", "title": "VRIO Analysis", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Test which resources and capabilities give a lasting advantage, and which are just table stakes.", "tryp": "Run VRIO on our capabilities: proprietary claims data, 1,200 hospital network, in-house underwriting AI, brand in South India.", "when": ["Defining 'right to win'", "Testing an acquisition or investment thesis", "Deciding which capabilities to invest in"], "inputs": ["List of resources and capabilities (or let the skill draft one)", "Competitor context"], "pairs": ["swot-tows", "strategic-options", "build-buy-partner", "ai-opportunity-scan"], "body": "# VRIO Analysis\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** an honest view of what actually differentiates the organisation, what is merely table stakes, and what is an advantage the organisation owns but isn't using. Each conclusion comes with the evidence and the investment implication.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Capabilities over assets.** Durable advantage usually sits in how things are done (integrated routines, know-how, relationships, data plus the processes that use it) rather than in things that can be bought.\n2. **Valuable means customers or economics show it.** It raises willingness to pay or lowers cost in a way that shows up in margins, share or retention.\n3. **Rare is relative to the competitive set** customers actually choose between.\n4. **Costly to imitate needs a mechanism:**\n   - **History:** decades of accumulation.\n   - **Causal ambiguity:** competitors can't see what makes it work.\n   - **Social complexity:** culture, relationships.\n   - **Legal protection:** IP, licences.\n   - **Network effects or scale.**\n5. **Organised is often the binding constraint.** Structures, incentives, systems and decision rights determine whether the advantage is exploited.\n6. **Advantages decay.** Assess how long imitation would take and how technology (especially AI) changes it.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Strategic question: right to win in what market or segment, against whom?\n- Candidate resources and capabilities (from management, customer research, performance data).\n- Evidence: margins vs peers, customer choice drivers, retention, cost benchmarks, patent and licence positions, talent data.\n\n## Method\n1. **List candidates:** tangible assets, intangible assets (brand, data, IP, licences, relationships) and capabilities (e.g. \"underwriting thin-file MSMEs\", \"launching products in 9 months\", \"running complex projects in remote sites\").\n2. **Decompose key capabilities** into routines, people, data, systems and partners, to see where the advantage really lives.\n3. **Apply V-R-I-O in sequence** with evidence:\n   - **V:** link to customer value or cost (metrics).\n   - **R:** how many competitors have it at comparable quality?\n   - **I:** the imitation mechanism and estimated time and cost to replicate; substitutes.\n   - **O:** structures, processes, incentives and leadership attention that exploit it.\n4. **Classify:**\n   - not valuable → competitive disadvantage;\n   - valuable but not rare → parity;\n   - valuable, rare, imitable → temporary advantage;\n   - V, R, I but not organised → unused advantage;\n   - all four → sustained advantage.\n5. **Durability:** how long until imitation or substitution (technology shifts, AI, regulation)?\n6. **Implications:**\n   - **Sustained:** invest, protect (IP, talent retention), extend to adjacent markets.\n   - **Unused:** fix organisation (incentives, structures, data access).\n   - **Temporary:** exploit fast, build the next advantage.\n   - **Parity:** run efficiently, don't over-invest.\n   - **Disadvantage:** fix, partner or exit.\n7. **Link to strategy and M&A:** which capabilities to build, buy or partner for (see `build-buy-partner`).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Data is rarely an advantage on its own.** Proprietary, relevant data plus the processes and talent to use it, plus feedback loops, can be.\n- **AI erodes some advantages** (codified expertise, content production) and strengthens others (proprietary data, distribution, trust, integration).\n- **Culture claims need behaviour evidence:** speed of decisions, retention of top performers, customer outcomes.\n- **Beware the founder's favourite capability.** Test it like any other.\n\n## Red flags\n- Everything rated \"yes\" on all four.\n- Assets listed without the capability that makes them useful.\n- No imitation mechanism stated.\n- \"Organised\" ignored, so unused advantages go unfixed.\n- No investment implications.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **M&A thesis:** VRIO on the target's claimed advantages; integration risks to \"Organised\" after the deal.\n- **Technology / platform firms:** network effects, switching costs, ecosystem, developer adoption.\n- **Industrial:** process know-how, cost position, scale, licences and permits, customer qualifications.\n- **Services / consulting / GCC:** talent models, domain IP, client relationships, delivery systems.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## VRIO Analysis: [organisation], right to win in [market]\n\n| Resource / capability | Key routines / components | V (evidence) | R (evidence) | I (mechanism, time to copy) | O (evidence) | Result | Durability |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Sustained advantages:** ... → invest / protect / extend\n**Unused advantages:** ... → organisational fixes\n**Temporary advantages:** ... → exploit, build next\n**Parity / disadvantage:** ... → efficiency / fix / partner / exit\n**Implications for strategy and M&A:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Capabilities are decomposed, not just assets listed.\n- [ ] Each V/R/I/O judgement is evidenced; the imitation mechanism and time are stated.\n- [ ] Results are classified correctly; unused advantages are identified.\n- [ ] Durability is considered, including technology and AI shifts.\n- [ ] Clear investment and organisational implications.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Strategic recommendations reflect only the information provided. Test them with people who know your market, organisation and constraints.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `swot-tows`: Do SWOT properly: evidence-based factors, then turn them into concrete strategic moves (TOWS).\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `build-buy-partner`: Decide whether to build a capability, acquire it, or partner, with a clear rationale.\n- `ai-opportunity-scan`: Find where AI can create real value in a function or business, grounded in processes and pain points.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Resource-based view of the firm and the VRIO framework:** core strategy-curriculum concepts on how valuable, rare, inimitable and organised resources create sustained competitive advantage.\n- **Isolating mechanisms:** historical conditions, causal ambiguity, social complexity, legal protection, network effects and economies of scale.\n- **Dynamic capabilities:** the ability to sense, seize and reconfigure resources as environments change; relevant to durability assessments.\n- **Core competence concept:** competencies that provide access to multiple markets, contribute to customer value, and are hard to imitate."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional health insurer, South India focus)\n\n**Question:** where is our right to win in retail health insurance in South India over the next 5 years?\n\n| Resource / capability | Components | V | R | I | O | Result | Durability |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 10 years of claims data (2.1M claims) | Data + actuarial team + coding standards | Yes: loss ratio 6 pts better than market in 3 states | Yes: new entrants lack history | Yes: history takes years to build | **No:** siloed; pricing and underwriting don't use it systematically | **Unused advantage** | 5+ yrs if used |\n| Hospital network (1,200, with negotiated package rates) | Contracting team, rate cards, relationships | Yes: lower claim cost | Partly: 2 rivals have similar networks | Medium: 3-4 years to replicate | Yes | Temporary advantage | 3-4 yrs |\n| Claims settlement speed (cashless approval in 45 min avg) | Process, rules engine, TPA management | Yes: a top choice driver in customer research | No: leaders now match it | Low | Yes | **Parity** | — |\n| Agency distribution in Tier-2/3 towns (14,000 agents) | Recruitment, training, local trust | Yes: 70% of new business | Yes: few rivals have rural depth | High: social complexity, local trust | Partly: agent tech and incentives outdated | Sustained if organised | 5+ yrs (threat: digital aggregators) |\n| Brand awareness | Advertising | Moderate | No | Low (money can buy it) | Yes | Parity | — |\n\n**Implications:**\n- **Fix \"Organised\" for the claims data:** a pricing analytics team embedded in underwriting; a data platform; incentives tied to loss-ratio outcomes. This is the highest-value move.\n- **Protect and modernise agency distribution:** an agent app, faster commissions, AI-assisted needs analysis. Defend against aggregators by serving segments they reach poorly.\n- **Network:** exploit while it lasts; deepen exclusive arrangements in key cities.\n- **Claims speed and brand:** run efficiently; don't over-invest beyond parity."}]}, {"id": "growth-options", "pack": "strategy", "title": "Growth Options", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Lay out all the ways to grow (core, adjacent, new) and size and rank them.", "tryp": "Lay out growth options for a ₹800 Cr specialty chemicals firm that sells mainly to Indian textile makers.", "when": ["Growth strategy and planning", "Revenue gap to a target", "Investor or board growth narrative"], "inputs": ["Current business: products, customers, geographies", "Growth target and time frame", "Constraints: capital, capabilities, risk appetite"], "pairs": ["strategic-options", "portfolio-matrix", "market-sizing", "build-buy-partner"], "body": "# Growth Options\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a growth plan that adds up. It shows how big the gap is, which options fill it, how much each contributes and when, how risky the portfolio is, and which bets need watching.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Start from the gap.** Target minus momentum (what the current business delivers without new moves, including decline) equals the gap. Many plans overstate momentum.\n2. **Adjacency distance matters.** Success rates fall as moves get further from the core (new customers *and* new products *and* new capabilities). Score the distance honestly.\n3. **Size every option:** revenue and margin by year, investment, time to impact, probability of success. Risk-weight before adding up.\n4. **Balance horizons:**\n   - **H1:** defend and extend the core, 0-2 years.\n   - **H2:** adjacencies, 2-4 years.\n   - **H3:** new businesses or options, 4+ years.\n   A plan that is all H3 is a hope; one that is all H1 is a decline.\n5. **Route choice is strategic.** Organic, partnership or acquisition change speed, cost, control and risk.\n6. **Capacity is finite.** Leadership attention, capital and talent cap how many options can run at once.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Growth target and horizon; why this target (investor expectations, scale economics)?\n- Current business: segments, products, geographies, share, growth and margins; momentum forecast.\n- Strategic assets and capabilities (from `vrio-analysis`) and constraints (capital, risk appetite, regulation).\n- Market data for adjacent segments and geographies.\n\n## Method\n1. **Momentum case:** project the current business without new initiatives (market growth, share trends, price, churn, product life cycles). Compute the **gap** to target.\n2. **Generate options:**\n   - **Penetration** (existing products, existing markets): share gain, pricing, cross-sell, retention, channel expansion.\n   - **Market development** (existing products, new markets): new geographies, segments, channels.\n   - **Product development** (new products, existing markets): new offerings, services, solutions, digital and AI-enabled products.\n   - **Diversification** (new, new): related or unrelated moves; usually via partnership or acquisition.\n   - Plus **business-model moves:** subscription, platform, services wrapping products.\n3. **Adjacency distance score** (0-3): count of what's new among customer, product or technology, channel, capabilities and geography.\n4. **Size each option:**\n   - market size and attainable share;\n   - revenue ramp;\n   - margin;\n   - investment (capex, opex, M&A);\n   - time to impact;\n   - probability of success (lower for distant moves);\n   - the risk-weighted contribution.\n   Use code for the roll-up.\n5. **Route analysis** for the major options: organic vs partner vs acquire (speed, cost, control, integration risk).\n6. **Portfolio construction:** select options that close the risk-weighted gap with buffer; check balance across horizons, risk and capital; check management capacity.\n7. **Key bets and signposts:** for the 2-3 biggest bets, define the leading indicators and decision points (scale up, pivot, stop).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Core first.** Underexploited core growth (pricing, retention, share in key segments) is usually the highest-return, lowest-risk option.\n- **Adjacencies should share a strong advantage** (customers, capabilities, channels, data) with the core; otherwise they are diversification in disguise.\n- **Acquisitions buy growth but rarely buy value** unless synergies are real and the integration is planned. Weight probability accordingly.\n- **Option value:** small H3 bets can be worthwhile as learning options. Fund them as options, not as committed revenue.\n- **Don't double count.** Cross-sell and new-product revenue often overlap.\n\n## Red flags\n- No momentum case; the gap assumed equal to the target.\n- Options as a wish list without sizing.\n- Probability of success ignored; distant moves sized like the core.\n- A portfolio that requires five simultaneous transformations.\n- No signposts or stop rules.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Industrial / B2B:** product-to-solutions shift, services and aftermarket, new end-markets, exports.\n- **Consumer:** premiumisation, new channels (quick commerce, direct-to-consumer), adjacent categories, geography.\n- **Financial services:** new segments (MSME, affluent), cross-sell, embedded finance partnerships, new geographies.\n- **Tech / SaaS:** expansion revenue, new modules, verticalisation, international, platform and ecosystem.\n- **Professional services / GCC:** new service lines, AI-enabled offerings, captive-to-hub expansion, new business units served.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Growth Options: [company], target [..] by [year]\n**Momentum case:** ... → **Gap:** ... (risk-weighted plan must exceed it by ...%)\n\n| Option | Matrix quadrant | Horizon | Adjacency distance (0-3) | Route | Revenue by Y3 / Y5 | Margin | Investment | Time to impact | P(success) | Risk-weighted contribution |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Recommended portfolio:** closes ...% of the gap (risk-weighted); horizon mix H1/H2/H3 = ...\n**Capacity check:** leaders, capital, talent\n**Key bets and signposts:** bet - leading indicator - decision point\n**Options parked and why:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Momentum case and gap quantified.\n- [ ] Options span all four quadrants plus business-model moves.\n- [ ] Each option sized with probability; roll-up computed with code.\n- [ ] Adjacency distance and route considered.\n- [ ] Portfolio balanced across horizons and checked for capacity.\n- [ ] Signposts and stop rules for the key bets.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Strategic recommendations reflect only the information provided. Test them with people who know your market, organisation and constraints.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `portfolio-matrix`: Plot business units or products by attractiveness and strength, and decide invest, hold, fix or exit.\n- `market-sizing`: Size a market top-down and bottom-up, reconcile the two, and show every assumption.\n- `build-buy-partner`: Decide whether to build a capability, acquire it, or partner, with a clear rationale.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Product-market growth matrix:** market penetration, market development, product development, diversification. A standard strategy-curriculum tool.\n- **Three horizons of growth:** balancing core (H1), emerging (H2) and options (H3) businesses.\n- **Adjacency strategy:** evidence that success rates decline with the number of dimensions that change (customer, product, channel, geography, capability).\n- **Gap analysis and momentum case:** standard strategic-planning practice.\n- **Real options thinking:** small, staged investments to preserve the right to scale later.\n- **M&A success factors:** synergy realism, integration planning, and the risk of paying for growth. Covered in standard corporate-finance and strategy texts."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional ₹800 Cr specialty-chemicals company selling mainly to textile makers in India)\n\n**Target:** ₹1,500 Cr revenue by FY31.\n**Momentum case:**\n- Textile chemicals grow ~6%/yr, but share erodes from Chinese imports (−1 pt/yr).\n- Momentum ≈ ₹950 Cr by FY31.\n- **Gap = ₹550 Cr.** The risk-weighted plan should cover ~₹650 Cr (a ~20% buffer).\n\n| Option | Quadrant | Horizon | Distance | Route | Revenue FY31 | EBITDA margin | Investment | Time | P(success) | Risk-weighted |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Key-account programme + technical service for top 50 textile mills | Penetration | H1 | 0 | Organic | ₹90 Cr | 18% | ₹15 Cr | 1-2 yrs | 0.8 | ₹72 Cr |\n| Value pricing on specialty finishes (vs cost-plus) | Penetration | H1 | 0 | Organic | ₹40 Cr | (margin) | ₹3 Cr | <1 yr | 0.7 | ₹28 Cr |\n| Exports to Bangladesh and Vietnam textile hubs | Market dev. | H1-H2 | 1 (geography) | Distributor partners | ₹150 Cr | 15% | ₹25 Cr | 2-3 yrs | 0.6 | ₹90 Cr |\n| Technical-textile chemicals (coatings for PPE, filtration) | Product dev. | H2 | 1 (product) | Organic + licensing | ₹180 Cr | 22% | ₹70 Cr | 2-4 yrs | 0.55 | ₹99 Cr |\n| Leather and paper chemicals (similar chemistries) | Product + market | H2 | 2 | **Acquisition** of a ₹200 Cr player | ₹230 Cr | 16% | ₹350 Cr | 1-2 yrs post-deal | 0.6 | ₹138 Cr |\n| Sustainable / bio-based dyes and auxiliaries for export brands | Product dev. | H2-H3 | 2 (tech, customers) | Partnership with a research institute | ₹120 Cr | 25% | ₹40 Cr | 3-5 yrs | 0.4 | ₹48 Cr |\n| Water-treatment chemicals for industrial clusters | Diversification | H3 | 3 | Joint venture | ₹150 Cr | 14% | ₹60 Cr | 4+ yrs | 0.3 | ₹45 Cr |\n| Digital colour-matching service for mills (AI-assisted) | Business model | H2 | 1 | Organic | ₹30 Cr + pull-through | 30% | ₹12 Cr | 2-3 yrs | 0.5 | ₹15 Cr |\n\n**Recommended portfolio:**\n- **H1:** key accounts, value pricing, exports.\n- **H2:** technical textiles, the leather/paper acquisition, digital colour-matching.\n- **H3 option:** bio-based dyes (small funded option).\n- Risk-weighted total ≈ ₹490 Cr. **Short of the ₹650 Cr goal.**\n\n**Choices:**\n- (a) Accept a revised target of ~₹1,350-1,400 Cr; or\n- (b) add a second acquisition in H2 (raises capital need and integration risk); or\n- (c) accelerate exports with a Southeast Asia technical centre.\n\n**Recommendation:** (a) plus (c) now; keep (b) as a contingent option.\n\n**Capacity check:** one acquisition plus one new product platform at a time. The water-treatment JV is parked.\n\n**Key bets and signposts:**\n| Bet | Leading indicator | Decision point |\n|---|---|---|\n| Technical textiles | Qualified at 5 converters by Q4 FY27 | Scale plant investment, or stay toll-manufactured |\n| Acquisition | Synergy capture ≥70% of plan by month 18 | Integration review at month 12 |\n| Exports | Distributor sell-out ≥₹40 Cr by FY28 | Open a Southeast Asia technical centre |"}]}, {"id": "strategic-options", "pack": "strategy", "title": "Strategic Options Evaluation", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.", "tryp": "Evaluate options for our underperforming European subsidiary: fix, merge with UK, sell, or close.", "when": ["Board or ExCo choices between alternatives", "When debate is stuck on preferences", "Documenting why an option was chosen"], "inputs": ["The decision and options (or ask the skill to generate them)", "What matters: criteria and constraints", "Data on each option"], "pairs": ["decision-memo", "sensitivity-analysis", "scenario-planning", "board-paper"], "body": "# Strategic Options Evaluation\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a decision that can be defended later. It shows real alternatives, transparent criteria, honest trade-offs, a recommendation and the conditions under which you would change your mind.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Real options, not straw men.** 3-5 options that a reasonable person could choose, including the status quo or \"do minimum\". If one option is obviously superior, you're not facing a strategic decision.\n2. **Criteria before scores.** Agree criteria and weights with decision-makers before evaluating, to avoid post-hoc rationalisation.\n3. **Quantify where it matters.** Value (NPV or EBITDA impact), capital, risk (probability × impact), and time can usually be estimated. Use scores only for genuinely qualitative criteria.\n4. **Make the trade-off explicit.** Good decisions name what is given up: \"B gives ₹200 Cr more value but takes 18 months longer and concentrates risk in one market.\"\n5. **Reversibility and option value.** Prefer choices that keep future options open when uncertainty is high; commit decisively when it is low.\n6. **Execution realism.** An option the organisation can't execute is not an option.\n7. **Conditions and reversal triggers.** State what must be true for the recommendation to hold, and what would change it.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision, decision-maker, timing and what's at stake.\n- Constraints (capital, regulation, contracts, stakeholder red lines).\n- Strategic objectives and risk appetite (the source of criteria).\n- Available analysis on each option.\n\n## Method\n1. **Frame the decision:** the question, scope, horizon, and what is out of scope.\n2. **Generate options:** widen first (including hybrids and phased paths), then narrow to 3-5 distinct ones; describe each in 2-3 lines.\n3. **Agree criteria and weights.** Typical set:\n   - strategic fit;\n   - financial value;\n   - capital required;\n   - risk (and its type);\n   - time to impact;\n   - reversibility;\n   - execution feasibility;\n   - stakeholder impact (customers, employees, regulators, community).\n   Define 1-5 scales with anchors.\n4. **Evidence per option per criterion:** numbers where possible; one-line rationale per score.\n5. **Score and weight;** show the table.\n6. **Sensitivity:**\n   - **Weights:** would reasonable stakeholders' weightings change the winner?\n   - **Key assumptions:** what would flip the ranking (switching values)?\n7. **Qualitative overlay:** red lines, regulatory or reputational risks, strategic signalling, option value.\n8. **Recommendation:** the chosen option, the trade-off accepted, conditions, first steps, and reversal triggers. Where uncertainty is high, consider a staged or hybrid path.\n9. **Decision record:** the decision, date, rationale, dissent and review date.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Hybrid options** are often best but can be \"everything bagels\" that do nothing well. Test whether the hybrid is coherent.\n- **Status quo is rarely free.** Price the cost of inaction (decline, risk build-up).\n- **Don't let scoring replace judgement.** Close scores mean the decision rests on risk appetite and values. Say so and let leaders decide.\n- **Watch for anchoring and sunk-cost bias,** especially when an option has internal sponsors or past investment.\n- **Disagreement is data.** Capture dissenting views in the record.\n\n## Red flags\n- Only one option seriously developed.\n- Criteria or weights changed after seeing the scores.\n- No cost of inaction.\n- Scores without evidence; false precision (scores to 2 decimals).\n- No reversal triggers or review date.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Portfolio / business-unit decisions** (fix, grow, sell, close): include buyer appetite and separation costs.\n- **Location / footprint:** talent, cost, risk, incentives, infrastructure, time.\n- **Structure / operating model:** control, speed, cost, talent, transition risk.\n- **Vendor / technology:** total cost of ownership, fit, lock-in, vendor viability, migration risk.\n- **Crisis decisions:** speed and reversibility dominate; decide with partial information and set a review in days.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Options Evaluation: [decision], decision by [date]\n**Decision-maker:** ...  |  **Constraints / red lines:** ...\n\n### Options\nA. ... B. ... C. ... D. Status quo (cost of inaction: ...)\n\n### Criteria and weights (agreed [date] with [who])\n| Criterion | Weight | Scale anchors |\n\n### Evaluation\n| Criterion (weight) | A | B | C | Status quo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Financial value (25%) | NPV ₹.. → 4 | | | |\n| ... | | | | |\n| **Weighted score** | | | | |\n\n### Sensitivity (weights and key assumptions)\n### Trade-off in one sentence\n### Recommendation, conditions and first steps\n### Reversal triggers and review date\n### Decision record (decision, rationale, dissent)\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] 3-5 distinct, credible options, including the status quo with its cost.\n- [ ] Criteria and weights fixed before scoring, with anchors.\n- [ ] Evidence and numbers behind the scores.\n- [ ] Sensitivity to weights and key assumptions shown.\n- [ ] Explicit trade-off; recommendation with conditions and reversal triggers.\n- [ ] Decision record format included.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Strategic recommendations reflect only the information provided. Test them with people who know your market, organisation and constraints.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `decision-memo`: Write a crisp memo that frames a decision, the options, the recommendation and what is being asked.\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n- `scenario-planning`: Build 3-4 plausible futures from key uncertainties, and find moves that work across them.\n- `board-paper`: Write a board paper directors can read in 10 minutes: purpose, recommendation, analysis, risks and the resolution.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught methods)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA):** weighted scoring with anchored scales; public appraisal guidance (e.g. UK HM Treasury *Green Book*: options appraisal, long-list to short-list, do-minimum baseline).\n- **Decision quality principles:** an appropriate frame, creative alternatives, reliable information, clear values and trade-offs, sound reasoning, and commitment to action. Widely taught in decision-analysis curricula.\n- **Real options and reversibility:** preferring staged, reversible commitments under high uncertainty.\n- **Cognitive biases in strategic decisions:** anchoring, sunk cost, confirmation, overconfidence; debiasing practices such as pre-mortems and red-teaming.\n- **Decision records:** documenting rationale and dissent, consistent with board minute-taking practice."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional Indian engineering group with an underperforming European subsidiary)\n\n**Decision:** future of the German subsidiary (revenue €95M; EBITDA −€4M; 420 staff; holds the group's only EU manufacturing licence for rail components). Decide by the March board.\n**Red lines:** no breach of EU customer supply contracts (penalties); works-council consultation is mandatory for restructuring.\n\n**Options:**\n- **A.** Turnaround in place (cost programme + product-mix shift).\n- **B.** Merge operations into the UK subsidiary, keep a German sales and service office, and transfer the licence (if transferable; legal view needed).\n- **C.** Sell to a strategic buyer, with a supply agreement.\n- **D.** Close and exit the EU rail market.\n- **Status quo:** cost of inaction ≈ −€4-6M EBITDA a year and rising, plus €12M of equipment refresh due in 2028.\n\n## Criteria (agreed with the CEO, CFO and Board Chair on 10 Jan)\nFinancial value 30%; EU market access 20%; risk 15%; capital required 10%; time to impact 10%; people and reputation 10%; reversibility 5%.\n\n| Criterion (weight) | A Turnaround | B Merge with UK | C Sell | D Close | Status quo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Financial value (30%) | NPV +€8M (if the plan delivers) → 3 | NPV +€15M → 4 | Proceeds €25-40M + supply margin → 4 | −€30M (closure costs, lost margin) → 1 | −€25M → 1 |\n| EU market access (20%) | Full → 5 | Depends on licence transfer → 3 | Via supply agreement → 3 | Lost → 1 | Full → 5 |\n| Risk (15%) | Execution risk high → 2 | Licence and integration risk → 3 | Buyer and pricing risk → 3 | Reputational, contract penalties → 1 | Continuing losses → 1 |\n| Capital (10%) | €14M → 2 | €12M → 3 | Nil → 5 | €18M (social plan, penalties) → 1 | €12M refresh → 2 |\n| Time to impact (10%) | 24-30 months → 2 | 18 months → 3 | 9-12 months → 4 | 12 months → 3 | — → 1 |\n| People and reputation (10%) | Positive → 4 | ~150 roles affected → 2 | Jobs largely preserved → 4 | 420 roles → 1 | — → 3 |\n| Reversibility (5%) | High → 4 | Medium → 3 | Low → 1 | Very low → 1 | High → 5 |\n| **Weighted** | **3.20** | **3.20** | **3.60** | **1.20** | **2.30** |\n\n**Sensitivity:**\n- If EU market access is weighted 30% (cutting financial value to 20%), the gap narrows to A 3.40 vs C 3.50.\n- **If the licence is not transferable, B drops to 2.80.**\n- The ranking is **close between A and C.** It hinges on (1) belief in turnaround execution, and (2) how strategic direct EU access is.\n\n**Trade-off:** selling (C) crystallises €25-40M and removes execution risk but gives up direct control of EU access; a turnaround (A) keeps access but needs €14M and 2+ years of management attention with high execution risk.\n\n**Recommendation:** run a **dual track for 6 months**:\n- Launch a buyer sounding (C).\n- In parallel, implement the no-regret turnaround steps from A (pricing, procurement, footprint study).\n- Decide in September based on the bids received vs turnaround progress.\n\n**Conditions:** an independent legal opinion on licence transfer; works-council consultation plan; customer contract review.\n\n**Reversal triggers:**\n- Bids below €20M → favour A.\n- Turnaround milestones missed by >20% at month 6 → favour C.\n\n**Review date:** board meeting, September.\n\n**Decision record:** dissent from the COO, who favours B. The record notes this to be revisited if the licence is confirmed transferable."}]}, {"id": "portfolio-matrix", "pack": "strategy", "title": "Portfolio Matrix", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Plot business units or products by attractiveness and strength, and decide invest, hold, fix or exit.", "tryp": "Here are our 9 product lines with revenue, growth, margin and market share. Build a portfolio matrix and recommend roles.", "when": ["Capital allocation and budgeting", "Product or SKU rationalisation", "Project portfolio reviews"], "inputs": ["List of units/products/projects with data (revenue, growth, margin, share, capital)", "Strategic priorities"], "pairs": ["growth-options", "strategic-options", "business-case", "board-paper"], "body": "# Portfolio Matrix\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear, defensible view of where to put money and management attention across the portfolio, and where to pull them out. Each unit gets an explicit role with matching targets and capital.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Roles drive resources.** Each unit gets a role, and the role sets its capital, targets and KPIs. Growth units are not judged on short-term margin; harvest units are not given growth capex.\n2. **Transparent criteria.** Attractiveness and strength are scored on stated, weighted criteria with evidence. No black-box positions.\n3. **Economics overlay.** Add economic profit (return above the cost of capital) and cash generation. A unit can look attractive yet destroy value.\n4. **The best-owner test.** Would this unit be worth more with another owner (scale, synergies, focus)? If so, divestment may create value even for a good business.\n5. **Balance matters.** Cash generators should fund growth bets, and the portfolio's risk should be acceptable as a whole.\n6. **Reallocation is the point.** Firms that actively shift capital toward better opportunities tend to outperform those that allocate in proportion to last year's budget.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Units in scope and data per unit: revenue, growth, EBITDA, capital employed, ROCE, cash flow, market size and growth, share and relative share, competitive position evidence.\n- Strategic priorities and the cost of capital.\n- Constraints: interdependencies (shared customers, plants), regulatory licences, people.\n\n## Method\n1. **Define criteria and weights:**\n   - **Market attractiveness:** size, growth, profit pool and margins, intensity of competition (Five Forces), cyclicality, regulatory and ESG risk, strategic fit.\n   - **Competitive strength:** relative share, relative cost position, brand and customer loyalty, capabilities (VRIO), innovation pipeline, channel access.\n2. **Score each unit 1-5 per criterion** with evidence; compute weighted scores; show the table.\n3. **Plot** on a 3×3 grid (high/medium/low on each axis); bubble size = revenue or capital employed; arrows for expected movement.\n4. **Economics overlay:** ROCE vs cost of capital; economic profit; cash conversion; capital intensity.\n5. **Assign roles:**\n   - **Invest to grow:** high attractiveness and high strength.\n   - **Selectively invest / build:** high attractiveness, medium strength; invest where you can win.\n   - **Maintain / earn:** medium-medium; optimise.\n   - **Fix:** attractive market, weak position, with a credible plan and a deadline.\n   - **Harvest:** low attractiveness, decent strength; maximise cash, limit capex.\n   - **Divest / exit:** low-low, or a better owner exists.\n6. **Best-owner test** for fix, harvest and divest candidates (and any unit with low synergies).\n7. **Capital reallocation:** shift capex, opex and talent according to the roles; show the before/after mix.\n8. **Portfolio check:** cash balance, risk concentration, cyclicality, and capability coherence.\n9. **Targets per role:** growth, margin and ROCE, with time-bound conditions (e.g. fix units must reach X by date Y or move to divest).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Interdependencies can override the grid.** A weak unit may supply a strong one or anchor key customers. Quantify the dependency before exiting.\n- **Fix needs a clock.** Fix plans without a deadline become permanent subsidies.\n- **Don't divest at the bottom of the cycle** unless value is truly eroding. Consider timing and buyer appetite.\n- **Small units can be options** (H3 bets). Treat them as an innovation portfolio with stage gates, not as nine-box failures.\n\n## Red flags\n- Scores without evidence; positions matching management's preferences.\n- Every unit gets \"invest\".\n- No economics overlay; ROCE below cost of capital ignored.\n- No capital shifts after the analysis.\n- Fix roles without deadlines or credible plans.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Product / SKU portfolios:** use contribution after cost-to-serve; complexity costs; cannibalisation.\n- **Project portfolios (IT, R&D, AI):** value vs feasibility; risk; strategic fit; stage-gate status; dependencies.\n- **Geographic portfolios:** market attractiveness by country; risk (political, FX); scale economics.\n- **Conglomerates and holding companies:** capital allocation across sectors; holding-company discount; listing or demerger options.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Portfolio Matrix: [portfolio], [year]\n**Criteria and weights:** Attractiveness: ... | Strength: ...\n\n| Unit | Revenue | Growth | EBITDA % | ROCE vs WACC | Attractiveness score | Strength score | Box | Role | Best owner? | Capital shift |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Grid summary:** (positions, arrows)\n**Portfolio balance:** cash generators vs users; risk concentration\n**Capital reallocation:** before → after\n**Targets and conditions by role:** ...\n**Interdependencies to manage:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Criteria and weights are stated, with evidence per score.\n- [ ] Economics overlay (ROCE vs cost of capital, cash) included.\n- [ ] Every unit has a role with matching targets and capital.\n- [ ] Best-owner test applied to weak or low-synergy units.\n- [ ] Capital reallocation is quantified; portfolio balance checked.\n- [ ] Fix roles have deadlines; interdependencies are considered.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Strategic recommendations reflect only the information provided. Test them with people who know your market, organisation and constraints.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `growth-options`: Lay out all the ways to grow (core, adjacent, new) and size and rank them.\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `board-paper`: Write a board paper directors can read in 10 minutes: purpose, recommendation, analysis, risks and the resolution.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Portfolio matrices:** the growth-share matrix and the nine-box attractiveness-vs-strength matrix are standard strategy-curriculum tools for multi-business portfolios.\n- **Economic profit and value-based management:** return on capital vs cost of capital as the test of value creation (corporate-finance curricula).\n- **Parenting advantage and the best-owner test:** the corporate-strategy principle that a parent should own a business only if it adds more value than other potential owners.\n- **Dynamic resource reallocation:** research evidence that companies which reallocate capital more actively across units tend to deliver higher shareholder returns.\n- **Five Forces and VRIO:** inputs to the attractiveness and strength scores (see the related skills)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional diversified Indian manufacturer, 6 business units; WACC 12%)\n\n**Attractiveness weights:** market growth 25%, profit pool / margins 25%, competitive intensity 20%, cyclicality 15%, ESG and regulatory risk 15%.\n**Strength weights:** relative share 30%, relative cost 25%, capabilities 25%, customer access 20%.\n\n| Unit | Revenue (₹ Cr) | Growth | EBITDA % | ROCE | Attract. | Strength | Box | Role | Best owner? | Capital shift (3-yr capex) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Specialty polymers | 1,200 | 14% | 21% | 24% | 4.2 | 4.0 | High-High | **Invest to grow** | Us | ₹600 → ₹900 Cr |\n| Engineering plastics compounds | 900 | 11% | 15% | 16% | 3.8 | 2.9 | High-Med | **Selectively invest** (auto EV segment only) | Us | ₹250 → ₹300 Cr |\n| Industrial adhesives | 650 | 8% | 17% | 19% | 3.1 | 3.4 | Med-Med | **Maintain / earn** | Us | ₹120 → ₹100 Cr |\n| Commodity PVC pipes | 1,500 | 6% | 9% | 11% | 2.3 | 3.2 | Low-Med | **Harvest** | Possibly a scale player | ₹300 → ₹120 Cr |\n| Solar encapsulants | 180 | 30% | 6% | 5% | 4.0 | 1.8 | High-Low | **Fix** (24 months: cost parity or partner) | Unclear | ₹150 → ₹80 Cr (conditional) |\n| Textile auxiliaries | 220 | 3% | 7% | 8% | 1.9 | 1.7 | Low-Low | **Divest** | A focused textile-chemicals player | ₹40 → ₹10 Cr |\n\n**Portfolio balance:**\n- Pipes and adhesives generate ~60% of free cash flow, which funds polymers and EV compounds.\n- After reallocation, the share of capex going to the two growth-role units (invest and selectively invest) rises from 58% (₹850 of ₹1,460 Cr) to 79% (₹1,200 of ₹1,510 Cr).\n- Commodity-cycle exposure is reduced.\n\n**Conditions:**\n- **Solar encapsulants:** reach cost within 5% of import landed cost by Q4 FY28, or form a JV / exit.\n- **Pipes:** maximise cash; review sale options if a buyer offers ≥8x EBITDA.\n- **Textile auxiliaries:** sale process in FY27; a transitional supply agreement for shared customers (₹30 Cr of revenue overlaps with adhesives).\n\n**Interdependency:** pipes share a plant and utilities with adhesives. A separation-cost study is needed before any pipes sale."}]}, {"id": "scenario-planning", "pack": "strategy", "title": "Scenario Planning", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Build 3-4 plausible futures from key uncertainties, and find moves that work across them.", "tryp": "Build 2030 scenarios for an Indian IT services firm, given uncertainty about GenAI's impact on headcount-based revenue.", "when": ["Long-term strategy under uncertainty", "Big investments with long payback", "Board strategy days"], "inputs": ["The focal question and time horizon", "Key trends and uncertainties (or a PESTEL scan)"], "pairs": ["pestel-scan", "strategic-options", "sensitivity-analysis", "risk-appetite"], "body": "# Scenario Planning\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a disciplined way to make decisions when the future cannot be forecast. You get several plausible futures, a test of today's strategy against each, a set of moves that work regardless, and early-warning signposts that tell you which future is arriving.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Scenarios serve a decision.** Start with a focal question (\"Should we commit ₹2,000 Cr to a new plant by 2030?\"), not \"the future of the industry\".\n2. **Uncertainties, not trends.** Demographics or committed regulation are predetermined: include them in every scenario. Axes must be genuinely uncertain *and* high-impact.\n3. **Plausible, not probable.** Scenarios are not best/base/worst. Each must be internally consistent and challenging, and none should be the \"official future\".\n4. **Quantify the implications:** demand, prices, margins, capital needs in each world, so strategy can be tested.\n5. **Robustness over prediction.** Look for no-regret moves, options (small bets that can be scaled), and hedges.\n6. **Signposts make it live.** Define observable indicators and thresholds that signal which scenario is unfolding, and review them on a fixed rhythm.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The focal decision, horizon (5-15 years typical) and decision-makers.\n- Current strategy and its key assumptions.\n- Available inputs: PESTEL scan, industry analysis, expert views, data.\n- Who should take part. Scenario work benefits from diverse perspectives, including sceptics and outsiders.\n\n## Method\n1. **Focal question and horizon.**\n2. **Driving forces:** brainstorm 20-40 across PESTEL, industry, technology and customer behaviour.\n3. **Classify each:** predetermined (high certainty) vs uncertain; rate impact on the focal question.\n4. **Select two critical uncertainties:** high impact, high uncertainty and **independent of each other** (test: can both extremes combine plausibly?). Define clear polar outcomes for each.\n5. **Build the 2×2.** Give each scenario a memorable name and write a short narrative of how the world got there. Include the predetermined trends in every scenario.\n6. **Quantify:** key variables per scenario (market size, price, cost, regulation, technology adoption), plus implications for the business: revenue, margin and capital.\n7. **Stress-test the current strategy:** performance in each scenario (works / struggles / fails), and why.\n8. **Identify moves:**\n   - **No-regret:** good in all scenarios.\n   - **Options:** low-cost moves that create the right to scale if a scenario emerges (pilots, partnerships, land, licences).\n   - **Hedges:** limit damage in adverse scenarios (contract terms, flexible capacity, diversification).\n   - **Big bets:** only if you choose to back one scenario. Make the bet explicit.\n9. **Signposts and triggers:** 2-4 indicators per scenario, with thresholds and the pre-agreed action when crossed.\n10. **Governance:** review signposts quarterly or semi-annually; refresh the scenarios every 1-2 years or on major shocks.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Avoid a \"middle\" scenario.** Teams gravitate to it and ignore the others. If four are too many, use three plus a \"wildcard\".\n- **Correlated axes produce two meaningful scenarios and two impossible ones.** Pick different axes.\n- **Wildcards** (low probability, high impact: pandemic, war, a technology leap) are better treated as stress tests than as axes.\n- **Narratives persuade; numbers decide.** You need both.\n- **Use scenarios to prompt decisions now:** which options to buy today, and which commitments to defer.\n\n## Red flags\n- Best / base / worst cases labelled as scenarios.\n- Axes that are trends, or correlated.\n- Stories with no numbers and no strategic implications.\n- No signposts; scenarios filed after the workshop.\n- Strategy unchanged after the exercise.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Energy transition / climate:** carbon price path × technology cost curves; policy stringency; physical-risk scenarios. Align with recognised climate scenario sets for disclosure (e.g. under IFRS S2).\n- **Technology and AI disruption:** the speed of capability gains × the regulatory or adoption response.\n- **Geopolitics and trade:** openness of trade × supply-chain regionalisation.\n- **Financial services:** rate path × credit cycle; digital competition × regulation.\n- **Healthcare:** payer models × technology adoption; public-funding levels.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Scenarios: [focal question], horizon [year]\n**Predetermined trends (in all scenarios):** ...\n**Critical uncertainties:** A: [pole 1 ↔ pole 2]; B: [pole 1 ↔ pole 2] (independence check: ...)\n\n| | B: pole 1 | B: pole 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A: pole 1** | **Scenario 1: name** | **Scenario 2: name** |\n| **A: pole 2** | **Scenario 3: name** | **Scenario 4: name** |\n\n### Narratives (one paragraph each) and key numbers\n| Variable | S1 | S2 | S3 | S4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Strategy stress test\n| Strategy element | S1 | S2 | S3 | S4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Moves\n- **No-regret:** ...\n- **Options:** ...\n- **Hedges:** ...\n- **Big bets (if any):** ...\n\n### Signposts\n| Indicator | Threshold | Points to | Action when crossed | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Review cadence:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] A focal decision and horizon are defined.\n- [ ] Trends separated from uncertainties; the axes are independent and high-impact.\n- [ ] Four plausible, distinct, named scenarios with numbers.\n- [ ] The current strategy is stress-tested.\n- [ ] No-regret moves, options and hedges are identified.\n- [ ] Signposts have thresholds, actions and owners; a review cadence is set.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Strategic recommendations reflect only the information provided. Test them with people who know your market, organisation and constraints.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `pestel-scan`: Scan political, economic, social, technological, environmental and legal shifts, and rank what really matters.\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n- `risk-appetite`: Draft a board risk appetite statement with qualitative positions and measurable limits.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught methods and public frameworks)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Scenario planning:** the established intuitive-logics method (focal question, driving forces, critical uncertainties, 2×2 matrix, narratives, implications, signposts), taught in strategy and foresight curricula.\n- **Strategic foresight toolkits** published by governments and international organisations (e.g. OECD strategic foresight resources; UK Government Office for Science futures toolkit).\n- **Climate scenarios for disclosure:** IFRS S2 climate-related scenario analysis requirements; publicly available climate scenario sets from central-bank and international networks. Use current versions.\n- **Real options and robust decision-making under deep uncertainty:** no-regret moves, options and hedges.\n- **ISO 31000:2018** and **ISO 31010:2019:** scenario analysis as a risk-assessment technique."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional Indian IT services firm, $2B revenue)\n\n**Focal question:** what mix of capabilities, delivery model and pricing should we commit to by 2030, given GenAI and agentic AI's impact on effort-based services?\n\n**Predetermined trends:** clients will keep demanding AI-driven productivity gains; the talent pool keeps growing; cloud and data modernisation continue; data-sovereignty rules tighten in key markets.\n\n**Critical uncertainties:**\n- **A. Speed of AI productivity in delivery:** incremental (10-20% by 2030) ↔ step-change (40-60% by 2030).\n- **B. Client commercial model:** effort and time-and-materials persist ↔ outcome and platform-based pricing dominates.\n\nIndependence check: fast AI could coexist with sticky T&M contracts (client procurement inertia), so the axes are independent.\n\n| | B: effort-based persists | B: outcome-based dominates |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A: incremental** | **\"Familiar Ground\":** slow erosion of pricing; utilisation the key lever | **\"Outcome Pioneers\":** early movers win with IP-led outcomes; others commoditise |\n| **A: step-change** | **\"Deflation Trap\":** clients demand 30-40% price cuts on the same effort contracts | **\"Platform Economy\":** value captured by firms with AI platforms, domain IP and agent operations |\n\n| Variable (2030) | Familiar Ground | Outcome Pioneers | Deflation Trap | Platform Economy |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue per delivery employee | +8% | +25% | −10% | +45% |\n| Share of revenue from outcome / platform deals | 10% | 35% | 15% | 55% |\n| Headcount vs 2026 | +15% | +5% | −10% | −20% |\n| EBIT margin (industry) | 19% | 21% | 14% | 24% (leaders), 12% (laggards) |\n\n**Stress test of the current strategy** (scale headcount, add AI as tools):\n- Works in Familiar Ground.\n- Struggles in Outcome Pioneers.\n- **Fails** in Deflation Trap and Platform Economy.\n\n**Moves:**\n- **No-regret:**\n  - Embed AI in all delivery and share the gains selectively.\n  - Build 2-3 vertical IP assets (e.g. claims, KYC, reconciliation agents).\n  - Retrain 30% of staff for AI-augmented roles.\n  - Agent-operations capability with governance.\n- **Options:**\n  - Outcome-priced pilots with 5 anchor clients.\n  - Acquire a small vertical AI platform.\n  - A joint venture with a data-sovereign cloud provider in one region.\n- **Hedges:**\n  - Contract clauses sharing productivity gains.\n  - Variable workforce models.\n  - Diversify into regulated-industry managed services where trust and compliance matter.\n\n**Signposts:**\n| Indicator | Threshold | Points to | Action | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Measured delivery productivity gain in our AI-enabled programmes | >30% sustained over 4 quarters | Step-change | Accelerate platform build; re-plan hiring | CTO |\n| Share of new deals (TCV) priced on outcomes | >25% for 2 quarters | Outcome dominance | Scale outcome pricing; adjust risk policy | CRO / CFO |\n| Average price-per-FTE on renewals | <−8% YoY | Deflation Trap | Trigger cost restructuring plan B | COO |\n\n**Review:** signposts quarterly at ExCo; scenarios refreshed annually at the board strategy day."}]}, {"id": "build-buy-partner", "pack": "strategy", "title": "Build, Buy or Partner", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Decide whether to build a capability, acquire it, or partner, with a clear rationale.", "tryp": "Should we build our own GenAI underwriting assistant, buy a vendor product, or partner with an insurtech?", "when": ["Technology or platform choices", "Capability gaps in a strategy", "M&A vs organic debates"], "inputs": ["The capability needed and why", "Options known (vendors, targets, partners)", "Time, budget and talent constraints"], "pairs": ["strategic-options", "business-case", "ai-business-case", "vrio-analysis"], "body": "# Build, Buy or Partner\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a reasoned choice of route for a specific capability, with the guardrails that make that route work (contract terms, integration plan, exit options), and a sensible sequence if a hybrid is best.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Differentiation decides control.** Capabilities that create competitive advantage warrant control (build or buy). Necessary but generic capabilities can be sourced (partner, outsource, license).\n2. **Speed vs control vs cost.** Building is slow but controlled; buying is fast but expensive and risky to integrate; partnering is fast and flexible but shares value and control. Make the trade-off explicit.\n3. **Be honest about the capability gap.** Can the organisation really build this (talent, leadership, time), or is \"build\" a default born of pride?\n4. **Total cost over the life of the capability,** including integration, switching and exit, not just the year-one price.\n5. **Data and IP terms are strategic.** In AI and technology especially, who owns data, models, fine-tuning and outputs determines future advantage.\n6. **Design the exit before you enter.** Partnerships and outsourcing need termination rights, transition support and data return.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The capability, why it is needed, and by when (the strategic driver).\n- How differentiating it is (customer value, uniqueness) and how fast it changes.\n- Internal starting point: talent, assets, track record.\n- Market landscape: potential targets, licensors, partners, vendors; their maturity.\n- Constraints: capital, regulation (data localisation, outsourcing rules), risk appetite.\n\n## Method\n1. **Strategic importance × internal capability** (2×2):\n   - **High importance, strong base** → build (or accelerate with a small acqui-hire).\n   - **High importance, weak base** → buy, or partner-then-build.\n   - **Low importance, strong base** → maintain efficiently or outsource.\n   - **Low importance, weak base** → partner, outsource or license.\n2. **Urgency:** a time-to-market window may override the default (e.g. partner now, build later).\n3. **Options per route:**\n   - **Build:** team, investment, timeline, risks.\n   - **Buy:** targets (fit, valuation range, integration complexity, cultural fit, key-person risk), or licensing.\n   - **Partner:** alliance, JV or outsourcing; partner candidates; what each side contributes and receives; governance.\n4. **Compare routes** on time to value, total cost (3-5+ years), control and IP, data ownership, talent, integration risk, flexibility and exit, regulatory implications, and strategic optionality. Quantify where possible.\n5. **Recommend a route or hybrid sequence** (e.g. partner for 18 months → build the differentiating layer → renegotiate or exit the partnership).\n6. **Guardrails by route:**\n   - **Build:** milestones, talent plan, kill criteria, budget gates.\n   - **Buy:** walk-away price, diligence focus (technology, people, IP, data, liabilities), retention packages, an integration management office, synergy tracking.\n   - **Partner / outsource:** scope, SLAs, governance board, IP and data clauses, model and data usage limits (for AI), audit rights, step-in rights, termination and transition, exclusivity limits, pricing mechanics.\n7. **Decision criteria for revisiting** (e.g. when the partner's roadmap diverges or the internal team reaches maturity).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Acqui-hire vs acquisition:** buying a small team can accelerate a build. Retention is the whole value, so design for it.\n- **JVs are hard.** They need aligned incentives, clear governance and exit mechanics. Use them when complementary assets are essential and neither side can go alone.\n- **For AI platforms,** keep the differentiating layers (proprietary data pipelines, domain workflows, evaluation sets, guardrails) in-house. Rent commodity capabilities (foundation models, infrastructure), with portability in mind.\n- **Outsourcing doesn't outsource accountability.** Regulators and customers still hold you responsible.\n- **GCC (captive) vs outsourcing:** a captive gives control, IP and talent depth; outsourcing gives speed and flexibility. Hybrid models (build-operate-transfer) can bridge them.\n\n## Red flags\n- \"Build\" chosen with no realistic talent plan.\n- Acquisition justified by \"capability\" with no integration or retention plan.\n- Partnership contracts silent on data, IP, exit or model training.\n- Comparing year-1 cost only.\n- No plan to revisit the choice.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **AI and technology:** foundation models (partner or rent), proprietary data and workflows (build), specialised point solutions (buy or license); portability and lock-in.\n- **Market access:** distributor or partner vs subsidiary vs acquisition of a local player; regulatory licences.\n- **Manufacturing capability:** contract manufacturing vs own plant vs acquisition; IP protection; quality control.\n- **Shared services / GCC:** captive vs outsource vs build-operate-transfer; location; talent market.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Build / Buy / Partner: [capability]\n**Strategic importance:** differentiating / necessary-generic - why\n**Internal starting point:** strong / partial / weak - evidence\n**Urgency:** ...\n\n| Criterion | Build | Buy (target / licence) | Partner (alliance / JV / outsource) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Time to value | | | |\n| 5-year total cost | | | |\n| Control and IP | | | |\n| Data ownership | | | |\n| Talent | | | |\n| Integration / execution risk | | | |\n| Flexibility and exit | | | |\n| Regulatory | | | |\n\n**Recommendation:** route or hybrid sequence - rationale - trade-off accepted\n**Guardrails:** ...\n**Revisit triggers:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Strategic importance and internal capability are assessed with evidence.\n- [ ] All three routes are considered, with concrete options.\n- [ ] Total cost over time and exit costs are included.\n- [ ] Data, IP and model-usage terms addressed where relevant.\n- [ ] Route-specific guardrails are defined.\n- [ ] Revisit triggers set.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Strategic recommendations reflect only the information provided. Test them with people who know your market, organisation and constraints.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `ai-business-case`: Build an honest business case for an AI use case, including model, data, change and run costs.\n- `vrio-analysis`: Test which resources and capabilities give a lasting advantage, and which are just table stakes.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts; verify regulatory specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Make-or-buy and transaction-cost reasoning:** asset specificity, uncertainty and frequency as drivers of integrating vs contracting (economics and strategy curricula).\n- **Resources and capabilities pathways:** choosing between internal development, contracts and licensing, alliances, and acquisitions according to resource relevance and the organisation's ability to integrate (strategy curricula).\n- **M&A integration practice:** synergy tracking, integration management offices, retention, cultural integration.\n- **Outsourcing regulation (India; verify current versions):** RBI directions on managing risks in outsourcing (financial and IT services) for regulated entities; IRDAI outsourcing regulations for insurers; DPDP Act 2023 and Rules 2025 on processors and cross-border transfer.\n- **Technology contracts:** standard practice on IP ownership, data rights, service levels, audit, step-in, and termination assistance.\n- **AI-specific risks:** NIST AI RMF third-party risk considerations; ISO/IEC 42001 supplier controls."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional mid-size general insurer, GenAI underwriting assistant for commercial lines)\n\n**Capability:** an assistant that reads broker submissions (proposals, loss runs, surveys), extracts risk data, flags underwriting-guideline issues and drafts a risk summary and pricing inputs.\n\n**Strategic importance:** **differentiating.** Faster, more consistent quoting in mid-market commercial lines is a stated growth priority. The underwriting rules and loss experience are proprietary.\n**Internal starting point:** partial. Strong actuarial and underwriting expertise; a data team of 12; no LLM engineering experience.\n**Urgency:** high. Two competitors launched faster quote turnaround; brokers are moving business.\n\n| Criterion | Build | Buy (licensed vertical product) | Partner (insurtech co-development) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Time to value | 12-15 months | 3-4 months | 5-7 months |\n| 5-year total cost (est.) | ₹22 Cr (team, platform, evaluation) | ₹18 Cr (licences + integration + per-document fees) | ₹15 Cr + revenue share on new products |\n| Control and IP | Full | Low: vendor roadmap; our rules configured, not owned | Shared; negotiate ownership of our rules and evaluation sets |\n| Data ownership | Full | Risk: vendor terms allow aggregated model training | Negotiable |\n| Talent | Hire 6-8 scarce engineers | Minimal | Build skills alongside the partner |\n| Integration / execution risk | High (new skills) | Medium | Medium (partner viability) |\n| Flexibility and exit | High | Low (3-year minimum) | Medium (needs a clear exit clause) |\n| Regulatory | Full control of outsourcing and data-location compliance | Vendor outside India for some processing → review | Hosting in our cloud tenancy |\n\n**Recommendation: hybrid.**\n1. **Partner now** for document extraction and the drafting layer, hosted in our cloud tenancy.\n2. **Build in-house** the differentiating layer: underwriting-guideline checks, pricing inputs, an evaluation set of 1,500 historical submissions, and guardrails.\n3. **Month 18 review:** bring extraction in-house or switch providers if needed.\n\n*Trade-off accepted:* a slightly slower start than buying, in return for owning the differentiating logic and the data rights.\n\n**Guardrails:**\n- **Contract:**\n  - no training on our data without written consent;\n  - our rules, prompts and evaluation sets are our IP;\n  - 30-day notice of model changes, with regression testing;\n  - audit rights;\n  - termination for convenience after 12 months, with transition support and data return in usable formats;\n  - processing in India.\n- **Build:** hire a 4-person AI engineering core plus an underwriting product owner; gates at months 4, 8 and 12 on accuracy (≥95% on key fields) and underwriter adoption (≥60%).\n- **Governance:** an AI risk tier assessment; human underwriter decides every quote.\n\n**Revisit triggers:**\n- The partner's roadmap diverges or its financial health deteriorates.\n- The internal team reaches the accuracy target independently.\n- Licence pricing rises >25%."}]}, {"id": "business-case", "pack": "numbers", "title": "Business Case", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.", "tryp": "Build a business case for automating accounts payable at a 5,000-employee manufacturer: ~180,000 invoices a year, 22 AP staff.", "when": ["Investment committee or budget approval", "Programme or project funding", "Comparing investments for a portfolio"], "inputs": ["The proposal and the problem it solves", "Cost and benefit estimates you have", "Company discount rate (hurdle rate), time horizon", "Approver and decision date"], "pairs": ["financial-model-spec", "sensitivity-analysis", "benefits-tracker", "playbook-investment-case"], "body": "# Business Case\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a business case that a CFO can audit and a board can approve. The baseline is honest, only incremental cash counts, benefits are owned, downside risk is quantified, and money is released against evidence.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **The baseline is the most important number.** Compare against a realistic \"do nothing\" (or \"do minimum\"), which often includes cost growth, lost revenue or rising risk. Not against zero.\n2. **Only incremental, cash-based effects count in NPV.** Sunk costs are excluded; allocated overheads that don't change are excluded; opportunity costs (using an asset you could sell or rent) are included.\n3. **Benefits have owners and ramps.** No benefit without a named owner who accepts it in their budget, and a realistic phasing (rarely 100% in year 1).\n4. **Optimism bias is the norm.** Large projects routinely overrun costs and under-deliver benefits. Use contingency and reference-class evidence where available, and be explicit about the uplift you applied.\n5. **Show the answer's fragility.** Present the break-even values of the key drivers and a downside case; decision-makers trust honest uncertainty more than precision.\n6. **Stage the commitment.** Release funds against gates with kill criteria, especially for uncertain or innovative initiatives.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Decision-maker, approval route and timing; the company's hurdle rate or WACC, evaluation horizon and template.\n- What problem or opportunity, why now, and the strategic objective it serves.\n- What has already been spent or decided (sunk; excluded).\n- Capex vs opex treatment, tax and depreciation conventions, inflation and FX assumptions.\n- Data available for costs, volumes, prices and benefits; the sources and confidence of each.\n\n## Method\n1. **Problem and fit:** a 3-5 sentence case for change, linked to strategy and measured pain (cost, risk, lost revenue).\n2. **Options:** do-nothing / do-minimum (as the baseline), the proposed option, and at least one credible alternative (different scope, phasing, or delivery model). Screen out non-viable options with reasons.\n3. **Baseline projection:** what happens to costs, revenue and risk without the investment, e.g. rising maintenance, a hardware refresh, regulatory penalties, or competitive loss.\n4. **Costs (incremental):**\n   - **One-off:** capex, implementation, data migration, testing, change and training, internal staff time (backfill or opportunity cost), dual-running, decommissioning, contingency.\n   - **Recurring:** licences, run and support, maintenance, staff, hosting; with escalation.\n5. **Benefits (incremental), each with a type, formula, owner and ramp:**\n   - **Cash-releasing:** budget reductions, revenue, avoided capex.\n   - **Non-cash-releasing:** productivity absorbed into existing budgets.\n   - **Risk reduction:** expected loss avoided (probability × impact).\n   - **Non-financial:** customer, employee, ESG, compliance.\n   Include only cash-releasing benefits (and risk reduction, if quantified robustly) in the headline NPV; show the others separately.\n6. **Financials** (compute with code or a spreadsheet):\n   - Annual incremental cash flows (option minus baseline), tax effects if material, and terminal or residual value if justified.\n   - NPV at the hurdle rate; IRR; simple and discounted payback; break-even.\n   - Show the cash-flow table.\n7. **Sensitivity and scenarios:**\n   - One-at-a-time on the key drivers (benefit size, adoption or ramp, cost overrun, delay).\n   - Break-even values.\n   - Downside and upside scenarios with linked assumptions.\n8. **Risks:** delivery, adoption, technology, vendor, regulatory; with mitigations, and how the risks are reflected in contingency and phasing.\n9. **Funding and stage gates:** tranches, the criteria to release each, and kill criteria.\n10. **Benefits management:** benefits register (baseline, target, measure, owner, timing, how banked), reporting and post-implementation review date.\n11. **The ask:** amount, phasing, decision required and date, conditions.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Do-minimum is often the real competitor.** A cheaper, smaller fix may deliver 70% of the value at 30% of the cost. Show it, even if you recommend more.\n- **Be careful with \"strategic\" benefits.** They are real but unquantified. Name them, don't inflate hard numbers to compensate.\n- **Productivity ≠ cash.** Count hours released as cash only if budgets or hiring plans actually change.\n- **Discount rate debates hide weak cases.** If the case only works at a lower rate than policy, say so.\n- **Terminal value can dominate.** If more than ~40% of NPV comes from beyond the explicit horizon, flag it and test it.\n- **Don't over-model.** A precise model of uncertain assumptions is false comfort. Spend effort on the 3-4 drivers that matter.\n\n## Red flags\n- No baseline, or a baseline of zero.\n- Sunk costs included; allocated overheads counted as savings.\n- 100% benefits from year 1; no ramp; no owners.\n- No contingency on a large or novel project.\n- Sensitivity only on drivers that don't matter.\n- The full budget requested at once for an uncertain initiative.\n- NPV driven by terminal value or by soft benefits.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Capex / plant:** capacity, utilisation, maintenance capex, working capital, depreciation and tax shields, salvage value.\n- **Technology / ERP / platform:** dual-running, data migration, decommissioning savings (often late or partial), the internal resource drain.\n- **Transformation / cost programmes:** cost-to-achieve vs run-rate savings; the realisation curve; double-counting across initiatives.\n- **Public sector / not-for-profit:** social value and cost-benefit approaches; the government discount rate; distributional effects.\n- **Regulatory or compliance spend:** compare against the cost of non-compliance (penalties × probability, licence risk); NPV may be negative but required. Frame it as the least-cost compliant option.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Business Case: [proposal]\n**Recommendation and ask:** Approve ... over ... in [n] tranches. NPV ... @ ...%; IRR ...; payback ...; key break-even: ...\n\n### 1. Problem and strategic fit\n### 2. Options (baseline / do-minimum / proposed / alternative)\n| Option | Description | NPV | Key pros / cons | Status |\n### 3. Incremental cash flows (proposed vs baseline)\n| ₹/$ | Y0 | Y1 | Y2 | Y3 | Y4 | Y5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| One-off costs | | | | | | |\n| Recurring costs | | | | | | |\n| Cash-releasing benefits | | | | | | |\n| Net incremental cash flow | | | | | | |\n### 4. Returns and sensitivity\n| Scenario / driver change | NPV | Payback |\n### 5. Benefits register\n| Benefit | Type | Formula | Baseline | Target | Owner | Timing | How banked |\n### 6. Non-financial benefits\n### 7. Risks and mitigations\n### 8. Funding tranches, stage gates and kill criteria\n### 9. Post-implementation review\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] A realistic baseline and a do-minimum option are included.\n- [ ] Only incremental cash is in NPV; sunk costs and unchanged overheads are excluded.\n- [ ] Every benefit has a type, formula, owner, ramp and banking method.\n- [ ] Contingency and optimism bias are addressed explicitly.\n- [ ] Calculations done with code or a spreadsheet; the table is shown.\n- [ ] Break-even and downside are presented.\n- [ ] Funding is staged with kill criteria; a review date is set.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `financial-model-spec`: Design a clean, auditable financial model: structure, drivers, sheets and checks, ready to build.\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n- `benefits-tracker`: Make sure promised benefits are defined, owned, measured and actually delivered.\n- `playbook-investment-case`: From idea to approval-ready investment case: sizing, economics, model, sensitivity, risks and the ask.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public guidance and widely taught methods; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **UK HM Treasury, *The Green Book*** (central government guidance on appraisal and evaluation): options appraisal, do-minimum baseline, optimism-bias adjustments, the five-case model (strategic, economic, commercial, financial, management). Freely available and widely adapted by private-sector firms.\n- **Reference-class forecasting and optimism bias:** widely documented evidence that large projects tend to overrun on cost and under-deliver benefits. Use your organisation's own history of past projects as the reference class where possible.\n- **Capital budgeting:** NPV, IRR, payback, incremental cash flows, sunk and opportunity costs, as taught in standard corporate-finance curricula.\n- **Benefits realisation:** public-sector benefits-management guidance and professional project-management bodies' standards.\n- **Accounting treatment:** capitalisation under IFRS / Ind AS (e.g. Ind AS 16 property, plant and equipment; Ind AS 38 intangibles, for software development). Confirm with Finance and auditors."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional figures; recompute with real data)\n\n**Proposal:** automate accounts payable at a 5,000-employee manufacturer (180,000 invoices a year, 22 AP staff).\n\n**Recommendation and ask:** approve ₹3.4 Cr one-off, in 2 tranches, plus ₹0.62 Cr a year run cost.\n- NPV @ 12% (5 yrs) ≈ ₹4.1 Cr; IRR ≈ 38%; payback ≈ 2.3 years.\n- **Break-even:** NPV turns negative if touchless processing reaches only 34% (plan: 65%).\n\n## Options\n| Option | Description | NPV (₹ Cr) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | Current process; volumes +6%/yr → 2 more AP hires by Y3; late-payment penalties continue | — | Reference |\n| Do-minimum | Enable e-invoicing intake + rules-based matching in the existing ERP | 1.6 | Cheap; caps touchless at ~40% |\n| **Proposed** | Do-minimum + AI capture and exception handling + supplier portal | **4.1** | Recommended |\n| Outsource AP | Third-party BPO | 2.2 | Lower control; 3-year lock-in |\n\n## Incremental cash flows (₹ Cr, proposed vs baseline)\n| | Y0 | Y1 | Y2 | Y3 | Y4 | Y5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| One-off (software set-up, integration, migration, change, internal backfill, 15% contingency) | -3.40 | | | | | |\n| Recurring (licences, support, 0.5 FTE admin) | | -0.62 | -0.64 | -0.66 | -0.68 | -0.70 |\n| Hiring avoided (2 planned AP hires from Y2; +2 more by Y4) | | | 0.30 | 0.30 | 0.60 | 0.60 |\n| Attrition not backfilled (4 roles over Y1-Y2, redeployed elsewhere: counted only when budget is cut) | | 0.30 | 0.60 | 0.60 | 0.60 | 0.60 |\n| Early-payment discounts captured (ramp 30% → 80%) | | 0.25 | 0.55 | 0.70 | 0.75 | 0.80 |\n| Duplicate / erroneous payments avoided | | 0.15 | 0.25 | 0.30 | 0.30 | 0.30 |\n| Late-payment penalties avoided | | 0.10 | 0.15 | 0.15 | 0.15 | 0.15 |\n| **Net** | **-3.40** | **0.18** | **1.21** | **1.39** | **1.72** | **1.75** |\n\n(Recompute NPV, IRR and payback with code; the figures above are illustrative.)\n\n## Sensitivity\n| Change | NPV (₹ Cr) |\n|---|---|\n| Touchless 50% instead of 65% | 2.3 |\n| Discounts captured −40% | 2.9 |\n| One-off cost +25% | 3.3 |\n| 6-month delay | 3.2 |\n| Downside (all of the above, linked) | 0.4 |\n\n## Benefits register (extract)\n| Benefit | Type | Owner | Baseline | Target | How banked |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hiring avoided | Cash-releasing | CFO / CHRO | Plan: +4 AP FTE by Y4 | 0 added | Headcount plan revised |\n| Early-payment discounts | Cash-releasing | Treasurer | ₹0.2 Cr/yr captured | ₹1.0 Cr/yr | Finance-income budget |\n| Faster close (AP cut-off −1 day) | Non-financial | Controller | 9-day close | 8-day | — |\n\n## Stage gates\n- **Tranche 1 (₹1.6 Cr):** do-minimum plus the AI capture pilot on 2 plants.\n- **Tranche 2 (₹1.8 Cr):** released if, after 3 months on the pilot plants, touchless is ≥50%, the exception backlog is ≤2 days, and duplicate payments are down ≥50%.\n- **Kill / rethink:** touchless <40% after 4 months → stop at do-minimum.\n\n**Post-implementation review:** month 18."}]}, {"id": "financial-model-spec", "pack": "numbers", "title": "Financial Model Spec", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Design a clean, auditable financial model: structure, drivers, sheets and checks, ready to build.", "tryp": "Spec a 5-year financial model for a new D2C skincare brand: revenue by channel, CAC, gross margin, working capital, funding needs.", "when": ["Before anyone opens spreadsheet", "Briefing an analyst to build a model", "Fixing an existing model that nobody trusts"], "inputs": ["Purpose and decisions the model supports", "Business model and key drivers", "Time horizon and granularity", "Who will use it"], "pairs": ["business-case", "unit-economics", "sensitivity-analysis", "kpi-driver-tree"], "body": "# Financial Model Spec\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a model design that answers the decision-makers' questions, can be reviewed by someone else in an hour, and doesn't break when assumptions change.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Purpose drives design.** List the 3-5 questions the model must answer (fund or not? price at what level? how much cash do we need, and when?). Every sheet must serve them.\n2. **Separate inputs, calculations and outputs.** All assumptions in one place, labelled with units, source and owner. No hard-coded numbers inside formulas.\n3. **Drivers, not plugs.** Revenue and cost come from operational drivers (volume × price; headcount × cost; utilisation) that managers recognise and can challenge.\n4. **Consistency.** One formula per row across time; consistent time axis; consistent sign convention; no circularity unless deliberately controlled.\n5. **Integrity checks are part of the model.** Balance sheet balances, cash ties, totals match, no error values. A check summary is visible on the cover.\n6. **Simple beats clever.** Avoid complex nested formulas, hidden sheets and macros unless essential. Another analyst must be able to follow it.\n7. **Scenarios are designed in,** not patched in later.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Decisions and audience (board, investors, lenders, internal).\n- Business model and the 5-10 key drivers.\n- Horizon and periodicity: monthly for cash-critical early periods, then quarterly or annual.\n- Required outputs: P&L only, a cash view, full three statements, valuation (DCF), covenant tests, unit economics.\n- Data availability: actuals for calibration; the owners of each assumption.\n- Tools and constraints: spreadsheet standards, model-review requirements.\n\n## Method\n1. **Questions and outputs first:** sketch the output pages (KPIs, charts, scenario comparison) before any calculation.\n2. **Driver trees:**\n   - **Revenue:** customers or volume × price or mix × retention, by segment and channel.\n   - **Costs:** variable (per unit), semi-variable (step-fixed by capacity), fixed (headcount × cost, rent, technology).\n   - **Capex:** growth vs maintenance; useful lives; depreciation.\n   - **Working capital:** receivable days, inventory days, payable days.\n   - **Financing:** debt drawdowns and repayments, interest, equity, dividends; covenants.\n   - **Tax:** the effective approach, plus deferred-tax simplifications if needed.\n3. **Sheet architecture:**\n   - Cover (purpose, version, checks)\n   - Inputs (by theme, with scenario columns)\n   - Time and flags (period index, operating flags)\n   - Calculation blocks (Revenue, Opex, Headcount, Capex & Depreciation, Working Capital, Debt, Tax)\n   - Statements (P&L, Balance Sheet, Cash Flow)\n   - Outputs (dashboard, scenario comparison, sensitivity tables)\n   - Checks\n   - Log (changes, issues)\n4. **Scenario design:** a selector on the inputs sheet (Base / Downside / Upside / custom), with linked driver sets rather than independent tweaks, and a data table or script for sensitivity.\n5. **Calibration:** reconcile the first periods to historic actuals, or to a pilot or benchmark; explain differences.\n6. **Checks:**\n   - BS balance;\n   - cash in the BS equals cash in the CF;\n   - retained earnings roll-forward;\n   - debt schedule ties;\n   - no negative cash (or a flag);\n   - sum of segments equals the total;\n   - no errors;\n   - inputs within plausible ranges.\n7. **Standards:**\n   - input cells visually distinct;\n   - units in labels;\n   - consistent signs;\n   - named ranges for key inputs;\n   - no merged cells in calculations;\n   - one row, one formula;\n   - left-to-right time;\n   - no hidden rows with logic.\n8. **Review protocol:** self-review checklist, peer review (formula sampling, logic trace of the top 5 outputs), and sign-off; version naming and a change log.\n9. **Optionally draft the model** if tools allow. Produce the spreadsheet with this architecture and placeholder inputs clearly marked.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Granularity is costly.** Model detail where decisions or uncertainty sit; summarise elsewhere.\n- **Monthly modelling for cash.** Start-ups, projects and turnarounds need monthly cash for at least 12-24 months.\n- **Circularity** (interest on average cash, or a revolver) is sometimes needed. Isolate it with a switch, or use prior-period balances.\n- **Keep the model and the story aligned.** Every key output on the deck must trace to a model cell.\n- **Calibrate before forecasting.** A model that can't reproduce last year shouldn't predict the next five.\n\n## Red flags\n- Hard-coded numbers in formulas; plugs to make the balance sheet balance.\n- Scenarios built by overwriting inputs.\n- No checks, or checks failing but ignored.\n- Revenue as a growth % on last year with no drivers.\n- 40-tab models with no map or documentation.\n- Outputs on slides that can't be traced.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Start-up / venture:** cohorts, CAC and LTV, burn and runway, funding rounds and dilution.\n- **Project finance / capex:** construction period, ramp-up, debt sculpting, DSCR covenants, reserve accounts.\n- **Valuation (DCF):** explicit forecast plus terminal value; WACC build-up; the bridge from enterprise to equity value.\n- **Budget / annual plan:** the bridge from last year's actuals; cost-centre detail; phasing by month.\n- **Transformation / cost programme:** baseline vs initiatives; cost-to-achieve; run-rate vs in-year.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Financial Model Spec: [model]\n**Purpose / decisions:** ...  |  **Audience:** ...  |  **Horizon / periods:** ...  |  **Currency / units:** ...\n\n**Sheet map**\n| Sheet | Contents | Key outputs |\n|---|---|---|\n\n**Driver trees:** revenue ... | costs ... | capex ... | working capital ... | financing ...\n\n**Key assumptions register**\n| Driver | Unit | Base | Downside | Upside | Source | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Scenario and sensitivity design:** ...\n**Calibration plan:** ...\n**Integrity checks:** ...\n**Standards and review protocol:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Questions and outputs are defined before calculations.\n- [ ] Inputs are separated, with units, sources and owners.\n- [ ] Driver trees cover revenue, costs, capex, working capital and financing as needed.\n- [ ] A scenario selector with linked driver sets.\n- [ ] Integrity checks listed and visible.\n- [ ] A calibration-to-actuals step.\n- [ ] Standards and the review protocol stated.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `unit-economics`: Work out whether each customer, order or unit makes money, and which levers fix it.\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n- `kpi-driver-tree`: Break a top metric into the drivers people can act on, with owners and targets.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught practice; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Spreadsheet modelling standards:** publicly available, industry-developed best-practice standards for financial models: separation of inputs, calculations and outputs; consistency; transparency; integrity checks. Several free standards exist and are widely used by banks, project-finance practitioners and model auditors.\n- **Spreadsheet risk research:** academic and practitioner research consistently finds error rates in a high share of operational spreadsheets, which supports mandatory checks and peer review.\n- **Three-statement linkages, DCF valuation, working-capital days, project-finance metrics (DSCR, LLCR):** as taught in standard corporate-finance and financial-modelling curricula.\n- **Accounting:** Ind AS / IFRS (e.g. Ind AS 115 revenue; Ind AS 116 leases; Ind AS 16 PP&E) for how items should appear in statements. Confirm with Finance and auditors."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional D2C skincare brand, 5-year plan for a Series A raise)\n\n**Purpose / decisions:**\n1. How much capital is needed, and when, to reach EBITDA break-even?\n2. Which channel mix maximises contribution?\n3. What happens if CAC rises 30%?\n\n**Audience:** board and investors. **Horizon:** monthly for 24 months, then quarterly to Y5. **Currency:** ₹ lakh.\n\n## Sheet map\n| Sheet | Contents | Key outputs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cover | Purpose, version, check summary | All checks green? |\n| Inputs | Drivers by theme; scenario columns; selector | — |\n| Time | Period index, month/quarter flags, launch flags | — |\n| Rev_D2C | Sessions × conversion × AOV; new vs repeat cohorts | D2C revenue, orders |\n| Rev_Marketplace | Units × net realised price (after commission and returns) | Marketplace revenue |\n| Rev_Retail | Doors × throughput × trade margin (from month 13) | Retail revenue |\n| COGS | Unit cost by SKU family; MOQ price breaks | Gross margin |\n| Marketing | Spend by channel; CAC by channel; payback | New customers |\n| People | Headcount plan × loaded cost | Payroll |\n| Opex | Fulfilment per order, payment fees, tech, rent | Contribution, EBITDA |\n| WC | Inventory days (by MOQ cycles), receivable days (marketplace 15-30), payable days | Working capital |\n| Capex_Dep | Moulds, warehouse fit-out | Depreciation |\n| Funding | Equity rounds; venture debt | Cash, runway |\n| Statements | P&L, BS, CF | — |\n| Outputs | Dashboard; scenario comparison; CAC sensitivity grid | Peak funding need, break-even month |\n| Checks | BS balance, cash tie, cohort totals, no errors | Pass/fail |\n| Log | Changes and open issues | — |\n\n## Key assumptions (extract)\n| Driver | Unit | Base | Downside | Upside | Source | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Site conversion | % | 2.1 | 1.7 | 2.5 | Last 6 months' analytics | Growth lead |\n| AOV | ₹ | 1,150 | 1,050 | 1,250 | Order data | Growth lead |\n| 6-month repeat rate | % of cohort | 32 | 24 | 38 | Cohort analysis | CRM lead |\n| Blended CAC | ₹ | 780 | 1,015 (+30%) | 700 | Channel data | Marketing |\n| Marketplace commission + returns | % of GMV | 28 + 6 | 30 + 9 | 26 + 5 | Contracts, history | Ops |\n| Inventory days | days | 75 | 95 | 65 | Supplier MOQ cycles | Supply chain |\n\n**Scenario design:** the selector switches linked sets. The downside combines lower conversion, higher CAC and higher returns (all of which fall in a weaker market).\n\n**Calibration:** months 1-6 of the model are set to reproduce Jan-Jun actuals within ±3% on revenue and contribution before forecasting.\n\n**Checks:** BS balances in all periods; CF closing cash = BS cash; cohort customer counts reconcile to orders; marketplace receivables never negative; flag any month with cash < 3 months of burn.\n\n**Outputs:** peak funding need and month; EBITDA break-even month; runway by scenario; a CAC × repeat-rate sensitivity grid on cumulative burn."}]}, {"id": "unit-economics", "pack": "numbers", "title": "Unit Economics", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Work out whether each customer, order or unit makes money, and which levers fix it.", "tryp": "Our subscription meal kit: ₹2,400 per box, food cost ₹1,150, delivery ₹220, packaging ₹140, discounts avg 12%, CAC ₹1,900, 38% churn per month. Do the unit economics.", "when": ["Start-up or new product evaluation", "Channel or marketing efficiency", "Investor due diligence"], "inputs": ["Price, variable costs, discounts", "Acquisition costs", "Retention or repeat data"], "pairs": ["pricing-strategy", "financial-model-spec", "kpi-driver-tree", "customer-segmentation"], "body": "# Unit Economics\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear view of whether each unit creates or destroys value, why, and which two or three levers would change the answer, backed by cohort evidence rather than averages.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Pick the right unit.** The unit is where economics are decided: an order for quick commerce, a customer for subscriptions, a loan for lending, a store for retail roll-outs.\n2. **Contribution, not revenue.** Every variable cost a unit triggers must be included: COGS, discounts, returns and refunds, payment fees, fulfilment, packaging, last-mile, variable support, and fraud or credit losses.\n3. **Cohorts, not blended averages.** Retention, frequency and margins differ by acquisition month, channel and segment. Blended numbers hide deteriorating new cohorts.\n4. **Fully loaded CAC.** Include sales and marketing salaries, agency fees, tools and onboarding incentives where they scale with acquisition. Show paid and blended CAC separately.\n5. **Time value and uncertainty.** Long lifetimes are discounted and capped. A 10-year LTV on 18 months of data is fiction.\n6. **Unit economics ≠ company profitability.** Positive contribution must also cover fixed costs at realistic scale.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Business model and the candidate units; which decision this analysis informs (raise, price, cut a channel, expand a city).\n- Data: order or transaction data, cohort retention, cost lines, channel spend.\n- Time period and seasonality; promotions in the period.\n- Stage: early (few cohorts, more assumptions) vs mature (robust cohorts).\n\n## Method\n1. **Define the unit** and the time window.\n2. **Contribution waterfall per unit:**\n   gross price → discounts and promotions → returns and refunds → net revenue → COGS → payment fees → packaging and fulfilment → last-mile → variable customer support → losses (fraud, credit) → **contribution margin (CM1/CM2 as used)**.\n3. **CAC by channel:** spend (+ attributable people and tools) ÷ new customers. Note attribution method limits, and organic vs paid.\n4. **Retention and frequency by cohort:** a cohort table (by month of first purchase) of active rate and orders per active customer; fit a decay curve if needed.\n5. **LTV:** the sum of discounted contribution per customer over a capped horizon (e.g. 3 years) using cohort curves. Show the simple version (contribution per period ÷ churn) only as a cross-check.\n6. **Ratios:** LTV:CAC; CAC payback (months of contribution to recover CAC). Context benchmarks vary by model, so state any rule of thumb as indicative.\n7. **Fixed-cost absorption:** units or customers needed per month to cover fixed costs at target scale; implied break-even volume.\n8. **Diagnose:** the weakest link (low net price, high return rate, costly fulfilment, poor retention, expensive acquisition); by segment and channel.\n9. **Levers:** quantify the effect of realistic changes (price +5%, returns −3 pts, fulfilment −₹20, 6-month retention +5 pts, shift 20% of spend to the best channel) on contribution, LTV:CAC and payback. Rank them by impact and feasibility. Use code for all calculations.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Discounts are a cost of acquisition or retention.** Allocate first-order discounts to CAC if they exist to acquire. Be consistent.\n- **Marketplaces and platforms:** the unit may be a transaction with two sides. Model take-rate, subsidies on both sides, and liquidity effects.\n- **Beware survivor bias in mature cohorts.** Early cohorts may have been acquired cheaply in a different market.\n- **Contribution can be positive while cash is negative** (inventory, receivables). Add a working-capital view for inventory-heavy models.\n- **Unit economics can improve with scale only for specific reasons** (density, purchasing, fixed-cost leverage). Name the mechanism; don't assume it.\n\n## Red flags\n- LTV on revenue; CAC only on paid media.\n- \"LTV:CAC of 5\" built on 6 months of data and a churn assumption.\n- No returns, payment fees or support costs.\n- Blended averages hiding a bad new channel.\n- Improvement plans that rely on \"scale\" without a mechanism.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Subscriptions / SaaS:** MRR, gross and net revenue retention, expansion revenue, gross margin after hosting and support, payback on gross margin.\n- **E-commerce / quick commerce:** AOV, contribution per order, orders per customer, dark-store or warehouse utilisation, last-mile density.\n- **Lending:** yield, cost of funds, credit losses (expected loss), servicing cost, CAC per disbursed loan, contribution per loan over its tenor.\n- **Retail roll-out:** store-level contribution, payback on capex, ramp curve, cannibalisation.\n- **B2B services:** contribution per client after delivery cost; sales-cycle cost; utilisation.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Unit Economics: [business], unit = [unit], period [..]\n\n### Contribution waterfall (per unit)\n| Line | ₹ | % of gross |\n|---|---|---|\n\n### Acquisition\n| Channel | Spend | New customers | CAC (paid) | CAC (fully loaded) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Retention and LTV (by cohort)\n(cohort table summary; LTV horizon and discount rate)\n\n**LTV:CAC ... | CAC payback ... months | break-even volume ...**\n**Weakest link:** ...\n\n### Levers (ranked)\n| Lever | Change | Effect on contribution | LTV:CAC | Payback | Feasibility |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Path to healthy unit economics:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The unit is justified for the decision.\n- [ ] The waterfall includes discounts, returns, payment, fulfilment, support and losses.\n- [ ] CAC by channel, fully loaded; attribution limits noted.\n- [ ] LTV from cohorts, on contribution, discounted and capped.\n- [ ] Fixed-cost absorption and break-even volume shown.\n- [ ] Levers quantified with code and ranked.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `pricing-strategy`: Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.\n- `financial-model-spec`: Design a clean, auditable financial model: structure, drivers, sheets and checks, ready to build.\n- `kpi-driver-tree`: Break a top metric into the drivers people can act on, with owners and targets.\n- `customer-segmentation`: Group customers by needs and value so you can focus, price and serve them differently.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts; verify any benchmarks before use)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Customer lifetime value and cohort analysis:** standard marketing-analytics and corporate-finance methods (discounted contribution over a finite horizon; retention curves by cohort).\n- **CAC, LTV:CAC and payback:** widely used in venture investing and subscription businesses. Rules of thumb (e.g. LTV:CAC ≥3, payback under 12-18 months) are context-dependent indicators, not standards.\n- **Contribution margin and break-even analysis:** management-accounting fundamentals.\n- **Lending unit economics:** expected loss = probability of default × loss given default × exposure at default, as used in credit-risk practice and Ind AS 109 / IFRS 9 expected-credit-loss accounting.\n- **Attribution:** multi-touch and incrementality-testing approaches have known limits. Prefer holdout or geo experiments for major channel decisions."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional meal-kit subscription, unit = customer, monthly boxes)\n\n## Contribution waterfall (per box)\n| Line | ₹ | % of gross |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gross price | 2,400 | 100% |\n| Discounts (avg, incl. first-box offers) | −288 | −12% |\n| Refunds and credits | −48 | −2% |\n| **Net revenue** | **2,064** | 86% |\n| Food cost | −1,150 | −48% |\n| Packaging (insulated) | −140 | −6% |\n| Delivery | −220 | −9% |\n| Payment fees (2%) | −41 | −2% |\n| Customer support (variable) | −35 | −1.5% |\n| **Contribution** | **478** | **20%** |\n\n## Acquisition\n| Channel | Spend (₹ L/month) | New customers | Paid CAC | Fully loaded CAC (incl. team, agency, tools) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Social ads | 42 | 2,300 | 1,826 | 2,050 |\n| Search | 18 | 1,250 | 1,440 | 1,620 |\n| Referral (₹500 credit each side) | 8 | 900 | 889 | 980 |\n| **Blended** | 68 | 4,450 | 1,528 | 1,710 |\n\n## Retention (cohort average, last 6 cohorts)\n- Boxes ordered per customer: month 1 = 1.0; month 2 = 0.55; month 3 = 0.41; month 6 = 0.26; month 12 = 0.15.\n- 12-month cumulative ≈ 4.1 boxes per acquired customer. A 24-month cap adds ~1.0 box.\n- **LTV (24-month cap, 12% annual discount)** ≈ 5.0 boxes × ₹478 ≈ **₹2,350**.\n- **LTV:CAC** (fully loaded, blended) ≈ 1.4: well below a healthy level. **CAC payback** ≈ 10 months (cumulative contribution passes ₹1,710 at ~3.6 boxes), which looks fine, but most customers churn soon after, so there is little value beyond payback.\n- **Weakest links:** steep month-2 drop-off (45% never reorder) and social CAC.\n\n## Levers (computed with code; illustrative)\n| Lever | Change | Contribution / box | LTV | LTV:CAC |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Month-2 retention | 0.55 → 0.65 (onboarding and menu fit) | — | +₹420 | 1.6 |\n| Shift 30% of social spend to referral | Blended CAC 1,710 → 1,420 | — | — | 1.7 |\n| Delivery density (2 days/week per zone) | −₹45/box | 523 | +₹225 | 1.5 |\n| First-box discount 40% → 25% | Fewer but stickier customers; CAC +10% | +₹35 avg | +₹180 | 1.4 |\n| **Combined (retention + referral + density)** | | 523 | ~₹2,990 | **2.1** |\n\n**Path:** fix month-2 retention first (onboarding call, swap-friendly menus, a \"skip a week\" option), rebalance acquisition towards referrals, and densify delivery days. Re-test with the next 3 cohorts before scaling spend."}]}, {"id": "sensitivity-analysis", "pack": "numbers", "title": "Sensitivity Analysis", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.", "tryp": "Run a sensitivity on this business case: volume 10,000 units, price ₹4,500, unit cost ₹2,900, fixed cost ₹1.2 Cr, capex ₹3 Cr, 5 years, 12% discount rate.", "when": ["Before presenting any number-based recommendation", "When challenged 'what if you're wrong?'", "To decide which assumptions need more research"], "inputs": ["The model or the key formula and base-case inputs", "Reasonable ranges for each input (or let the skill propose)"], "pairs": ["business-case", "scenario-planning", "break-even-payback", "financial-model-spec"], "body": "# Sensitivity Analysis\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear answer to \"How wrong can we be and still be right?\" It names the few assumptions that decide the outcome, shows how far each can move before the decision flips, and says what to do about them.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Decision first.** Sensitivity is about whether the *decision* changes, not whether NPV moves. Define the decision threshold (NPV < 0, IRR < hurdle, covenant breach, cash < minimum).\n2. **Evidence-based ranges.** Ranges come from history, peer data, pilot variance or expert elicitation, never a blanket ±10%.\n3. **Switching values are the headline.** \"NPV turns negative if volumes are 22% below plan\" is more useful than a table of outputs.\n4. **Linked scenarios beat independent swings.** In a downturn, volume, price and receivable days move together. Build coherent stories.\n5. **Know when to simulate.** Monte Carlo helps when several uncertain drivers interact and the distribution of outcomes matters (e.g. covenant breach probability). Otherwise it adds false precision.\n6. **Close the loop.** Each critical driver gets a response: research it, stage the investment, hedge it, contract it, or monitor it with a trigger.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The model or formula, and the base case.\n- The decision metric and threshold.\n- Candidate drivers and what evidence exists on their uncertainty.\n- Correlations (which drivers move together) and constraints (capacity caps, contractual floors).\n\n## Method\n1. **List the drivers** in the model; shortlist the 6-12 most likely to matter.\n2. **Set ranges** per driver: low and high (e.g. a 10th to 90th percentile view), with the source of the range stated.\n3. **One-at-a-time analysis:** move each driver to its low and high while holding others at base; record the output; compute the swing; rank (tornado).\n4. **Switching values:** for the top 3-5 drivers, find the value at which the decision metric hits its threshold, expressed as a % change from base, and compare it with the plausible range. If the switching value lies within the range, the decision is fragile on that driver.\n5. **Linked scenarios:** 2-4 coherent stories (e.g. \"slow adoption\", \"cost inflation\", \"competitive price war\") with consistent driver movements; output for each.\n6. **Two-way grids** for the two most important interacting drivers (e.g. price × volume, CAC × retention).\n7. **Probabilistic simulation** (optional): distributions for key drivers, with correlations; outputs as a probability of meeting the threshold, P10/P50/P90. Report assumptions transparently.\n8. **Interpret:**\n   - Which drivers matter.\n   - How confident we are in each.\n   - Is the decision robust or fragile?\n   - What is reducible uncertainty (research, a pilot, supplier quotes) vs irreducible (macro)?\n9. **Responses:** for each critical driver: research action, design response (stage gate, option, phasing), contractual response (take-or-pay, price escalation clause), hedge, or a monitoring trigger.\n10. **Compute everything with code.** Show the tables and a short narrative.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Asymmetric ranges are normal.** Costs overrun more often than they underrun; adoption disappoints more often than it surprises.\n- **Don't flex what's contractually fixed.** Focus on what is genuinely uncertain.\n- **Correlation matters more than precision.** Two drivers that fail together create the real downside.\n- **Communicate in the audience's terms:** \"We break even at 81 patients a day; we plan 95; the nearby clinic averages 88.\"\n- **If the decision flips within the plausible range,** recommend design changes (staging, smaller first step), not just \"monitor closely\".\n\n## Red flags\n- A blanket ±10% on everything.\n- A tornado chart with no conclusion.\n- Scenarios where drivers move inconsistently (downturn with higher prices).\n- Monte Carlo with arbitrary distributions and no correlations, presented as precise probabilities.\n- No link from sensitivity to actions.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Capex / projects:** capex overrun, schedule delay, ramp-up, utilisation, commodity prices, FX.\n- **Product / pricing:** price elasticity, volume, mix, competitor response.\n- **Lending / credit:** default rates, recovery rates, funding cost, prepayment.\n- **Start-up:** CAC, conversion, retention, burn; runway sensitivity.\n- **Budget / plan:** the top 5 revenue drivers and cost inflation; the reforecast triggers.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Sensitivity Analysis: [model], decision metric = [metric], threshold = [..]\n**Base case:** ...  |  **Verdict:** Robust / Fragile on [driver] - one line\n\n### Tornado (one at a time)\n| Driver | Low | Base | High | Range source | Output @low | Output @high | Swing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Switching values\n| Driver | Switching value | % from base | Within plausible range? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Scenarios\n| Scenario | Story | Key driver settings | Output | Meets threshold? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Two-way grid: [driver A] × [driver B]\n### (Optional) Simulation: P(meets threshold), P10/P50/P90, key correlations\n### What this means and what we'll do\n| Critical driver | Reducible? | Response (research / design / contract / hedge / monitor) | Owner | Trigger |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The decision metric and threshold are defined.\n- [ ] Ranges are evidence-based and sourced; asymmetry considered.\n- [ ] A tornado ranking plus switching values for the top drivers.\n- [ ] Coherent linked scenarios.\n- [ ] Simulation used only if it adds value, with correlations stated.\n- [ ] A plain-language verdict and a response per critical driver.\n- [ ] Computed with code; tables shown.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `scenario-planning`: Build 3-4 plausible futures from key uncertainties, and find moves that work across them.\n- `break-even-payback`: Quickly find the break-even volume, payback period and margin of safety for a decision.\n- `financial-model-spec`: Design a clean, auditable financial model: structure, drivers, sheets and checks, ready to build.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught methods; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Sensitivity, switching values and scenario analysis:** public appraisal guidance (e.g. UK HM Treasury *Green Book*: sensitivity analysis and switching values) and standard corporate-finance curricula.\n- **Monte Carlo simulation:** standard decision-analysis practice; use correlated inputs and report percentile outcomes. See risk-analysis guidance in ISO 31010:2019 (risk assessment techniques).\n- **Expert elicitation of ranges:** structured elicitation practices (e.g. asking for P10/P50/P90 with reasons; calibration training) reduce overconfidence.\n- **Optimism bias:** documented tendencies in cost overruns and benefit shortfalls. Set ranges asymmetrically where history supports it."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional figures; recompute with real data)\n\n**Decision:** build a new 10,000-units-a-year assembly line.\n- Base: volume 10,000/yr; price ₹4,500; unit variable cost ₹2,900; fixed cost ₹1.2 Cr/yr; capex ₹3.0 Cr; 5-year life; 12% discount rate; no salvage value.\n- **Decision metric:** NPV ≥ 0.\n- **As first presented:** contribution 10,000 × ₹1,600 = ₹1.6 Cr/yr, less fixed ₹1.2 Cr = ₹0.4 Cr/yr; × 3.605 (5-yr annuity at 12%) = ₹1.44 Cr, less ₹3.0 Cr capex → **NPV ≈ −₹1.56 Cr**.\n\n**Correction caught in review:** most of the ₹1.2 Cr is shared overhead that exists anyway. **Only ₹0.5 Cr is incremental.**\n- Incremental operating cash = 1.6 − 0.5 = ₹1.1 Cr/yr.\n- PV = 1.1 × 3.605 = ₹3.97 Cr.\n- **Base NPV ≈ +₹0.97 Cr.**\n\n*Lesson: test what is incremental before testing sensitivities.*\n\n## Tornado (incremental base, NPV ₹ Cr)\n| Driver | Low | High | Range source | NPV @low | NPV @high | Swing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Volume | 7,500 | 11,000 | Order pipeline P10/P90 | −0.47 | 1.55 | 2.02 |\n| Price | ₹4,200 | ₹4,600 | Last 3 yrs' realised prices | −0.11 | 1.33 | 1.44 |\n| Unit variable cost | ₹3,150 | ₹2,850 | Commodity range | 0.07 | 1.15 | 1.08 |\n| Capex | ₹3.6 Cr | ₹2.9 Cr | Vendor quotes ±, contingency | 0.37 | 1.07 | 0.70 |\n| Incremental fixed | ₹0.7 Cr | ₹0.45 Cr | Staffing plan | 0.25 | 1.15 | 0.90 |\n\n## Switching values\n| Driver | Switching value | % from base | Within plausible range? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Volume | ~8,320 | −17% | **Yes (fragile)** |\n| Price | ~₹4,232 | −6.0% | **Yes (fragile)** |\n| Unit variable cost | ~₹3,170 | +9% | At the edge |\n| Capex | ~₹3.97 Cr | +32% | No |\n\n## Scenarios\n| Scenario | Story | Settings | NPV | Meets? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Price war | A competitor enters | Price ₹4,250, volume 9,000 | −0.42 | No |\n| Commodity spike | Steel +15% | Unit cost ₹3,100 | 0.25 | Yes (thin) |\n| Strong demand | Export order | Volume 11,000, price ₹4,550 | 1.73 | Yes |\n\n## Verdict and responses\n**Fragile on volume and price.**\n| Critical driver | Reducible? | Response | Owner | Trigger |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Volume | Partly | Secure 2 anchor contracts covering ≥6,000 units with take-or-pay before committing capex | CCO | No signed contracts by Mar → defer |\n| Price | Partly | Price-escalation clause linked to the steel index; value-based pricing for premium variants | CCO | Realised price < ₹4,400 for 2 quarters |\n| Unit cost | Hedge | Quarterly steel contracts with a band | CPO | Index +10% |"}]}, {"id": "break-even-payback", "pack": "numbers", "title": "Break-even and Payback", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Quickly find the break-even volume, payback period and margin of safety for a decision.", "tryp": "We're opening a new clinic: fit-out ₹1.8 Cr, monthly fixed cost ₹22 lakh, average bill ₹1,400, variable cost 35%. Break-even and payback?", "when": ["Quick screening of an idea or project", "New site, product or store decisions", "Explaining economics to non-finance colleagues"], "inputs": ["Fixed costs, variable cost per unit (or %), price per unit", "Up-front investment", "Expected volume and ramp-up"], "pairs": ["sensitivity-analysis", "business-case", "unit-economics", "pricing-strategy"], "body": "# Break-even and Payback\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a fast, correct view of how much must be sold to stop losing money, how long it takes to get the investment back, and how much cash the project burns before it turns, expressed in numbers an operator can picture.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Contribution, not revenue.** Break-even = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit (price − variable cost per unit).\n2. **Classify costs honestly.** Many costs are step-fixed: a second shift, a new supervisor at a volume threshold. Model the steps.\n3. **Ramp-up dominates payback.** New sites and products rarely start at steady state. Model month by month.\n4. **Working capital is cash.** Inventory and receivables tie up cash before profits arrive; include them in payback and the cash trough.\n5. **Make it tangible.** Convert break-even into operating terms: patients per day, covers per night, units per shift, loans per month.\n6. **Compare with evidence.** Is the required volume plausible versus existing sites, competitors or capacity?\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Price per unit (net of discounts and returns), variable cost per unit, fixed costs (and their steps), investment, working-capital needs, ramp profile, capacity, operating days.\n- Discount rate (for discounted payback) and the decision rule (e.g. payback < 3 years).\n- Comparable benchmarks: existing sites, pilot, market data.\n\n## Method\n1. **Unit contribution:** net price − variable costs (materials, consumables, variable labour, commissions, payment fees, logistics). Contribution margin %.\n2. **Fixed costs:** monthly or annual; identify the step thresholds.\n3. **Break-even volume** = fixed ÷ unit contribution. **Break-even revenue** = fixed ÷ CM%. With steps, compute break-even for each capacity tier.\n4. **Translate to operations:** per day, per shift, per seat or per bed; as a % of capacity (break-even utilisation).\n5. **Margin of safety** = (expected − break-even) ÷ expected. **Operating leverage** = contribution ÷ operating profit at the expected volume. This shows how sensitive profit is to volume.\n6. **Payback** (compute with code):\n   - monthly cash flow with ramp-up;\n   - working-capital build;\n   - any maintenance capex;\n   - simple payback (cumulative cash turns positive);\n   - discounted payback;\n   - the cash trough (maximum cumulative negative).\n7. **Quick sensitivity:** break-even and payback if price −5%, variable cost +5%, ramp 3 months slower.\n8. **Verdict:** plausibility versus benchmarks; the key risk; what would make it safer (phasing, lower fixed cost, pre-sales).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Fixed today, variable tomorrow.** Leases and permanent staff look fixed but are decisions. Consider flexible models to lower break-even.\n- **Cannibalisation:** volume taken from your own other sites isn't incremental.\n- **Seasonality:** monthly break-even may fail in low months even if the annual average passes. Check cash in the lowest month.\n- **Payback ignores value after payback.** Use it as a risk screen, alongside NPV for value.\n\n## Red flags\n- Break-even computed on revenue or gross margin that excludes variable costs like commissions or logistics.\n- Steady-state volumes from month 1.\n- No working capital in payback for inventory-heavy businesses.\n- Break-even utilisation above realistic capacity (e.g. >85% for hospitals or hotels).\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Healthcare:** per bed-day or per patient; payer mix changes the net price; doctor fee-share is variable.\n- **Retail and restaurants:** per store; footfall × conversion × basket; rent as the key fixed cost; ramp 6-18 months.\n- **Manufacturing:** per unit; shift-based steps; minimum economic batch sizes.\n- **SaaS / digital:** per customer; low variable cost; break-even driven by fixed team cost and CAC.\n- **Lending:** per loan; funding cost and credit loss are variable; fixed branch or tech cost.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Break-even and Payback: [project]\n**Unit contribution:** ₹... (CM ...%)  |  **Fixed costs:** ₹... /month (steps: ...)\n**Break-even:** ... units/month = **... per day** (... % of capacity) | revenue ₹...\n**Expected:** ... → **margin of safety ...%** | operating leverage ...x\n**Payback:** simple ... months | discounted ... months | **cash trough ₹...** in month ...\n\n| Month | Volume | Contribution | Fixed | Working capital | Net cash | Cumulative |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Sensitivity:** ...\n**Plausibility vs benchmarks:** ...\n**In plain words:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Contribution includes all variable costs; fixed-cost steps modelled.\n- [ ] Break-even translated into daily or shift terms and % of capacity.\n- [ ] Ramp-up and working capital included in payback; cash trough shown.\n- [ ] Margin of safety and operating leverage stated.\n- [ ] Benchmarked for plausibility; computed with code.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `unit-economics`: Work out whether each customer, order or unit makes money, and which levers fix it.\n- `pricing-strategy`: Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Cost-volume-profit (CVP) analysis, contribution margin, margin of safety, operating leverage:** management-accounting fundamentals (e.g. the cost and management accounting syllabi of professional accounting bodies).\n- **Payback and discounted payback:** capital-budgeting basics; use alongside NPV.\n- **Working-capital cycle:** receivable, inventory and payable days, from standard corporate-finance practice.\n- **Benchmarks:** use your own comparable sites or pilots first; external industry ratios vary by city tier, format and payer mix."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional multi-specialty day-care clinic, Tier-2 city)\n\n**Inputs:**\n- Fit-out and equipment: ₹1.8 Cr.\n- Monthly fixed costs: ₹22 L (rent ₹4 L, salaried staff ₹13 L, utilities and maintenance ₹2.5 L, IT and admin ₹2.5 L).\n- A second-shift step at >110 patients/day adds ₹6 L/month.\n- Average net bill ₹1,400 (after the payer mix and insurer discounts).\n- Variable cost 35% of the bill (consumables, doctor fee-share, payment and TPA fees) → contribution ₹910 per visit (65%).\n- Working capital: insurer receivables of 45 days on 40% of revenue.\n- Operating 26 days a month.\n\n**Break-even:**\n- ₹22 L ÷ ₹910 = **2,418 visits/month ≈ 93 per day**.\n\n**Plausibility:** the existing clinic in a similar city averages 88 a day after 18 months, and capacity on one shift is 110 a day, so break-even sits at ~85% utilisation. **Too close.**\n\n**Redesign test:** cut fixed costs to ₹18.5 L (a smaller footprint, rent ₹2.8 L; nurse rostering to demand) and raise the specialty mix (net bill ₹1,520, contribution ₹988).\n- Break-even = **1,872 visits/month ≈ 72 per day** (65% of capacity).\n- Expected at month 12: 90/day → **margin of safety 20%**.\n- Operating leverage at 90/day ≈ 5x (profit is very volume-sensitive).\n\n**Payback (redesigned; ramp 30 → 90/day over 12 months; computed with code; illustrative):**\n- Cash trough ≈ ₹2.6 Cr in month 9 (fit-out + operating losses + receivables build).\n- Simple payback ≈ month 34.\n- Discounted payback (12%) ≈ month 40.\n\n**Sensitivity:**\n- Ramp 3 months slower → payback +5 months; trough +₹0.4 Cr.\n- Net bill −5% → break-even 77/day.\n\n**In plain words:** the clinic needs about 72 patients a day to cover its costs. We expect 90 by the end of year 1. That's a reasonable buffer only with the smaller footprint and the higher specialty mix. Plan funding for a cash dip of about ₹2.6 Cr."}]}, {"id": "kpi-driver-tree", "pack": "numbers", "title": "KPI Driver Tree", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Break a top metric into the drivers people can act on, with owners and targets.", "tryp": "Build a driver tree for EBITDA margin of a cement plant, down to operational levers.", "when": ["Setting targets and cascading them", "Designing dashboards", "Value creation and turnaround plans"], "inputs": ["The top metric", "Business model", "Current numbers if available"], "pairs": ["kpi-dashboard", "issue-tree", "cost-takeout", "benefits-tracker"], "body": "# KPI Driver Tree\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a single picture showing how front-line levers add up to the number the board cares about. It shows which levers move the result most, who owns each one, and which early indicators will tell you, weeks ahead, whether you'll hit the target.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **The maths must add up.** Every split is an identity (sum or product). If the leaves don't reconstruct the top number, the tree isn't a driver tree.\n2. **End at controllable levers.** Leaves are things a named person can act on this quarter: uptime, conversion, price realisation, days of inventory, first-time-right %.\n3. **Sensitivity ranks attention.** Compute the impact on the top metric of a realistic improvement in each lever. Effort follows impact, not org charts.\n4. **Lead and lag.** Pair lagging financial outcomes with leading operational indicators that move first.\n5. **One tree, many cuts.** The same tree supports targets, budgets, dashboards and initiative sizing. Avoid parallel, inconsistent metric sets.\n6. **Ownership is singular.** Each leaf has one owner, even if many contribute.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Top metric and why: ROCE for capital-intensive firms, contribution for marketplaces, cost-to-serve for service operations.\n- Business model and the value chain; the units of activity (tonnes, orders, cases, beds, loans).\n- Available data per node and its frequency; known pain points.\n- Purpose: target setting, diagnosis of underperformance, or sizing initiatives.\n\n## Method\n1. **Top-level identity.** Examples:\n   - ROCE = EBIT ÷ capital employed.\n   - EBIT = revenue − costs.\n   - Capital employed = fixed assets + working capital.\n2. **Level-by-level decomposition:**\n   - **Revenue** = volume × net price. Volume = capacity × utilisation, or customers × frequency. Net price = list price × (1 − discount %) × mix.\n   - **Costs:** variable cost = volume × unit cost (materials × yield; energy = specific consumption × tariff); fixed = headcount × cost + other fixed.\n   - **Working capital** = receivable days × revenue ÷ 365 + inventory days × COGS ÷ 365 − payable days × purchases ÷ 365.\n3. **Stop at operational levers** with owners and data, typically 3-5 levels deep and 15-30 leaves.\n4. **Populate:** current value, target, data source and frequency, owner, leading or lagging.\n5. **Sensitivity:**\n   - For each leaf, compute the impact on the top metric of a realistic change (e.g. +1 point uptime, −1 day DSO, +1% price realisation). Use code.\n   - Rank the leaves.\n   - Flag levers with high impact and high controllability.\n6. **Validate:** reconcile the tree to the last actual period (the leaves must recreate the reported top number within a small tolerance); review with each owner.\n7. **Cascade:** targets per leaf that add up to the top target, checked for feasibility against history and benchmarks.\n8. **Instrument:** map leaves to dashboard tiles and review rhythms (daily or shift for operations, weekly for commercial, monthly for financial).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Ratios hide volumes.** Prefer multiplicative splits (volume × rate) over a list of ratios, so effects can be combined.\n- **Watch the interactions.** Raising price may lower volume; higher utilisation may lower yield. Note the trade-offs on the tree.\n- **Don't drown the tree.** If 60 leaves have owners, nobody will look. Keep the operational detail in sub-trees per function.\n- **Use the tree to kill vanity metrics.** A metric that doesn't connect to the tree doesn't belong on the top dashboard.\n\n## Red flags\n- Splits that don't multiply or add up; \"efficiency\" as a leaf with no formula.\n- Leaves without owners or data.\n- No reconciliation to actuals.\n- Targets set top-down per leaf with no feasibility check.\n- Only lagging financial metrics.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Manufacturing:** OEE (availability × performance × quality), yield, specific energy consumption, conversion cost per tonne, inventory days.\n- **Retail / consumer:** footfall × conversion × basket; gross margin by category; shrinkage; rent and staff per square foot.\n- **Banking / NBFC:** NII = average assets × NIM; credit cost; cost-to-income; fee income per customer; ROA and ROE trees.\n- **Healthcare:** occupancy × ARPOB × length of stay; case mix; staff per occupied bed; payer mix.\n- **SaaS:** ARR = customers × ARPA; net revenue retention; CAC payback; gross margin after hosting.\n- **Shared services / GCC:** cost per transaction = (people + tech + overhead) ÷ volume; first-time-right; automation rate; SLA adherence.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Driver Tree: [top metric], [business], baseline [period]\n\n[Indented tree with formulas]\n- ROCE (18.2%) = EBIT ÷ Capital employed\n  - EBIT (₹...) = Revenue − Costs\n    - Revenue = Volume × Net price\n      - Volume = Capacity × Utilisation\n  ...\n\n| Leaf lever | Formula link | Current | Target | Owner | Lead/Lag | Data / frequency | Impact on top metric of a realistic change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Reconciliation to actuals:** ...\n**Top 5 levers by impact:** ...\n**Trade-offs to manage:** ...\n**Dashboard and review rhythm:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Every split is an arithmetic identity; the tree reconciles to actuals.\n- [ ] Leaves are controllable levers with single owners and data sources.\n- [ ] Sensitivities computed with code and ranked.\n- [ ] Leading indicators are identified.\n- [ ] Targets add up and are checked for feasibility.\n- [ ] Trade-offs between levers are noted.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `kpi-dashboard`: Design a one-page dashboard leaders will actually use: right metrics, targets, layout and alerts.\n- `issue-tree`: Break a big question into a logical tree of smaller questions you can actually answer.\n- `cost-takeout`: Build a structured cost-reduction plan: baseline, levers, sizing, phasing and risks to service.\n- `benefits-tracker`: Make sure promised benefits are defined, owned, measured and actually delivered.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Return-on-investment decomposition** (return = margin × asset turnover, extended into profitability trees): a classic management-accounting and corporate-finance technique.\n- **Value-based management and economic profit** (operating profit − capital charge): corporate-finance curricula.\n- **Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE)** = availability × performance × quality: standard in manufacturing and lean practice; see ISO 22400 (KPIs for manufacturing operations management).\n- **Leading vs lagging indicators:** standard performance-management practice, including balanced-scorecard-style approaches.\n- **Sector metrics** (e.g. specific heat consumption in cement, NIM in banking, ARPOB in hospitals): use industry-association definitions and your regulator's reporting definitions for consistency."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional cement plant, 3.0 MTPA clinker capacity, FY baseline)\n\n```\nEBITDA / tonne (₹1,050)  = Net realisation/t − Variable cost/t − Fixed cost/t\n├─ Net realisation/t (₹5,200)  = Gross price × (1 − trade discounts %) − freight-out/t\n│   ├─ Gross price (₹5,600/t)              Owner: Head Sales\n│   ├─ Trade discounts (5.4%)              Owner: Head Sales        [lead: weekly discount report]\n│   └─ Freight-out (₹96/t)  = lead distance × ₹/t-km               Owner: Head Logistics\n├─ Variable cost/t (₹3,280)\n│   ├─ Power & fuel/t (₹1,650) = heat consumption (kcal/kg clinker) × fuel cost (₹/kcal) × clinker factor\n│   │   │                        + power (kWh/t) × tariff (₹/kWh)\n│   │   ├─ Heat consumption (735 kcal/kg)   Owner: Plant Head        [lead: daily kiln data]\n│   │   ├─ Fuel mix: alternative fuel share (8%)  Owner: AFR Manager\n│   │   ├─ Power (72 kWh/t cement)          Owner: Electrical Head\n│   │   └─ Clinker factor (0.70)            Owner: Quality Head\n│   ├─ Raw materials/t (₹620)               Owner: Mines Head\n│   └─ Packing & other variable (₹1,010)    Owner: Packing Head\n└─ Fixed cost/t (₹870) = Fixed cost ÷ volume; volume = capacity × utilisation (78%)\n    ├─ Kiln availability (88%) × run-rate (92% of rated)   Owner: Plant Head\n    └─ Fixed cost (₹/yr)                                  Owner: Plant Controller\n```\n(Fuel cost is shown per kcal so the heat-consumption lever is multiplicative.)\n\n**Reconciliation:** the leaves recreate FY EBITDA within 0.8% of reported (the difference is inventory change and one-offs).\n\n## Lever sensitivity (realistic change → EBITDA/t; computed with code; illustrative)\n| Lever | Realistic change | ΔEBITDA/t | Annual Δ (₹ Cr, at 3.2 Mt cement) | Owner | Controllability |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Trade discounts | −0.5 pt | +₹28 | +9.0 | Head Sales | Medium |\n| Heat consumption | 735 → 715 kcal/kg | +₹24 | +7.7 | Plant Head | High |\n| Utilisation | 78% → 82% (fixed-cost absorption) | +₹42 | +13.4 | Plant Head / Sales | Medium |\n| Alternative fuel share | 8% → 15% | +₹30 | +9.6 | AFR Manager | Medium |\n| Clinker factor | 0.70 → 0.68 (more blending) | +₹22 | +7.0 | Quality Head | High (within standards) |\n| Power | 72 → 69 kWh/t | +₹19 | +6.1 | Electrical Head | High |\n| Freight lead distance | −10 km | +₹14 | +4.5 | Logistics | Medium |\n\n**Trade-offs:**\n- Higher utilisation may require selling into farther markets (more freight) or at higher discounts.\n- Alternative fuels can raise heat consumption.\n- The clinker factor is constrained by product standards and customer specifications.\n\n**Review rhythm:**\n- Daily: heat, power, kiln availability.\n- Weekly: discounts, dispatches, lead distance.\n- Monthly: EBITDA/t bridge."}]}, {"id": "kpi-dashboard", "pack": "numbers", "title": "KPI Dashboard", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Design a one-page dashboard leaders will actually use: right metrics, targets, layout and alerts.", "tryp": "Design a monthly executive dashboard for a 300-bed hospital's CEO.", "when": ["Setting up management or board reporting", "Replacing a 40-page pack nobody reads", "Tracking a transformation programme"], "inputs": ["Audience and decisions the dashboard supports", "Candidate metrics and data available", "Frequency"], "pairs": ["kpi-driver-tree", "chart-selection", "exhibit-builder", "steerco-update"], "body": "# KPI Dashboard\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a one-page dashboard leaders actually use. It shows what's on track, what isn't and why, and what is being done about it, with definitions precise enough that nobody argues about the numbers.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Decisions, not data.** Start from the 3-5 decisions the audience takes with it; every metric must inform one.\n2. **Few metrics, well defined.** 8-15 at the top level. Each has a formula, source, owner, frequency and a definition that's signed off.\n3. **Balance lag and lead.** Outcomes (revenue, EBITDA, NPS) plus leading drivers (pipeline, utilisation, defect rate) that predict them.\n4. **Thresholds trigger action.** Green, amber and red are defined in advance, linked to tolerances and escalation, not set by mood.\n5. **Exceptions first.** The eye should land on what needs attention, not on 30 green tiles.\n6. **Narrative and ownership.** Each red or amber has a \"why\" and an owner with an action and a date.\n7. **Trustworthy data.** Lineage, refresh time and data-quality flags are visible. A dashboard nobody trusts is worse than none.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Audience (board, ExCo, business unit, programme SteerCo), frequency, and the meeting where it's used.\n- The decisions and questions they repeatedly ask.\n- The existing reports and their pain (too long, late, contradictory).\n- Data sources, latency and quality; tool constraints.\n- The driver tree or strategy objectives to link to.\n\n## Method\n1. **Decisions and questions:** list them; map each to candidate metrics.\n2. **Select metrics** using the driver tree:\n   - 3-5 outcomes;\n   - 4-8 leading drivers;\n   - 1-3 health or risk indicators (e.g. safety, compliance, cyber);\n   - a stakeholder metric if relevant (customers, people).\n   Drop anything that doesn't inform a decision.\n3. **Define each metric:** name, business definition, formula (numerator / denominator, inclusions and exclusions), unit, source system, refresh frequency and latency, owner, data steward, and known caveats.\n4. **Targets and thresholds:**\n   - target from the plan;\n   - green within tolerance;\n   - amber when outside tolerance but recoverable;\n   - red when breached or the trend is off-track.\n   Consider trend-based rules (e.g. 3 consecutive declines = amber).\n5. **Visual choice per metric:**\n   - bullet chart (actual vs target);\n   - line with target band for trends;\n   - sparkline in tiles;\n   - sorted bar for comparisons across units;\n   - small multiples for many units.\n   Avoid gauges, 3D, pie charts with many slices, and dual axes unless essential.\n6. **Layout:**\n   - **Top:** headline status and exceptions.\n   - **Middle:** outcomes with trends.\n   - **Bottom:** drivers by theme.\n   - **Side:** commentary (what changed, why, action, owner, date).\n   - One screen or page; drill-downs elsewhere.\n7. **Data and governance:** lineage map; data-quality checks; \"as of\" timestamp; a change process for definitions; access controls.\n8. **Rhythm:** who reviews which tiles, when, and how actions are tracked to closure. Retire metrics that never drive action.\n9. **Mock-up:** produce a layout sketch or, if tools allow, a working prototype with sample data clearly marked.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Board vs management.** Boards need fewer, more strategic and risk-oriented metrics, with trends and peer context. Management needs operational drivers at higher frequency.\n- **Targets can distort.** Pair metrics that can be gamed (e.g. handling time with quality; sales volume with margin).\n- **Normalise for comparability:** per unit, per FTE, per ₹ revenue; adjust for seasonality when comparing months.\n- **Red is useful.** If everything is always green, the thresholds are too loose or the metrics are wrong.\n- **Show uncertainty** for forecasts (ranges), not just point estimates.\n\n## Red flags\n- 40+ metrics; no definitions; numbers differ from finance reports.\n- Thresholds missing, or changed after the fact.\n- No commentary or owners for reds.\n- Gauges and traffic lights without trends.\n- Data refreshed manually days late, with no timestamp.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board:** strategy KPIs, financial headline, risk-appetite metrics, people and culture, ESG, major programme status. Quarterly, with a trend over 5-8 quarters.\n- **ExCo / monthly business review:** P&L bridge, drivers, forecast vs plan, top risks, initiatives.\n- **Operations:** daily or shift metrics: throughput, quality, safety, backlog.\n- **Programme / transformation:** milestones, benefits realised vs plan, adoption, risks, budget.\n- **AI programme:** value realised, adoption, model performance and drift, incidents, cost per transaction.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## KPI Dashboard: [audience], [frequency]\n**Decisions supported:** 1. ... 2. ...\n\n| Metric | Definition / formula | Source | Owner | Frequency | Target | Green / Amber / Red rule | Visual | Lead/Lag |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Layout sketch:**\n[Row 1: status summary + top 3 exceptions]\n[Row 2: outcome tiles with trend and target band]\n[Row 3: driver tiles grouped by theme]\n[Right: commentary - what changed, why, action, owner, date]\n\n**Data lineage and quality:** ...\n**Review rhythm and action tracking:** ...\n**Metrics retired from the old pack (and why):** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Each metric maps to a decision; 8-15 metrics at the top level.\n- [ ] Precise definitions with formulas, sources and owners.\n- [ ] Thresholds defined in advance, with gaming-prone metrics paired.\n- [ ] Lead and lag balance; links to the driver tree.\n- [ ] The layout puts exceptions first; chart types fit the data.\n- [ ] Data lineage, timestamp and review rhythm included.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `kpi-driver-tree`: Break a top metric into the drivers people can act on, with owners and targets.\n- `chart-selection`: Pick the right chart for the message, and avoid charts that confuse or mislead.\n- `exhibit-builder`: Design a complete, board-ready exhibit: title, chart, callouts, source, and the code to draw it.\n- `steerco-update`: Write a crisp steering committee update: status, decisions needed, risks, and next steps.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught practice)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Information-design principles:** maximise the data-to-ink ratio; prefer position and length encodings (bars, lines, bullet charts) over angle and area (gauges, pies); show trends and targets; use small multiples for comparisons. These are standard in data-visualisation curricula.\n- **Performance management:** leading and lagging indicators; balanced-scorecard-style coverage of financial, customer, process and people perspectives; target-setting and tolerance practice.\n- **Data quality and governance:** ISO 8000 (data quality); data-lineage and metric-definition (semantic layer) practices.\n- **ISO 22400:** KPIs for manufacturing operations; healthcare quality indicators from national accreditation standards (e.g. NABH in India) for clinical metric definitions.\n- **Board reporting:** risk-appetite metrics and governance reporting (see the `risk-appetite` and `board-paper` skills)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional 300-bed multi-specialty hospital, monthly CEO dashboard)\n\n**Decisions supported:**\n1. Where to add or shift capacity (beds, OT slots, doctors).\n2. Which service lines need commercial or clinical intervention.\n3. Whether quality or safety issues need escalation to the board.\n4. Cash and payer actions.\n\n| Metric | Definition / formula | Source | Owner | Freq. | Target | G / A / R rule | Visual | Lead/Lag |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | Net inpatient + outpatient revenue (after payer discounts) | ERP | CFO | Monthly | Plan | G ≥98% of plan; A 95-98%; R <95% | Bullet + 13-mo line | Lag |\n| EBITDA margin | EBITDA ÷ net revenue | ERP | CFO | Monthly | 18% | G ≥17.5; A 16-17.5; R <16 | Line with band | Lag |\n| Bed occupancy | Occupied bed-days ÷ available bed-days | HIS | COO | Weekly | 78% | G 75-85; A 70-75 or >88; R <70 | Line | Lead |\n| ARPOB | Inpatient revenue ÷ occupied bed-days | HIS + ERP | CFO | Monthly | ₹52k | G ≥plan; A −3%; R −6% | Line | Lag |\n| Average length of stay | Inpatient days ÷ discharges (excl. day care) | HIS | Medical Director | Monthly | 4.2 days | G ≤4.3; A 4.3-4.6; R >4.6 | Line, by specialty small multiples | Lead |\n| OT utilisation | Used OT minutes ÷ scheduled OT minutes | OT system | COO | Weekly | 80% | G ≥78; A 70-78; R <70 | Bar by theatre | Lead |\n| Referral inflow | New referrals from network doctors | CRM | Head Marketing | Weekly | 1,100/mo | Trend: 3 declines = A | Sparkline | Lead |\n| Patient experience | Net promoter score, inpatient discharge survey | Survey tool | Head Patient Experience | Monthly | 55 | G ≥55; A 45-55; R <45 | Line | Lag |\n| Hospital-acquired infection rate | HAIs per 1,000 patient-days | Infection control | Medical Director | Monthly | ≤1.5 | G ≤1.5; A 1.5-2.0; R >2.0 | Line + count | Lag (safety) |\n| Serious safety events | Count of sentinel / serious events | Incident system | Medical Director | Monthly | 0 | Any = R and immediate CEO notice | Count tile | Lag (safety) |\n| Insurer receivable days | Insurer receivables ÷ insurer revenue × 30 | ERP | CFO | Monthly | 45 | G ≤45; A 45-55; R >55 | Bar by payer | Lead (cash) |\n| Claim denial rate | Denied ÷ submitted claims (value) | RCM system | Head RCM | Monthly | ≤4% | G ≤4; A 4-6; R >6 | Bar by payer | Lead |\n| Nurse attrition (annualised) | Leavers ÷ average headcount × 12 | HRMS | CHRO | Monthly | ≤22% | G ≤22; A 22-28; R >28 | Line | Lead |\n\n**Paired to prevent gaming:**\n- Length of stay with 30-day readmission rate (in the drill-down).\n- OT utilisation with surgical-site infection rate.\n\n**Layout:**\n- **Row 1:** status summary (3 R, 4 A, 6 G) and the top 3 exceptions with owners.\n- **Row 2:** revenue, EBITDA margin, occupancy, ARPOB.\n- **Row 3:** clinical (ALOS, HAI, safety), operations (OT, referrals), cash (receivables, denials), people (attrition).\n- **Right panel:** commentary, e.g. \"Denial rate 7.1% (R): one insurer changed pre-auth rules on 1 Aug. Action: payer-specific checklist plus training; Head RCM; 30 Sep.\"\n\n**Data:** \"As of\" timestamp; HIS and ERP reconciled monthly; definitions owned by the CFO and the Medical Director; any change logged.\n\n**Rhythm:**\n- Weekly operations huddle on lead metrics.\n- Monthly CEO review.\n- Quarterly board version: 8 metrics, 8-quarter trends, plus quality and safety detail."}]}, {"id": "cost-takeout", "pack": "numbers", "title": "Cost Take-out", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Build a structured cost-reduction plan: baseline, levers, sizing, phasing and risks to service.", "tryp": "We need to cut ₹40 Cr (8%) from a ₹500 Cr cost base in a retail chain over 18 months without hurting customer experience. Build the plan.", "when": ["Margin pressure or budget cuts", "Post-merger synergy plans", "Zero-based budgeting"], "inputs": ["Cost baseline (by category, function or site)", "Target and timeframe", "Things that are off-limits"], "pairs": ["value-chain", "kpi-driver-tree", "benefits-tracker", "change-plan"], "body": "# Cost Take-out\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a cost programme that delivers real, lasting savings without damaging what drives revenue, quality or safety. Every rupee is traceable from idea to the budget line where it is banked.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Baseline before ideas.** A reconciled, agreed cost baseline (ties to the P&L) by category, function, site and driver. Arguments about savings are usually arguments about the baseline.\n2. **Cut costs by managing the drivers, not by percentage.** Across-the-board cuts punish the efficient and return within 18 months. Address demand (what we do), price (what we pay), process (how we do it), structure (who and where) and technology (automation and AI).\n3. **Protect what grows the business.** Explicitly ring-fence spend that drives revenue, quality, safety, compliance and critical capability.\n4. **Run-rate vs in-year vs cost-to-achieve.** Distinguish annualised run-rate savings, savings realised this year, and one-off costs (severance, systems, consultants).\n5. **Pipeline above target.** Aim for 120-130% of target identified, because slippage is normal.\n6. **Bank it.** Savings count only when removed from budgets or forecasts, with Finance validating them. Otherwise costs creep back.\n7. **People with dignity.** Where roles are affected, follow legal process, consult early, prefer redeployment and natural attrition, and communicate honestly.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Target (₹ and %), timeframe, and why (margin, competitiveness, funding growth, a crisis).\n- Cost base: P&L by nature and function; headcount by function and location; third-party spend by category and supplier; capex.\n- Benchmarks available (internal across units, external).\n- Constraints and protected areas; labour law and union context; contracts with lock-ins.\n- Governance: sponsor, PMO, Finance validation.\n\n## Method\n1. **Baseline:** reconcile to the P&L; segment by function, category, site and controllability; identify cost drivers (volume, headcount, complexity, policy).\n2. **Diagnose:** benchmarks (cost as % revenue, per unit, per FTE, spans and layers); internal variation between units; spend cube (category × supplier × business unit); activity analysis for support functions.\n3. **Generate levers** across five types:\n   - **Demand:** stop or reduce activities, reports, SKUs, service levels, policies (travel, fleet), and complexity.\n   - **Price:** renegotiate, consolidate suppliers, specification changes, should-cost models, payment terms.\n   - **Process:** lean redesign, standardisation, first-time-right, self-service.\n   - **Structure:** spans and layers, location (shared services or GCC), outsourcing or insourcing, footprint consolidation.\n   - **Technology and AI:** automation, AI-assisted work, decommissioning legacy systems, cloud cost optimisation.\n   - **Zero-based challenge** for discretionary categories: rebuild from need, not last year.\n4. **Size each initiative:** formula and assumptions; run-rate saving; in-year saving by month; cost-to-achieve; owner; dependencies; risk to revenue, quality, safety and people.\n5. **Prioritise:** value, ease, time and risk; quick wins (0-3 months), medium (3-12), structural (12+).\n6. **Pipeline management:** stage gates (L0 idea → L1 validated → L2 approved plan → L3 implemented → L4 banked in budget), each with defined evidence; weekly tracking.\n7. **People and change:** impact assessment; legal and HR process; consultation; redeployment; communications; retention of critical talent.\n8. **Lock in:** budget reductions; policy changes; headcount controls; supplier-contract changes; KPIs per initiative; post-programme review at 12 months to check for cost creep.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Cost-to-serve thinking.** Sometimes the biggest saving is in complexity (SKUs, customer customisations, report requests), which needs commercial decisions, not just operations.\n- **Price levers are fast but finite;** structural and process levers are slower but durable. Balance the portfolio.\n- **Avoid cutting maintenance, safety, cyber or compliance** to hit a number. These risks surface later at a higher cost.\n- **Use savings to fund growth** where possible (\"fuel for growth\"). It helps morale and strategy.\n- **Measure the unintended effects:** service levels, employee engagement, attrition of key people, customer complaints.\n\n## Red flags\n- No reconciled baseline; savings measured against the budget rather than actuals.\n- Across-the-board % cuts.\n- No cost-to-achieve, or severance excluded.\n- Savings \"identified\" but never removed from budgets.\n- Initiatives with no owners; pipeline at 100% of target or less.\n- Revenue-generating or safety-critical spend cut without assessment.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Manufacturing:** conversion cost, yield, energy, maintenance strategy, procurement of raw materials, logistics.\n- **Services / IT / GCC:** pyramid and span optimisation, location strategy, automation and AI, vendor rationalisation, cloud FinOps.\n- **Retail:** store labour scheduling, shrinkage, rent renegotiation, supply-chain network, assortment.\n- **Banks / insurers:** branch network, digital migration, operations centres, technology run-cost, procurement.\n- **Post-merger:** overlap elimination, systems consolidation, footprint, procurement scale. Track synergies separately from base-business savings.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Cost Take-out Programme: target ₹... (..%) by [date]\n\n### Baseline (reconciled to P&L)\n| Category / function | Cost | % of total | Controllable? | Key driver | Benchmark gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Initiatives\n| # | Initiative | Lever type | Sizing logic | Run-rate saving | In-year | Cost-to-achieve | Owner | Stage (L0-L4) | Risk (revenue / quality / people) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Pipeline vs target:** identified ...% | validated ...% | banked ...%\n**Protected spend and why:** ...\n**Phasing:** quick wins / medium / structural\n**People impact and process:** ...\n**Lock-in mechanisms:** ...\n**Unintended-effect KPIs:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The baseline is reconciled to the P&L, with drivers.\n- [ ] Levers span demand, price, process, structure and technology.\n- [ ] Each initiative is sized by formula, with run-rate, in-year and cost-to-achieve.\n- [ ] Protected spend is explicit; risks assessed.\n- [ ] Pipeline ≥120% of target, with stage gates and owners.\n- [ ] Banking and lock-in mechanisms; people process addressed.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not investment, tax, valuation or accounting advice. Models and calculations are unaudited; have material numbers independently checked before decisions or disclosure.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `value-chain`: Map where value and cost sit across the chain, and find where to compete, cut or integrate.\n- `kpi-driver-tree`: Break a top metric into the drivers people can act on, with owners and targets.\n- `benefits-tracker`: Make sure promised benefits are defined, owned, measured and actually delivered.\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts and public guidance; verify legal specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Zero-based budgeting:** a long-established budgeting method, rebuilding budgets from activities and needs rather than prior-year baselines.\n- **Activity-based costing and cost-to-serve analysis:** management-accounting fundamentals.\n- **Spend analysis and category management:** standard procurement practice (spend cube; should-cost modelling; supplier consolidation).\n- **Organisation design:** spans of control and layers; the principles of shared-services and global-capability-centre location strategy.\n- **Lean process improvement:** waste elimination, standard work, first-time-right.\n- **India employment law** (verify with counsel and HR): Industrial Relations Code 2020 and applicable state rules on retrenchment, closure, notice and compensation; Shops and Establishments Acts; contract labour rules. Other jurisdictions have their own consultation and redundancy laws.\n- **Accounting for restructuring costs:** Ind AS 37 / IAS 37 provisions; confirm with Finance and auditors."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional apparel retail chain, ₹500 Cr operating cost base, target ₹40 Cr (8%) in 18 months)\n\n## Baseline (FY, reconciled to P&L; ₹ Cr)\n| Category | Cost | % | Controllable? | Key driver | Benchmark gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Store staff | 145 | 29% | Yes | Hours scheduled vs footfall | Hours per ₹ lakh sales 12% above best quartile of own stores |\n| Rent and CAM | 120 | 24% | Partly (renewals) | Area, rates | 18 stores above 14% rent-to-sales |\n| Logistics and warehousing | 70 | 14% | Yes | Network, replenishment frequency | Cost per unit +9% vs internal target |\n| Marketing | 55 | 11% | Yes | Channel mix | Paid social ROI below threshold for 30% of spend |\n| HQ support functions | 60 | 12% | Yes | Spans, layers, processes | Avg span 4.1 vs 6-8 norm |\n| Technology | 30 | 6% | Yes | Licences, legacy systems | 3 overlapping POS / ERP contracts |\n| Utilities and other | 20 | 4% | Partly | Energy, maintenance | — |\n\n## Initiatives (extract)\n| # | Initiative | Type | Sizing logic | Run-rate | In-year | Cost-to-achieve | Owner | Stage | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Labour scheduling to footfall | Process + tech | 6% of 9,000 store-hours/day × ₹190/hr × 360 | 3.7 | 2.0 | 1.5 (tool + training) | COO | L2 | Queue times: track checkout wait <4 min |\n| 2 | Renegotiate or relocate 18 high-rent stores at renewal | Price / structure | Avg −15% on ₹26 Cr rent | 3.9 | 1.2 | 0.8 (fit-out for 4 relocations) | Head Real Estate | L1 | Footfall loss on relocation |\n| 3 | Close 6 loss-making stores (after a 9-month turnaround test) | Structure | Store-level EBITDA losses | 4.5 | 2.0 | 3.0 (lease exits, severance) | CEO | L1 | Brand presence; staff redeployed to nearby stores |\n| 4 | Warehouse consolidation (3 → 2) and twice-weekly replenishment | Structure + process | Lease + handling + freight | 6.0 | 2.5 | 4.0 | Head Supply Chain | L1 | Stock-outs: track availability ≥96% |\n| 5 | Shift underperforming paid social to CRM and owned channels | Demand / price | 30% of ₹22 Cr social × 50% cut | 3.3 | 2.5 | 0.2 | CMO | L2 | Sales impact: run a holdout test first |\n| 6 | HQ spans and layers (5 → 4 layers; spans 4 → 6) | Structure | 38 roles via attrition and redeployment | 5.7 | 2.0 | 2.5 (severance where needed) | CHRO | L1 | Key-talent retention; legal process |\n| 7 | AI-assisted merchandising and allocation reduces markdowns | Technology | Markdown 14% → 12.5% of ₹1,600 Cr sales (gross-margin benefit, reported separately) | (margin) | — | 1.8 | CMO / Head Merchandising | L1 | Model accuracy; pilot in 2 categories |\n| 8 | Technology contract consolidation | Price / tech | Decommission 2 overlapping systems | 2.4 | 0.6 | 1.2 | CIO | L2 | Migration risk |\n| 9 | Energy: LED + HVAC scheduling | Process | 18% of ₹14 Cr power | 2.5 | 1.5 | 2.0 | Head Facilities | L2 | — |\n| 10 | Indirect procurement (packaging, security, housekeeping) | Price | 8% on ₹40 Cr | 3.2 | 2.0 | 0.3 | CPO | L1 | Service levels |\n\n**Pipeline:** ₹35.2 Cr cost run-rate identified (**88% of target**), plus the markdown margin benefit (reported separately). **Gap to a 120% pipeline (₹48 Cr): ~₹13 Cr.** Candidates:\n- zero-based review of HQ discretionary spend and travel policy (est. ₹4-5 Cr);\n- packaging specification changes (₹2-3 Cr);\n- store maintenance and security contract re-tender (₹2 Cr);\n- the next wave of rent renewals (₹3-4 Cr).\n\n**Protected:** store experience in the top 40 stores, e-commerce growth marketing, IT security, fire and safety maintenance.\n\n**Banking:** savings are removed from FY budgets on reaching L3. Finance validates monthly. Headcount controls on HQ roles. A 12-month review checks for cost creep.\n\n**Unintended-effect KPIs:** checkout wait time, stock availability, NPS, regretted attrition, like-for-like sales."}]}, {"id": "scqa-storyline", "pack": "story", "title": "SCQA Storyline", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Build the storyline every executive document needs: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer.", "tryp": "Build an SCQA storyline: we want approval to consolidate 4 regional data centres into one hybrid cloud setup.", "when": ["Starting any deck, memo, board paper or speech", "Fixing a draft that is 'all data, no message'", "Preparing a 2-minute verbal summary"], "inputs": ["Topic and audience", "Your recommendation (or findings so far)", "Key facts"], "pairs": ["pyramid-structure", "executive-summary", "ghost-deck", "audience-brief"], "body": "# SCQA Storyline\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a storyline that gets an executive to \"yes\" quickly. The context is shared, the tension is clear, the answer is unambiguous, and three solid reasons hold it up, before a single slide is designed.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Story before slides.** Agree the storyline with the sponsor before building exhibits. It saves days of rework.\n2. **The situation is uncontroversial.** It states facts the audience already accepts, so they nod.\n3. **The complication creates tension** with a number or trigger: what changed, what's at risk, what happens if we do nothing.\n4. **The question is the one the audience is now asking,** usually \"What should we do?\" or \"How do we…?\"\n5. **The answer is an action,** specific enough to approve, with scale and timing.\n6. **The key-line holds the answer up.** 3-5 supporting points, each a full sentence, MECE, and each provable with evidence.\n7. **Order for the audience.** Executives usually want the answer first (A-S-C or A then the key-line). Sceptical or unfamiliar audiences need more S-C before the answer.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Audience and their current beliefs (use `audience-brief`).\n- The recommendation (or current best hypothesis) and the decision needed.\n- Key evidence available.\n- The format: a 2-minute verbal, a 1-page memo, a 10-slide deck, a board paper.\n\n## Method\n1. **Draft the answer first**, in one sentence: action + scale + timing (+ cost or benefit).\n2. **Situation:** 1-2 sentences of accepted context, briefly; avoid history lessons.\n3. **Complication:** 1-2 sentences, with the trigger or tension and a number.\n4. **Question:** the natural question raised (may stay implicit in the final document).\n5. **Key-line:** 3-5 reasons why the answer is right, typically covering why (it solves the problem), why this option (vs the alternatives), why it's feasible or affordable, and why now or how risk is managed. Write each as a sentence with a number.\n6. **Check the logic:**\n   - Does S → C → Q flow naturally?\n   - Does the answer answer Q?\n   - Are the key-line points MECE and sufficient?\n   - Can each be proven with available evidence?\n7. **Choose the ordering** for the audience; draft the opening paragraph accordingly.\n8. **Produce:** a one-line headline; a 30-second verbal version; a section or slide outline mapping the key-line to sections.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Complication vs crisis.** Overstating the complication damages credibility. Use the real number.\n- **Multiple complications** usually mean multiple storylines. Pick the one tied to the decision.\n- **When the answer is unwelcome,** spend more time on S-C to build shared understanding, and acknowledge the costs openly in the key-line.\n- **For discussion papers** (no recommendation yet), the \"answer\" is the framing of options and the decision to be made, not a pretend conclusion.\n\n## Red flags\n- A situation that reads like a company history.\n- A complication with no number or trigger.\n- An answer that is a topic (\"Our digital strategy\").\n- Key-line points that overlap, or that are activities rather than reasons.\n- The storyline drafted after the slides are built.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board paper:** answer-first executive summary; key-line becomes the paper's sections.\n- **Investor pitch:** situation = market; complication = unmet need or inefficiency; answer = company and plan; key-line = traction, model, team, use of funds.\n- **Bad news / incident update:** answer = what we're doing; key-line = what happened, impact, fix, prevention.\n- **Change communication to employees:** situation = shared reality; complication = why staying the same isn't an option; answer = the change; key-line = what it means for you, support, timeline.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Storyline: [topic]\n**Headline (one line):** ...\n\n**Situation:** ...\n**Complication:** ...\n**Question:** ...\n**Answer:** ...\n\n**Key-line**\n1. [Reason, with number]\n2. ...\n3. ...\n\n**Ordering for this audience:** answer-first / S-C-A - why\n**30-second verbal version:** ...\n**Section / slide outline:** key-line point → sections or slides\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The situation is uncontroversial; the complication has a number or trigger.\n- [ ] The answer is an action with scale and timing.\n- [ ] Key-line points are full sentences, MECE, evidence-backed, 3-5 of them.\n- [ ] The ordering suits the audience; the verbal version fits 30 seconds.\n- [ ] The outline maps the key-line to sections.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `pyramid-structure`: Structure any argument top-down: answer first, grouped support, evidence below.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n- `ghost-deck`: Draft the whole deck as titles and sketched exhibits before doing the analysis, so the work stays focused.\n- `audience-brief`: Profile who you are persuading: what they care about, what they already believe, and what would change their mind.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Situation-Complication-Question-Answer (SCQA):** a standard introduction structure taught in executive-writing and consulting communication training.\n- **Answer-first (top-down) communication:** pyramid-style structuring of arguments; \"bottom line up front\" as used in military and government writing guidance.\n- **Narrative structure:** classical story arcs (context, tension, resolution) applied to business communication.\n- **Plain-language guidance:** public plain-language writing standards (e.g. ISO 24495-1:2023, Plain language: governing principles and guidelines)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional manufacturer, data-centre consolidation)\n\n**Headline:** consolidate four ageing data centres into one hybrid-cloud platform to avoid a ₹48 Cr refresh and halve outage risk.\n\n- **Situation:** our four regional data centres have run core systems reliably for over a decade.\n- **Complication:** three of the four reach end-of-life in 2027, needing ₹48 Cr of hardware refresh. Unplanned outages have doubled in two years (14 in FY26), each costing an estimated ₹35 lakh in lost production.\n- **Question:** how do we avoid this spend and reduce outage risk without disrupting plants?\n- **Answer:** approve ₹31 Cr to consolidate into one hybrid-cloud platform over 24 months, keeping plant control systems on-site.\n\n**Key-line:**\n1. It costs less: 5-year total cost is ₹12 Cr (est.) lower than refreshing, including migration and dual running.\n2. It is more resilient: a multi-zone design targets 99.95% availability vs 99.6% today.\n3. It is safe for plants: production control systems stay on-site, and cutovers are scheduled in planned shutdowns.\n4. Risks are controlled: data stays in Indian regions; workloads are portable to avoid lock-in; there is an exit plan.\n\n**Ordering:** answer-first for the ExCo. For the audit and risk committee, open with the complication (outage trend), then the answer, and lead the key-line with point 4.\n\n**30-second version:** \"Three of our four data centres need a ₹48 Cr refresh next year, and outages have doubled. We propose ₹31 Cr to move to one hybrid-cloud platform over two years. It's cheaper over five years, more resilient, and plant systems stay on-site. We're asking for approval today.\"\n\n**Slide outline:**\n1. Exec summary.\n2. Situation and complication (outage trend, refresh cost).\n3. Option comparison.\n4. Cost (point 1).\n5. Resilience (2).\n6. Plant safety (3).\n7. Risk controls (4).\n8. Plan and ask."}]}, {"id": "pyramid-structure", "pack": "story", "title": "Pyramid Structure", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Structure any argument top-down: answer first, grouped support, evidence below.", "tryp": "Restructure this draft email to the CEO into a pyramid: [paste draft].", "when": ["Organising any recommendation or report", "Restructuring a long or rambling draft", "Preparing for a tough Q&A"], "inputs": ["The draft, notes or findings", "The main message (or let the skill infer it)"], "pairs": ["scqa-storyline", "mece-check", "executive-summary", "executive-email"], "body": "# Pyramid Structure\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** writing that a busy reader understands in the first paragraph and can trust in the detail. Every section answers the question raised by the one above it, and nothing is out of place.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **One governing thought.** If you can't state the main point in one sentence, the thinking isn't finished.\n2. **Vertical logic:** each level answers the \"why?\" or \"how?\" raised by the level above.\n3. **Horizontal logic:** siblings are MECE, at the same level of abstraction, and in a deliberate order (time, structure or importance).\n4. **Inductive by default.** Independent reasons that each support the point are robust to one being challenged. Use deductive chains (fact → implication → therefore) sparingly; they break if one link fails.\n5. **Summarise, don't list.** Each grouping's heading states the insight of the group, not its category (\"Costs will rise 12% because of three drivers\", not \"Cost analysis\").\n6. **Evidence at the base.** Data, examples and sources sit under the claims they support.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Audience and purpose (decide, inform, persuade).\n- The draft or notes; the intended main message, if known.\n- Constraints (length, format, house style).\n\n## Method\n1. **Extract all points** from the draft or notes, one idea per line.\n2. **Find the governing thought:** ask \"What single thing must the reader remember or decide?\" Write it as a sentence.\n3. **Group the points** into 3-5 clusters. For each, write the summary insight: what do these points together say?\n4. **Check grouping logic:**\n   - **Inductive:** each group independently supports the governing thought.\n   - **Deductive:** the groups form a chain. Is each link sound?\n5. **Order siblings:**\n   - **Chronological:** steps, phases.\n   - **Structural:** parts of a whole, e.g. by region.\n   - **By importance:** most important first.\n6. **Check MECE and abstraction** within each group; fix overlaps or mixed levels.\n7. **Place evidence** under each claim; flag claims with no evidence.\n8. **Test with the reader's questions:** the top answers \"what?\", the next level \"why?\" or \"how?\". Any unanswered obvious question means a missing branch.\n9. **Rewrite** in pyramid order:\n   - an opening that states the governing thought (often via SCQA);\n   - sections in key-line order, with message headings;\n   - evidence in the body or annexes.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Bad news or sensitive content:** still lead with the point, but provide context and empathy in the first lines; don't bury it.\n- **Discussion documents:** the governing thought can be \"We need to choose between A and B, and the key trade-off is X.\"\n- **Emails:** the pyramid compresses to the first line (the point or ask), 2-4 bullets (reasons), then details.\n- **Over-structuring:** short documents don't need four levels. Match depth to length.\n\n## Red flags\n- Chronological narration of what the team did (\"First we analysed…\").\n- Section headings that are labels (\"Background\", \"Analysis\").\n- More than 5-7 points in a group.\n- Mixed levels (\"Revenue grew 9%\" alongside \"We need a new strategy\").\n- A conclusion appearing only at the end.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board paper:** the governing thought is the recommendation and resolution; sections follow the key-line.\n- **Status report:** the governing thought is overall status plus decisions needed; groups are achievements, issues and next steps.\n- **Analytical report:** the governing thought is the main finding; groups are supporting findings, each with evidence.\n- **Speech or verbal briefing:** the same pyramid, with signposting (\"Three reasons. First…\").\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Pyramid: [document]\n**Governing thought:** ...\n├─ 1. [Claim, as an insight sentence]\n│   ├─ evidence\n│   └─ evidence\n├─ 2. [Claim]\n└─ 3. [Claim]\n\n**Logic type:** inductive / deductive - why  |  **Order:** chronological / structural / importance\n**Gaps found:** ...\n\n### Rewritten version\n[Opening paragraph with the governing thought]\n[Sections with message headings]\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] One governing thought, in a single sentence.\n- [ ] Groups are MECE, at the same abstraction, deliberately ordered.\n- [ ] Headings state insights, not labels.\n- [ ] Evidence sits under each claim; unsupported claims are flagged.\n- [ ] The rewritten text leads with the point.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `scqa-storyline`: Build the storyline every executive document needs: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer.\n- `mece-check`: Audit any list, structure or storyline for overlaps and gaps, then fix it.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n- `executive-email`: Write a short email or message that gets a senior person to read, decide and reply.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Pyramid-style, top-down structuring:** a governing thought supported by grouped, MECE arguments with inductive or deductive logic. A standard executive-writing method in consulting and business-school communication training.\n- **\"Bottom line up front\" (BLUF):** government and military writing guidance.\n- **Plain-language standards:** ISO 24495-1:2023 (plain language) and public-sector plain-language guidelines.\n- **Logic:** inductive vs deductive reasoning, from introductory logic and critical-thinking curricula."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative): restructuring an email to the CEO\n\n**Original draft (chronological):**\n\"Over the last month the team reviewed the Brazil launch plan. We met the regulator's consultants, who mentioned that approval timelines have been extended. We also heard that our distributor may be acquired. Finance re-ran the numbers… We therefore think it might be better to consider pausing.\"\n\n**Governing thought:** pause the Brazil launch for six months, to protect ₹15 Cr of launch investment from avoidable risk.\n\n```\nPause the Brazil launch for six months\n├─ 1. Regulatory approval is likely to slip to Q3 (not Q1)\n│   ├─ Consultants report the backlog doubled since the new rules\n│   └─ Two peers' approvals took 9-11 months this year\n├─ 2. Our distributor is being acquired, so terms may change\n│   ├─ Acquisition announced 3 Oct; closing expected Q2\n│   └─ The acquirer has its own competing brand\n└─ 3. A pause costs < ₹2 Cr vs ₹15 Cr at risk\n    ├─ Holding costs: team and storage ₹1.6 Cr\n    └─ Launching without approval or distribution risks the full launch spend\n```\n\n**Logic:** inductive (three independent reasons). **Order:** importance.\n\n**Rewritten email:**\n\n> **Subject:** Recommend pausing the Brazil launch for six months (decision by Friday)\n>\n> I recommend we pause the Brazil launch for six months. It protects the ₹15 Cr launch investment for under ₹2 Cr in holding costs.\n>\n> - **Approval will likely slip to Q3:** the regulator's backlog has doubled, and two peers waited 9-11 months.\n> - **Our distributor is being acquired:** closing is expected in Q2, and the acquirer has a competing brand, so our terms may change.\n> - **The cost of pausing is small:** ₹1.6 Cr in holding costs, vs ₹15 Cr at risk.\n>\n> If you agree, we'll inform the Brazil team Monday and return in April with a revised plan, including a backup distributor."}]}, {"id": "executive-summary", "pack": "story", "title": "Executive Summary", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.", "tryp": "Write an executive summary of this 30-page vendor assessment for our CIO: [paste or attach].", "when": ["Any document longer than 3 pages", "Board or ExCo pre-reads", "Handing over findings to a busy sponsor"], "inputs": ["The source material", "Audience and the decision they own"], "pairs": ["scqa-storyline", "board-paper", "decision-memo", "insight-synthesis"], "body": "# Executive Summary\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** one page that lets a senior leader understand, decide and act without reading the rest, while being honest about what could go wrong.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Answer and ask first.** The first two lines tell the reader what you recommend or found, and what you need from them, by when.\n2. **Only what changes the decision.** Background, method and history only if they change trust in the answer.\n3. **Numbers make points credible.** Each supporting point carries a figure (₹, %, time, count) and its basis.\n4. **Candour.** State the main risk, uncertainty or trade-off, and what you're doing about it. Readers distrust summaries with no downside.\n5. **Stand-alone.** Someone who reads only this page must be able to act correctly.\n6. **Length discipline:** half a page for an update, one page for a major decision. Not a table of contents in prose.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Reader(s) and the decision or action you need.\n- The source material, and its key findings and recommendations.\n- Sensitivities (confidential numbers, politics).\n- Format conventions (board template, investment-committee memo, client report).\n\n## Method\n1. **Identify the one message:** the recommendation or headline finding, plus the ask.\n2. **Select the 3-5 supporting points** that most justify it; each a sentence with a number and source.\n3. **Name the main risk or trade-off,** and the mitigation or the condition attached.\n4. **Next steps:** who, what, by when; include the decision date.\n5. **Draft** in this order: answer and ask → why (supporting points) → risk / trade-off → next steps.\n6. **Cut:** remove adjectives, methodology, history, hedging. Keep each sentence under ~25 words where possible.\n7. **Stand-alone test:** read it as the decision-maker who sees nothing else. Could you decide? What would you ask? Add that answer.\n8. **Consistency check:** every number matches the main document.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Ranges beat false precision** when uncertainty is high (\"₹40-55 Cr\"), but say what drives the range.\n- **Bad news first** in incident or underperformance summaries, followed by the response.\n- **Several recommendations?** Number them by priority; consider whether they belong in separate decisions.\n- **For information-only summaries,** still state the \"so what\" and any implications for decisions coming up.\n\n## Red flags\n- The answer appears in the last paragraph.\n- No numbers; adjectives such as \"significant\" and \"robust\".\n- No risks or trade-offs.\n- More than one page, or a list of what the report contains.\n- Numbers that differ from the main document.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board pre-read:** purpose label, resolution sought, key risks and conflicts (see `board-paper`).\n- **Investment committee:** returns vs hurdle, key sensitivities, deal terms, conditions, red flags from diligence.\n- **Programme update:** RAG status with reason, decisions needed, top risks, next milestones.\n- **Client report:** business impact first; implementation implications; investment and benefits.\n- **Research or due diligence:** key findings, confidence level, implications, open questions.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Executive Summary: [title]\n**Recommendation / finding and ask:** ... [decision needed by ...]\n\n**Why**\n- [Point with number and basis]\n- ...\n- ...\n\n**Main risk / trade-off:** ... → mitigation / condition: ...\n\n**Next steps:** who - what - when\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Answer and ask are in the first two lines.\n- [ ] 3-5 supporting points, each with a number.\n- [ ] The main risk or trade-off is stated, with a response.\n- [ ] Next steps have owners and dates.\n- [ ] ≤1 page; stands alone; numbers match the source.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `scqa-storyline`: Build the storyline every executive document needs: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer.\n- `board-paper`: Write a board paper directors can read in 10 minutes: purpose, recommendation, analysis, risks and the resolution.\n- `decision-memo`: Write a crisp memo that frames a decision, the options, the recommendation and what is being asked.\n- `insight-synthesis`: Turn analysis outputs into true insights: what it means, why it matters, what to do.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Answer-first executive writing:** pyramid-style structure and \"bottom line up front\", as taught in business-communication curricula.\n- **Plain language:** ISO 24495-1:2023 and public plain-language guidelines (short sentences, active voice, concrete numbers).\n- **Board and committee paper conventions:** company-secretarial guidance (see the `board-paper` skill).\n- **Investment-committee memo practice:** returns vs hurdle, risks, conditions (see the `business-case` skill)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional bank, core-banking vendor assessment, 30-page report)\n\n## Executive Summary: core banking platform selection\n**Recommendation and ask:** select Vendor B for the core banking upgrade, and approve ₹62 Cr (7-year total cost: ₹148 Cr) by 15 December so contracting can complete before the current vendor's support ends in March 2028.\n\n**Why:**\n- **Lowest 7-year total cost:** ₹148 Cr vs ₹162 Cr (Vendor A) and ₹171 Cr (Vendor C), including licences, implementation, infrastructure and run costs (Finance-validated model, Annex 3).\n- **Proven at our scale in India:** live at 3 banks of similar size. Reference calls confirmed on-time go-lives and stable month-end processing (Annex 5).\n- **Fastest migration:** an 18-month plan vs 27 months for A, so the old platform is retired 9 months sooner (saving ₹6 Cr of dual running).\n- **Strongest regulatory fit:** native support for current RBI reporting formats and data localisation. The other two need customisation.\n\n**Main risk / trade-off:** Vendor B's local support team is smaller (40 vs 120 at A).\n→ **Mitigation:** contracted service levels with financial penalties; a dedicated on-site team of 8 during migration; escrow of the source code.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- CIO: complete negotiations by 30 Nov.\n- General Counsel: contract and escrow terms by 10 Dec.\n- Board approval on 15 Dec.\n- Programme mobilisation from January."}]}, {"id": "insight-synthesis", "pack": "story", "title": "Insight Synthesis", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Turn analysis outputs into true insights: what it means, why it matters, what to do.", "tryp": "Here are 15 observations from our sales analysis. Turn them into the 4-5 real insights for the leadership team.", "when": ["After analysis, before building slides", "When a team has findings but no 'so what'", "Preparing a board narrative from data"], "inputs": ["Observations, findings or charts", "The decision or question"], "pairs": ["research-synthesis", "action-titles", "scqa-storyline", "exhibit-builder"], "body": "# Insight Synthesis\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** five or fewer insights that change what leaders do, each stated in one sentence with a number, a cause, a consequence and an implied action, rather than 30 observations restating the charts.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **An observation is not an insight.** \"Sales fell 6% in West\" is an observation. \"West's 6% drop is entirely from two distributors who lost credit lines; restoring terms recovers about ₹11 Cr a quarter\" is an insight: cause, impact and action.\n2. **So what, now what.** Every insight answers why it matters (money, risk, customers, strategy) and what should be done.\n3. **Root causes over symptoms.** Merge observations that share a cause; the cause is the insight.\n4. **Quantify.** Size the impact or opportunity, with a range if uncertain.\n5. **Confidence is explicit.** State the evidence strength and what would change the view.\n6. **Fewer, stronger.** Rank by impact on the decision. Park the rest.\n7. **Surprising and true beats obvious.** Insights that overturn an assumption are the most valuable, and need the strongest evidence.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision or question the analysis serves.\n- The analyses done and the observations generated (charts, tables, interview themes).\n- The audience's current beliefs (what would be surprising?).\n\n## Method\n1. **List observations** (what the data shows), one per line, with their source.\n2. **Ask \"why?\"** for each, until you reach a plausible cause supported by evidence; link related observations.\n3. **Cluster** by shared cause or theme.\n4. **For each cluster, write:**\n   - **What:** the pattern, with a number.\n   - **So what:** the impact on the decision, money, risk or customers.\n   - **Now what:** the implied action.\n5. **Quantify** impact: size the prize or the problem (₹, %, customers, time). Use code for arithmetic.\n6. **Rate confidence:** high (multiple consistent sources, causal evidence), medium, or low (single source, correlation only). Note the test that would raise confidence.\n7. **Rank** by impact × confidence × decision relevance. Keep the top 3-5.\n8. **Write headline statements** (≤25 words): subject + finding + consequence. These become action titles.\n9. **Park** remaining observations for the appendix, with a one-line reason.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Correlation vs cause.** Where causality is uncertain, say \"is associated with\" and propose a test (A/B, natural experiment, interviews).\n- **Averages hide insight.** Look at distributions, segments and outliers; the insight often lives in a sub-segment.\n- **Counter-intuitive findings** need extra validation before you present them. They are high-value but high-risk.\n- **Don't force an action** where the right response is \"investigate further\". Say what to investigate and why.\n\n## Red flags\n- Insights that restate a chart title.\n- No numbers; no consequence; no action.\n- Too many \"insights\" (10+).\n- Causal claims from correlations without caveats.\n- Insights not linked to the decision.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Customer research:** insights about needs and behaviours, with segment sizes and implications for offers.\n- **Financial performance:** variance drivers with a bridge, the controllable vs uncontrollable split, and actions.\n- **Operational analytics:** bottlenecks and failure modes, with capacity or cost impact.\n- **Employee surveys:** drivers of engagement or attrition, by segment, with action areas.\n- **Board narrative:** strategic insights tied to strategy, risk and performance.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Insights: [topic / decision]\n\n| # | Insight headline (≤25 words) | What (evidence) | So what (impact, quantified) | Now what (action) | Confidence | Test to raise confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Parked observations (appendix):** observation - reason parked\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Each insight has what, so what and now what.\n- [ ] Root causes are identified; related observations merged.\n- [ ] Impact is quantified, with arithmetic done in code.\n- [ ] Confidence and causal caveats are stated.\n- [ ] 3-5 insights, ranked; the rest parked.\n- [ ] Headlines are usable as action titles.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `research-synthesis`: Turn piles of notes, reports and links into a few clear findings with evidence and implications.\n- `action-titles`: Rewrite slide titles so each one states the takeaway, and the titles alone tell the story.\n- `scqa-storyline`: Build the storyline every executive document needs: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer.\n- `exhibit-builder`: Design a complete, board-ready exhibit: title, chart, callouts, source, and the code to draw it.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **\"What, so what, now what\":** a reflective-analysis structure widely used in management education and analytics.\n- **Root-cause analysis:** \"5 whys\" and cause-and-effect techniques from quality management.\n- **Causal inference basics:** correlation vs causation, confounders, experiments and quasi-experiments, as taught in statistics and analytics curricula.\n- **Analytical storytelling:** turning findings into decision-oriented messages (see `scqa-storyline` and `action-titles`)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional FMCG company, quarterly sales review with 15 observations)\n\n**Observations (extract):**\n1. West region sales −6% QoQ.\n2. Two West distributors' orders −40%.\n3. Those distributors' credit lines were reduced in July.\n4. Modern trade +12%.\n5. E-commerce +25%, but returns rose to 9%.\n6. Premium SKU share +3 pts.\n7. Rural general trade flat.\n8. Promotions spend +18%.\n9. Promotion ROI below 1.0 on 40% of schemes.\n…\n\n| # | Insight headline | What | So what | Now what | Confidence | Test |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | West's 6% decline comes entirely from two distributors whose credit was cut, not from consumer demand | Obs 1-3; retail offtake data in West flat | ~₹11 Cr per quarter of lost sell-in; shelves at risk of stock-outs | Restore credit with security (bank guarantee) or appoint an interim distributor | High (offtake data confirms demand) | — |\n| 2 | 40% of promotion schemes lose money; reallocating them would fund growth without extra spend | Obs 8-9; scheme-level ROI analysis | ₹14 Cr of the ₹35 Cr promotion budget has ROI < 1.0 | Stop or redesign 18 schemes; shift funds to modern-trade visibility (ROI 1.6) | Medium (ROI method excludes halo effects) | Holdout test in 2 states |\n| 3 | E-commerce growth is real, but returns are eroding a third of its margin gain | Obs 5; returns by SKU | 9% returns cost ~₹4 Cr per quarter; concentrated in 3 SKUs with packaging damage | Fix packaging on the 3 SKUs; returns target 5% | High | — |\n| 4 | Premiumisation is working in metros and can extend to Tier-2 modern trade | Obs 4, 6; Tier-2 basket data | Premium margin is 8 pts higher; Tier-2 modern-trade premium share is half the metro level | Pilot a premium range in 300 Tier-2 stores | Medium | Pilot |\n\n**Parked:** rural flat (in line with the category; no specific action); new-SKU launches on plan."}]}, {"id": "action-titles", "pack": "story", "title": "Action Titles", "time": "5 min", "summary": "Rewrite slide titles so each one states the takeaway, and the titles alone tell the story.", "tryp": "Rewrite these slide titles as action titles: 1) Revenue overview 2) Customer survey results 3) Cost analysis 4) Next steps.", "when": ["Every deck before review", "When a deck feels descriptive, not persuasive", "Turning an analysis deck into a decision deck"], "inputs": ["Current slide titles (and ideally slide content)", "The overall message of the deck"], "pairs": ["ghost-deck", "slide-copy-editor", "deck-quality-check", "exhibit-builder"], "body": "# Action Titles\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a deck whose titles alone tell the story, so a busy executive can skim the titles in a minute and know the argument, with each slide's evidence proving its title.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Title = takeaway.** A full sentence with a subject, a verb and a so-what. Not a topic (\"Market overview\").\n2. **Numbers where possible.** \"Revenue grew 9%, but all of it came from price\" beats \"Revenue growth was price-led\".\n3. **Titles prove the story horizontally.** Read in sequence, titles should form the storyline (SCQA plus key-line) with no gaps or leaps.\n4. **The body proves the title vertically.** If the chart doesn't show it, change the title or the chart. No overclaiming.\n5. **One message per slide.** Two messages means two slides, or one that isn't clear yet.\n6. **Short and plain:** ~15 words, two lines maximum; no undefined acronyms; active voice.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The deck, or its slide list with content.\n- The storyline (answer and key-line). If none exists, draft one first (see `scqa-storyline`).\n- Audience vocabulary and sensitivity (e.g. a regulator or client).\n\n## Method\n1. **Extract** the current titles and summarise each slide's actual content in one line.\n2. **For each slide,** identify the single takeaway the evidence supports, and why it matters to the storyline.\n3. **Write the action title:** [subject] [finding with a number] [implication]. Examples:\n   - \"Three product lines earn 81% of profit on 46% of revenue.\"\n   - \"Delaying six months costs ₹40 lakh a month in lost margin.\"\n4. **Calibrate the claim** to the evidence strength (\"suggests\" or \"indicates\" for directional evidence; firm verbs for strong evidence).\n5. **Horizontal read:** list all titles in order. Check the flow (situation → complication → answer → support → next steps), and fix gaps, repetition and leaps.\n6. **Vertical check:** does the body prove the title? Note required changes to exhibits.\n7. **Flag slides** whose title can't be written (no message) → cut, merge or move to the appendix.\n8. **Section dividers** carry message headlines too (\"Three reasons to act now\").\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Sensitive audiences:** keep titles factual and non-inflammatory (\"Complaint volumes rose 30% after the policy change\" rather than \"The policy change failed customers\").\n- **Appendix slides** can have descriptive titles; main-deck slides cannot.\n- **Agenda and summary slides** are the exceptions; the executive summary slide has the governing thought as its title.\n- **Avoid cleverness.** Puns and rhetorical questions slow comprehension.\n\n## Red flags\n- Topic labels (\"Financial analysis\", \"Next steps\").\n- Titles over three lines, or full of acronyms.\n- Titles that claim more than the chart shows.\n- A horizontal read that doesn't make sense.\n- Multiple messages packed into one title.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board decks:** titles linked to the decision and risks; conservative claims.\n- **Client or consulting decks:** titles carry the recommendation logic; each section opens with a summary title.\n- **Investor decks:** titles show traction and economics with numbers.\n- **Internal updates:** status-oriented titles (\"Migration is 3 weeks late because of data quality; recovery plan needs ₹45 lakh\").\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Action Titles: [deck]\n\n| # | Old title | New action title | Body supports it? (change needed) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Storyline read (titles only):**\n1. ...\n2. ...\n\n**Slides to cut / merge / move to appendix:** ...\n**Exhibit changes needed:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Every main-deck title is a sentence with a so-what, ≤ ~15 words, numbers where possible.\n- [ ] Claims are calibrated to the evidence.\n- [ ] The titles alone tell the storyline without gaps.\n- [ ] Slides with no message are flagged.\n- [ ] Needed exhibit changes are listed.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ghost-deck`: Draft the whole deck as titles and sketched exhibits before doing the analysis, so the work stays focused.\n- `slide-copy-editor`: Tighten slide text: fewer words, clearer claims, consistent style, no jargon.\n- `deck-quality-check`: A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.\n- `exhibit-builder`: Design a complete, board-ready exhibit: title, chart, callouts, source, and the code to draw it.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Action titles / \"headline\" slide titles:** standard practice in consulting and executive presentation training; research on assertion-evidence slide design shows better comprehension when titles state a full-sentence claim supported by visual evidence.\n- **Horizontal and vertical logic in decks:** structured-communication practice (see `pyramid-structure` and `scqa-storyline`).\n- **Plain language:** ISO 24495-1:2023 principles."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; quarterly performance deck for an ExCo)\n\n| # | Old title | New action title | Body supports it? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Executive summary | Q3 profit is 4% below plan; pricing and two plant issues explain all of it, and both are fixable by Q1 | Yes |\n| 2 | Revenue overview | Revenue grew 9%, but all growth came from price; volumes fell 3% | Add a volume-price bridge |\n| 3 | Customer survey results | Customers rate us best on quality but worst on delivery reliability, which drives 60% of switching intent | Yes (driver analysis on slide) |\n| 4 | Cost analysis | Two plant outages added ₹14 Cr of cost; underlying unit cost is on plan | Split one-offs vs underlying in the chart |\n| 5 | Competitor update | A new entrant is undercutting us by 8% in two regions, but has won only 3% share so far | Add a share trend |\n| 6 | Market trends | (no clear message: background only) | **Move to appendix** |\n| 7 | Initiatives | Delivery reliability programme can recover 2 points of share by Q2 for ₹6 Cr | Needs a sizing exhibit |\n| 8 | Next steps | Three decisions today secure ₹20 Cr of FY27 profit | Yes: list the decisions |\n\n**Storyline read:**\n1. Q3 profit is 4% below plan, and fixable.\n2. Growth was all price; volume fell.\n3. Delivery reliability is our weakness and drives switching.\n4. The cost overrun is one-off.\n5. A new entrant is a contained threat.\n6. The delivery programme recovers share.\n7. Three decisions today.\n\n**Exhibit changes:** add a volume-price bridge (slide 2); split one-offs (slide 4); add a share trend (slide 5); add a sizing waterfall (slide 7)."}]}, {"id": "ghost-deck", "pack": "story", "title": "Ghost Deck", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Draft the whole deck as titles and sketched exhibits before doing the analysis, so the work stays focused.", "tryp": "Build a ghost deck for a board presentation recommending entry into the Middle East market for our edtech company.", "when": ["Early in a project, to focus analysis", "Planning any important deck", "Aligning a team on what each person produces"], "inputs": ["The storyline or hypothesis", "Audience and length limit"], "pairs": ["scqa-storyline", "action-titles", "engagement-workplan", "exhibit-builder"], "body": "# Ghost Deck\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** the whole deck on one page before anyone builds a slide. Analysis is focused on what the story needs, data gaps are visible early, and the team knows exactly which exhibit each person is producing.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Hypothesis titles.** Write titles as you expect the answer to be. Mark them as hypotheses and change them as evidence arrives. It's a plan, not a conclusion.\n2. **Exhibit sketch per slide.** Describe the chart that would prove the title (type, axes, comparison, highlight) and the data it needs. This turns the storyline into a work plan.\n3. **Story discipline.** Sections follow the storyline; each slide earns its place; the main deck fits the length limit (e.g. 10-15 slides for an ExCo).\n4. **Visible gaps.** Status per slide (data in hand / data requested / hypothesis only) shows risk to the timeline.\n5. **Iterate.** Update the ghost deck weekly; it becomes the final deck.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The key question, storyline or hypothesis tree.\n- Audience, length limit and format.\n- The team and timeline (for owners and dates).\n\n## Method\n1. **Sections from the storyline:**\n   - executive summary;\n   - situation and complication;\n   - findings (one section per key-line point);\n   - options and recommendation;\n   - plan, risks and the ask;\n   - appendix.\n2. **Slide list:** for each, a draft action title (hypothesis); flag uncertain ones.\n3. **Exhibit sketch:**\n   - chart type;\n   - x and y axes or table columns;\n   - comparison (vs plan, peers, time, segments);\n   - what to highlight;\n   - source.\n4. **Data needed and status;** link to the data request list.\n5. **Owner and due date** per slide.\n6. **Main deck vs appendix:** keep the main deck within the limit; move supporting detail to the appendix.\n7. **Read the titles only:** does the story hold? Fix gaps.\n8. **Review with the sponsor** early. Agreeing the ghost deck aligns expectations before analysis effort is spent.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **If a hypothesis title turns out wrong, celebrate.** Change the title and the story; that's the method working.\n- **Keep alternative storylines in mind** when evidence is mixed. Sketch the slide you'd show if the opposite were true.\n- **Paper or whiteboard first** for team sessions. Fast iteration beats polished software early.\n\n## Red flags\n- Slides with topic titles and no exhibit sketch.\n- A main deck of 40 slides.\n- No status or owner; data needs discovered late.\n- The ghost deck never updated after week 1.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board decks:** shorter (8-12 slides) with a paper; the ghost deck aligns with the paper's sections.\n- **Due diligence reports:** sections by diligence area; red-flag slides first.\n- **Investor pitches:** fixed narrative arc (problem, solution, market, traction, model, team, ask).\n- **Workshops:** the ghost deck includes the exercise slides and the expected outputs.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Ghost Deck: [title], [audience], max [n] slides\n\n| # | Section | Draft action title (hypothesis) | Exhibit sketch (type; axes; comparison; highlight) | Data needed | Status | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Appendix slides:** ...\n**Titles-only read:** ...\n**Biggest data risks:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Sections follow the storyline; the main deck is within the length limit.\n- [ ] Every slide has a hypothesis action title and an exhibit sketch.\n- [ ] Data needs, status, owners and dates are present.\n- [ ] The titles-only read tells the story; data risks are flagged.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `scqa-storyline`: Build the storyline every executive document needs: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer.\n- `action-titles`: Rewrite slide titles so each one states the takeaway, and the titles alone tell the story.\n- `engagement-workplan`: Turn the issue tree into a week-by-week plan: analyses, owners, data, meetings and deliverables.\n- `exhibit-builder`: Design a complete, board-ready exhibit: title, chart, callouts, source, and the code to draw it.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Storyboarding / ghost decks:** standard practice in consulting and corporate strategy teams for hypothesis-driven deck planning.\n- **Assertion-evidence slide design:** full-sentence claims supported by visual evidence.\n- **Hypothesis-driven problem solving:** linking analyses to expected answers (see `hypothesis-tree`, `engagement-workplan`)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; board deck recommending edtech market entry into the Gulf, max 12 slides)\n\n| # | Section | Draft action title (hypothesis) | Exhibit sketch | Data needed | Status | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Exec summary | Enter the UAE and Saudi K-12 tutoring market via a school partnership model; break-even in year 3 on ₹85 Cr | Text + key numbers box | All | Hypothesis | Lead | Wk 5 |\n| 2 | Situation | Gulf private K-12 spend grows ~8% a year, twice India's rate | Bar: spend 2021-2028 by country; CAGR labels | Govt education statistics; operator filings | Requested | Analyst A | Wk 2 |\n| 3 | Complication | Our India growth is slowing to 6%; we need a second engine by 2028 | Line: our growth vs plan | Internal | In hand | Analyst B | Wk 1 |\n| 4 | Market | English-medium supplementary learning is a ~$1.1B (est.) market with no clear leader | Market map: segments × players; share bars | Market sizing; competitor scan | Hypothesis | Analyst A | Wk 3 |\n| 5 | Customer | Parents value curriculum alignment and Arabic-English bilingual support above price | Ranked bar: purchase drivers (survey n=400) | Survey | Fieldwork in progress | Research lead | Wk 4 |\n| 6 | Right to win | Our adaptive-learning content and cost base give a 30% price advantage at equal quality | Comparison table vs 3 competitors | Benchmarks; content audit | Partial | Product lead | Wk 3 |\n| 7 | Entry options | A school partnership beats direct-to-consumer and acquisition on risk-adjusted value | Options table: NPV, time, risk, capital | Business case | Hypothesis | Strategy lead | Wk 4 |\n| 8 | Economics | Break-even in year 3; ₹85 Cr peak funding | Cash-flow curve; sensitivity tornado | Model | Building | Finance | Wk 4 |\n| 9 | Risks | Regulatory licensing and localisation are the key risks; both are mitigated by the partner model | Risk table | Legal advice | Requested | Legal | Wk 4 |\n| 10 | Plan and ask | Approve ₹25 Cr for phase 1 (UAE, 20 partner schools) with a gate to Saudi in 18 months | Timeline with gates | — | Hypothesis | Lead | Wk 5 |\n\n**Appendix:** sizing method; survey details; competitor profiles; model assumptions.\n\n**Titles-only read:** the Gulf grows fast → we need a new engine → an open market → clear customer needs → we can win → partnership is the best route → economics work → risks are manageable → approve phase 1.\n\n**Biggest data risks:** government statistics in a comparable format (slide 2); survey completion by week 4 (slide 5)."}]}, {"id": "chart-selection", "pack": "story", "title": "Chart Selection", "time": "5 min", "summary": "Pick the right chart for the message, and avoid charts that confuse or mislead.", "tryp": "What's the best chart to show how our cost per claim compares across 12 regions and against the target?", "when": ["Making any chart", "Fixing a chart that confuses people", "Building a dashboard"], "inputs": ["The message you want to show", "The data (shape and size)"], "pairs": ["exhibit-builder", "kpi-dashboard", "action-titles", "survey-analysis"], "body": "# Chart Selection\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** the chart that makes your point obvious at a glance and can't be accused of misleading, with a precise build specification anyone can follow.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Message first.** Write the one-sentence takeaway before choosing the chart. The chart exists to prove it.\n2. **Match the relationship to the form:**\n   - **Comparison or ranking:** sorted bar (horizontal for long labels).\n   - **Change over time:** line (many periods) or column (few periods).\n   - **Part-to-whole:** stacked bar or 100% bar; a pie only for 2-3 parts.\n   - **Bridge or variance:** waterfall.\n   - **Distribution:** histogram or box plot.\n   - **Correlation:** scatter (bubble size for a third variable).\n   - **Deviation from target:** bullet chart or diverging bar.\n   - **Flow:** flow diagram.\n   - **Geography:** map, only if location matters.\n3. **Perception hierarchy.** People judge position and length more accurately than angle, area or colour intensity. Prefer bars and lines.\n4. **Honesty.**\n   - Bar charts start at zero.\n   - Axes are consistent across comparable charts.\n   - No 3D.\n   - No truncated axes that exaggerate.\n   - Dual axes only when essential, and clearly labelled.\n5. **Focus.** Grey for context; one accent colour for the point; direct labels instead of legends; annotate the key number.\n6. **Accessibility.** Colour-blind-safe palettes; never rely on colour alone; readable font sizes.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The message (one sentence) and the audience.\n- The data: number of categories, periods, series; units; precision.\n- The medium: slide, report, dashboard, mobile.\n\n## Method\n1. **Write the message.**\n2. **Classify the relationship** (list above).\n3. **Choose the chart,** and one alternative with the trade-off.\n4. **Specify:**\n   - orientation;\n   - axes and scales (start at zero for bars; log only if needed and labelled);\n   - sort order (by value, unless there is a natural order);\n   - what is highlighted;\n   - labels (direct, with rounded numbers);\n   - title (the message);\n   - subtitle (units, period);\n   - source line;\n   - annotations.\n5. **Check for misleading risks:** truncation, cherry-picked periods, mismatched scales, area distortions.\n6. **Simplify:** remove gridlines, borders, legends where direct labels work, and decimal noise.\n7. **Describe the final chart precisely** so it can be built in any tool.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Tables beat charts** when readers need exact values or look up individual numbers.\n- **Small multiples** beat one crowded chart for many series or units.\n- **Pie charts** are acceptable for one dominant part vs the rest. Avoid them for comparisons.\n- **Show uncertainty** (ranges, error bars, forecast bands) when decisions depend on it.\n- **Indexing** (base = 100) helps compare growth of differently sized series. Say so in the subtitle.\n\n## Red flags\n- Chart chosen before the message.\n- Pie with 6+ slices; 3D; gauges.\n- Rainbow colours; legends far from the data.\n- Bar axis not starting at zero.\n- Dual axes implying false correlation.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board:** fewer, simpler charts; trends with targets; risk heat maps used sparingly.\n- **Dashboards:** consistent chart types per metric type; sparklines; bullet charts.\n- **Analytical reports:** distributions and scatter plots to show variation, not just averages.\n- **Mobile or narrow formats:** horizontal bars; fewer categories; large labels.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Chart Recommendation\n**Message:** ...\n**Relationship:** ...\n**Chart:** ... (alternative: ... - trade-off)\n**Spec:**\n- Orientation / axes / scale: ...\n- Sort: ...\n- Highlight: ...\n- Labels / annotations: ...\n- Title: \"...\" | Subtitle: units, period | Source: ...\n**Avoid:** ...\n**Misleading-risk check:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The message is written first; the relationship is classified.\n- [ ] The chart matches the relationship; an alternative is considered.\n- [ ] Honest scales; zero baseline for bars; no 3D.\n- [ ] One accent colour; direct labels; a message title; a source.\n- [ ] Accessibility considered.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `exhibit-builder`: Design a complete, board-ready exhibit: title, chart, callouts, source, and the code to draw it.\n- `kpi-dashboard`: Design a one-page dashboard leaders will actually use: right metrics, targets, layout and alerts.\n- `action-titles`: Rewrite slide titles so each one states the takeaway, and the titles alone tell the story.\n- `survey-analysis`: Turn survey results into a few significant, decision-relevant insights with honest caveats.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Graphical perception research:** the experimentally established ranking of visual encodings (position along a common scale is read most accurately; angle, area and colour saturation least).\n- **Information-design principles:** data-to-ink ratio, small multiples, avoiding chart junk; standard in data-visualisation curricula.\n- **Accessibility:** WCAG 2.2 contrast guidance; colour-vision-deficiency-safe palettes; not relying on colour alone.\n- **Statistical graphics conventions:** zero baselines for bars, consistent scales, showing uncertainty."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked examples (illustrative)\n\n**1. Cost per claim across 12 regions vs target**\n- **Message:** \"Four of 12 regions are above target cost per claim; the East is 28% above.\"\n- **Relationship:** comparison plus deviation from target.\n- **Chart:** horizontal bar, sorted high to low, with a vertical target line at ₹1,850.\n  *Alternative:* a diverging bar of the % deviation from target (sharper on deviation, but loses the absolute values).\n- **Spec:** x-axis ₹ from 0; the 4 regions above target in the accent colour, the rest grey; value labels at bar ends; \"Target ₹1,850\" labelled on the line.\n- **Title:** the message. **Subtitle:** \"Average cost per motor claim, H1 FY27, ₹\". **Source:** claims system.\n- **Avoid:** a map (region size distorts perception); a pie.\n\n**2. Revenue change from FY26 to FY27**\n- **Message:** \"Price added ₹38 Cr, but volume lost ₹21 Cr and mix lost ₹6 Cr.\"\n- **Chart:** waterfall, from the FY26 bar → price (+) → volume (−) → mix (−) → FY27 bar. Positive steps in one colour, negative in another (shape plus label, not colour alone).\n\n**3. Customer satisfaction vs response time across 40 branches**\n- **Message:** \"Branches answering within 2 minutes score 15 points higher on satisfaction.\"\n- **Chart:** scatter, with response time on x and satisfaction on y; a trend line with R² noted; the 5 outlier branches labelled.\n- **Caveat in the subtitle:** \"association, not proof of cause\"."}]}, {"id": "exhibit-builder", "pack": "story", "title": "Exhibit Builder", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Design a complete, board-ready exhibit: title, chart, callouts, source, and the code to draw it.", "tryp": "Build an exhibit from this data showing that 3 product lines generate 80% of our profit: [paste table].", "when": ["Turning data into a slide visual", "Rebuilding a messy chart", "Making charts consistent across a deck"], "inputs": ["The data", "The message", "Brand colours or template, if any"], "pairs": ["chart-selection", "action-titles", "deck-quality-check", "insight-synthesis"], "body": "# Exhibit Builder\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a finished exhibit that a senior reader trusts and understands in five seconds: a correct calculation, the right chart, a clear title, focused highlights, and a source line.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Numbers are right.** Recompute from the source data in code; reconcile totals to known figures (P&L, reported KPIs).\n2. **One message per exhibit,** stated as the action title.\n3. **Chart fits the message** (see `chart-selection`).\n4. **Callouts guide the eye:** 1-3 annotations pointing to the key number or change.\n5. **Consistency across the deck:** units, decimals, colours, fonts, period labels, sort conventions.\n6. **Provenance:** source line, date or period, definitions and estimates footnoted.\n7. **Print and accessibility test:** readable in black and white and by colour-blind readers; legible when projected.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The data (file or table), the message, the audience, and the template or brand colours if any.\n- Calculations needed (shares, growth, indices, bridges, averages, cumulative).\n- Output format: image, chart code, or a spec for a designer.\n\n## Method\n1. **Load and check the data:** totals, missing values, outliers, units, period definitions.\n2. **Compute** the derived metrics in code (e.g. share of profit, CAGR, contribution to change); keep a calculation note.\n3. **Confirm the message** against the results. If the data doesn't support the intended title, change the title.\n4. **Choose the chart** and specify the layout: orientation, sort, scale, highlight colour, grey context.\n5. **Title and subtitle:** the action title (≤15 words); the subtitle with units, period and scope.\n6. **Callouts:** label the key bar or point with its value; add a bracket or arrow for differences; an annotation for an event.\n7. **Formatting:**\n   - rounded numbers (e.g. ₹ Cr to 1 decimal, % to whole numbers unless small);\n   - consistent thousand separators (Indian lakh/crore or international, as the audience expects);\n   - remove clutter.\n8. **Source and footnotes:** data source, extraction date, definitions, estimates marked \"(est.)\".\n9. **Produce:** generate the exhibit (image or chart code) if tools are available; otherwise a build spec with exact values.\n10. **QA:** numbers vs source; title vs visual; consistency with other exhibits; accessibility.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Rounding can change stories** (e.g. 49.6% shown as 50%). Check that rounded figures still support the title.\n- **Indian vs international number formats:** use lakh/crore for Indian audiences and millions/billions for global ones. Never mix them in one deck.\n- **Annotate events** (price change, outage, policy) on time series. It prevents misreading.\n- **When data quality is weak,** say so in a footnote and soften the title.\n\n## Red flags\n- Totals that don't match reported figures.\n- A title claiming more than the visual shows.\n- Inconsistent colours or units across exhibits.\n- No source line.\n- A chart produced by a tool's defaults (legend, gridlines, 3D, rainbow).\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board exhibits:** fewer callouts; trend plus target; conservative claims.\n- **Due diligence:** data-room source references per exhibit; normalisation adjustments shown.\n- **Dashboards:** standard templates; automated refresh; consistent scales.\n- **Public reports:** stricter sourcing; accessibility; alt text.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Exhibit: [id]\n**Title:** ...\n**Subtitle:** units, period, scope\n**Chart:** type and spec (or generated image / code)\n**Key values:** ...\n**Callouts:** ...\n**Source:** ... | **Notes:** definitions, estimates\n**Calculation note:** how the derived figures were computed\n**QA:** reconciled to [figure] ✓ | title supported ✓ | consistent formats ✓\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Numbers are computed in code and reconciled to the source.\n- [ ] The title states the message and is supported by the visual.\n- [ ] Chart choice and design follow good practice; 1-3 callouts.\n- [ ] Source, period, units and footnotes are present.\n- [ ] Consistent formats; readable in black and white.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `chart-selection`: Pick the right chart for the message, and avoid charts that confuse or mislead.\n- `action-titles`: Rewrite slide titles so each one states the takeaway, and the titles alone tell the story.\n- `deck-quality-check`: A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.\n- `insight-synthesis`: Turn analysis outputs into true insights: what it means, why it matters, what to do.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Graphical perception and information design:** see the `chart-selection` sources.\n- **Assertion-evidence slide design:** claim titles supported by visual evidence.\n- **Accessibility:** WCAG 2.2; providing text alternatives for charts in digital reports.\n- **Number formatting conventions:** the Indian numbering system (lakh, crore) vs the international system; consistent rounding and significant figures (statistical-reporting practice).\n- **Data reconciliation:** standard financial-reporting control practice (tie exhibits back to audited or reported figures)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; profit concentration across 9 product lines)\n\n**Data (FY27, ₹ Cr):**\n| Line | A | B | C | D | E | F | G | H | I |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 410 | 280 | 260 | 190 | 160 | 150 | 120 | 110 | 120 |\n| Operating profit | 92 | 61 | 41 | 12 | 9 | 7 | 4 | 2 | −5 |\n\n**Calculation (in code):**\n- Total revenue ₹1,800 Cr; total profit ₹223 Cr (reconciled to the segment note).\n- A + B + C = profit ₹194 Cr (87%) on revenue ₹950 Cr (53%).\n\n**Message check:** the intended title said \"80% of profit on 46% of revenue\". The data shows **87% on 53%**, so the title was updated.\n\n**Exhibit:**\n- **Title:** \"Three product lines earn 87% of profit on 53% of revenue.\"\n- **Subtitle:** \"Operating profit by product line, FY27, ₹ Cr; cumulative share of total profit (line).\"\n- **Chart:** columns sorted by profit (A to I), with a cumulative-share line on a secondary % axis, clearly labelled. A, B and C in the accent colour; others grey; I shown below zero in grey with its label.\n- **Callouts:** bracket over A-C, \"87% of profit\"; label on I, \"loss-making: −₹5 Cr\".\n- **Source:** segment reporting, FY27 audited accounts. **Note:** operating profit after allocated overheads.\n\n**QA:** total profit ₹223 Cr matches the annual report ✓ | colours consistent with deck ✓ | readable in greyscale (accent plus pattern) ✓"}]}, {"id": "slide-copy-editor", "pack": "story", "title": "Slide Copy Editor", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Tighten slide text: fewer words, clearer claims, consistent style, no jargon.", "tryp": "Edit this slide text for a board audience: [paste text].", "when": ["Final polish of a deck or one-pager", "Text-heavy slides", "Making a deck written by many people sound like one voice"], "inputs": ["The text", "Audience and any style guide"], "pairs": ["action-titles", "deck-quality-check", "executive-email", "executive-summary"], "body": "# Slide Copy Editor\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** text that is shorter, sharper and consistent, reading as one confident voice, with every claim precise and every number formatted the same way, plus a list of what still needs confirming.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Every word earns its place.** Remove filler (\"in order to\", \"it is important to note that\"), redundancy and throat-clearing.\n2. **Claims, not topics.** \"Improve customer experience\" becomes \"Cut average wait time from 9 to 4 minutes.\"\n3. **Specific beats vague.** Replace \"significant\", \"robust\", \"leverage\", \"synergies\", \"best-in-class\", \"world-class\" and \"holistic\" with numbers or concrete nouns.\n4. **Parallel structure:** bullets in a list share a grammatical form (all start with verbs, or all are full sentences).\n5. **Consistency:** one style for numbers (₹ Cr vs ₹ crore; 1 decimal), dates, units, abbreviations (defined at first use), capitalisation and product names.\n6. **Accuracy and calibration.** Flag claims beyond the evidence; soften or source them. Don't remove necessary caveats.\n7. **Meaning preserved.** Editing must not change substance. Flag anything that needs the author's decision.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Audience (board, client, regulator, staff) and register (formal, neutral).\n- House style or brand guide, if any (spelling convention, number format, terminology).\n- The scope of the edit: light polish, or structural tightening.\n\n## Method\n1. **Read for meaning;** note the main message per slide or section.\n2. **Cut:** remove filler, duplicates and unnecessary qualifiers; aim for 30-50% fewer words on text-heavy slides.\n3. **Convert topics to claims;** add numbers where the source supports them.\n4. **Fix structure:** parallel bullets; one idea per bullet; ≤2 lines per bullet where possible; logical order.\n5. **Replace vague language;** remove or define jargon and acronyms.\n6. **Standardise:** numbers, units, currencies, dates, terminology, capitalisation, spelling convention.\n7. **Check claims:** flag unsupported or overstated claims with a suggested calibrated wording.\n8. **Voice:** harmonise tone across sections written by different people; active voice; avoid hedging stacks (\"may potentially possibly\").\n9. **Return:** the edited text, a change summary, and open questions for the author (facts to confirm, figures to source).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Regulatory or legal text:** don't simplify away required wording. Flag it instead.\n- **Brevity vs clarity:** sometimes adding three words removes ambiguity. Do it.\n- **Respect the author's intent** on emphasis; offer alternatives rather than overriding a strategic message.\n- **Local conventions:** Indian English (lakh/crore, \"upskilling\") vs international audiences; be consistent within one document.\n\n## Red flags\n- Edits that change numbers or meaning.\n- Removed caveats that were needed.\n- Inconsistent formats remaining after the edit.\n- Acronyms never defined.\n- Bullets that mix fragments and sentences.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board and regulator:** formal, precise, conservative claims, defined terms.\n- **Client proposals:** benefit-focused, client language, no internal jargon.\n- **Employee communications:** plain, warm, direct; answer \"what does it mean for me?\"\n- **Marketing / external:** brand voice; claims must be substantiated (advertising-standards rules).\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Edited copy: [deck / document]\n[Edited text, slide by slide or section by section]\n\n**Word count:** before ... → after ... (−..%)\n**Changes made:** cuts, conversions, standardisations (summary)\n**Claims flagged:** original → suggested wording - reason\n**Please confirm:** facts, figures or sources to verify\n**Style decisions applied:** number format, spelling, terminology\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The meaning and numbers are unchanged, unless flagged.\n- [ ] 30-50% shorter on text-heavy content, where possible.\n- [ ] Topics converted to claims; vague words replaced.\n- [ ] Parallel structure; consistent formats; acronyms defined.\n- [ ] Overclaims flagged with calibrated alternatives; necessary caveats kept.\n- [ ] Open questions are listed for the author.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `action-titles`: Rewrite slide titles so each one states the takeaway, and the titles alone tell the story.\n- `deck-quality-check`: A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.\n- `executive-email`: Write a short email or message that gets a senior person to read, decide and reply.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **ISO 24495-1:2023:** plain language (principles: relevant, findable, understandable, usable).\n- **Public-sector plain-language and style guides:** concise sentences, active voice, defined terms, consistent number formats.\n- **Advertising standards:** substantiation of claims (national advertising self-regulatory codes and consumer-protection law for consumer-facing claims).\n- **Editing practice:** parallelism, concision and consistency, as taught in professional editing and business-writing courses."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; one slide from a transformation update)\n\n**Before (92 words):**\n> **Key achievements**\n> - We have significantly leveraged synergies across the organisation to drive robust improvements in operational efficiency\n> - Customer experience enhancements\n> - It is important to note that the new warehouse management system was implemented successfully in Q2 across most of the sites\n> - Team morale has improved a lot\n> - Going forward, we will continue to focus on best-in-class processes in order to deliver holistic value to all our stakeholders\n\n**After (47 words, −49%):**\n> **Merging warehouses and the new system cut cost per order 14% in Q2**\n> - Cost per order fell from ₹62 to ₹53 after merging the Pune and Nashik warehouses.\n> - Order-to-delivery time fell from 3.4 to 2.6 days.\n> - The new warehouse system is live at 7 of 9 sites; the last 2 go live in October.\n> - Employee engagement rose from 61 to 68 (pulse survey, n=420).\n\n**Changes:**\n- The title is now a claim with a number.\n- Vague phrases replaced with metrics.\n- \"Customer experience\" made specific (delivery time).\n- \"Most of the sites\" quantified.\n- Morale claim sourced.\n- The final forward-looking bullet was cut (no content).\n\n**Claims flagged:** \"significantly leveraged synergies\" had no evidence; replaced with the warehouse-merger metric.\n\n**Please confirm:**\n- The ₹62 → ₹53 figures (Finance).\n- The engagement survey sample size (HR).\n- Go-live dates for the 2 remaining sites."}]}, {"id": "deck-quality-check", "pack": "story", "title": "Deck Quality Check", "time": "15 min", "summary": "A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.", "tryp": "Do a full quality check on this deck before it goes to the board on Monday: [attach].", "when": ["Before sending any important deck", "Reviewing a team member's work", "Last check before a board or client meeting"], "inputs": ["The deck or document", "Audience and purpose"], "pairs": ["action-titles", "slide-copy-editor", "red-team-review", "source-validation"], "body": "# Deck Quality Check\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a candid review that catches what would embarrass you in the room: the storyline problem, the number that doesn't tie, the overclaim, the missing alternative. Every fix is specific and prioritised, so the team can act fast.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Story first, formatting last.** Most decks fail on the storyline, not on fonts. Review in that order.\n2. **Read titles only.** If the titles don't tell a coherent, answer-first story, nothing else matters yet.\n3. **Every title must be proven by its slide.** Overclaims damage credibility more than gaps.\n4. **Numbers tie.** The same metric shows the same value everywhere and reconciles to its source; totals add; periods and units match.\n5. **Anticipate the room.** Have alternatives and counter-arguments been addressed? What's the first hard question?\n6. **Specific, prioritised fixes.** \"Slide 7: the title claims X; the chart shows Y; change the title to Z\" beats \"tighten this\".\n7. **Protect the organisation:** confidential data, UPSI, personal data, client names, third-party claims, compliance statements.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Audience, purpose (decide, inform) and meeting format.\n- The deck and its sources (model, data, previous decks).\n- Time available before it is sent.\n\n## Method\n1. **Titles-only read:** is the answer in the first 1-2 slides? Is the storyline logical? Are there gaps or repetitions? Is there a clear ask?\n2. **Slide-by-slide:**\n   - one message;\n   - title supported by the body;\n   - exhibit appropriate and honest;\n   - source present;\n   - text concise.\n3. **Logic:** MECE groupings; inductive support is sufficient; deductive chains are valid; alternatives considered; risks and trade-offs present.\n4. **Numbers:** build a quick tie-out list of every key number with slide references. Check consistency and reconcile against the source (model, financials). Recompute derived figures in code.\n5. **Visual consistency:** fonts, colours, chart styles, alignment, number formats, terminology, abbreviations, spelling convention.\n6. **The ask:** a decision, owner and date are stated; next steps are clear.\n7. **Risk and compliance:** confidential or personal data; price-sensitive information; claims about competitors; legal or regulatory statements needing review; correct attribution of sources.\n8. **Anticipated questions:** the top 5 tough questions; is each answered in the deck or a backup slide?\n9. **Prioritise fixes:**\n   - **Must-fix:** would mislead, embarrass or block the decision.\n   - **Should-fix:** weakens persuasion.\n   - **Nice-to-have:** polish.\n10. **Verdict:** ready / ready after must-fixes / needs rework, with a one-line reason.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Be kind and direct.** Review the work, not the person; explain why each must-fix matters.\n- **Don't rewrite the deck in your own style.** Focus on what changes outcomes.\n- **When time is short,** fix must-fixes and the ask; leave the polish.\n- **If the storyline is wrong,** say so early, even if it means rework. It is cheaper than failing in the room.\n\n## Red flags (in the deck)\n- The answer appears on slide 15.\n- The same metric shows different values on different slides.\n- Charts without sources; truncated axes.\n- \"Next steps\" with no owners or dates; no decision ask.\n- Confidential client names or personal data in an external deck.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board:** check against `board-paper` requirements (purpose, resolution, conflicts, risks).\n- **Investor:** consistency with past guidance and disclosures; forward-looking statement caveats; no selective disclosure of price-sensitive data.\n- **Client proposal:** client's language; no other client's confidential data; pricing consistency.\n- **Regulatory submission:** strict accuracy, defined terms, legal review.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Deck Quality Check: [deck], [date]\n**Verdict:** Ready / Ready after must-fixes / Needs rework - one line\n\n**Storyline (titles only):** assessment + suggested reorder if needed\n\n| Priority | Slide | Issue | Why it matters | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Must-fix | | | | |\n| Should-fix | | | | |\n| Nice-to-have | | | | |\n\n**Number tie-out:** metric - values found (slides) - source value - status\n**Anticipated tough questions:** question - answered on slide? - suggested backup\n**Risk / compliance flags:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The titles-only storyline is assessed first.\n- [ ] Every must-fix has a slide reference, a reason and a specific fix.\n- [ ] Numbers are tied out across slides and to sources (recomputed in code where possible).\n- [ ] The ask, owners and dates are checked.\n- [ ] Confidentiality and compliance are reviewed.\n- [ ] A clear verdict.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `action-titles`: Rewrite slide titles so each one states the takeaway, and the titles alone tell the story.\n- `slide-copy-editor`: Tighten slide text: fewer words, clearer claims, consistent style, no jargon.\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n- `source-validation`: Grade every source and claim for reliability so you never put a weak number in front of a board.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts; verify regulatory specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Structured communication review:** storyline (answer-first), horizontal and vertical logic, MECE groupings (see `pyramid-structure`, `action-titles`).\n- **Graphical integrity:** honest scales and sourcing (see `chart-selection`).\n- **India regulatory considerations for listed companies:** SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015 on UPSI; SEBI LODR on disclosures. Investor materials must not selectively disclose price-sensitive information (verify with the Company Secretary).\n- **Data protection:** DPDP Act 2023 (personal data in documents shared internally and externally).\n- **Review practice:** prioritised feedback (must, should, nice) common in quality-assurance and peer-review processes."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; 18-slide board deck proposing an acquisition)\n\n**Verdict:** Ready after must-fixes. The storyline is strong, but two numbers don't tie and the risk section is thin for a board.\n\n**Storyline (titles only):** answer-first ✓. Slides 9-11 repeat the synergy argument. Merge them into one slide and move the detail to the appendix. The ask on slide 18 lacks the resolution wording.\n\n| Priority | Slide | Issue | Why it matters | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Must-fix | 3 vs 12 | Target EBITDA shown as ₹84 Cr (slide 3) and ₹87 Cr (slide 12) | Directors will spot it and question every number | Use ₹84 Cr (audited FY26); slide 12 used management accounts. Footnote the difference |\n| Must-fix | 7 | Title: \"Synergies of ₹40 Cr are secure\"; the chart shows ₹40 Cr *identified*, with ₹14 Cr at low confidence | Overclaim; the board will discount it | Title: \"₹40 Cr of synergies identified; ₹26 Cr high-confidence\" |\n| Must-fix | 15 | Risks list has no mitigations or owners | The board cannot assess residual risk | Add mitigations, owners and residual ratings (see `risk-assessment`) |\n| Must-fix | 18 | No resolution or approval limit | The board can't approve a vague ask | Add a draft resolution with price cap and delegation |\n| Should-fix | 9-11 | Repetitive synergy slides | Loses attention | Merge into one slide |\n| Should-fix | 5 | Market-share chart axis starts at 20% | Exaggerates the share gain | Start at 0, or use a table |\n| Nice-to-have | All | Mixed \"Rs\" / \"₹\" and \"crore\" / \"Cr\" | Polish | Standardise on ₹ Cr |\n\n**Number tie-out (extract):**\n| Metric | Values found | Source | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Purchase price | ₹1,150 Cr (2, 16, 18) | Term sheet | ✓ |\n| Target EBITDA | ₹84 (3), ₹87 (12) | Audited FY26: ₹84 | ✗ fix slide 12 |\n| EV/EBITDA | 13.7x (2) | 1,150 ÷ 84 = 13.7 | ✓ |\n\n**Anticipated tough questions:**\n- \"What do we pay for synergies we may not get?\" → add a premium-vs-synergy slide.\n- \"Who leads the integration?\" → name the leader on slide 17.\n\n**Compliance:** the deck contains UPSI. Mark it confidential and restrict circulation. Remove the target employee names on slide 14 (personal data)."}]}, {"id": "speaker-notes", "pack": "story", "title": "Speaker Notes", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Write natural speaker notes and a timing plan, with likely questions and crisp answers.", "tryp": "Write speaker notes for my 12-slide, 20-minute presentation to the investment committee: [attach deck].", "when": ["Before any important presentation", "When someone else built the deck you must present", "Preparing for tough Q&A"], "inputs": ["The deck", "Time available", "Audience"], "pairs": ["audience-brief", "deck-quality-check", "meeting-brief", "red-team-review"], "body": "# Speaker Notes\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a presenter who sounds natural, stays on time, handles the hardest question calmly, and leaves the room with a decision, without reading slides aloud.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Senior meetings are discussions, not presentations.** Plan for 30-50% of the time in discussion; prepare to jump to any slide.\n2. **Say the title, then the why.** Each slide opens with its message in spoken words, then the evidence. Never read bullets.\n3. **Transitions carry the logic.** \"So if that's the problem, what are the options?\" keeps the audience in the storyline.\n4. **Prepare for the worst question,** not the easy ones. Answer first, then evidence, then offer the backup.\n5. **Time discipline.** Know which slides to skip if time is cut by half.\n6. **Close with the ask.** Restate the decision, and confirm what happens next before people leave.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Audience (see `audience-brief`), time slot, format (in person, virtual, hybrid), and whether a pre-read was sent.\n- The deck and its storyline; your role (owner or presenting for others).\n- Known sensitivities and the likely hardest questions.\n\n## Method\n1. **Time plan:** allocate minutes per slide or section. Key slides get more time, context slides less. Reserve discussion time. If a pre-read was sent, plan to summarise in 5 minutes and spend the rest discussing.\n2. **Opening (≤60 seconds):** purpose, the ask, and why now (\"We're asking you to approve X today because Y\").\n3. **Per slide:**\n   - an **opening line** (the title in natural speech);\n   - **2-3 talking points** (evidence, example, implication);\n   - a **transition;**\n   - **cues** (pause for questions; emphasise a number).\n4. **Skip markers:** which slides to drop if time is cut, and the one-sentence bridge if you skip them.\n5. **Hard-question bank (8-12):** answer-first responses (≤30 seconds), the supporting fact, and a backup slide reference. Include \"What if you're wrong?\", \"Why not option B?\", \"Who's accountable?\", \"What does it cost if it slips?\"\n6. **Difficult moments:**\n   - **Hostile challenge:** acknowledge, answer the substance, offer a follow-up.\n   - **Off-track discussion:** park it with an owner.\n   - **\"I don't know\":** commit to a follow-up by a date.\n   - **Time cut:** go to the summary and the ask.\n7. **Close (≤60 seconds):** restate the ask; summarise the conditions; confirm next steps and owners.\n8. **Virtual or hybrid:** shorter segments; call on remote participants by name; have a co-host manage chat; share the deck link as a backup.\n9. **Rehearsal plan:** one full run-through out loud; a Q&A drill with a colleague playing the toughest audience member.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Pre-read meetings:** don't re-present. Ask, \"What questions do you have?\" after a 3-minute summary.\n- **If the decision-maker signals early agreement,** stop presenting, confirm the decision and conditions, and move on.\n- **Numbers to memorise:** the 5-7 key figures. Looking them up mid-answer erodes credibility.\n- **Co-presenters:** agree handovers and who answers which topics.\n\n## Red flags\n- Notes that duplicate slide text.\n- No discussion time planned.\n- Only easy questions prepared.\n- No closing ask; the meeting ends with \"any questions?\"\n- Running over time with the key slides still unshown.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board:** the Chair controls the agenda; address the Chair; be concise; don't argue with directors, but clarify and offer follow-ups.\n- **Investors:** a consistent narrative; careful forward-looking statements; no new price-sensitive information beyond what's disclosed.\n- **Client pitch:** client-centred opening; questions to engage; tailored examples.\n- **Town hall:** plain language, empathy, honest answers about impact; a Q&A moderator.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Speaker Notes: [deck], [audience], [time]\n**Opening (60s):** ...\n\n| Slide | Time | Say (opening line) | Points | Cue | Transition | Skip if short? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Hard questions**\n| Question | Answer (first line) | Supporting fact | Backup slide |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Difficult-moment plays:** ...\n**Close (60s):** ...\n**Key numbers to memorise:** ...\n**Rehearsal plan:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The time plan includes 30-50% discussion; skip markers are set.\n- [ ] Each slide has a spoken opening line and a transition; no reading of bullets.\n- [ ] 8-12 hard questions with answer-first responses and backups.\n- [ ] An opening and a close that state the ask.\n- [ ] Key numbers listed; a rehearsal plan.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `audience-brief`: Profile who you are persuading: what they care about, what they already believe, and what would change their mind.\n- `deck-quality-check`: A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.\n- `meeting-brief`: A one-page brief before any important meeting: objective, people, positions, your asks and your lines.\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts; verify regulatory specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Executive presentation practice:** answer-first openings, discussion-led meetings with pre-reads, and Q&A preparation, from business-communication curricula.\n- **Message discipline:** key-message and bridging techniques from media and communication training.\n- **Investor communications (India):** SEBI LODR disclosure obligations and the prohibition on selective disclosure of UPSI (verify with the Company Secretary).\n- **Meeting facilitation:** parking lots, timeboxing and decision confirmation (see `workshop-design`)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; investment committee, 20 minutes, 12 slides, pre-read sent)\n\n**Opening (45s):** \"Thank you. We're asking you to approve ₹420 crore for Phase 1 of the Dahej plant today, released in three tranches. The reason for deciding now is simple: two anchor customers qualify suppliers this year, and missing that window puts around ₹200 crore of annual revenue at risk. You've had the pre-read, so I'll take 5 minutes on the key points and then open it up.\"\n\n| Slide | Time | Say | Points | Cue | Transition | Skip? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 Exec summary | 2 min | \"Here's the case in one page.\" | NPV ₹212 Cr; IRR 18% vs 15% hurdle; break-even at −22% volume | Pause after the break-even number | \"Let me show you why demand is real.\" | No |\n| 4 Demand | 1.5 min | \"The demand isn't a forecast; it's letters of intent.\" | 11 KTPA under LOI; qualification timeline | — | \"Of course, the question is risk.\" | No |\n| 7 Sensitivity | 1 min | \"Volume is the one assumption that matters.\" | Break-even −22%; take-or-pay gate | Emphasise \"gate\" | \"That's why we've staged the money.\" | No |\n| 9 Stage gates | 1 min | \"You only commit ₹160 crore today.\" | Tranche criteria; kill criterion | — | \"Which brings me to the ask.\" | No |\n| 5, 6, 8 | — | (backup) | Market, options, capex detail | — | — | Yes |\n| 12 Ask | 30s | \"We're asking for three things today.\" | Resolution; tranche release by the board; delegation limits | Stop and wait | — | No |\n\n**Hard questions:**\n| Question | Answer (first line) | Fact | Backup |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"What if the anchors don't sign take-or-pay?\" | \"Then we don't release tranche 2. The maximum loss is the ₹160 Cr already committed, mostly recoverable land and long-lead items.\" | Recoverability est. 55% | 9 |\n| \"Why not debottleneck Ankleshwar instead?\" | \"We will, but it adds only 6 KTPA. The anchors need 11.\" | Options table | 6 |\n| \"Who's accountable for delivery?\" | \"Our new project director, reporting monthly to the audit committee on cost.\" | Appointment confirmed | 10 |\n| \"What does a 9-month delay cost?\" | \"About ₹44 Cr of NPV; still positive.\" | Sensitivity | 7 |\n\n**Difficult moment:** if a director challenges the demand forecast as optimistic, say: \"That's the right challenge. That's exactly why tranche 2 is conditional on signed take-or-pay contracts. If they're not there, we stop.\"\n\n**Close (30s):** \"So: approval of ₹420 crore in three tranches, with ₹160 crore today and tranche 2 back to this board. If you're comfortable, we'll proceed with the EPC contract this month.\"\n\n**Key numbers:** ₹420 Cr / ₹160 Cr; NPV ₹212 Cr; IRR 18.4%; break-even −22% volume; 11 KTPA under LOI; net debt/EBITDA peak 1.9x."}]}, {"id": "decision-memo", "pack": "story", "title": "Decision Memo", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Write a crisp memo that frames a decision, the options, the recommendation and what is being asked.", "tryp": "Write a decision memo for the ExCo: should we move our customer support to a 24x7 model using AI agents plus a smaller human team?", "when": ["ExCo or leadership decisions", "When a narrative beats slides", "Recording why a decision was taken"], "inputs": ["The decision and options", "Data and analysis", "Decision-maker and deadline"], "pairs": ["strategic-options", "executive-summary", "board-paper", "pyramid-structure"], "body": "# Decision Memo\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a memo that lets leaders make a decision in one meeting, often after silent reading at the start, and creates a durable record of what was decided, why, and under what conditions.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Title the decision.** \"Decision: whether to pilot AI-assisted 24x7 support (by 30 Nov)\" tells the reader why they are reading.\n2. **Recommendation and ask in the first paragraph,** with the scale, cost and timing.\n3. **Narrative over bullets for reasoning.** Full sentences force complete logic; bullets hide gaps. Use tables only for option comparison.\n4. **Real options, status quo included,** each with benefits, costs and risks.\n5. **Name the trade-off** in one sentence. Decisions are choices between goods or between risks.\n6. **Reversal triggers.** State what evidence would make us change course, and when we'll review.\n7. **Record the decision.** Outcome, conditions, dissent, owner, review date: the institutional memory.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The decision, decision-maker(s), deadline and forum (ExCo, committee, board).\n- Analysis available (options, numbers, risks); stakeholder positions.\n- Approvals needed (budget, legal, regulatory, works council).\n- The culture: some forums read memos silently at the start of the meeting. Design for that.\n\n## Method\n1. **Title:** \"Decision: [what] by [date]\".\n2. **Paragraph 1 (recommendation and ask):** what we recommend; cost or benefit; timing; what we need from the reader (approve, choose, comment).\n3. **Context (≤½ page):** only what's needed to judge the options; link to background documents.\n4. **Options (2-4, including status quo):** a short narrative each, then a comparison table: benefits, costs, risks, time, reversibility, stakeholder impact.\n5. **Recommendation and rationale:** why it beats the alternatives against the agreed criteria; the trade-off accepted.\n6. **Risks and mitigations;** **reversal triggers** with dates and metrics.\n7. **Implementation outline:** owner, first 30-60-90 days, milestones, budget phasing.\n8. **Approvals and consultations** required, and their status.\n9. **FAQ (optional):** the 3-5 questions readers will ask, answered briefly.\n10. **Decision record** (completed after the meeting): decision, date, decided by, conditions, dissent, review date.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Length:** 1-2 pages for most decisions; up to 6 for complex, irreversible ones, with appendices.\n- **Neutral options section.** Write the options fairly, as the advocate of each would; save advocacy for the recommendation.\n- **When leaders disagree,** present the strongest version of each view, and the criteria on which they differ.\n- **Reversible, low-cost decisions** deserve speed: a short memo, a fast decision, and a review date.\n\n## Red flags\n- The recommendation appears on page 3.\n- Options that are variations of the same thing; no status quo.\n- No costs or risks for the recommended option.\n- No reversal triggers or review date.\n- The decision record not completed after the meeting.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Investment approval:** add returns vs hurdle, sensitivities and stage gates (see `business-case`).\n- **Policy decisions:** add the compliance and legal analysis and the communication plan.\n- **People and organisation decisions:** confidentiality; legal and HR process; a communication sequence.\n- **AI deployments:** add the risk tier, guardrails and pilot metrics (see `ai-pilot-design`).\n- **Board-level:** convert to `board-paper` format with a resolution.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n# Decision: [what], by [date]\n**Recommendation and ask:** ...\n\n## Context\n## Options\n(short narrative per option)\n| | Status quo | Option A | Option B |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Benefits | | | |\n| Costs | | | |\n| Risks | | | |\n| Time / reversibility | | | |\n\n## Recommendation and rationale (trade-off: ...)\n## Risks, mitigations and reversal triggers\n## Implementation outline\n## Approvals and consultations\n## FAQ (optional)\n## Decision record\nDecided by: ... | Date: ... | Outcome: ... | Conditions: ... | Dissent: ... | Review date: ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The title states the decision and deadline; the ask is in the first paragraph.\n- [ ] Real options, including the status quo, compared fairly.\n- [ ] The trade-off is stated in one sentence.\n- [ ] Risks, mitigations, reversal triggers and a review date.\n- [ ] Implementation owner and approvals.\n- [ ] A decision record section.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Check that the final message, numbers and claims are accurate and approved before sharing outside your organisation.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n- `board-paper`: Write a board paper directors can read in 10 minutes: purpose, recommendation, analysis, risks and the resolution.\n- `pyramid-structure`: Structure any argument top-down: answer first, grouped support, evidence below.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Narrative memos in place of slides:** a widely adopted executive practice, in which a short written narrative is read at the start of a meeting to improve decision quality.\n- **Decision quality principles:** frame, alternatives, information, values and trade-offs, reasoning, commitment (decision-analysis curricula).\n- **Options appraisal:** public appraisal guidance (e.g. UK HM Treasury *Green Book*) on status quo / do-minimum baselines.\n- **Decision records:** architecture-decision and governance-minute practices for capturing rationale and dissent."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional retail bank)\n\n# Decision: whether to pilot AI-assisted 24x7 customer support, by 30 November\n\n**Recommendation and ask:** pilot AI-assisted 24x7 support for English-language retail banking queries for 90 days (₹1.1 Cr), with go/no-go on 15 March. We ask the ExCo to approve the pilot and the guardrails below.\n\n## Context\n- Contact volume rose 22% this year.\n- 38% of calls arrive outside our 8am-8pm hours and go to voicemail or a callback.\n- Complaint-response time breaches have doubled.\n- Hiring a night shift (45 staff) would cost ₹6.8 Cr a year.\n\n## Options\n- **Status quo:** no cost, but out-of-hours service gaps persist. Breach rates and complaints are likely to keep rising.\n- **A. Human night shift:** full coverage and the highest quality, but ₹6.8 Cr a year, and recruitment takes 6 months.\n- **B. AI-assisted 24x7 (recommended for pilot):**\n  - An AI assistant answers routine queries, grounded in approved content.\n  - It hands over to a smaller human night team (8 staff) for complex or sensitive cases.\n  - ₹1.1 Cr pilot; est. ₹2.4 Cr a year at scale.\n\n| | Status quo | A. Night shift | B. AI-assisted 24x7 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Benefits | None | Full human coverage | 24x7 coverage of routine queries; faster responses |\n| Costs | Rising complaints and penalties | ₹6.8 Cr/yr | ₹1.1 Cr pilot; ~₹2.4 Cr/yr at scale |\n| Risks | Regulatory scrutiny of service | Hiring delays; attrition | Wrong answers; customer trust; data protection |\n| Time / reversibility | — | 6 months; hard to reverse | 6 weeks; easy to stop |\n\n## Recommendation and rationale\nOption B meets the service gap at about a third of A's cost, can start in 6 weeks, and is easy to stop.\n**Trade-off:** we accept some risk of AI errors on routine queries in exchange for speed and cost. This is mitigated by grounding, human handover and close monitoring.\n\n## Risks, mitigations and reversal triggers\n- **Wrong answers:** answers come only from approved content, with citations; refusal plus handover when unsure; 5% QA sampling.\n- **Vulnerable customers:** detection of hardship and bereavement keywords triggers immediate human handover.\n- **Data:** processing stays in our cloud tenancy; no vendor training on our data.\n- **Reversal triggers:**\n  - CSAT below 4.0 for 2 consecutive weeks;\n  - any critical regulatory-language breach;\n  - an escalation rate above 45%.\n  Any one of these pauses the pilot.\n\n## Implementation\n- **Owner:** Head of Customer Service.\n- **Weeks 1-6:** build and test (offline evaluation on 800 queries).\n- **Weeks 7-19:** live pilot.\n- **15 March:** go/no-go.\n\n## Approvals\n- AI risk committee (scheduled 20 Nov).\n- Privacy review (in progress).\n- Union consultation on the night-team roles (completed).\n\n## Decision record\nDecided by: ExCo | Date: 28 Nov | Outcome: **Approved** | Conditions: monthly update to the CRO; Hindi added only after a separate review | Dissent: COO prefers a hybrid with 20 night staff; to be revisited at go/no-go | Review date: 15 March"}]}, {"id": "implementation-roadmap", "pack": "deliver", "title": "Implementation Roadmap", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.", "tryp": "Build an 18-month roadmap to implement a new finance operating model with a shared-services centre in Pune.", "when": ["After a recommendation is approved", "Board or sponsor asks 'how will this actually happen?'", "Re-planning a stalled programme"], "inputs": ["The recommendation and scope", "Timeframe, budget, constraints", "Teams and capacity available"], "pairs": ["raci-builder", "change-plan", "risk-assessment", "steerco-update"], "body": "# Implementation Roadmap\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a roadmap that leaders can steer and teams can execute. It is clear about outcomes, sequencing and dependencies, realistic about capacity, and has gates where evidence decides whether to continue.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Outcomes before activities.** Define the end state and 3-5 measurable outcomes; milestones are evidence of progress toward them (\"40% of invoices touchless\"), not activities (\"system configured\").\n2. **Sequence by dependency and learning.** Foundations (data, platforms, policies, people) come early; scale follows proof.\n3. **Early wins matter.** Visible, valued results within 90 days build credibility and funding; choose ones aligned with the end state.\n4. **Capacity is the binding constraint.** The same experts, managers and change-weary teams are usually overloaded. Plan per quarter; limit concurrent initiatives.\n5. **Stage gates with teeth.** Each gate has criteria, evidence and a decision-maker; options include continue, adjust, pause or stop.\n6. **One accountable owner per workstream,** with clear decision rights and escalation paths.\n7. **Living plan.** Re-plan quarterly on evidence; keep a change log.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- What was approved (scope, budget, benefits, timeline) and what's non-negotiable.\n- Current-state baseline; known constraints (systems, regulation, people, contracts).\n- Teams, partners and capacity; other programmes competing for the same people.\n- Governance expectations (board reporting, SteerCo).\n\n## Method\n1. **End state and outcomes:** describe the target state; define 3-5 outcome KPIs with targets and dates.\n2. **Workstreams (4-7):** e.g. process, technology, data, people and change, commercial, governance and risk. Name the owners.\n3. **Initiatives per workstream:** scope, outputs, outcome contribution, dependencies, effort, cost.\n4. **Phasing:**\n   - **Mobilise (0-3 months):** team, governance, baseline, quick wins, detailed design.\n   - **Foundations (3-9):** platforms, data, policies, pilots.\n   - **Scale (9-18):** roll-out by wave (region, unit, product).\n   - **Embed (18+):** decommission legacy, institutionalise, transfer to business-as-usual.\n5. **Early wins:** 2-4 within 90 days, each with an owner, a metric and a communication plan.\n6. **Dependencies and critical path:** identify cross-workstream dependencies and external ones (vendors, regulators); highlight the critical path and the float.\n7. **Capacity and budget:** quarterly view of key roles and spend vs approved; resolve overloads by resequencing.\n8. **Stage gates:** criteria (outcomes, readiness, risk, budget), evidence and decision-maker per gate; define pivot and stop options.\n9. **Governance:** SteerCo cadence and decisions; workstream rhythm; escalation thresholds; reporting to the board.\n10. **Risks:** top roadmap risks and mitigations (see `risk-assessment`, `pre-mortem`).\n11. **Visualise:** a table, plus a simple text timeline by quarter.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Waves beat big bangs** for most organisational and technology change. Choose the first wave for learning (representative but forgiving).\n- **Decommissioning is where benefits hide.** Plan legacy shutdown explicitly, or costs persist.\n- **Don't let the roadmap become a wish list.** If capacity doesn't fit, drop or defer. Say what's not happening.\n- **Integrate change management** into every workstream, not as a separate afterthought.\n\n## Red flags\n- Milestones that are activities with no evidence of outcome.\n- Everything starting in month 1.\n- No owners, or committees as owners.\n- No gates; funding fully released upfront.\n- Dependencies discovered during execution.\n- No decommissioning plan.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Technology programmes (ERP, cloud, data):** design, build, test, migrate and cutover phases; data migration and testing as the critical path; hypercare.\n- **Cost transformation:** quick wins first; structural levers later; savings tracking (see `cost-takeout`).\n- **AI programmes:** foundations (data, platform, governance) in parallel with wave-1 pilots; scale by use-case family (see `playbook-ai-roadmap`).\n- **GCC set-up:** entity and site; leadership hires; process transitions by wave; knowledge transfer and stabilisation.\n- **M&A integration:** day 1, 100 days, synergy waves; clean-team rules before close.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Implementation Roadmap: [programme], [period]\n**End state:** ...  |  **Outcome KPIs:** ...\n\n| Workstream (owner) | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | Q6 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Early wins (≤90 days):** win - metric - owner\n**Key milestones (outcome-based):** date - milestone - evidence\n**Dependencies / critical path:** ...\n**Capacity and budget by quarter:** ...\n**Stage gates:**\n| Gate | Date | Criteria | Evidence | Decision-maker | Options |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n**Governance:** ...\n**Top risks:** ...\n**Not in scope / deferred:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] End-state outcomes with KPIs; milestones are outcome-based.\n- [ ] Single owners per workstream; early wins within 90 days.\n- [ ] Dependencies, critical path and capacity checked by quarter.\n- [ ] Gates with criteria, evidence and stop or pivot options.\n- [ ] Governance, risks, decommissioning and deferred items stated.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `raci-builder`: Clarify who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each activity or decision.\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n- `risk-assessment`: Identify, score and plan responses to the risks of a project, decision or business.\n- `steerco-update`: Write a crisp steering committee update: status, decisions needed, risks, and next steps.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **ISO 21502:2020** (guidance on project management) and **ISO 21503** (programme management): planning, governance, stage gates, benefits.\n- **Stage-gate governance:** phase-gate decision practices used in capital projects and product development.\n- **Critical-path and dependency management:** project-scheduling fundamentals.\n- **Benefits-led programme management:** public-sector programme guidance (outcome-based milestones, benefits realisation).\n- **Change integration:** see `change-plan`; organisational-change practice of embedding change activities in each workstream."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional manufacturer, 18-month finance shared-services centre in Pune)\n\n**End state:** transactional finance for 6 business units (AP, AR, general accounting, T&E, fixed assets) delivered from Pune with standard processes on one ERP instance.\n**Outcome KPIs:**\n- Cost per invoice −35%.\n- Close in 6 days (from 9).\n- First-time-right ≥97%.\n- BU satisfaction ≥4.0/5.\n\n| Workstream (owner) | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | Q6 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Site and people (CHRO) | Lease, leadership hires | Hire wave 1 (40) | Hire wave 2 (35) | Training academy | Hire wave 3 (20) | Stabilise |\n| Process (Group Controller) | Standard process design | Wave 1 transition (Services, Mfg AP) | Hypercare | Wave 2 (Retail, AR) | Wave 3 (GL, fixed assets) | Continuous improvement |\n| Technology (CIO) | ERP harmonisation design | Workflow + document capture live | ERP config for wave 2 | Legacy decommission (BU 1-2) | Wave 3 config | Decommission complete |\n| Controls and risk (CRO) | Control design; segregation-of-duties matrix | Transition control testing | Internal audit review wave 1 | — | Internal audit review wave 2-3 | Annual review |\n| Change and comms (Programme Director) | Stakeholder map; town halls | BU readiness checks | Wave 2 readiness | — | Wave 3 readiness | Handover to BAU |\n\n**Early wins (≤90 days):**\n- Duplicate-payment check across all BUs (₹1.2 Cr recovered) — Controller.\n- A single vendor-onboarding portal — CIO.\n\n**Key milestones:**\n- Q2: wave 1 at ≥95% first-time-right for 2 consecutive closes.\n- Q4: 60% of volume in Pune.\n- Q6: close in 6 days, achieved twice.\n\n**Critical path:** ERP harmonisation for the Retail BU (Q3), since wave 2 depends on it.\n**Capacity flag:** the Retail BU is also running a POS upgrade in Q3-Q4, so its wave is moved to Q4.\n\n**Stage gates:**\n| Gate | Date | Criteria | Decision-maker | Options |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| G1: Wave 2 go | End Q3 | Wave 1 first-time-right ≥95%; backlog ≤2 days; BU sign-off | SteerCo | Go / delay one quarter / re-scope |\n| G2: Wave 3 go | End Q5 | Waves 1-2 KPIs met; legacy decommission on track | SteerCo + CFO | Go / partial |\n\n**Governance:**\n- Monthly SteerCo, chaired by the Group CFO.\n- Weekly workstream leads meeting.\n- Quarterly Audit Committee update on controls.\n\n**Deferred:** treasury and tax processes (to a phase 2 decision in Q6)."}]}, {"id": "operating-model", "pack": "deliver", "title": "Operating Model Design", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Design how the organisation will work: structure, processes, people, technology, governance and locations.", "tryp": "Design the target operating model for a new AI and data Global Capability Centre serving a US parent.", "when": ["Reorganisations and function redesign", "Shared services / GCC set-up", "Post-merger integration"], "inputs": ["Strategy and goals the model must serve", "Current model (if any)", "Constraints: budget, locations, regulation, talent"], "pairs": ["raci-builder", "implementation-roadmap", "change-plan", "ai-governance-review"], "body": "# Operating Model Design\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a coherent target model linked to strategy, showing how work flows, who decides, where it's done, what it costs and how to get there, with the structural options and trade-offs made explicit.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Strategy drives design.** Translate strategy into 5-8 design principles (e.g. \"customer decisions close to the market\", \"one global data platform\", \"scale functions in GCCs\") and use them to judge options.\n2. **More than an org chart.** Structure is one of eight elements; most failures come from unclear decision rights, misaligned incentives or broken processes.\n3. **Decision rights are the core.** For the 10-20 decisions that matter, state who decides, who recommends, who must agree, who gives input and who executes.\n4. **Options, not a single answer.** Compare 2-3 structures (functional, business-unit or product, matrix, hub-and-spoke, platform) against the principles.\n5. **Spans and layers matter.** Too many layers slow decisions; very narrow spans waste cost. Use benchmarks as indicators, not rules.\n6. **Location and sourcing are strategic:** capability depth, talent markets, risk, regulatory constraints, and cost.\n7. **Transition is part of design:** sequencing, people impact, controls during change.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Strategy and the reason for change (growth, cost, control, capability, an integration).\n- Current model across the eight elements; pain points (slow decisions, duplication, gaps).\n- Constraints: regulation, labour law, works councils, contracts, systems, budget.\n- Stakeholders and their concerns.\n\n## Method\n1. **Design principles (5-8)** from strategy and pain points; agree them with the sponsor.\n2. **Current-state assessment** across eight elements:\n   - value and services;\n   - processes and capabilities;\n   - structure and roles;\n   - decision rights and governance;\n   - locations and sourcing;\n   - technology and data;\n   - performance and incentives;\n   - culture and ways of working.\n   Include evidence (spans, layers, cost, cycle times, surveys).\n3. **Capability map:** which capabilities are differentiating (keep close, invest), which are scale or commodity (consolidate, shared service, GCC, outsource), and which are missing.\n4. **Structural options (2-3):** describe each; evaluate them against the principles, cost, risk and transition complexity; pick one.\n5. **Target design:** high-level structure; key roles and accountabilities; spans and layers; interfaces (service catalogues, SLAs); governance forums.\n6. **Decision-rights matrix** for the key decisions (see `raci-builder`).\n7. **Locations and sourcing:** what is done where (onshore, nearshore, GCC, partner), with criteria and risk.\n8. **Technology and data enablers:** platforms, data ownership, automation and AI roles.\n9. **Performance and incentives:** KPIs per unit; how targets cascade; incentive alignment.\n10. **Sizing:** headcount and cost, current vs target; one-off transition costs; benefits.\n11. **Transition plan:** waves, people process (selection, redeployment, consultation), control continuity, communication; risks.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Matrix structures** need very clear decision rights and conflict-resolution mechanisms, or they create paralysis.\n- **Centralise what benefits from scale and standardisation;** keep close to the market what needs speed and customer intimacy.\n- **GCCs succeed** when they own outcomes, products and capabilities, not just tasks. Design for an evolution from transactions to expertise.\n- **Culture eats structure.** Plan behaviours, rituals and leadership role-modelling, not just boxes.\n- **People-impact decisions** must follow legal and ethical processes; communicate early and with respect.\n\n## Red flags\n- An org chart with no process, decision-rights or incentive design.\n- Design principles written after the design.\n- A single option presented.\n- Spans of 2-3 across management, or 8+ layers.\n- No transition plan or people process.\n- Location chosen on cost alone.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **GCC / shared services:** service catalogue, SLAs, chargeback model, governance with business units, career paths, a maturity roadmap (transactional → expertise → innovation).\n- **AI and data function:** hub-and-spoke (central platform, standards and governance; embedded product teams), product ownership, a model-risk function.\n- **Post-merger:** day-1 model vs target model; leadership selection; integration of cultures and systems.\n- **Function redesign (finance, HR, IT):** business partnering, centres of excellence, shared services, and digital and AI enablement.\n- **Public sector:** statutory roles; accountability to ministers or boards; procurement constraints.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Operating Model: [unit], [current → target]\n**Design principles:** 1. ... 2. ...\n\n| Element | Current (evidence) | Target | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Value / services | | | |\n| Processes / capabilities | | | |\n| Structure / roles | | | |\n| Decision rights / governance | | | |\n| Locations / sourcing | | | |\n| Technology / data | | | |\n| Performance / incentives | | | |\n| Culture / ways of working | | | |\n\n**Structural options:** A / B / C - evaluation - chosen ... because ...\n**Key decision rights:** decision - recommends - agrees - decides - executes\n**Sizing:** headcount and cost, current vs target; one-off costs\n**Transition:** waves, people process, control continuity, risks\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Design principles derive from strategy and are used to compare options.\n- [ ] All eight elements are addressed, with evidence on the current state.\n- [ ] Options compared; decision rights defined for key decisions.\n- [ ] Location and sourcing logic stated; spans and layers checked.\n- [ ] People and cost sizing; a transition plan with people process and control continuity.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `raci-builder`: Clarify who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each activity or decision.\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n- `ai-governance-review`: Assess how well an organisation governs AI: policy, roles, inventory, risk, controls, and board oversight.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts; verify legal specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Operating-model design:** multi-element models (structure, process, people, technology, governance, location, performance, culture), as taught in organisation-design and strategy-execution curricula.\n- **Organisation design principles:** spans and layers, decision rights, differentiation and integration, centres of excellence, shared services.\n- **Capability-based planning:** distinguishing differentiating vs commodity capabilities.\n- **Global capability centres:** evolution models from transactional delivery to product and innovation ownership (industry body reports; verify current data).\n- **Employment law** (India and other jurisdictions): consultation, transfer and redundancy rules for people changes (verify with HR and counsel).\n- **AI governance roles:** NIST AI RMF GOVERN function; ISO/IEC 42001 roles and responsibilities."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; target operating model for a new AI and data GCC in India serving a US parent)\n\n**Design principles:**\n1. The GCC owns data and AI products end-to-end, with US business co-owners.\n2. One global data platform and one set of AI standards.\n3. Business value is measured per product, not per headcount.\n4. AI risk oversight is independent of delivery teams.\n5. Talent is grown through clear career paths in India.\n\n| Element | Current | Target | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Value / services | Ad hoc analytics support; tickets from 9 US teams | 6 data and AI product lines with roadmaps (pricing, customer analytics, finance automation, risk models, GenAI platform, data platform) | From services to products |\n| Processes | Request-driven; no intake prioritisation | Quarterly portfolio planning; product discovery; an MLOps lifecycle | New intake and portfolio process |\n| Structure | 40 people under 3 managers by skill | Product teams (product manager + engineers + data scientists) in 2 tribes; platform team; AI governance cell | Skill pools → cross-functional teams |\n| Decision rights | US approves everything | GCC head decides internal model deployment; US business owner decides customer-facing release; AI risk cell can veto high-risk launches | Delegated, with guardrails |\n| Locations / sourcing | 100% Bengaluru; contractors 30% | Bengaluru hub + Pune satellite; contractors ≤15%; partner for 24x7 platform operations | Reduce contractor dependency |\n| Technology / data | Copies of US data in multiple places | One cloud data platform; access via governed data products | Platform build in year 1 |\n| Performance | Utilisation % | Product outcome KPIs; adoption; value delivered | New scorecards |\n| Culture | Order-taking | Ownership; experimentation with guardrails; knowledge-sharing guilds | Leadership role-modelling; rituals |\n\n**Structural options:**\n- **A.** Functional pools (status quo+).\n- **B.** Product tribes + platform (chosen).\n- **C.** Embedded teams inside each US business.\n\nB best fits principles 1-3. C fragments standards; A keeps order-taking.\n\n**Key decision rights:**\n| Decision | Recommends | Agrees | Decides | Executes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Product roadmap priority | GCC product manager | US business owner | Joint portfolio council | Product team |\n| Deploy an internal ML model | Product team | AI risk cell (tier check) | GCC head | Platform team |\n| Customer-facing GenAI release | Product team | AI risk cell, Legal | US business owner | Product team |\n\n**Sizing:** 40 → 85 FTE over 18 months; cost per FTE +8% (higher skill mix); contractors −50%.\n**Transition:**\n- Q1: product lines defined; first 2 tribes.\n- Q2-Q3: platform build; hiring.\n- Q4: decision rights live.\n- Throughout: monthly communications to US stakeholders."}]}, {"id": "raci-builder", "pack": "deliver", "title": "RACI Builder", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Clarify who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each activity or decision.", "tryp": "Build a RACI for our monthly financial close across Group Finance, BU finance, shared services and IT.", "when": ["New programmes, processes or org changes", "When decisions stall or get re-opened", "Audit findings on unclear ownership"], "inputs": ["Activities or decisions", "Roles or teams involved"], "pairs": ["operating-model", "implementation-roadmap", "stakeholder-map", "change-plan"], "body": "# RACI Builder\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** clarity about who does what and who decides, so work stops falling between chairs, decisions stop being reopened, and control requirements such as segregation of duties are visibly met.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **One Accountable per row.** Two A's means none. The Accountable owns the outcome and signs off.\n2. **Roles, not names.** Matrices outlive people. Map names to roles separately.\n3. **Decisions deserve special treatment.** For the critical decisions, specify who recommends, who must agree (a veto), who gives input, who decides and who executes. Fewer vetoes means faster decisions.\n4. **Consult sparingly.** Each Consulted adds time. Include only those whose input changes the outcome, or who have a legal or control role.\n5. **Check the columns, not just the rows.** Overloaded roles (many R's or A's) and idle roles reveal design problems.\n6. **Controls built in.** Segregation of duties (preparer ≠ approver; requester ≠ payer) must be visible and respected.\n7. **Agree and publish.** A RACI that hasn't been agreed with the people in it is a draft.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Scope: a process, programme, forum, operating model.\n- Roles involved; known pain points (delays, disputes, audit findings).\n- Control requirements (financial, regulatory, AI or model governance).\n\n## Method\n1. **List activities and decisions** in process order, as verb + object (\"Approve vendor bank-detail change\"). Mark key decisions.\n2. **List roles** (columns).\n3. **Assign R, A, C, I;** exactly one A per row; at least one R.\n4. **For key decisions,** add the decision-rights view: recommend, agree (veto), input, decide, perform.\n5. **Row checks:** missing A or R; A without the authority to decide; too many C's.\n6. **Column checks:** overload (too many R's or A's for the role's capacity); roles with no involvement (unnecessary?); an A far from the work (too senior?).\n7. **Control checks:** segregation of duties (the same role can't be R for preparation and A for approval of the same transaction where controls require separation); delegation limits.\n8. **Escalation paths:** when there is disagreement or an exception, who decides, and in what time frame.\n9. **Validate** with role-holders; resolve conflicts with the sponsor; publish with a version and a review date.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **The A should sit at the lowest level with the authority and information** to decide. Pushing every A to the top creates bottlenecks.\n- **In matrices,** agree tie-breakers explicitly (e.g. product owner vs function head).\n- **Programmes:** distinguish the project RACI (temporary) from the business-as-usual RACI (permanent), and plan the handover.\n- **Regulated activities** may have mandated roles (e.g. a compliance officer's approval). Reflect them, and don't dilute them.\n\n## Red flags\n- Two A's on a row; no A.\n- Everyone consulted on everything.\n- Names instead of roles.\n- The same role preparing and approving payments.\n- A RACI never shared with the people in it.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Financial close and payments:** segregation of duties, approval limits, reconciliation ownership.\n- **AI and model governance:** model owner, validator (independent), risk approver, business owner, and the incident decision-maker.\n- **Programme governance:** sponsor, SteerCo, programme director, workstream leads, PMO.\n- **Shared services / GCC:** the service owner in the centre vs the business owner; SLA governance.\n- **Board and committees:** matters reserved to the board vs delegated to committees and management.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## RACI: [scope], v[x], [date]\n\n| Activity / decision | Role 1 | Role 2 | Role 3 | Role 4 | Role 5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Key decisions: decision rights**\n| Decision | Recommends | Agrees (veto) | Input | Decides | Performs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Checks:** rows without a single A: ... | overloaded roles: ... | excess C's: ... | segregation-of-duties conflicts: ...\n**Escalation paths:** ...\n**Open issues for sponsor:** ...\n**Role-to-name mapping (separate, confidential):** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Verb-object activities; roles as columns.\n- [ ] Exactly one A per row; at least one R.\n- [ ] Decision rights specified for key decisions.\n- [ ] Overload, consultation and segregation-of-duties checks done.\n- [ ] Escalation paths; a validation and publication plan.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `operating-model`: Design how the organisation will work: structure, processes, people, technology, governance and locations.\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n- `stakeholder-map`: Map who matters, where they stand, and the plan to move each of them.\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Responsibility assignment matrices (RACI and variants):** standard project- and process-management tools (e.g. ISO 21502:2020 roles and responsibilities; professional project-management standards).\n- **Decision-rights frameworks:** recommend, agree, input, decide and perform role definitions, widely used in organisation design (generic terms).\n- **Internal control:** segregation of duties and authorisation, as in internal-control frameworks (e.g. the COSO Internal Control - Integrated Framework); Companies Act 2013 s.134(5)(e) internal financial controls in India.\n- **AI governance roles:** NIST AI RMF GOVERN; ISO/IEC 42001 roles, responsibilities and authorities."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; group month-end close across business units, shared services and IT)\n\nRoles:\n- **GFC** = Group Financial Controller\n- **BUF** = BU Finance Head\n- **SSC** = Shared Services Lead\n- **SSA** = Shared Services Accountant\n- **IT** = ERP Support Lead\n- **IA** = Internal Audit\n\n| Activity / decision | GFC | BUF | SSC | SSA | IT | IA |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Publish close calendar | A | C | R | I | I | I |\n| Post accruals (BU-specific) | I | A | C | R | — | — |\n| Prepare intercompany reconciliations | I | C | A | R | — | — |\n| Approve intercompany adjustments > ₹50 lakh | **A** | C | R | — | — | I |\n| Approve manual journals > ₹1 Cr | A | C | R (prepares) | — | — | I |\n| Run ERP period close | I | I | A | — | R | — |\n| Review BU results and commentary | C | **A** | R | — | — | — |\n| Sign off group consolidation | **A** | C | R | — | — | I |\n| Test close controls | I | I | C | — | — | **A/R** |\n\n**Key decisions: decision rights**\n| Decision | Recommends | Agrees (veto) | Input | Decides | Performs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Post a material late adjustment after the close cut-off | SSC | BUF (for own BU) | IA | GFC | SSA |\n| Change accounting policy for a BU | BUF | Statutory auditor (consulted), Audit Committee (for material changes) | GFC team | CFO | SSC |\n\n**Checks:**\n- Earlier draft had 2 A's on \"Review BU results\" (BUF and GFC) → fixed: BUF A, GFC C.\n- **Segregation of duties:** the SSA prepares journals, and the SSC can't approve its own journals above ₹1 Cr; the GFC approves.\n- SSC overloaded at close (R on 5 of 9 rows) → add a close coordinator role.\n\n**Escalation:** a disagreement between BUF and SSC on an adjustment goes to the GFC within 24 hours during close."}]}, {"id": "change-plan", "pack": "deliver", "title": "Change Management Plan", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.", "tryp": "Build a change plan for rolling out an AI copilot to 3,000 relationship managers in a bank.", "when": ["System rollouts and process changes", "Reorganisations", "AI or automation adoption"], "inputs": ["What is changing and why", "Groups affected and how", "Timeline", "Known concerns"], "pairs": ["stakeholder-map", "implementation-roadmap", "ai-pilot-design", "workshop-design"], "body": "# Change Management Plan\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a change plan that makes adoption likely, because it is built around what changes for each group and why, how managers will lead it, how people will be supported, and how you'll know it's working before it's too late.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Adoption is the benefit.** A system or structure delivers value only when people use it as intended. Measure adoption and proficiency, not go-live.\n2. **Impact by group, not \"the organisation\".** Different groups lose and gain different things. Plan for each.\n3. **Sponsors must act visibly.** Active, visible sponsorship is consistently linked to success. Define specific sponsor behaviours.\n4. **Managers are the channel.** People hear about change from their manager first. Equip managers before staff.\n5. **Two-way communication.** Listen, adapt and close the loop. Change done *with* people sticks; change done *to* them doesn't.\n6. **Resistance is information.** Diagnose its cause (awareness, desire, knowledge, ability, reinforcement) and respond specifically.\n7. **Reinforce and remove old ways.** Switch off legacy tools, update incentives and recognise the new behaviours.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- What exactly is changing (process, systems, roles, structure, behaviours) and why.\n- Who is affected, and how much (numbers by group and location).\n- Change history and saturation (other changes running concurrently).\n- Culture and labour relations (unions, works councils), and legal requirements for consultation.\n- Timeline and go-live points.\n\n## Method\n1. **Case for change:** why, why now, what happens if we don't, what's in it for the organisation and for people. Honest about the downsides.\n2. **Impact assessment by group:** what changes (process, tools, role, skills, location, workload); degree (H/M/L); timing; likely reaction; specific concerns.\n3. **Readiness and capacity:** awareness, leadership support, change load, skills gaps (survey or interviews).\n4. **Sponsor coalition:** the executive sponsor and the sponsors in each affected unit; specific actions (announce, explain, attend training, remove barriers, recognise early adopters).\n5. **Manager enablement:** a briefing pack, a FAQ, talking points, escalation routes; managers trained before their teams.\n6. **Communication plan:** audience × message × channel × sender × timing; sequence (leaders → managers → staff); feedback channels (pulse surveys, Q&A, champions); respond to feedback visibly.\n7. **Training and support:** role-based, just-in-time, practice environments, floor walkers or super-users at go-live, help content, a support SLA.\n8. **Resistance management:** anticipate by group; diagnose the cause; respond (involve, adjust the design, coach, support, hold firm on non-negotiables with explanation).\n9. **Reinforcement:** incentives and KPIs aligned; recognition; decommission old tools and processes; celebrate milestones; follow-up coaching.\n10. **Metrics and checkpoints:**\n    - **Adoption:** usage, % of transactions in the new process.\n    - **Proficiency:** error rate, time per task.\n    - **Outcomes:** benefits.\n    - **Sentiment:** pulse surveys.\n    Review weekly around go-live, then monthly.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Change saturation is real.** If groups face several changes at once, sequence them or bundle the support.\n- **Job-impacting changes** (role loss, relocation) need legal process, early and respectful communication, and practical support (redeployment, reskilling, outplacement). Never spin.\n- **Champions networks work** when champions are respected peers with time and real influence, not volunteers with no capacity.\n- **AI adoption has unique fears** (job loss, being monitored, deskilling). Address them directly, with clear policies and evidence.\n\n## Red flags\n- Communication equated with change management.\n- Training once, weeks before go-live, and never again.\n- Measuring logins instead of proficiency and outcomes.\n- Managers learning about the change with their teams.\n- Old systems left running \"just in case\".\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Technology roll-out:** super-users, cutover support, hypercare, legacy switch-off.\n- **Reorganisation:** selection processes, a fair and transparent people process, new team formation, leader onboarding.\n- **AI and automation:** clear human-AI roles, policies on monitoring, reskilling pathways, quality safeguards.\n- **M&A:** culture integration, retention of key talent, a \"day 1\" communication, a joint leadership signal.\n- **Frontline and distributed workforces:** mobile-first communication, shift-friendly training, local languages.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Change Plan: [change]\n**Case for change:** ...\n\n| Group | # people | What changes | Impact | Likely reaction / concerns | Key message | Training / support | Sponsor / manager actions |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Sponsor coalition and actions:** ...\n**Manager enablement:** ...\n**Communication timeline:** week - audience - message - channel - sender - feedback loop\n**Resistance plan:** group - likely cause - response\n**Reinforcement:** ...\n**Metrics and checkpoints:** adoption / proficiency / outcomes / sentiment - target - date\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] An honest case for change, including what's in it for the people affected.\n- [ ] Impact by group, with degree and concerns.\n- [ ] Specific sponsor actions; managers equipped first.\n- [ ] Two-way communications sequenced; role-based training and go-live support.\n- [ ] Resistance diagnosed by cause; reinforcement and legacy switch-off.\n- [ ] Metrics beyond logins, with checkpoints.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `stakeholder-map`: Map who matters, where they stand, and the plan to move each of them.\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n- `ai-pilot-design`: Design an AI pilot that proves value with clear success criteria, guardrails and a scale decision.\n- `workshop-design`: Design a workshop or offsite that produces decisions, not just discussion.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts; verify legal specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Organisational change management:** established models covering the case for change, guiding coalitions, communication, empowerment, short-term wins and anchoring change in culture; individual-transition models (awareness, desire, knowledge, ability, reinforcement). Taught widely in change-management curricula.\n- **Research on sponsorship and adoption:** practitioner and academic studies consistently link active, visible sponsorship and manager involvement to successful outcomes.\n- **ISO 10018:2020** (quality management: guidance for people engagement) and **ISO 30414** (human-capital reporting) for people metrics.\n- **Employment law and consultation:** applicable labour laws and works-council or union consultation requirements (India: Industrial Relations Code 2020 and state rules; verify with HR and counsel).\n- **AI at work:** employer guidance on transparency and monitoring; data-protection obligations (DPDP Act 2023) for employee data."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional bank rolling out an AI copilot to 3,000 relationship managers)\n\n**Case for change:**\n- Relationship managers (RMs) spend ~40% of their time on notes, proposals and compliance documentation.\n- The copilot drafts call notes, proposal sections and product comparisons from approved sources, freeing an estimated 5 hours a week for clients.\n- It does not make credit or suitability decisions; RMs remain accountable.\n- Not acting means falling behind peers on service speed, and continued compliance-documentation errors (22% of audit findings).\n\n| Group | # | What changes | Impact | Concerns | Key message | Support | Sponsor / manager actions |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RMs (affluent) | 2,100 | Notes and proposals drafted by AI; RM reviews | High | \"Will AI replace me? Will it be used to monitor me?\" | \"It takes the paperwork; you keep the relationship. No individual productivity rankings from copilot data\" (policy) | 2-hour hands-on training; 120 champions; in-app tips | Regional heads use it in their own reviews; share wins weekly |\n| RMs (business banking) | 900 | Same + credit-memo drafting support | High | \"Credit memos must be accurate\" | \"Credit judgement stays yours; drafts cite sources\" | Credit-memo clinic; checker guidance | Credit heads co-design templates |\n| Team leaders | 260 | Coach usage; review quality | Medium | \"More work for me?\" | \"Fewer rework loops on documentation\" | Leader briefing a week before teams; FAQ | Weekly usage and quality huddle |\n| Compliance and QA | 40 | New review of AI-assisted documents | Medium | \"New risks\" | \"Every draft cites its source; you get a sampling dashboard\" | Early access; co-define the QA checklist | Head of Compliance endorses |\n| Employee union representatives | — | Policy on monitoring and roles | — | Job security | Formal briefing; written policy | — | CHRO meeting before launch |\n\n**Sponsor coalition:**\n- The Head of Retail Banking announces, explains why, and commits to \"no ranking by copilot metrics\".\n- Regional heads demo it in town halls.\n\n**Communication timeline (extract):**\n- Week −4: leaders.\n- Week −3: team leaders.\n- Week −2: union briefing and policy published.\n- Week −1: RM town halls and Q&A.\n- Go-live: daily tips.\n- Weeks 1-8: pulse survey every 2 weeks; \"you said, we did\" updates.\n\n**Resistance plan:**\n- Fear of monitoring → published policy + union endorsement.\n- Accuracy doubts → source citations + champion demos with real cases.\n- Low ability in some regions → floor walkers and regional-language guides.\n\n**Reinforcement:**\n- Documentation quality added to the QA scorecard.\n- Recognition for teams sharing best prompts.\n- The old proposal templates retired at week 10.\n\n**Metrics:**\n| Type | Metric | Target | Date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Adoption | Weekly active RMs | ≥70% | Week 8 |\n| Proficiency | Notes completion time | −40% | Week 8 |\n| Outcome | Client meetings per RM per week | +15% | Q2 |\n| Quality | Documentation audit findings | −50% | Q3 |\n| Sentiment | \"Copilot helps me serve clients\" | ≥65% agree | Week 6 |"}]}, {"id": "risk-assessment", "pack": "deliver", "title": "Risk Assessment", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Identify, score and plan responses to the risks of a project, decision or business.", "tryp": "Do a risk assessment for launching our lending app in Indonesia in 2027.", "when": ["Project or programme planning", "Investment cases and board papers", "Periodic risk reviews"], "inputs": ["The project or decision", "Context: scope, timeline, constraints", "Known risks or past issues"], "pairs": ["pre-mortem", "risk-appetite", "ai-risk-register", "implementation-roadmap"], "body": "# Risk Assessment\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a register that identifies the risks that could really derail the objective, tells you which controls actually work, and assigns clear actions and early-warning indicators, instead of a generic list nobody reads.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Risk is uncertainty about objectives.** Start from what you're trying to achieve; risks are what could stop it (or create upside opportunities).\n2. **Write risks properly:** \"Because of [cause], [event] may occur, leading to [consequence].\" This distinguishes risks from issues (already happening) and from vague worries.\n3. **Calibrated scales.** Define what each likelihood and impact level means (in ₹, time, safety, reputation, regulatory) so ratings are comparable and defensible.\n4. **Controls are assessed, not listed.** Is the control designed well, and does it operate? Evidence beats assertion.\n5. **Residual vs appetite.** Compare residual risk with the organisation's appetite; act where it exceeds tolerance.\n6. **Owners and indicators.** Each significant risk has one owner and a key risk indicator (KRI) that moves before the risk materialises.\n7. **Interdependencies and concentrations.** Risks cluster (e.g. one vendor behind three risks). Show them.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Objectives and scope (project, unit, decision); time horizon.\n- Risk appetite and existing scales (use the organisation's if they exist).\n- Known incidents, audit findings and near misses.\n- Stakeholders to involve (operations, finance, legal, IT, HR, partners).\n\n## Method\n1. **Context:** objectives, scope, key assumptions, stakeholders, internal and external context.\n2. **Identify risks** using several lenses:\n   - **Categories:** strategic, financial, operational, technology and cyber, people, legal and regulatory, third-party and supply chain, reputational, ESG, AI.\n   - **Process walk-through:** what can fail at each step.\n   - **Assumptions:** what if each key assumption is wrong.\n   - **Past incidents** and peers' failures.\n   - **Pre-mortem** (see `pre-mortem`).\n3. **Write each risk** as cause-event-consequence. Merge duplicates; separate issues into an issue log.\n4. **Rate inherent risk:** likelihood (1-5) × impact (1-5) using defined scales. Note the rationale.\n5. **Controls:** list existing preventive and detective controls; rate effectiveness (effective, partially effective, ineffective) with evidence.\n6. **Residual rating** after controls; compare with appetite and tolerance.\n7. **Treatment:**\n   - **reduce** (new controls or actions);\n   - **accept** (record who accepts, why, until when);\n   - **transfer** (insurance, contracts);\n   - **avoid** (change the plan).\n   Assign an owner and a due date.\n8. **KRIs:** a leading indicator and threshold per significant risk.\n9. **Interdependencies:** link related risks; identify concentrations and single points of failure.\n10. **Summary:** a heat map (inherent → residual), the top 5-10 risks, actions overdue, risks outside appetite, and emerging risks.\n11. **Review cadence:** monthly for projects, quarterly for business units; on major change.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Opportunities** (upside risks) can be included. Many decisions need both sides.\n- **Low-likelihood, high-impact risks** (cyber, safety, fraud, regulatory) need attention even if their scores look moderate. Consider scenario and stress tests.\n- **Avoid the \"everything amber\" register.** If nothing is red, ask whether the scales are calibrated or whether people are reluctant to report.\n- **Risk acceptance is a decision** by someone with authority, not a default.\n\n## Red flags\n- Risks written as single words (\"Cyber\", \"People\").\n- Issues logged as risks.\n- Controls listed without effectiveness ratings.\n- No owners or KRIs; actions without dates.\n- Ratings unchanged quarter after quarter.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Projects:** schedule, cost, scope, vendor, integration, adoption; contingency linked to quantified risks.\n- **Investments and M&A:** valuation, integration, key-person, regulatory approval, hidden liabilities.\n- **Operations:** safety, quality, supply continuity, asset failure, cyber and OT.\n- **Financial services:** credit, market, liquidity, operational, conduct, model and AI risk; regulatory taxonomies.\n- **AI systems:** use `ai-risk-register` for AI-specific taxonomies.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Risk Assessment: [scope], [date]\n**Objectives:** ...  |  **Scales:** likelihood 1-5 (...), impact 1-5 (...)\n\n| # | Risk (cause - event - consequence) | Category | L | I | Inherent | Controls (effectiveness) | Residual | vs appetite | Treatment | Owner | Due | KRI (threshold) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Heat map:** inherent → residual (counts by zone)\n**Top risks:** ...\n**Accepted risks:** risk - accepted by - rationale - until\n**Interdependencies / concentrations:** ...\n**Issues (already happening):** ...\n**Review cadence:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Risks are tied to objectives and written as cause-event-consequence.\n- [ ] Calibrated scales are defined; the rating rationale is noted.\n- [ ] Control effectiveness is assessed with evidence.\n- [ ] Residual risk is compared with appetite; treatments have owners and dates.\n- [ ] KRIs with thresholds for significant risks.\n- [ ] Interdependencies, accepted risks and issues are separated and shown.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `pre-mortem`: Imagine the project has failed, explain why, and fix those causes now.\n- `risk-appetite`: Draft a board risk appetite statement with qualitative positions and measurable limits.\n- `ai-risk-register`: Build a risk register for AI use cases: classify, score, control and assign owners.\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards; verify sector specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **ISO 31000:2018:** risk management principles, framework and process (risk = effect of uncertainty on objectives).\n- **ISO 31010:2019:** risk assessment techniques (e.g. bow-tie, scenario analysis, FMEA, checklists).\n- **COSO Enterprise Risk Management: Integrating with Strategy and Performance (2017).**\n- **India:** Companies Act 2013 (s.134(3)(n) risk management policy statement; s.177 audit committee evaluation of risk management); SEBI LODR Reg 17(9) and Reg 21 (risk management committee for the top 1,000 listed entities); sector regulators' risk-management directions (RBI, IRDAI, SEBI-intermediaries).\n- **Operational resilience:** Basel Committee principles (2021); RBI guidance note on operational risk management and operational resilience (2024)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional Indian digital lender launching in Indonesia in 2027)\n\n**Objectives:**\n- Launch by Q3 2027.\n- 100,000 active borrowers by end-2028.\n- Credit loss ≤4%.\n- No regulatory sanctions.\n\n**Scales:**\n- **Impact 5:** >$5M loss, launch delay >12 months, regulatory sanction or licence risk.\n- **Impact 4:** $2-5M or a 6-12 month delay.\n- **Likelihood 5:** >50% in the horizon.\n- **Likelihood 4:** 30-50%.\n- **Likelihood 3:** 10-30%.\n\n| # | Risk | Cat. | L | I | Inh. | Controls (effectiveness) | Res. | vs appetite | Treatment | Owner | Due | KRI |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Because licences require a local partner, approval may take 12-18 months, delaying launch and burning ~$4M | Regulatory | 4 | 5 | 20 | Local counsel engaged (effective) | 15 | Outside | **Reduce:** shortlist 3 licensed partners; pursue partnership and licence in parallel | Head Strategy | Feb 2027 | Regulator meeting scheduled by Feb |\n| 2 | Because we lack local credit data, underwriting models may under-predict default, causing losses above 4% | Credit / model | 3 | 4 | 12 | Bureau partnership (partial); conservative limits (effective) | 8 | Within tolerance | Reduce: test-and-learn limits; champion-challenger models | CRO | Ongoing | 30+ DPD on first cohorts >6% |\n| 3 | Because aggressive third-party collection practices are common, agents may breach conduct rules, causing sanctions and reputational damage | Conduct | 3 | 5 | 15 | Code of conduct (design only) | 12 | Outside | Reduce: in-house collections for 90 days; call recording; complaint triggers | Head Ops | Before launch | Complaints per 1,000 loans |\n| 4 | Because personal data crosses borders for model training, we may breach local data-protection law | Legal / privacy | 3 | 5 | 15 | Not yet designed | 15 | Outside | **Avoid:** train in-country; anonymise; legal opinion | DPO | Q1 2027 | — |\n| 5 | Because one cloud region hosts all services, an outage may halt disbursements | Technology | 2 | 4 | 8 | Multi-zone (effective) | 4 | Within | Accept (CTO, to Dec 2027) | CTO | — | Uptime <99.9% monthly |\n\n**Heat map:** inherent 3 red, 2 amber → residual 1 red (#1), 3 amber, 1 green.\n**Concentration:** risks 1 and 3 both depend on partner selection, so it is a key decision.\n**Issue (not a risk):** the country head is not yet hired (already happening) → issue log."}]}, {"id": "pre-mortem", "pack": "deliver", "title": "Pre-mortem", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Imagine the project has failed, explain why, and fix those causes now.", "tryp": "Run a pre-mortem: it's December 2027 and our ERP migration has failed badly. Why?", "when": ["Before launching a project or approving an investment", "When a team seems overconfident", "Before a big commitment that is hard to reverse"], "inputs": ["The plan, decision or project", "Timeline and success criteria"], "pairs": ["risk-assessment", "red-team-review", "hypothesis-tree", "implementation-roadmap"], "body": "# Pre-mortem\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** the failure modes your team already suspects but hasn't said, surfaced safely before launch, and converted into concrete changes and early-warning tripwires.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Assume failure as a fact.** \"It is [date]. The project failed badly. Why?\" Imagining failure as certain releases more and better explanations than asking \"what could go wrong?\"\n2. **Independent generation first.** Everyone writes failure reasons silently before any discussion, to counter groupthink and seniority bias.\n3. **Psychological safety.** The most junior or the most sceptical voices often know the real risks. The leader speaks last.\n4. **Root causes and broken assumptions.** Cluster stories into causes; name the assumption each one breaks.\n5. **Change the plan, not just the risk log.** The output is plan changes and tripwires, not a longer list.\n6. **Tripwires have pre-agreed responses.** Decide now what you'll do if the signal appears, while judgement isn't clouded by sunk cost.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The plan or decision, its success criteria, timeline and key assumptions.\n- Participants: a cross-functional mix, including frontline, sceptics, and someone external if possible.\n- Format: a 60-90 minute workshop, or an asynchronous written exercise.\n\n## Method\n1. **Brief:** the plan in 5 minutes; success criteria; the main assumptions.\n2. **Set the scene:** \"It is [date, e.g. 18 months from now]. The project has failed clearly: [define failure, e.g. benefits <30% of plan, a major incident, cancelled].\"\n3. **Silent generation (5-10 min):** each person writes 3-7 reasons, as specific stories.\n4. **Round-robin share:** one reason per person per round until exhausted; no debate yet; capture verbatim.\n5. **Cluster** into themes (people, process, technology, market, money, politics, external); identify root causes and broken assumptions.\n6. **Rate** each cluster on likelihood (1-5) and severity (1-5). Discuss outliers; prioritise the top 5-8.\n7. **For each priority cause:**\n   - **Preventive action** (what we change now), with an owner and a date.\n   - **Tripwire:** an observable early signal with a threshold.\n   - **Pre-agreed response** if the tripwire fires (pause, add resources, change scope, stop).\n8. **Plan changes:** consolidate into explicit changes to scope, timeline, budget, governance or design.\n9. **Close:** \"What's the failure we're still not talking about?\" Then capture any final items.\n10. **Record and follow up:** add to the risk register; review the tripwires at each SteerCo.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Facilitate neutrally.** If the sponsor facilitates, people self-censor. Use a neutral facilitator.\n- **Don't let it become a complaint session.** Steer toward specific, actionable failure mechanisms.\n- **A pre-mortem with no plan changes probably failed.** Push for at least 2-3 real changes.\n- **Repeat at major gates** with fresh eyes. Failure modes change as the project progresses.\n\n## Red flags\n- Generic risks (\"lack of resources\") without mechanisms.\n- The leader opens with their own view.\n- No tripwires or pre-agreed responses.\n- Outputs filed and never revisited.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Investment decisions:** failure = value destruction; include valuation, integration, market and key-person causes.\n- **Technology roll-outs:** data, integration, adoption, cutover and vendor causes.\n- **Strategy:** competitor response, customer adoption, capability gaps, execution bandwidth.\n- **Events and launches:** logistics, communications, safety, reputational triggers.\n- **AI deployments:** model errors, misuse, data leakage, adoption, regulatory and trust failures.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Pre-mortem: [plan], failure date [..]\n**Failure defined as:** ...  |  **Participants:** roles\n\n| Root cause (cluster) | Example failure stories | Broken assumption | L | S | Preventive action (owner, date) | Tripwire (threshold) | Pre-agreed response |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Changes to the plan:** ...\n**Still not talking about:** ...\n**Follow-up:** added to the risk register; tripwires reviewed at [forum]\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Failure defined concretely at a future date.\n- [ ] Independent generation before discussion (or simulated, if done solo).\n- [ ] Root causes and broken assumptions identified.\n- [ ] Top causes have preventive actions, tripwires and pre-agreed responses.\n- [ ] Concrete plan changes; a follow-up mechanism.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `risk-assessment`: Identify, score and plan responses to the risks of a project, decision or business.\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n- `hypothesis-tree`: Start from a likely answer, then list exactly what must be true and how you would prove or kill it.\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Pre-mortem / prospective hindsight:** a decision-science technique. Research on prospective hindsight finds that imagining an event has already occurred increases the ability to identify reasons for the outcome.\n- **Groupthink and debiasing:** independent idea generation (nominal group technique), devil's advocacy, and psychological safety, from organisational-behaviour research.\n- **Tripwires and pre-commitment:** decision-making practices that set signals and responses in advance to counter sunk-cost and escalation-of-commitment biases.\n- **Risk assessment techniques:** ISO 31010:2019 (e.g. scenario analysis, structured \"what-if\")."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; ERP migration for a 14-entity group, go-live planned April 2027)\n\n**Failure defined as:** it is December 2027. Go-live slipped twice, month-end close takes 15 days (from 8), and the programme is 45% over budget.\n**Participants:** finance controllers, IT, plant accountants, an implementation-partner lead, internal audit, and 2 frontline AP clerks.\n\n| Root cause | Example stories | Broken assumption | L | S | Preventive action | Tripwire | Response |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Data migration underestimated | \"Vendor master had 30% duplicates\"; \"Open items didn't reconcile at cutover\" | Legacy data is ~90% clean | 4 | 5 | Data profiling now; dedicated cleansing squad of 6 (CFO, Jan) | <70% of vendor records pass validation by month 3 | Add a cleansing vendor; move go-live by one quarter |\n| Business SMEs pulled back into day jobs | \"Controllers missed design workshops during the audit season\" | SMEs can give 50% of their time | 4 | 4 | Backfill 8 SMEs; protect their calendars (CHRO, Dec) | SME attendance <70% for 2 weeks | Escalate to the CFO; pause design sign-off |\n| Custom changes bloat scope | \"We rebuilt the old reports in the new system\" | Standard processes are acceptable | 3 | 4 | Design authority; change requests need a business case (Programme Director) | >25 change requests by the design freeze | Design authority rejects non-essential ones; re-baseline |\n| Testing compressed | \"UAT squeezed to 2 weeks; close failed in parallel run\" | Build finishes on time | 3 | 5 | Two full parallel closes required before go-live (non-negotiable) | Parallel close variance >0.5% | No go-live |\n| Partner staffing churn | \"Three architects rotated off\" | Partner keeps its core team | 3 | 3 | Contractual key-person clause and penalties | Any named architect leaves | Invoke the clause; replacement within 2 weeks |\n\n**Changes to the plan:**\n1. Add a 6-week data-cleansing phase now.\n2. Make 2 parallel closes a hard gate.\n3. Backfill SMEs (₹1.4 Cr added to the budget).\n4. A design authority with change control.\n\n**Still not talking about:** \"The acquired entities' processes are completely different. We haven't mapped them.\" → Add a discovery sprint for the acquired entities.\n\n**Follow-up:** all items in the risk register; tripwires reviewed at every monthly SteerCo."}]}, {"id": "steerco-update", "pack": "deliver", "title": "SteerCo Update", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Write a crisp steering committee update: status, decisions needed, risks, and next steps.", "tryp": "Write this month's SteerCo update for our CRM programme from these notes: [paste notes].", "when": ["Programme and project status reporting", "SteerCo or board programme updates", "When status reports are long and say nothing"], "inputs": ["Progress notes, milestones, KPIs, risks, issues", "Decisions you need"], "pairs": ["implementation-roadmap", "benefits-tracker", "risk-assessment", "executive-summary"], "body": "# SteerCo Update\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a status update that gets the SteerCo to make the decisions only it can make, tells the truth early, and takes five minutes to read.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Decisions first.** The SteerCo's job is to decide and unblock. Lead with what you need from them, with options and a recommendation.\n2. **Honest status with defined criteria.** Green, amber and red are defined in advance (e.g. amber = at risk but recoverable within tolerance with current resources; red = needs SteerCo intervention or will breach tolerance). Report trends, not just colours.\n3. **Forecast, not just progress.** Show forecast dates vs baseline, and forecast cost at completion vs budget. Explain variances.\n4. **Benefits, not only activity.** Once in delivery, report adoption and benefits against plan.\n5. **Risks and issues with owners and dates.** Show what's changed since last time.\n6. **One page, consistent format** every period, so trends are visible.\n7. **No surprises.** Pre-wire the sponsor on reds before the meeting.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The audience (SteerCo membership) and their decision rights.\n- Baseline plan, budget, benefits and tolerances.\n- This period's progress data, risks, issues and decisions needed.\n- The previous update (for trends and commitments).\n\n## Method\n1. **Decisions required:** for each, the context in one line, options with consequences, the recommendation, and the deadline.\n2. **Overall status:** a colour by the defined criteria, with the trend (↑ ↓ →) and a one-line reason. Sub-status for schedule, cost, scope, benefits, risk and people.\n3. **Milestones:** baseline vs forecast vs actual; variance reason; recovery action.\n4. **Budget:** approved, spent to date, committed, forecast at completion; variance and drivers; contingency used.\n5. **Benefits and KPIs:** plan vs actual for adoption and benefits (if in delivery).\n6. **Top risks and issues (3-5 each):** what changed, owner, action, date; escalations.\n7. **Dependencies and asks:** what you need from other teams or programmes.\n8. **Achievements (brief) and next-period focus.**\n9. **Commitments from the last meeting:** done / not done.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Amber is not a hiding place.** If an item has been amber for three periods without recovery, call it red.\n- **Bring options, not problems.** Even for reds, propose paths and trade-offs.\n- **Separate facts from forecasts.** Label estimates and confidence.\n- **Tailor depth.** A board programme update is shorter, more about benefits, risk and decisions; a working SteerCo needs more operational detail.\n\n## Red flags\n- Activity lists (\"held 12 workshops\") instead of progress against milestones.\n- Status always green until suddenly red.\n- Decisions buried at the end, or none.\n- Budget shown as spent-to-date only, with no forecast.\n- Risks unchanged for months.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Technology programmes:** testing progress (pass rates, defects by severity), cutover readiness, data migration status.\n- **Transformation:** initiative pipeline and benefits banked (see `benefits-tracker`).\n- **M&A integration:** day-100 plan, synergy capture, people retention, customer churn.\n- **AI programmes:** use-case pipeline by stage, model performance, adoption, incidents, governance approvals.\n- **Board-level programme reporting:** quarterly, benefits- and risk-focused, linked to the original business case.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## SteerCo Update: [programme], [period]\n**Overall:** 🟢/🟠/🔴 (trend ↑/→/↓) - reason\nSchedule 🟠 | Cost 🟢 | Scope 🟢 | Benefits 🟠 | Risk 🟠 | People 🟢\n\n### Decisions required today\n| Decision | Options (consequences) | Recommendation | Needed by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Milestones\n| Milestone | Baseline | Forecast | Status | Variance reason / recovery |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Budget\nApproved ... | Spent ... | Committed ... | Forecast at completion ... | Variance ... | Contingency used ...%\n\n### Benefits / KPIs (plan vs actual)\n### Top risks and issues (changes since last)\n| Item | Type | Owner | Action | Date | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Dependencies / asks\n### Last period's commitments: done / open\n### Next period focus\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Decisions first, with options and a recommendation.\n- [ ] Status uses defined criteria, with a trend and a reason; no long-running amber.\n- [ ] Milestone and cost forecasts vs baseline, with variance reasons.\n- [ ] Benefits reported when in delivery.\n- [ ] Risks and issues with owners, dates and changes.\n- [ ] One page, consistent format.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n- `benefits-tracker`: Make sure promised benefits are defined, owned, measured and actually delivered.\n- `risk-assessment`: Identify, score and plan responses to the risks of a project, decision or business.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Programme and project governance:** ISO 21502:2020 (project management) and ISO 21503 (programme management): reporting, tolerances, escalation.\n- **Management by exception:** tolerance-based escalation, a widely used project-governance principle.\n- **Earned value and forecasting basics:** forecast at completion, variance analysis (project-controls practice).\n- **Benefits management:** public-sector programme guidance on tracking benefits during delivery."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; CRM programme, month 7 of 14)\n\n## SteerCo Update: Customer 360 CRM, October\n**Overall:** 🟠 (trend ↓). Data migration is 3 weeks late because duplicate customer records are 38% (vs 10% assumed). Go-live is at risk without a decision today.\nSchedule 🔴 | Cost 🟢 | Scope 🟢 | Benefits 🟠 | Risk 🟠 | People 🟢\n\n### Decisions required today\n| Decision | Options (consequences) | Recommendation | Needed by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Recover data-migration delay | A) ₹45 lakh for a data-cleansing vendor: holds the Feb go-live. B) Accept a 6-week delay: no added cost, but ₹1.2 Cr of benefits deferred. C) Go live with dirty data: not recommended (sales adoption risk) | **A** | Today (vendor needs 2 weeks to mobilise) |\n\n### Milestones\n| Milestone | Baseline | Forecast | Status | Reason / recovery |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Design sign-off | Jun | Jun (done) | ✅ | — |\n| Data migration trial 2 | 15 Sep | 6 Oct | 🔴 | Duplicates; recovery via decision A |\n| UAT complete | 30 Nov | 30 Nov (if A) | 🟠 | Depends on the decision |\n| Go-live (wave 1) | 1 Feb | 1 Feb (if A) / 15 Mar (if B) | 🟠 | — |\n\n### Budget\nApproved ₹8.4 Cr | Spent ₹4.1 Cr | Committed ₹2.2 Cr | Forecast at completion ₹8.3 Cr (₹8.75 Cr with A, covered by contingency) | Contingency used 18%\n\n### Benefits / KPIs\nPilot sales team adoption 74% (target 70%) ✅ | lead response time −30% (target −25%) ✅\n\n### Top risks and issues\n| Item | Type | Owner | Action | Date | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Duplicate records | Issue | Data lead | Decision A; matching rules | 20 Oct | New |\n| Sales manager adoption beyond the pilot | Risk | Head Sales | Manager dashboards; usage in pipeline reviews | Nov | ↑ |\n| Integration with billing | Risk | IT lead | API performance test | 15 Nov | → |\n\n### Dependencies / asks\nThe billing team must deliver API changes by 10 Nov (confirmed by their lead).\n\n### Last period's commitments\n- Sales champions named ✅\n- Data-quality report ✅\n- Billing API date confirmed ✅\n\n### Next period\nMobilise the cleansing vendor; migration trial 3; start UAT scripts."}]}, {"id": "benefits-tracker", "pack": "deliver", "title": "Benefits Tracker", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Make sure promised benefits are defined, owned, measured and actually delivered.", "tryp": "Build a benefits tracker for our procurement transformation that promised ₹60 Cr savings over 3 years.", "when": ["After investment approval", "Transformation programmes", "Post-implementation reviews"], "inputs": ["The business case or promised benefits", "Data sources for measures", "Owners"], "pairs": ["business-case", "kpi-driver-tree", "steerco-update", "cost-takeout"], "body": "# Benefits Tracker\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** confidence that promised benefits will actually show up in the numbers, because each one is defined, owned by the business, measured against a fixed baseline, banked in budgets, and tracked with early indicators.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Benefits are owned by the business,** not the programme. The person whose budget or KPI changes owns the benefit.\n2. **Baseline first.** Record each baseline with its date, source and method before implementation starts. Benefits measured against moving baselines are fiction.\n3. **One benefit, one line.** Don't bundle; each has its own measure, owner and timing.\n4. **Bank cash benefits.** Reduce budgets, adjust hiring plans or lift revenue targets when benefits are committed; otherwise savings get absorbed.\n5. **Leading indicators.** Adoption, process metrics and usage predict benefits months before financials do.\n6. **Avoid double counting** across initiatives and programmes; reconcile with Finance.\n7. **Track beyond go-live.** Most benefits arrive after the project closes. Assign continued ownership and a post-implementation review.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The approved business case and its benefits.\n- Current baselines (or how to measure them now).\n- Owners' budgets and KPIs; other initiatives claiming similar benefits.\n- Finance's rules on what counts as banked.\n\n## Method\n1. **Benefits map:** link outcomes → benefits → enabling changes (process, technology, people) → project outputs. Identify dependencies.\n2. **Register each benefit:**\n   - description;\n   - type: cash-releasing, non-cash efficiency, revenue, working capital, risk reduction, quality or customer, ESG;\n   - measure and formula;\n   - data source and frequency;\n   - baseline value, date and method;\n   - target and timing profile (by quarter);\n   - owner;\n   - dependencies;\n   - how banked;\n   - confidence (H/M/L).\n3. **Dis-benefits:** record negative effects too (e.g. temporary productivity dip, higher licence cost).\n4. **Leading indicators:** adoption, usage, throughput, error rate; with targets.\n5. **Double-count check:** map benefits across initiatives; agree attribution rules with Finance.\n6. **Tracking template:** plan vs actual by period, with variance commentary and recovery actions.\n7. **Governance:** monthly owner review; quarterly SteerCo or board reporting; escalation for benefits at risk; benefits sign-off by Finance.\n8. **Post-implementation review:** date, scope (benefits, lessons, sustained adoption), reviewer independence.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Capacity benefits need a plan.** \"Hours saved\" become cash only if roles are not backfilled, hiring is avoided, or capacity is redeployed to measurable value. State which.\n- **Attribution is messy.** When several initiatives affect one KPI, agree attribution upfront (e.g. a control group or rules) rather than arguing later.\n- **Benefits can be re-planned, not silently dropped.** Re-baselining needs approval and a reason.\n- **Celebrate real realisation.** Credible benefit reporting builds trust for future investment.\n\n## Red flags\n- A project manager listed as benefit owner.\n- No baseline, or a baseline taken after changes started.\n- Bundled benefits (\"efficiency and quality\").\n- Savings counted but budgets unchanged.\n- Tracking stops at go-live.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Cost programmes:** link to `cost-takeout` stage gates (L0-L4); Finance validation monthly.\n- **Technology:** adoption leading indicators; decommissioning benefits.\n- **Revenue initiatives:** control groups or holdouts for attribution; margin, not just revenue.\n- **AI programmes:** adoption × accuracy × redeployment (see `ai-business-case`); monitor rework.\n- **Public sector:** social value, citizen outcomes, and value-for-money reviews.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Benefits Register and Tracker: [programme]\n\n| # | Benefit | Type | Measure / formula | Baseline (value, date, source) | Target and timing | Owner | Dependencies | How banked | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Dis-benefits:** ...\n**Leading indicators:** indicator - target - current\n**Double-count / attribution rules:** ...\n\n### Tracking (by quarter)\n| Benefit | Plan Q1 | Actual Q1 | Plan Q2 | Actual Q2 | Cumulative vs plan | Variance commentary / recovery |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Governance and review rhythm:** ...\n**Post-implementation review:** date, scope, reviewer\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Each benefit is separate, with a type, formula, owner (business) and confidence.\n- [ ] Baselines are dated and sourced before the change.\n- [ ] Banking method for cash benefits; dis-benefits recorded.\n- [ ] Leading indicators defined; double counting checked with Finance.\n- [ ] A tracking template with variance commentary; post-implementation review scheduled.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Plans and estimates are starting points. Validate effort, cost and people impacts with the teams who will deliver them, and follow your HR and legal processes for people changes.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `kpi-driver-tree`: Break a top metric into the drivers people can act on, with owners and targets.\n- `steerco-update`: Write a crisp steering committee update: status, decisions needed, risks, and next steps.\n- `cost-takeout`: Build a structured cost-reduction plan: baseline, levers, sizing, phasing and risks to service.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and guidance; verify legal specifics)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Benefits management:** public-sector programme guidance on benefits maps, registers, profiles and realisation reviews; ISO 21503 (programme management) on benefits.\n- **Investment appraisal and post-implementation review:** public appraisal guidance (e.g. UK HM Treasury *Green Book* on monitoring and evaluation).\n- **India MSME payment rules:** MSMED Act, 2006 provisions on payment timelines to MSME suppliers, and the related income-tax provision on deductibility of delayed MSME payments (verify the current rules) before extending payment terms.\n- **Management accounting:** budgeting and variance analysis for banking benefits."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; procurement transformation promising ₹60 Cr over 3 years)\n\n| # | Benefit | Type | Measure | Baseline | Target and timing | Owner | Dependencies | How banked | Conf. |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Packaging price reduction | Cash-releasing | (Baseline price − new price) × volume | ₹182 Cr spend at FY26 prices (ERP, Mar 2026) | 7% = ₹12.7 Cr/yr from Q2 FY27 | CPO | Supplier consolidation (3 → 2); spec change | BU budgets cut on contract signature | High |\n| 2 | Logistics rate renegotiation | Cash-releasing | Rate × lanes × volume | ₹96 Cr (FY26) | 5% = ₹4.8 Cr/yr from Q3 FY27 | Head Logistics | Tender | Logistics budget | Medium |\n| 3 | Indirect spend under contract | Cash-releasing | Leakage reduction on off-contract spend | 38% off-contract (Jan 2026 audit) | 15% by Q4 FY27; ₹6 Cr/yr | CFO | Procure-to-pay workflow; catalogue | Opex budgets | Medium |\n| 4 | Working capital: payment terms 45 → 60 days | Working capital (one-off cash) | Δdays × daily spend | 45 days (Mar 2026) | 60 days for 70% of ~₹1,200 Cr annual spend by Q4 FY27 = 15 days × ₹3.3 Cr/day × 70% ≈ ₹35 Cr one-off cash | Treasurer | Supplier negotiation; MSME exclusion (statutory payment limits for MSME suppliers) | Cash-flow plan | Medium |\n| 5 | Procurement team productivity | Non-cash | POs per buyer per month | 210 (FY26) | 300 by FY28 | CPO | Workflow tool | Hiring avoided: 4 FTE (₹0.8 Cr) | Medium |\n\n**Dis-benefits:** procurement-tool licences (₹1.1 Cr/yr); a temporary dip in PO cycle time during roll-out.\n**Leading indicators:** % of spend on new contracts; catalogue usage; off-contract spend %; supplier acceptance of the new terms.\n**Attribution:** packaging savings from product redesign by R&D are excluded from #1 (claimed by the R&D programme); Finance-agreed rule.\n\n**Tracking (FY27, ₹ Cr):**\n| Benefit | Plan Q1 | Actual Q1 | Plan Q2 | Actual Q2 | Cumulative vs plan | Commentary |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0 | 0 | 3.2 | 2.6 | −0.6 | One supplier delayed the switch by 6 weeks; recovered in Q3 |\n| 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | Tender closes in Q2 |\n| 3 | 0.5 | 0.3 | 1.0 | 1.1 | −0.1 | Catalogue adoption 62% (target 70%) |\n\n**Governance:** monthly owner review; quarterly SteerCo; Finance sign-off on banked amounts.\n**Post-implementation review:** Q2 FY29, by internal audit."}]}, {"id": "board-paper", "pack": "board", "title": "Board Paper", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Write a board paper directors can read in 10 minutes: purpose, recommendation, analysis, risks and the resolution.", "tryp": "Write a board paper seeking approval to acquire a 51% stake in a logistics start-up for ₹120 Cr.", "when": ["Any board or committee submission", "Turning a management deck into a board paper", "Reviewing a paper before it goes to directors"], "inputs": ["The matter, purpose and ask", "Analysis, financials, risks", "Relevant policies or regulatory requirements", "Board or committee name and date"], "pairs": ["board-question-bank", "executive-summary", "risk-assessment", "playbook-board-pack"], "body": "# Board Paper\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a paper a time-poor, legally accountable director can read in 10-15 minutes and use to take a defensible decision. That means one with an informed basis, alternatives considered, risks understood and dissent possible.\n\n## The standard to write to\n\nA board paper is not a management presentation. Directors owe duties of care, skill, diligence and independent judgement (Companies Act 2013, s.166). They can only meet those duties if the paper gives them:\n1. **What is being asked**, in the first half page.\n2. **Why it is in the company's interest**, against the strategy and risk appetite the board has already approved.\n3. **What else was considered**, and why it was rejected.\n4. **What could go wrong**, how likely it is and how bad, and how the board will know early.\n5. **Who is conflicted**, and what law or policy applies.\n6. **What exactly will be resolved**, and what is delegated to whom, within what limits.\n\n**The test:** if this decision went badly in three years, would the minutes and this paper show the board asked the right questions on an informed basis? If not, the paper is not ready.\n\n## Before you draft: diagnose the paper\n\nAsk (or infer and state):\n- **Purpose:** For decision, For discussion (shaping a decision coming later) or For noting. Most bad papers are unclear on this. Never label a paper \"for noting\" when it carries a decision in disguise, such as a new risk accepted or a commitment made.\n- **Forum:** full board, or a committee (Audit, NRC, Risk Management, CSR, Stakeholders Relationship, Investment). Check that the matter falls within the forum's terms of reference and delegation of authority. Matters reserved for the board, or needing shareholder approval, must be flagged.\n- **Decision class:** reversible or irreversible; size relative to net worth or turnover; whether it triggers disclosure (SEBI LODR Reg 30 materiality), related-party approval (Reg 23 / s.188), or special majorities.\n- **Audience state:** what the board already knows and decided before (cite earlier resolutions), and which directors have expertise or known concerns. Plan pre-wiring with the Chair and the relevant committee chair.\n- **Timing:** SS-1 expects notes on agenda to be circulated at least seven days before the meeting. Shorter notice for unpublished price-sensitive information or urgent items needs the conditions in SS-1 to be met. Late papers erode trust; say why if unavoidable.\n\n## Structure (main paper 4-6 pages; detail in annexes)\n\n1. **Cover block:** title; meeting and date; agenda item number; purpose (Decision / Discussion / Noting); sponsor (accountable executive); author; classification (e.g. Confidential, contains UPSI); previous board references.\n2. **Executive summary (half a page, maximum):**\n   - **The ask**, in one sentence with the amount, scope and timing.\n   - **Why now**: the trigger, and the cost of delay.\n   - **The recommendation** and the two or three reasons that carry it.\n   - **The key risks** and the residual risk position after mitigation.\n   - **Any conflicts**, and the approvals needed beyond this board.\n3. **Strategic context:** link to the approved strategy, plan and risk appetite. State plainly if the proposal stretches or departs from them.\n4. **Options considered:** at least three, including \"do nothing / defer\". Use a comparison table on the criteria the board cares about (value, risk, capital, time, reversibility, stakeholder impact). Explain why the rejected options were rejected. Directors will ask.\n5. **Financial impact:**\n   - Investment, phasing and funding source.\n   - Effect on P&L, cash, balance sheet, key ratios and covenants (e.g. net debt/EBITDA headroom).\n   - Returns (NPV, IRR, payback) against the hurdle rate, and the sensitivity of those returns to the two or three assumptions that matter.\n   - Separate committed from contingent spend.\n6. **Risks:**\n   - The top five, each written as cause, event and consequence.\n   - Inherent and residual ratings, mitigations with owners, and early-warning indicators.\n   - Fit with risk appetite.\n   - Include execution capacity (people, management bandwidth) and reputational and ESG risk, not only financial risk.\n7. **Legal, regulatory, governance and stakeholder considerations:**\n   - Approvals needed (regulators, shareholders, lenders, CCI for combinations).\n   - Disclosure obligations.\n   - Related-party aspects and interested directors (s.184 disclosure; abstention).\n   - Employee, community and customer impacts.\n   - Data-protection or AI-governance implications where relevant.\n8. **Implementation and monitoring:** milestones, stage gates and kill criteria; what comes back to the board, when, and in what form (KPIs with thresholds).\n9. **Recommendation and draft resolution:** precise legal wording, including any delegation (to whom, what limits, until when) and authority to sign or file.\n10. **Annexes:** financial model summary, due diligence findings, legal opinions, detailed risk register, glossary.\n\nSee `references/worked-example.md` for a complete paper, and `references/deep-dive.md` for resolution wording patterns, checklists by decision type, and a reviewer's scoring rubric.\n\n## Judgement calls a senior author makes\n\n- **Lead with the uncomfortable fact.** If the IRR is close to the hurdle, or diligence found a gap, put it in the executive summary. Directors discover buried bad news, and trust does not recover.\n- **One recommendation, honestly argued.** Present real alternatives. Asking the board to \"choose between options\" without a management view usually signals unclear ownership. The exception is a Discussion paper seeking direction.\n- **Quantify uncertainty; don't decorate it.** Give ranges and the break-even value of the key assumption, e.g. \"NPV turns negative if volumes are 18% below plan\", instead of \"robust NPV\".\n- **Separate facts, estimates and judgements.** Label each one: audited, management estimate, adviser view, or management judgement.\n- **Make dissent easy.** Note where management or advisers disagreed and how it was resolved. Boards value seeing the debate.\n- **Size the paper to the decision.** A ₹5 crore renewal does not need 40 pages. An acquisition needs a full annex set. Length is not diligence.\n- **Write for the least-expert director in the room**, without patronising the most expert. Define terms once; give technical depth in annexes.\n- **Think about the minutes.** The paper should make it easy for the Company Secretary to minute the basis of the decision and any directions given.\n\n## Red flags when reviewing someone else's paper\n\n- The executive summary has no number in it, or the ask appears only on the last page.\n- Only one real option, plus straw men.\n- Returns shown without sensitivity, or sensitivity only on assumptions that don't matter.\n- \"Risks: low\" with no mechanism. Mitigations that are intentions (\"we will monitor closely\").\n- No mention of conflicts, related parties or approvals beyond the board, when the transaction obviously involves them.\n- A resolution broader than the paper, e.g. \"authorised to do all acts as may be necessary\" with no limits or end date.\n- New numbers that do not reconcile with the last plan or budget the board approved, and no bridge explaining the difference.\n- Circulated late, with \"to be tabled\" annexes.\n\n## Variants by context\n\n- **Listed company (India):** check Reg 30 disclosure timing; whether the item is UPSI (handle the trading window and structured digital database); Reg 23 for related-party transactions (audit committee approval; shareholder approval above materiality thresholds, so verify the current threshold); and Reg 17 / Schedule II board information requirements.\n- **Regulated financial entity (bank, NBFC, insurer):** map to sector directions on board approvals (e.g. outsourcing, IT and cyber, credit policy, AI or model risk where applicable) and to any fit-and-proper or regulator pre-approval requirement.\n- **Subsidiary of a global group:** note the parent's reserved matters and approvals, and transfer-pricing or FEMA aspects.\n- **Unlisted / private / family company:** shareholder-agreement reserved matters and investor consent rights often decide more than the board does. List them.\n- **Public sector:** DPE guidelines and CVC considerations on procurement; approval hierarchies.\n- **Committee papers:** Audit Committee papers need the internal-control and accounting-treatment angle. NRC papers need benchmarking and succession logic. Risk Committee papers need appetite fit and stress results.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n# [Title]\n| Meeting | Item | Purpose | Sponsor | Author | Classification | Previous references |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## 1. Executive summary\n**Ask:** ... **Why now:** ... **Recommendation:** ... **Key risks / residual position:** ... **Conflicts and further approvals:** ...\n\n## 2. Strategic context\n## 3. Options considered\n| Criterion | Option A (recommended) | Option B | Do nothing / defer |\n## 4. Financial impact\n(cash flows summary; returns vs hurdle; covenant headroom; sensitivity and break-even)\n## 5. Risks\n| Risk (cause - event - consequence) | Inherent | Mitigation (owner) | Residual | Early-warning indicator |\n## 6. Legal, regulatory, governance and stakeholder considerations\n## 7. Implementation, stage gates and board monitoring\n## 8. Recommendation and draft resolution\n\"RESOLVED THAT ... FURTHER RESOLVED THAT ...\"\n## Annexes\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/deep-dive.md`: detailed criteria, checklists, scales and patterns\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and texts this skill draws on, with dates and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, the deep dive for detailed criteria, and the sources before citing any law, standard or date.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The purpose label matches what is actually being asked.\n- [ ] The ask, amount and timing are in the first three lines.\n- [ ] At least one real alternative and \"do nothing\", with reasons for rejection.\n- [ ] Returns shown against the hurdle, with break-even on the key assumption.\n- [ ] Risks have owners, residual ratings and early-warning indicators, and fit with appetite is stated.\n- [ ] Conflicts, related parties, disclosures and further approvals are addressed, or explicitly marked not applicable.\n- [ ] The resolution is precise, with limits and an end date on any delegation.\n- [ ] Monitoring: what returns to the board, when, and the kill criteria.\n- [ ] Facts, estimates and judgements are labelled; regulatory points are flagged for Company Secretary or counsel to verify.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers (Company Secretary, legal counsel, auditors).\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or price-sensitive information they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal, company-secretarial or audit advice. Regulatory references (Companies Act, SEBI LODR, SS-1, sector regulators) must be confirmed with your Company Secretary, counsel or assurance provider before use.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `board-question-bank`: rehearse the questions directors will ask.\n- `risk-appetite`: check fit with the board's stated appetite.\n- `business-case` and `sensitivity-analysis`: build the numbers behind sections 4-5.\n- `playbook-board-pack`: the end-to-end flow.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "deep-dive.md", "text": "# Board Paper: deep dive\n\n## A. Checklists by decision type\n\n### Capital expenditure / new plant / major project\n- Strategic fit, and the capacity or demand case (utilisation of existing assets first).\n- Capex phasing; committed vs uncommitted; contingency (state the % and its basis).\n- Returns vs WACC or hurdle; payback; sensitivity to volume, price, capex overrun (+15-25% is common for greenfield), and delay.\n- Funding: internal accruals, debt (covenant headroom, rating impact), equity.\n- Execution: EPC model, vendor concentration, key-person risk, approvals (environmental clearance, land, utilities).\n- Stage gates: e.g. release 30% now; the balance subject to milestones and a demand check.\n\n### Acquisition / investment / JV\n- Thesis: why this asset, why now, why us (synergies by type, with phasing and cost to achieve).\n- Valuation: method triangulation (DCF, trading comparables, transaction comparables); premium paid vs synergies; what the board is paying for that it would not get standalone.\n- Diligence: financial, legal, tax, HR, IT/cyber, ESG and data protection. Red flags and how they are handled: price adjustment, indemnity, escrow, conditions precedent.\n- Structure: stake, control rights, put/call options, earn-outs, valuation of options.\n- Approvals: CCI (combination thresholds or deal-value test), sector regulator, FEMA/ODI for overseas, shareholders.\n- Integration plan, owner and 100-day priorities; how and when the board will review post-deal performance against the case.\n\n### Related-party transaction\n- Nature, value and terms; arm's-length evidence (benchmarks, independent valuation).\n- Ordinary course of business: yes or no, and why.\n- Approvals: audit committee (only independent directors vote on RPTs under LODR); board; shareholders if material; interested directors abstain.\n- Disclosure requirements; omnibus approval limits if used.\n\n### Policy approval (e.g. AI policy, risk management policy, whistle-blower, dividend)\n- What the policy changes vs the current state, and why.\n- Who it applies to; how compliance will be monitored and reported.\n- Regulatory basis (cite) and benchmark against peers.\n- Review cycle.\n\n### Annual plan / budget\n- Bridge from last year's actuals and from the last strategy plan.\n- Key assumptions and their sensitivities; stretch vs base.\n- Capital allocation across Run / Grow / Transform, or by business unit.\n- Risks to the plan and in-year triggers for reforecast.\n\n### Senior appointment / remuneration (NRC → Board)\n- Process followed (search, diversity of slate, assessment).\n- Fit with the skills matrix; independence where relevant.\n- Remuneration benchmarking and policy compliance; shareholder approval needs.\n\n## B. Resolution wording patterns (adapt with the Company Secretary)\n\n**Approval with limits and delegation**\n> RESOLVED THAT approval of the Board be and is hereby accorded for [action], at a total cost not exceeding ₹[x] crore (inclusive of contingency of ₹[y] crore), to be incurred during FY[..] to FY[..], substantially on the terms set out in the note placed before the Board.\n>\n> FURTHER RESOLVED THAT [Managing Director / CFO / Committee] be and is hereby authorised to [negotiate, finalise and execute definitive agreements / release funds in tranches as per the milestones in Annex X], provided that any variation exceeding [5]% of the approved cost or any material change in terms shall be placed before the Board for approval.\n>\n> FURTHER RESOLVED THAT [names/designations] be and are hereby severally authorised to sign and file such documents and do such acts as may be necessary to give effect to this resolution, [until (date)].\n\n**Discussion paper outcome (no resolution; minuted direction)**\n> The Board discussed [item] and directed management to [develop options A and B in detail / test assumption X / return by (meeting) with ...].\n\n**Noting**\n> The Board noted [item]. (Use only when no new decision, risk acceptance or commitment is involved.)\n\nDrafting cautions:\n- Avoid open-ended \"do all acts, deeds and things\" without limits.\n- Name the delegate by designation, not by person.\n- Tie delegation to the paper's annex, so the scope is evidenced.\n\n## C. Reviewer's scoring rubric (score 1-5 each; below 3 on any one means rework)\n\n| Dimension | 1 (weak) | 3 (adequate) | 5 (excellent) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Clarity of ask | Ask unclear or at end | Ask clear but buried in page 2 | Ask, amount and timing in the first 3 lines |\n| Options | One option | Alternatives listed, little analysis | Real alternatives compared on agreed criteria, with reasons for rejection |\n| Financial rigour | Totals only | Returns vs hurdle | Returns, break-even of the key driver, covenant and ratio impact, funding |\n| Risk | Generic list | Ratings and mitigations | Cause-event-consequence, residual vs appetite, early-warning indicators, owners |\n| Governance and compliance | Silent | Mentions approvals | Conflicts, RPTs, disclosures, regulators and reserved matters fully addressed |\n| Monitoring | None | Promises updates | Stage gates, KPIs with thresholds, kill criteria, reporting dates |\n| Readability | Deck pasted as paper | Structured, but long | Main paper ≤6 pages; annexes do the heavy lifting; terms defined |\n| Candour | Bad news absent | Mentioned in annex | Key weaknesses in the executive summary, with management's response |\n\n## D. Common director questions a paper should pre-empt\n1. What happens if we don't do this, or do it in a year?\n2. What did you consider and reject?\n3. What is the one assumption that, if wrong, sinks the case?\n4. Do we have the management capacity to deliver this alongside everything else?\n5. How does this change our risk profile, and is it within appetite?\n6. Who is conflicted, and what approvals remain?\n7. How will we know early that it is going wrong, and what will we do then?\n8. How does this look to regulators, investors and employees if it becomes public tomorrow?\n\n## E. India-specific mechanics to confirm with the Company Secretary\n- Notice and circulation of agenda and notes (SS-1); shorter-notice conditions.\n- Quorum and interested-director rules (s.174, s.184).\n- Matters that must be decided at a meeting, not by circular resolution (s.179(3) and rules).\n- Matters that need shareholder approval (e.g. s.180 borrowing and sale-of-undertaking limits; s.186 loans and investments beyond limits; s.188 / Reg 23 RPTs).\n- Disclosure timelines (Reg 30 and Schedule III).\n- UPSI handling and the structured digital database (SEBI PIT Regulations)."}, {"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources and what to verify\n\nPrimary sources the method draws on. **Laws, circulars and thresholds change, so confirm current versions with the Company Secretary or counsel before relying on any specific rule.**\n\n## India\n- **Companies Act, 2013:** s.166 (duties of directors), s.173-174 (meetings, quorum), s.179(3) (powers to be exercised at meetings), s.180 (restrictions on board powers), s.184 (disclosure of interest), s.186 (loans and investments), s.188 (related-party transactions), s.134(5) (directors' responsibility statement), Schedule IV (Code for Independent Directors).\n- **Secretarial Standard on Meetings of the Board of Directors (SS-1), ICSI:** notice, agenda and notes on agenda (circulation normally at least seven days before the meeting; conditions for shorter notice), minutes.\n- **SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015:** Reg 17 (board composition and information to be placed before the board, Schedule II Part A), Reg 18 (audit committee), Reg 21 (risk management committee), Reg 23 (related-party transactions: verify the current materiality thresholds), Reg 30 (disclosure of material events).\n- **SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015:** UPSI handling, structured digital database.\n- **SEBI Guidance Note on Board Evaluation** (January 2017): useful for what boards expect in information quality.\n\n## Global practice\n- **UK Corporate Governance Code 2024** (Financial Reporting Council), with the FRC's Guidance on Board Effectiveness: board information quality and timeliness. Provision 29 (declaration on material internal controls) applies to periods from 1 January 2026.\n- **G20/OECD Principles of Corporate Governance** (2023 revision).\n\n## Further reading\n- Professional-body guidance on writing board papers (e.g. ICSI in India).\n- Answer-first, pyramid-style structuring of arguments.\n\n## Verify each time\n- Reg 23 materiality thresholds and any recent SEBI amendments on RPTs.\n- Reg 30 timelines and the materiality criteria for the specific event.\n- Sector regulator (RBI / IRDAI / SEBI-intermediary) board-approval requirements for the specific matter.\n- Whether an item may be passed by circular resolution."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example: Board paper (illustrative; company and numbers are fictional)\n\n# Approval of Phase 1 investment in a new specialty-chemicals plant at Dahej\n| Meeting | Item | Purpose | Sponsor | Author | Classification | Previous references |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Board, 14 Feb 2027 | 6 | **For decision** | MD & CEO | Head of Strategy | Confidential - contains UPSI | Strategy 2030 (Board, Jul 2026, Item 4); Capex pipeline (Board, Nov 2026, Item 7) |\n\n## 1. Executive summary\n**Ask:** Approve ₹420 crore for Phase 1 of a 30 KTPA specialty-chemicals plant at Dahej. ₹160 crore is to be released now; ₹260 crore is released in two tranches against milestones and a demand check (Annex B). Commissioning is planned for Q3 FY29.\n\n**Why now:** our existing Ankleshwar capacity will run at 96% utilisation from FY28, and two anchor customers have signed letters of intent for 11 KTPA. A 12-month delay pushes commissioning past the window in which these customers qualify suppliers (est. revenue at risk ₹180-220 crore a year).\n\n**Recommendation:** proceed with a phased greenfield build (Option A). It gives the highest NPV (₹212 crore at 12.5% WACC; IRR 18.4% vs 15% hurdle). Phasing caps the irreversible commitment at ₹160 crore until demand is confirmed.\n\n**Key risks (residual):**\n- Demand shortfall: medium, mitigated by the tranche gate.\n- Capex overrun: medium; EPC lump-sum for 70% of scope.\n- Environmental clearance timing: medium-high, and the critical path.\n\nAll are within the capital-projects appetite approved in July 2026. **Note:** NPV turns negative if Phase 1 volumes average more than 22% below plan over FY29-33.\n\n**Conflicts and further approvals:** none of the directors is interested. No shareholder approval is required (within s.180 limits). Lenders' consent is needed under the existing term-loan covenant. Disclosure under Reg 30 follows the Board decision.\n\n## 2. Strategic context\nStrategy 2030 targets 40% of revenue from specialty grades (FY26: 24%). This plant is the largest single contributor (est. +9 percentage points by FY31). It is consistent with the approved capital allocation envelope (Grow: ₹1,100 crore over FY27-30, of which ₹310 crore is already committed).\n\n## 3. Options considered\n| Criterion | A. Phased greenfield, Dahej (recommended) | B. Brownfield debottleneck, Ankleshwar | C. Toll manufacturing | D. Defer 12 months |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Added capacity | 30 KTPA (two phases) | 6 KTPA | 8-10 KTPA, contract-dependent | 0 |\n| Capex | ₹420 Cr Phase 1 | ₹95 Cr | Nil | Nil |\n| NPV @12.5% | ₹212 Cr | ₹64 Cr | ₹38 Cr | -₹40 to -₹70 Cr (lost margin, est.) |\n| Control of quality / IP | Full | Full | Low: process IP exposed | Full |\n| Reversibility | Partial (tranches) | High | High | High |\n| Key weakness | Clearance timing | Does not meet the anchor demand | Customer qualification risk; margin leakage | Anchor customers likely lost |\n\nB is recommended as a **complement** (Board paper planned for May 2027), not as an alternative.\n\n## 4. Financial impact\n- Phase 1 capex: ₹420 Cr, including a 10% contingency (₹38 Cr) set on the basis of a ±15% Class-3 estimate.\n- Funding: 60% internal accruals, 40% from the existing term-loan facility. Peak net debt/EBITDA is 1.9x vs a 2.75x covenant and our policy ceiling of 2.25x.\n- Returns: NPV ₹212 Cr; IRR 18.4%; payback 5.8 years from first spend.\n- Sensitivity (NPV, ₹ Cr):\n\n| Change | NPV |\n|---|---|\n| Volume -10% | 141 |\n| Volume -22% | ~0 (break-even) |\n| Capex +20% | 141 |\n| Selling price -5% | 118 |\n| 9-month delay | 168 |\n\n## 5. Risks\n| Risk (cause - event - consequence) | Inherent | Mitigation (owner) | Residual | Early-warning indicator |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Customer qualification takes longer → volumes ramp more slowly → NPV erosion | High | LOIs converted to supply agreements with take-or-pay for 60% of volume before Tranche 2 (CCO) | Medium | Signed agreements by Sep 2027 |\n| Clearance delayed → commissioning slips | High | Pre-filing engagement started; consultants appointed; site-alternative study done (Head Projects) | Medium-high | Public hearing date fixed by Jun 2027 |\n| Capex overrun | Medium | Lump-sum EPC for 70% of scope; owner's engineer; monthly cost report to Audit Committee (CFO) | Medium | Cost-to-complete variance >5% |\n| Management bandwidth (3 major projects concurrent) | Medium | Dedicated project director hired; PMO (COO) | Low-medium | Milestone slippage >6 weeks |\n| Effluent / community incident at new site | Medium | Zero-liquid-discharge design; community engagement plan (Head EHS) | Low | Grievance log; consent compliance |\n\n## 6. Legal, regulatory, governance and stakeholder considerations\n- Environmental clearance, consent to establish, and GIDC land allotment (in hand).\n- Lender consent under clause 14.3 of the term-loan agreement.\n- Reg 30 disclosure of capacity addition after this decision; the item is treated as UPSI until disclosed.\n- No related-party aspects; the EPC tender was competitive (4 bidders, Annex D).\n- ESG: the plant increases absolute Scope 1-2 emissions by ~4% but reduces intensity by ~6%. The BRSR Core impact is included in the FY29 plan.\n\n## 7. Implementation, stage gates and board monitoring\n| Gate | Date | Criteria to release next tranche | Decision by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tranche 2 (₹170 Cr) | Q4 FY28 | Environmental clearance received; ≥60% Phase 1 volume under take-or-pay; cost-to-complete within +5% | Board |\n| Tranche 3 (₹90 Cr) | Q2 FY29 | Mechanical completion ≥70%; qualification batches passed for 2 anchors | Investment Committee |\n\nQuarterly reporting to the Board: milestones, cost-to-complete, contracted volumes, and risk indicators.\n\n**Kill criterion:** if take-or-pay contracts cover <40% of Phase 1 volume by Q4 FY28, management will return with options (pause, resize or sell the site) before any further release.\n\n## 8. Recommendation and draft resolution\n> RESOLVED THAT approval of the Board be and is hereby accorded for Phase 1 of the specialty-chemicals plant at Dahej at a total cost not exceeding ₹420 crore (including contingency of ₹38 crore), to be released in three tranches as per the stage gates set out in Annex B to the note placed before the Board, with an initial release of ₹160 crore.\n>\n> FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the release of Tranche 2 shall require the approval of the Board, and Tranche 3 the approval of the Investment Committee, in each case on satisfaction of the criteria in Annex B.\n>\n> FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the MD & CEO and CFO be and are hereby severally authorised to finalise and execute the EPC contract and related agreements within the approved cost, and to seek the lenders' consent, provided that any increase above 5% of the approved cost shall be placed before the Board, this authority being valid until 31 March 2029.\n\n## Annexes\nA. Financial model summary and sensitivity · B. Stage-gate criteria · C. Market and customer evidence (LOIs) · D. EPC tender evaluation · E. Environmental clearance plan · F. Risk register extract"}]}, {"id": "board-question-bank", "pack": "board", "title": "Board Question Bank", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Generate the sharp, fair questions a non-executive director should ask on any paper or topic.", "tryp": "I'm an independent director. Management is proposing a ₹500 Cr investment in a GenAI platform. What should I ask?", "when": ["Preparing for a board or committee meeting", "Management preparing for board scrutiny", "Board evaluation and training"], "inputs": ["The paper or topic", "Company context", "Your role (chair, committee member, NED)"], "pairs": ["board-paper", "ai-governance-review", "red-team-review", "risk-appetite"], "body": "# Board Question Bank\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** the 7-10 questions that actually test a proposal, asked in a way that holds management to account without managing for them, plus how to judge the answers.\n\n## The director's frame\n\nA director's job in the room is **oversight and judgement, not operations**. Good questions:\n- **Test the basis of the decision:** the assumptions, the evidence, the alternatives considered.\n- **Test the fit:** with strategy, risk appetite, capital and capacity.\n- **Test the controls:** who is accountable, how the board will know early, what triggers a stop.\n- **Test the stakeholder lens:** customers, employees, regulators, investors, community. Directors' duties extend to them (Companies Act 2013, s.166(2)).\n- **Are open, specific and fair:** \"What would have to be true for this to fail?\", not \"Why didn't you think of X?\"\n\nAvoid questions that pull the board into execution detail (\"which vendor for the cooling towers?\") unless the detail carries a material risk.\n\n## Before generating questions: read the situation\n1. **What is the board being asked to do?** Approve, discuss, note, or ratify something already done (ratification deserves the hardest questions).\n2. **Which of the director's duties is most engaged?** Strategy and value, stewardship of capital, risk oversight, compliance and integrity, people and culture.\n3. **Where is the information asymmetry largest?** Technical areas (AI, cyber, actuarial, complex accounting) need questions that force plain-English explanation and independent assurance.\n4. **What is the history?** Previous approvals, past overruns, prior audit findings, and promises made last time.\n5. **Who is in the room?** If you are the Chair, a committee chair or a new director, the right register differs (see `references/deep-dive.md`).\n\n## Method\n1. **Identify the 3-5 critical assumptions or judgements** in the paper: the ones that, if wrong, change the decision.\n2. **Generate questions across nine lenses.** Do not output all of them; use the lenses to find the best.\n   - Strategic fit and alternatives\n   - Assumptions and evidence\n   - Financial consequences and capital\n   - Risk and appetite\n   - Execution capacity and accountability\n   - Controls, compliance and integrity (including conflicts)\n   - Stakeholders, ethics and reputation\n   - Technology, data and AI (where relevant)\n   - Monitoring and exit\n3. **Rank** by impact on the decision × uncertainty × the board's current information gap. Select the top 7-10.\n4. **For each top question, write:**\n   - why it matters;\n   - what a **good answer** contains (evidence, specificity, ownership);\n   - **red flags** in an answer;\n   - one **follow-up** for a weak answer.\n5. **Add 3-5 \"ask for\" items:** information, independent assurance, or KPIs the board should require before or after approval.\n6. **Phrase for the room:** neutral, one question at a time, starting with \"Help me understand…\", \"What would…\", \"How will we know…\".\n7. **Optionally, produce a pre-meeting note** to send the Chair or CEO, so management can come prepared. This is good practice for complex items; it avoids ambush and improves the discussion.\n\n## What separates strong from average question sets\n- **Average:** \"What are the risks?\" **Strong:** \"Which single assumption, if it's 20% off, makes this value-destroying, and what evidence gives you confidence it isn't?\"\n- **Average:** \"Is this compliant?\" **Strong:** \"Which approvals or disclosures does this trigger, who has confirmed that, and what's our exposure if a regulator disagrees with our interpretation?\"\n- **Average:** \"Do we have the capability?\" **Strong:** \"Who is personally accountable, what else are they carrying, and what did we learn from the last project of this size?\"\n- **Average:** \"Is the AI model accurate?\" **Strong:** \"On what data was it tested, how does accuracy differ across customer groups, who can override it, and how many overrides happened last month?\"\n\n## Judging answers: universal red flags\n- **Confidence without evidence:** \"We're very comfortable\", \"the team is experienced\".\n- **Answers that redefine the question,** or move to a different metric.\n- **\"The consultants / vendor / model says…\"** with no management ownership of the judgement.\n- **No mention of what would cause a stop or change of course.**\n- **New numbers in the room** that were not in the paper.\n- **Assurance claimed but not independent:** for example, the first line marking its own homework.\n- **Repeated \"we'll come back to you\"** on fundamentals. That signals a paper not ready for decision. Consider deferring, or approving subject to conditions.\n\n## Topic packs\nUse `references/deep-dive.md` for ready, tested question sets by topic:\n- strategy and annual plan;\n- M&A / investment;\n- capex and projects;\n- risk and internal control;\n- audit committee (financial reporting, auditors, IFC);\n- cyber and resilience;\n- AI and data (GenAI, agents, model risk);\n- ESG, climate and BRSR;\n- people, culture, succession and remuneration;\n- crisis and incidents;\n- related-party transactions.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Board questions: [paper / topic], [meeting]\n**Decision asked:** ...  |  **Critical assumptions:** 1. ... 2. ... 3. ...\n\n### Top questions\n| # | Question | Why it matters | A good answer includes | Red flags | Follow-up |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Ask for (before approval / ongoing)\n- ...\n\n### Optional: note to Chair / CEO before the meeting\n...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/deep-dive.md`: detailed criteria, checklists, scales and patterns\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and texts this skill draws on, with dates and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, the deep dive for detailed criteria, and the sources before citing any law, standard or date.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Questions target the decision's critical assumptions, not generic topics.\n- [ ] Each top question has \"good answer\" criteria and red flags.\n- [ ] At least one question each on alternatives, risk appetite fit, accountability and monitoring/exit.\n- [ ] The stakeholder or ethical lens is covered where relevant.\n- [ ] No operational micromanagement; the tone is open and fair.\n- [ ] The \"ask for\" items include an independent assurance point where information asymmetry is high.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's terminology and jurisdiction; Indian company law and SEBI references by default for Indian users.\n- For legal or regulatory specifics, flag them for verification with the Company Secretary or counsel.\n- Remind users not to paste board papers containing UPSI or confidential data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal, company-secretarial or audit advice. Regulatory references (Companies Act, SEBI LODR, SS-1, sector regulators) must be confirmed with your Company Secretary, counsel or assurance provider before use.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `board-paper`: write or fix the paper the questions test.\n- `ai-governance-review` and `ai-risk-register`: depth for AI items.\n- `risk-appetite`: test appetite fit.\n- `red-team-review`: a full adversarial critique.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "deep-dive.md", "text": "# Board Question Bank: topic packs\n\nEach pack: core questions → what good looks like → red flags. Pick, adapt and rank; don't read them all out.\n\n## 1. Strategy and annual plan\n- What are the two or three choices in this strategy that we are deliberately *not* making, and why?\n- Which assumptions about customers, competitors, technology or regulation would most change this plan, and what are we watching to detect a shift?\n- How does the capital allocation (Run / Grow / Transform, or by business) reflect the strategy? Where are we under-investing?\n- What did we commit to last year, and what did we deliver? What explains the gap?\n- What is the plan if the market grows at half the assumed rate?\n\n**Good looks like:** explicit trade-offs, leading indicators, a variance bridge to the prior plan, a downside case with management actions.\n**Red flags:** a plan that is last year +10%; no downside case; strategy and budget owned by different people and not reconciled.\n\n## 2. M&A / strategic investment\n- Why is owning this better than partnering or building, and why are we the best owner?\n- What premium are we paying, and which specific synergies justify it? Who owns each synergy, by when, and at what cost to achieve?\n- What did diligence find that we didn't expect, and how is it reflected in price, structure or conditions?\n- What are the walk-away price and conditions, and who has authority to walk?\n- How will integration be led, and what are the first 100 days? What is the cultural risk?\n- What happened with our last two acquisitions against their cases?\n\n**Good looks like:** synergies phased and owned; price discipline (a walk-away agreed before negotiation); a post-deal review date set now.\n**Red flags:** \"strategic\" used to justify any price; synergies mostly revenue; integration planning starts after signing; deal-team fatigue or a sunk-cost tone.\n\n## 3. Capex and major projects\n- What is the estimate class and its accuracy range? What contingency is included, and on what basis?\n- What is the critical path, and what is the single biggest schedule risk?\n- What are the stage gates and kill criteria? Who decides at each gate?\n- What did our last project of this size overrun by, and what's different now?\n\n**Red flags:** contingency set as a round number with no basis; no independent cost review on large projects; benefits depend on a later phase not yet approved.\n\n## 4. Risk and internal control\n- What are our top risks today, and how has that list changed in the last 12 months?\n- Where are we operating outside appetite, and what is the plan and timeline to return?\n- Which risks are emerging but not yet on the register?\n- How do we know controls work, beyond management saying so? What do internal audit and other independent assurance say?\n- What near-misses or incidents happened this quarter, and what did we learn?\n\n**Red flags:** an unchanged risk register; all risks amber; no near-miss reporting (which means a non-reporting culture, not a safe one).\n\n## 5. Audit Committee: reporting, auditors and IFC\n- What were the significant judgements and estimates this period, and how sensitive are results to them?\n- Where did management and the auditors disagree, and how was it resolved?\n- Any changes in accounting policies or presentation? Why?\n- What did internal audit find, and how many past actions are overdue?\n- What is the status of internal financial controls (s.134(5)(e))? Any deficiencies, and their root causes?\n- Has the auditor's independence been tested, including non-audit services?\n- What do whistle-blower reports show about themes, closure times and retaliation?\n- What does the auditor want to tell us without management in the room?\n\n**Red flags:** no auditor-only session; overdue audit actions accepted repeatedly; aggressive estimates that are always favourable.\n\n## 6. Cyber and operational resilience\n- What are our critical business services, and what are the impact tolerances (maximum tolerable disruption)?\n- When did we last test recovery of a critical system end to end, and what failed?\n- How quickly can we detect, contain and report an incident? (Note mandatory reporting windows, such as CERT-In directions in India.)\n- Which third parties could take us down, and what is our concentration risk?\n- What would a ransomware attack cost us in a week, and how is it insured or mitigated?\n\n**Red flags:** no tested restores; \"we have a firewall\" answers; cyber reported only as a list of projects.\n\n## 7. AI and data (including GenAI and agents)\n- Where are we using AI today, including inside vendor products and staff use of public tools? Do we have a complete inventory?\n- Which AI uses affect customers', employees' or citizens' rights, money, health or access to services? How are they classified and controlled?\n- Who is accountable for each high-impact AI system, and who can stop it?\n- How do we test for accuracy, bias and robustness before launch and in production? What were the results?\n- What data trains or feeds these systems, and do we have the right to use it (consent, purpose, IP)?\n- For GenAI and agents: what stops harmful or false output, prompt injection, or data leakage? What can an agent do without human approval?\n- What incidents or complaints have there been, and how were they handled?\n- Which laws apply to us (e.g. DPDP in India, the EU AI Act if we serve the EU, sector rules) and what are the dates?\n- How do we measure value from AI, and which pilots have we stopped?\n\n**Red flags:** no inventory; \"the vendor handles that\"; accuracy reported only on average; no kill switch; GenAI policy without monitoring; every pilot \"promising\".\n\n## 8. ESG, climate and BRSR\n- What are our material topics, and how were they determined (double materiality, stakeholder input)?\n- What are our transition and physical climate risks, quantified where possible, and how do they enter strategy and capital allocation?\n- Are our targets credible? Show the decarbonisation pathway, capex and dependencies.\n- How assurance-ready are our BRSR Core data? Who owns each KPI, and what is the evidence trail?\n- What value-chain data do we depend on, and how reliable is it?\n- What is our exposure to export-market rules (e.g. carbon border mechanisms) or customer requirements?\n\n**Red flags:** targets with no capex; ESG data from spreadsheets with no controls; ESG handled only by CSR or communications.\n\n## 9. People, culture, succession and remuneration\n- Who could step in tomorrow for the CEO and each critical role, and in 2-3 years?\n- What do engagement, attrition (regretted), grievance and whistle-blower data tell us about culture?\n- Do incentives reward the right behaviours, including risk and compliance, not just growth?\n- What is the board hearing directly from employees below the leadership team?\n\n## 10. Crisis and incidents\n- What happened, what is the impact, and what is still unknown?\n- Who is in charge, and what decisions are needed from the board now?\n- What have we told regulators, customers, employees and the market, and when must we?\n- What is the root cause, and are there other places it could happen?\n- How will we look after the people affected?\n\n## 11. Related-party transactions\n- Why is this in the company's interest compared with an unrelated party?\n- What is the arm's-length evidence (independent valuation, benchmarks)?\n- Is it in the ordinary course of business? Who decided that, and on what basis?\n- Which directors are interested, and have they abstained?\n\n## Register by role\n- **Chair:** frame the decision; ensure every director is heard; summarise conditions; agree what comes back.\n- **Committee chair presenting to the board:** report the committee's view, the dissent and the conditions; don't re-run the committee debate.\n- **New director:** ask clarifying, context-seeking questions (\"Help me understand how we got here…\"). Read prior minutes first.\n- **Independent director:** prioritise minority shareholders and stakeholders, conflicts and integrity; you may need to insist on independent advice.\n\n## Phrasing toolkit\n- \"Help me understand…\" / \"Walk us through…\"\n- \"What would have to be true for…?\"\n- \"What's the strongest argument against this?\"\n- \"How will we know, early, that it isn't working?\"\n- \"What would you do if it were your own money?\"\n- \"If this appeared on the front page tomorrow, how would it read?\""}, {"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources and what to verify\n\n- **Companies Act, 2013:** s.166 (duties of directors, including acting in the interests of the company, its employees, shareholders, the community and the environment); s.134(5) (directors' responsibility statement, internal financial controls); s.177 (audit committee; vigil mechanism); Schedule IV (Code for Independent Directors: role, duties, separate meetings).\n- **SEBI LODR Regulations, 2015:** Reg 17 and Schedule II (information to be placed before the board), Reg 18 (audit committee role, Part C of Schedule II), Reg 21 (risk management committee), Reg 23 (related-party transactions), Reg 25 (independent directors).\n- **SEBI Guidance Note on Board Evaluation** (Jan 2017).\n- **G20/OECD Principles of Corporate Governance** (2023).\n- **UK Corporate Governance Code 2024** and **FRC Guidance on Board Effectiveness**.\n- **Question-based oversight:** the widely used practice of structured director question sets by topic (strategy, risk, technology, culture).\n- **CERT-In Directions** (April 2022): incident reporting obligations in India (verify current timelines).\n- **Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023** and **DPDP Rules, 2025** (notified 13 Nov 2025; most data-fiduciary obligations apply from 13 May 2027; verify).\n- **RBI FREE-AI Committee report** (Aug 2025) and **MeitY India AI Governance Guidelines** (Nov 2025): for AI questions in Indian financial services and generally.\n\n**Verify each time:** sector-regulator expectations (RBI / IRDAI / SEBI) on board oversight of the specific topic, and any recent circulars."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional company)\n\n**Paper:** Management seeks approval for ₹500 crore over 3 years for an enterprise GenAI platform (internal copilots, customer-service agents, document processing) at a mid-size Indian private-sector insurer.\n\n**Critical assumptions identified:**\n1. Productivity gains of 25-30% in claims and servicing translate into cost savings, not just \"time freed\".\n2. Customer-facing agents can operate within IRDAI and DPDP obligations without material conduct risk.\n3. The organisation can absorb the change (adoption ≥70% within 12 months).\n\n## Top questions\n| # | Question | Why it matters | A good answer includes | Red flags | Follow-up |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Of the ₹500 Cr, how much is committed vs released only when value is shown, and what are the stage gates? | Limits irreversible commitment | Tranches tied to measured outcomes; who decides at each gate | The full amount committed upfront; vendor multi-year minimums | \"What do we lose if we stop after year 1?\" |\n| 2 | How much of the claimed saving is cash we will actually remove from the cost base, and who signs up to it in their budget? | Most AI cases overstate cash benefits | Benefits split into hard and soft; budget owners named; phasing | \"Capacity released\" with no redeployment plan | \"Which budgets go down next year, and by how much?\" |\n| 3 | Which AI uses will make or influence decisions about customers (claims, pricing, servicing), and how are they classified and controlled? | Conduct, fairness and regulatory exposure | Inventory; risk tiers; human review for adverse decisions; testing across customer groups | No inventory yet; \"the model only assists\" with no evidence | \"Show us last month's override and complaint data from the pilot.\" |\n| 4 | Where does policyholder data go, and under which DPDP obligations and timelines do we operate? | Legal exposure; trust | Data flows mapped; processing location; vendor terms on training use; DPDP readiness plan by the May 2027 obligations | \"The vendor is compliant\" | \"Has counsel reviewed the vendor contract's data clauses?\" |\n| 5 | What can the customer-service agent do without a human, and how do we stop it if it misbehaves? | Agentic risk; kill switch | Defined autonomy limits; escalation rules; kill switch tested; logging | Broad tool access; no tested shutdown | \"When was the kill switch last tested?\" |\n| 6 | Who is personally accountable for the programme and for AI risk, and are they different people? | Separation of delivery and oversight | Named business owner; independent second-line AI risk oversight | Same person builds and approves | \"What does the CRO's team review before go-live?\" |\n| 7 | What did the pilots show on accuracy, hallucination and customer outcomes, and which pilot did we stop? | Evidence over enthusiasm | Measured results against a baseline; at least one stopped or changed | Every pilot \"successful\" | \"What was the error rate on the hardest 10% of cases?\" |\n| 8 | How dependent will we be on one model provider, and what is the exit plan? | Concentration and lock-in | Model-agnostic architecture; exit and data-return terms | Single proprietary stack, no exit clause | \"What would switching cost us in year 2?\" |\n| 9 | How will employees be affected, and what is the reskilling and communication plan? | Culture, adoption, reputation | Role-impact analysis; reskilling budget; union or works-council engagement where relevant | \"No job losses\" asserted with no plan | \"What are we telling staff, and when?\" |\n\n## Ask for\n- Before approval: an AI inventory and risk-tiering of the planned use cases; the second-line view; counsel's note on DPDP and IRDAI obligations.\n- Ongoing (quarterly): value realised vs plan (hard savings); adoption; incidents, complaints and overrides; model performance by customer segment; spend vs tranche.\n- Annually: independent review (internal audit or external) of AI governance against an accepted framework (e.g. ISO/IEC 42001 or NIST AI RMF).\n\n## Note to Chair / CEO before the meeting\n\"Ahead of item 6, it would help if management could bring: (1) the split of committed vs gated spend; (2) the pilot results, including error rates and the override and complaint data; (3) a one-page view from the CRO on AI risk tiering and controls; and (4) counsel's summary of DPDP obligations and timelines. Happy to discuss beforehand.\""}]}, {"id": "ai-governance-review", "pack": "board", "title": "AI Governance Review", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Assess how well an organisation governs AI: policy, roles, inventory, risk, controls, and board oversight.", "tryp": "Review our AI governance. We have 40+ AI use cases, no AI policy, and the data science team approves its own models.", "when": ["Setting up AI governance", "Board or audit committee oversight of AI", "Preparing for regulation or client due diligence"], "inputs": ["Description of current AI use and governance", "Industry and jurisdictions", "Any existing policies or committees"], "pairs": ["ai-risk-register", "responsible-ai-review", "board-question-bank", "risk-appetite"], "body": "# AI Governance Review\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** an evidence-based view of whether AI in the organisation is under control. It shows what exists on paper vs what actually works, where the biggest exposures are, and a sequenced plan a board can hold management to.\n\n## Principles that set the senior standard\n1. **Governance follows use, not policy.** Start from the real AI footprint, including AI embedded in vendor software and staff use of public GenAI tools. A policy without an inventory is decoration.\n2. **Proportionality.** Controls scale with impact on people and the business. Over-governing low-risk use cases kills adoption and pushes use into the shadows.\n3. **Design vs operating effectiveness.** \"We have a model-validation standard\" (design) is not \"every high-risk model was validated before go-live and re-validated on drift\" (operating). Score both.\n4. **Three lines, real independence.** Builders don't approve their own high-risk systems. Second-line risk, compliance and legal own the framework; internal audit tests it.\n5. **Lifecycle, not gate.** Risk appears after launch: drift, misuse, new data, vendor model updates, and agent tool changes.\n6. **Accountability is personal.** Every AI system has a named business owner who can explain it and stop it.\n7. **Regulation is a floor, and a moving one.** Map obligations per jurisdiction and sector, with dates, and keep a horizon scan.\n\n## Scoping questions (ask or state assumptions)\n- Sector and jurisdictions: where the organisation operates, and where its customers and the affected people are.\n- The AI footprint: in-house models, vendor AI, GenAI copilots, customer-facing chatbots, agents with tool access, and AI in HR, credit, claims, pricing, health or security.\n- Existing structures: policy, committee, risk framework, model-risk function, privacy office, CISO.\n- Trigger: board request, audit finding, regulator, incident, client due diligence, certification ambition (ISO/IEC 42001).\n- Evidence available: documents, interviews, system samples.\n\n## The 12 domains to assess\nScore each 1-5 on **Design** and **Operating effectiveness** separately. `references/deep-dive.md` has the criteria per level and the evidence to request.\n\n1. **Strategy and risk appetite for AI:** the board-approved stance on where AI may and may not be used; appetite statements and tolerances.\n2. **Policy and principles:** an AI policy or standard aligned to principles (e.g. fairness, transparency, safety, accountability, privacy); acceptable use of GenAI.\n3. **Accountability and structure:** board oversight (which committee); executive owner; AI risk or ethics committee; RACI across the three lines; competence of the people in it.\n4. **AI inventory:** completeness, including vendor-embedded and shadow AI; fields captured (purpose, owner, data, model, decision impact, tier, status).\n5. **Risk classification and impact assessment:** a tiering method (prohibited, high, limited, minimal, or an internal equivalent); impact assessments for high tiers, covering affected persons, rights, safety and financial harm.\n6. **Data governance for AI:** lawful basis and consent under DPDP and GDPR where applicable; purpose limitation; data quality and representativeness; IP and licensing of training data; retention.\n7. **Development and validation controls:** requirements, test and evaluation sets, bias and fairness testing across groups, robustness, explainability proportionate to use, documentation (model cards), independent validation for high tier.\n8. **GenAI and agentic controls:** grounding and hallucination management; content safety; prompt-injection and data-leakage defences; tool permissions and autonomy limits for agents; human-approval points; red-teaming; logging and traceability of agent actions.\n9. **Third-party and vendor AI:** due diligence; contract terms on data use for training, model changes, audit rights, incident notice, exit; concentration on a single foundation-model provider.\n10. **Deployment, monitoring and change:** go-live approval; performance and drift monitoring; override and complaint tracking; re-approval on material change, including vendor model upgrades; kill switch.\n11. **Incidents, transparency and redress:** AI incident definition and reporting; root-cause analysis; disclosure to affected people (that AI is used; explanation of adverse decisions); grievance and appeal routes; regulator notification where required.\n12. **People, literacy and culture:** role-based AI literacy (the EU AI Act Art. 4 literacy duty has applied since Feb 2025 for in-scope organisations); a speak-up culture; incentives that don't reward reckless deployment.\n\nPlus a cross-cutting check: **assurance**. Has internal audit or an external party reviewed AI governance? Is the organisation pursuing or ready for ISO/IEC 42001 certification?\n\n## Method\n1. **Build or obtain the inventory first.** If none exists, run a rapid discovery: procurement and vendor list scan, IT asset and SaaS logs, business-unit survey, and proxy/network data for public GenAI use. The first finding is often the size of the unknown.\n2. **Tier the inventory** with a simple, defensible method (see `ai-risk-register`). Sample all high-tier systems and some limited-tier ones for deep testing.\n3. **Collect evidence per domain:** documents (design) plus samples and records (operating), e.g. 5 go-live approvals, 3 validation reports, 1 incident file, a vendor contract, monitoring dashboards.\n4. **Interview** the board or committee chair, executive owner, CRO, DPO, CISO, a model owner, a frontline user and internal audit. Look for gaps between what each believes happens.\n5. **Map obligations** per jurisdiction and sector with dates (`references/sources.md`). Mark each obligation met, partial or gap, and flag anything needing legal interpretation.\n6. **Score** each domain on Design and Operating (1-5) with one-line evidence. Avoid averaging away a critical gap: any domain at 1 on a high-tier system is a priority finding regardless of the overall score.\n7. **Prioritise findings** by exposure (impact on people, money and regulation × likelihood × number of systems affected).\n8. **Roadmap:**\n   - **90 days:** stop the bleeding. Inventory, an interim GenAI acceptable-use policy, a high-tier freeze-or-approve rule, a named accountable owner, and a kill switch on customer-facing agents.\n   - **6 months:** build the framework. Policy, tiering, impact assessment, validation standard, vendor clauses, monitoring.\n   - **12 months:** embed and assure. Training, metrics, internal audit review, ISO/IEC 42001 readiness if wanted.\n9. **Board reporting design:** what the board sees quarterly (see output template).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Don't recommend a heavyweight framework for a 10-use-case organisation.** Right-size: a single AI risk committee and a two-page standard may beat ISO-style documentation.\n- **Shadow GenAI is a governance finding, not a disciplinary one.** Provide a sanctioned, safe tool before banning public ones; bans alone fail.\n- **Vendor AI is your AI.** Accountability for outcomes to customers and regulators does not transfer to the vendor.\n- **\"Human in the loop\" must be real.** If reviewers approve 99.8% of AI recommendations in seconds, it's a rubber stamp. Measure override rates and review time.\n- **Explainability is proportional.** Credit decline needs reasons a customer can understand; a demand forecast needs performance evidence, not a narrative.\n- **Agents change the risk class.** An LLM that drafts is different from one that executes payments or emails customers. Tier by *what it can do*, not *what it is*.\n\n## Red flags\n- No inventory, or one that excludes vendor and GenAI use.\n- The AI committee is all builders; no risk, legal or business-owner voice.\n- Validation only at launch; no drift or performance monitoring.\n- Vendor contracts silent on training on your data, model changes and incident notice.\n- Customer-facing GenAI with no grounding, no logging, or no tested kill switch.\n- Board receives AI only as a \"innovation update\", never as a risk report.\n- Overall maturity \"3/5\" hiding a \"1\" on high-tier operating effectiveness.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## AI Governance Review: [organisation], [date]\n**Overall:** [one-paragraph verdict, including the biggest exposure]\n**Scope and evidence:** systems sampled, documents reviewed, interviews held\n\n| # | Domain | Design (1-5) | Operating (1-5) | Key evidence | Gap / finding | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Regulatory and standards mapping (verify current position with counsel)\n| Obligation / framework | Applies? | Date | Status | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Priority findings (top 5)\n1. Finding - why it matters - recommendation - owner - date\n\n### Roadmap\n| Horizon | Actions | Owner | Outcome / evidence of done |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 90 days | | | |\n| 6 months | | | |\n| 12 months | | | |\n\n### Quarterly board AI report (proposed)\n- Inventory count by tier, new and retired; high-tier systems list\n- Value delivered vs plan (top use cases)\n- Incidents, complaints, overrides, drift alerts; actions\n- Regulatory horizon and readiness\n- Assurance status (internal audit / external / certification)\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/deep-dive.md`: detailed criteria, checklists, scales and patterns\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and texts this skill draws on, with dates and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, the deep dive for detailed criteria, and the sources before citing any law, standard or date.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The inventory and its completeness are addressed first.\n- [ ] Design and operating effectiveness scored separately, with evidence.\n- [ ] GenAI and agentic risks covered explicitly, not folded into \"models\".\n- [ ] Vendor AI covered.\n- [ ] Obligations mapped with dates and flagged \"verify with counsel\"; no legal conclusions stated as certain.\n- [ ] Findings prioritised by exposure; the roadmap is sequenced and owned.\n- [ ] Board reporting defined.\n- [ ] Recommendations are proportionate to the organisation's size and AI footprint.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Regulatory dates and applicability change. Use `references/sources.md` as a starting point, verify against official texts, and recommend legal review.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential system details or personal data they are not permitted to share.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-risk-register`: build the inventory and tiering.\n- `responsible-ai-review`: deep review of one system.\n- `agentic-workflow-design`: controls for agents.\n- `board-question-bank`: the board's AI questions.\n- `risk-appetite`: set the AI appetite statement.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "deep-dive.md", "text": "# AI Governance Review: scoring criteria and evidence\n\n## Maturity scale (use for both Design and Operating)\n| Score | Label | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Absent / ad hoc | Nothing defined, or depends on individuals |\n| 2 | Emerging | Partially defined; inconsistent; not applied to all systems |\n| 3 | Defined | Documented and approved; applied to most in-scope systems |\n| 4 | Managed | Applied consistently; measured; exceptions tracked; independently checked |\n| 5 | Optimised | Continuously improved from metrics, incidents and horizon scanning; benchmark-grade |\n\n**Operating can never score higher than Design + 1.** Things rarely work well without being defined. A Design score above Operating by 2+ is a finding in itself (\"paper governance\").\n\n## Domain criteria and evidence\n\n| Domain | What \"3 - Defined\" looks like | What \"4-5\" adds | Evidence to request |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Strategy and appetite | Board-approved AI strategy with stated no-go areas | Quantified appetite and tolerances; reviewed annually | Board minutes, appetite statement |\n| 2. Policy | AI policy plus GenAI acceptable-use policy approved | Standards per tier; mapped to frameworks; attested by staff | Policy docs, attestation records |\n| 3. Accountability | Named exec owner; committee with ToR; RACI | Second-line independence; competence plans; board committee oversight in its ToR | ToR, committee minutes, RACI |\n| 4. Inventory | Register covering in-house and major vendor AI | Automated discovery; shadow-AI monitoring; lifecycle status maintained | Register extract; discovery method |\n| 5. Classification and impact | Tiering method applied; impact assessments for high tier | Assessments include affected persons, rights and fairness; re-run on change | 3 sample assessments |\n| 6. Data | Lawful basis and purpose checks before use | Data lineage; representativeness testing; IP and licence checks; DPDP-aligned retention | Data protection impact assessments, data sheets |\n| 7. Development and validation | Testing standard; independent validation for high tier | Fairness metrics by group; robustness and adversarial testing; model cards | Validation reports |\n| 8. GenAI and agents | Grounding and content filters; logging | Red-team programme; prompt-injection testing; agent autonomy tiers; human approval for consequential actions; action-level audit trail | Red-team reports; agent permission matrix |\n| 9. Third-party | Vendor AI due-diligence questionnaire | Contract clauses (no training on our data without consent, change notice, audit, incident notice, exit); concentration review | 2 sample contracts |\n| 10. Monitoring and change | Go-live approval; basic performance monitoring | Drift alerts; override and complaint KPIs; re-approval on vendor model change; tested kill switch | Dashboards; change log |\n| 11. Incidents and redress | AI incident definition; route to existing incident process | AI-specific root cause analysis; disclosure to affected people; appeal route; regulator notice playbook | Incident files; customer notices |\n| 12. People and literacy | Training for builders and approvers | Role-based literacy for all users; board training; speak-up data | Training records |\n\n## Rapid discovery playbook (when no inventory exists; 2-4 weeks)\n1. **Procurement scan:** vendor master and contracts with keywords (AI, ML, machine learning, GPT, LLM, copilot, assistant, model, prediction, scoring, automation).\n2. **SaaS and IT asset logs:** look for AI features enabled in existing platforms (CRM, HR, ITSM, office suites).\n3. **Network and proxy logs:** traffic to public GenAI services; identify volume by department (not individuals) for a no-blame view.\n4. **Business-unit survey (10 questions):** what AI do you use, for what decision, on what data, and who owns it.\n5. **Developer repositories and cloud accounts:** model endpoints, ML pipelines, API keys for model providers.\n6. **Reconcile into a single register;** tier it; confirm owners.\n\n## Proportionality guide\n| Organisation profile | Right-sized governance |\n|---|---|\n| <15 AI use cases, mostly vendor copilots, no customer-facing decisions | GenAI acceptable-use policy, simple register, one accountable exec, quarterly review in the existing risk committee |\n| Mid-size with some customer-impacting AI | AI policy plus tiering; AI risk committee with second-line chair; validation for high tier; vendor clauses; board report twice a year |\n| Large or regulated (BFSI, health, public sector), or high-risk uses (credit, hiring, claims, medical) | Full framework aligned to NIST AI RMF / ISO/IEC 42001; independent model validation; red-teaming; quarterly board reporting; internal audit cycle; consider certification |\n\n## GenAI and agent control checklist\n- Approved model and providers list; data-classification rules for what can go into each.\n- Retrieval grounding with source citations for factual answers; refusal when no source is found.\n- Input and output filtering (PII, toxicity, policy violations); jailbreak and prompt-injection testing, including indirect injection via documents, emails and web content.\n- Agents: least-privilege tools; read/write separation; spend and volume limits; human approval for external, financial, irreversible or customer-affecting actions; rate limits; sandboxed execution.\n- Logging: prompts, retrieved sources, outputs, tool calls and approvals, kept per retention policy (DPDP Rules require some logs to be kept at least a year for specified purposes; verify).\n- Evaluation: task-specific test sets, regression tests on model upgrades, periodic red-team exercises.\n- Kill switch tested; fallback process documented.\n- Disclosure to users that they are interacting with AI where required or expected (e.g. EU AI Act Art. 50 transparency)."}, {"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources, obligations and dates (as researched November 2026; verify before relying)\n\n## Frameworks and standards\n- **NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0)**, Jan 2023: functions GOVERN, MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE. **NIST AI 600-1 Generative AI Profile**, Jul 2024.\n- **ISO/IEC 42001:2023:** AI management system (certifiable). **ISO/IEC 23894:2023:** AI risk management guidance. **ISO/IEC 42005:2025:** AI system impact assessment. **ISO 31000:2018:** risk management.\n- **OECD AI Principles** (2019, updated May 2024).\n- **OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025)** and OWASP guidance on agentic AI threats.\n\n## European Union: AI Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, as amended by the Digital Omnibus (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744)\n- In force 1 Aug 2024. Prohibited practices and AI-literacy duty applied from 2 Feb 2025. General-purpose AI model obligations from 2 Aug 2025.\n- Omnibus changes (reported July 2026, check the Official Journal text):\n  - **Annex III high-risk** obligations moved to **2 Dec 2027** (from 2 Aug 2026).\n  - **Annex I** (AI in regulated products) high-risk obligations moved to **2 Aug 2028**.\n  - Systems for public authorities have until **2 Aug 2030**.\n  - **Art. 50(2)** synthetic-content marking: systems already on the market before 2 Aug 2026 have until **2 Dec 2026**.\n  - New Art. 5 prohibitions (e.g. non-consensual intimate imagery / \"nudifier\" apps, CSAM) apply from **2 Dec 2026**.\n- Applies extraterritorially where AI output is used in the EU. Indian firms serving EU clients or EU persons may be in scope as providers or deployers.\n\n## India\n- **Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023** and **DPDP Rules, 2025** (G.S.R. 846(E), notified 13 Nov 2025):\n  - Data Protection Board provisions from 13 Nov 2025.\n  - Consent-manager rules from 13 Nov 2026.\n  - Most data-fiduciary obligations (notice, consent, rights, security safeguards, breach notice, children's data, cross-border) from **13 May 2027**.\n  - Breach reporting to the Board includes a detailed report within 72 hours.\n  - Significant Data Fiduciaries must verify that algorithmic software does not pose risks to data principals' rights (Rule 13).\n  - Penalties under the Act's Schedule (up to ₹250 crore for certain failures; verify).\n- **MeitY: India AI Governance Guidelines** (released Nov 2025, under the IndiaAI Mission): principle-based, no standalone AI law; relies on existing laws, sandboxes, an India-specific risk classification, and new institutions (an AI Governance Group, an AI Safety Institute). Principles include Trust, People First, Innovation over Restraint, Fairness and Equity, Accountability, Understandable by Design, and Safety, Resilience and Sustainability.\n- **RBI: FREE-AI Committee report** (Framework for Responsible and Ethical Enablement of AI, 13 Aug 2025): seven \"sutras\"; recommendations include board-approved AI policies, AI inventories, audit, incident reporting and AI disclosures in annual reports for regulated entities. Check for any subsequent RBI directions.\n- **SEBI:** consultation (2025) on guidelines for responsible AI/ML use by market participants; check for a final circular.\n- **CERT-In Directions** (Apr 2022): cyber-incident reporting, relevant where AI incidents are security incidents.\n- **IRDAI / sector rules:** check outsourcing, IT and cyber, and any AI-specific guidance.\n\n## Other jurisdictions to check where relevant\n- **UK:** principles-based, regulator-led approach; ICO guidance on AI and data protection.\n- **US:** sector regulators; state laws (e.g. Colorado AI Act, whose effective date was deferred; check); NYC Local Law 144 (automated employment decision tools: bias audits).\n- **Singapore:** Model AI Governance Framework (including for GenAI); AI Verify.\n\n**Always confirm:** current consolidated text, effective dates, and whether the organisation is a provider, deployer, importer or distributor (EU), or a significant data fiduciary (India)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional organisation)\n\n**Organisation:** a mid-size Indian NBFC; 4,200 staff; digital personal loans and MSME lending; one EU-based fintech partner.\n**Trigger:** the Audit Committee asked for assurance after a news story on biased lending algorithms.\n**Evidence:** 31 documents; 14 interviews; samples of 6 AI systems; vendor contracts for 3.\n\n**Overall:** AI governance is **emerging (Design 2.3, Operating 1.6)**. The biggest exposure is the **credit-underwriting scorecard v4 and the collections-prioritisation model**. Both influence decisions about borrowers. Neither has been tested for fairness across groups since launch, and neither has a re-approval trigger when the vendor updates the model. Staff use of public GenAI tools is widespread (est. 1,100 weekly users from proxy data) with no sanctioned alternative.\n\n| # | Domain | Design | Operating | Key evidence | Finding | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Strategy and appetite | 2 | 1 | Digital strategy mentions AI; no no-go areas | No board-level stance on AI in credit decisions | High |\n| 3 | Accountability | 2 | 2 | CTO \"owns AI\"; no second-line role | Builders approve their own models | High |\n| 4 | Inventory | 1 | 1 | None; discovery found 23 systems (9 vendor-embedded) | Unknown footprint | Critical |\n| 7 | Validation | 3 | 2 | Model-risk policy covers statistical models | Not applied to vendor models; no fairness metrics | High |\n| 8 | GenAI / agents | 1 | 1 | Collections chatbot pilot live with 2,000 borrowers | No grounding, no logging of conversations | Critical |\n| 9 | Third-party | 2 | 1 | Contracts silent on model changes and data use | Vendor retrained the model on NBFC data without notice | High |\n| 10 | Monitoring | 2 | 2 | Model discrimination power (AUC) monitored monthly | No drift-by-segment, override or complaint tracking | Medium |\n| 11 | Incidents and redress | 1 | 1 | No AI incident category | Borrowers cannot get reasons for decline in plain language | High |\n| ... | | | | | | |\n\n**Priority findings**\n1. **No inventory.** You cannot govern what you cannot see. → Complete the register for all 23 systems and tier them (CRO, 30 days).\n2. **Collections chatbot live without controls.** → Restrict to FAQ-only answers grounded in the approved knowledge base; log all conversations; human handover for hardship or dispute; re-launch only after red-team testing (COO, 2 weeks).\n3. **Credit models untested for fairness.** → Run a disparity analysis by gender, age band and geography proxy; set tolerance thresholds; add to monthly monitoring (CRO, 60 days).\n4. **Vendor model change without notice.** → Amend contracts: no training on NBFC data without written consent; 30-day change notice; audit rights; incident notice within 24 hours (General Counsel, 90 days).\n5. **Shadow GenAI.** → Launch an enterprise GenAI tool with data protection; publish an acceptable-use policy; block uploads of customer data to public tools (CIO, 60 days).\n\n**Regulatory mapping (verify with counsel)**\n| Obligation | Applies? | Date | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| RBI expectations on board-approved AI policy, inventory, audit (FREE-AI; check for final directions) | Likely | Check | Gap |\n| DPDP Act and Rules: notice, consent, rights, security, breach notice | Yes | 13 May 2027 (most duties) | Partial |\n| RBI digital lending directions: disclosures, grievance redress | Yes | In force | Partial |\n| EU AI Act: credit scoring of EU persons via the partner is Annex III high-risk | Possibly | 2 Dec 2027 (post-Omnibus) | Needs legal view |\n\n**Roadmap**\n- **90 days:** register and tiering; chatbot controls; interim GenAI policy and tool; AI Risk Committee chaired by the CRO; Audit Committee to oversee AI governance (update its ToR).\n- **6 months:** AI policy and tiered standards; fairness testing and thresholds; vendor contract amendments; plain-language decline reasons; AI incident process.\n- **12 months:** internal audit review; role-based literacy programme; ISO/IEC 42001 gap assessment; annual report AI disclosure."}]}, {"id": "ai-risk-register", "pack": "board", "title": "AI Risk Register", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Build a risk register for AI use cases: classify, score, control and assign owners.", "tryp": "Build an AI risk register for these use cases: resume screening, customer chatbot, fraud detection, marketing copy generation, credit limit setting.", "when": ["Starting AI governance", "Audit or regulatory review", "Quarterly AI risk reporting"], "inputs": ["List of AI use cases with a short description", "Sector and jurisdictions"], "pairs": ["ai-governance-review", "responsible-ai-review", "risk-assessment", "risk-appetite"], "body": "# AI Risk Register\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a register that a CRO, an internal auditor and a board can all use. Every AI system is listed, tiered by real-world impact, its specific risks are scored before and after controls, and each one has an owner and early-warning indicators.\n\n## What makes an AI risk register different from a normal one\n- **The unit is the AI system or use case, not the department.** The same model used for marketing and for credit decisions is two entries.\n- **Risk depends on use and autonomy, not on technology.** A simple rules engine that declines loans can be higher risk than a frontier LLM that drafts internal emails.\n- **Risks change after launch:** data drift, vendor model updates, new prompts and tools, new users. The register is a living control, reviewed on change, not annually.\n- **Some harms fall on people outside the organisation** (applicants, patients, citizens). Impact must be scored from their side too, not only as enterprise loss.\n\n## Step 1: Inventory fields (minimum)\nID · name · business purpose · business owner · technical owner · lifecycle status (idea / pilot / live / retired) · in-house or vendor (which) · model type (statistical, ML, LLM, agent) · data used (personal? sensitive? children?) · **decision impact** (informs / recommends / decides / acts) · affected persons · volume (decisions per month) · jurisdictions · customer-facing (Y/N) · human oversight point · last validation date · tier.\n\nDiscover it properly. Include vendor-embedded AI and staff GenAI use. Scan procurement and contracts, SaaS features, proxy logs for public GenAI, cloud model endpoints, and run a short business-unit survey. (The `ai-governance-review` skill has a full discovery playbook.)\n\n## Step 2: Tier each system (defensible, simple)\nScore four factors, 1-3 each:\n| Factor | 1 | 2 | 3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Impact on people** (rights, money, health, access, employment) | None / internal convenience | Moderate, reversible | Significant or hard to reverse (credit, hiring, claims, medical, legal status, safety) |\n| **Autonomy** | Human decides; AI informs | AI recommends; human approves each case | AI decides or acts without per-case human approval |\n| **Scale / exposure** | Few users, internal | Hundreds to thousands of cases, or external-facing | Large-scale or public-facing |\n| **Data sensitivity** | No personal data | Personal data | Sensitive personal data, children's data, or regulated data |\n\n- **Tier H (high):** Impact = 3, or a total ≥ 9, or legally designated high-risk (e.g. EU AI Act Annex III areas such as creditworthiness, employment and access to essential services, where in scope).\n- **Tier M (medium):** total 6-8.\n- **Tier L (low):** total ≤ 5.\n- **Prohibited / not permitted:** uses the organisation (or law) bars, such as manipulative techniques, or social scoring where prohibited. Check the EU AI Act Art. 5 if in scope.\n\nDocument the rationale in one line. Tiering must be explainable to an auditor.\n\n## Step 3: Identify risks with an AI taxonomy\nFor each H and M system, consider:\n1. **Fairness and discrimination:** disparate outcomes across protected or proxy groups.\n2. **Accuracy and reliability:** errors, hallucination (GenAI), brittleness on edge cases.\n3. **Robustness and drift:** performance decays as data or behaviour shifts; vendor silently changes the model.\n4. **Security:** prompt injection (direct and indirect), data exfiltration through the model, model or data poisoning, model theft, insecure plug-ins and tools.\n5. **Privacy and data protection:** unlawful basis, purpose creep, retention, training on personal data, re-identification, cross-border transfers (DPDP, GDPR).\n6. **Transparency and explainability:** cannot explain adverse decisions; users unaware they are dealing with AI.\n7. **Autonomy and control (agents):** actions beyond intended scope; runaway loops; financial or external actions without approval.\n8. **Intellectual property and content:** training-data licensing; output infringing others' rights; confidential data leaking into outputs.\n9. **Third-party and concentration:** dependence on one model provider; vendor failure; contract gaps.\n10. **Conduct and consumer harm:** mis-selling, unfair treatment, vulnerable customers.\n11. **Operational resilience:** outage of an AI service in a critical process; no fallback.\n12. **Legal and regulatory non-compliance:** sector rules, AI-specific laws.\n13. **Reputational and ethical:** public trust, employee trust, misuse.\n\nWrite each risk as: **Because [cause], [event] may occur, resulting in [impact on people / business].**\n\n## Step 4: Score, control, assign\n- **Inherent risk:** Likelihood (1-5) × Impact (1-5), where Impact is the worse of the enterprise impact and the impact on affected persons.\n- **Controls:** preventive (design, testing, access limits, grounding, approvals) and detective (monitoring, KRIs, sampling, complaints analysis). Name each control's **owner** and **evidence** of operation.\n- **Residual risk** after controls, compared with appetite: Accept / Reduce / Avoid (do not deploy) / Transfer (contract, insurance).\n- **Actions** for gaps: owner and date.\n\n## Step 5: Key risk indicators (examples; set thresholds)\n- Selection-rate or approval-rate ratio between groups (e.g. flag below 0.8; a common screening heuristic, not a legal test).\n- Error or hallucination rate on a monitored sample; groundedness score.\n- Drift metric (population stability index, or performance by segment).\n- Human override rate, and median review time (a rubber-stamp signal if it is very low).\n- Complaints and appeals mentioning automated decisions.\n- Blocked prompt-injection attempts; data-loss-prevention hits on GenAI.\n- Agent actions requiring rollback; actions over limit.\n- Vendor model-change notices received vs assessed.\n\n## Step 6: Governance rhythm\n- **On change:** new system, vendor model update, new data source, new tool for an agent, new user group → re-tier and reassess.\n- **Monthly:** KRI review by the AI risk owner.\n- **Quarterly:** committee review; board summary.\n- **Annually:** full refresh; internal audit sample.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Tier by the worst realistic use, not the intended use.** If a \"drafting assistant\" is used to answer customers, tier it as customer-facing.\n- **Don't let scoring create false precision.** Use the scores to rank. Discuss the top items in prose.\n- **Accepting risk is a decision.** Record who accepted it, why, until when, and under which appetite.\n- **Prefer fewer, sharper risks per system** (3-6) over a 40-line generic list copied to every system.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## AI Risk Register: [organisation / scope], [date], v[x]\n\n### Inventory and tiering\n| ID | System | Purpose | Owner | In-house / vendor | Decision impact | Affected persons | Impact | Autonomy | Scale | Data | Tier | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Risks (H and M tiers)\n| ID | System | Risk (cause - event - impact) | Category | L | I | Inherent | Key controls (owner, evidence) | Residual | Decision (accept/reduce/avoid/transfer) | Action (owner, date) | KRI and threshold |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Board summary\n- Systems by tier (change since last quarter)\n- Top 5 residual risks and status of actions\n- KRI breaches and incidents\n- Risks accepted outside tolerance (who, why, until when)\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and texts this skill draws on, with dates and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, the deep dive for detailed criteria, and the sources before citing any law, standard or date.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Inventory includes vendor-embedded AI and GenAI use; it notes known gaps in discovery.\n- [ ] Tiering uses stated factors with a one-line rationale per system.\n- [ ] Risks are system-specific, written as cause-event-impact.\n- [ ] Impact considers affected persons, not only the enterprise.\n- [ ] Each control has an owner and evidence; each H-tier risk has a KRI with a threshold.\n- [ ] Risk acceptances are recorded with an approver and expiry.\n- [ ] Legal designations (e.g. EU AI Act high-risk) are flagged for counsel to verify.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Plain English; define jargon (e.g. \"prompt injection: hidden instructions in content that make the AI do something unintended\").\n- Regulatory tiers and dates change. See `references/sources.md` and recommend legal review.\n- Remind users not to paste personal data or confidential system details they are not permitted to share.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-governance-review`: the organisation-level view.\n- `responsible-ai-review`: deep dive on one high-tier system.\n- `risk-appetite`: set AI tolerances.\n- `agentic-workflow-design`: controls for agents.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (as researched November 2026; verify before relying)\n\n- **NIST AI RMF 1.0** (Jan 2023): especially MAP (context, impact) and MEASURE (metrics); **NIST AI 600-1 GenAI Profile** (Jul 2024): GenAI-specific risks such as confabulation, information security, data privacy and IP.\n- **ISO/IEC 23894:2023** (AI risk management); **ISO/IEC 42001:2023** (AI management system, Annex A controls); **ISO/IEC 42005:2025** (AI impact assessment); **ISO 31000:2018**.\n- **EU AI Act** (Reg. 2024/1689): Art. 5 prohibited practices; Art. 6 and Annex III high-risk areas (e.g. biometrics, critical infrastructure, education, employment, access to essential private and public services including creditworthiness and insurance pricing for life and health, law enforcement, migration, justice). Digital Omnibus (Reg. 2026/1744) moved Annex III obligations to 2 Dec 2027 and Annex I to 2 Aug 2028 (verify).\n- **India DPDP Act 2023 / DPDP Rules 2025:** most duties from 13 May 2027; SDF duty on algorithmic software (Rule 13); log retention rules (verify).\n- **RBI FREE-AI report** (Aug 2025): AI inventory, incident reporting, audit and board policy recommendations for regulated entities; **MeitY India AI Governance Guidelines** (Nov 2025).\n- **OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications 2025** (prompt injection, sensitive information disclosure, supply chain, excessive agency, misinformation and others).\n- **Four-fifths (80%) rule:** originates in US EEOC Uniform Guidelines (1978) as a screening heuristic for adverse impact. Use it as an indicator, not as a legal standard in other jurisdictions.\n- Sector rules to check: medical-device regulation for clinical AI (e.g. CDSCO in India); RBI digital lending directions; IRDAI guidelines; SEBI circulars on AI/ML use by intermediaries."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional private hospital group, 9 hospitals, India)\n\n## Inventory and tiering (extract)\n| ID | System | Purpose | Owner | Source | Decision impact | Impact | Autonomy | Scale | Data | Tier | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AI-01 | Chest X-ray triage | Flags suspected abnormalities to prioritise the radiologist queue | Head of Radiology | Vendor (regulated device; check registration) | Recommends | 3 | 2 | 2 | 3 | **H** | Clinical; sensitive health data; affects time to diagnosis |\n| AI-02 | Discharge-summary drafter (LLM) | Drafts the summary for doctor edit | CMO | Vendor LLM via enterprise platform | Recommends | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | **H** | Total 10; errors could reach patients and other providers |\n| AI-03 | Insurance pre-auth assistant (agent) | Fills TPA portals from records; submits on approval | Head of RCM | In-house agent | Acts (after approval) | 2 | 2 | 2 | 3 | **H** | Total 9; external submissions; financial impact |\n| AI-04 | Patient messaging-app bot | Appointments, FAQs | Head of Patient Experience | Vendor | Informs | 2 | 3 | 3 | 2 | **H** | Total 10; public-facing; may receive symptom questions |\n| AI-05 | Staff rostering optimiser | Proposes nurse rosters | CNO | Vendor | Recommends | 2 | 2 | 1 | 2 | **M** | Employment-adjacent; fairness of shifts |\n| AI-06 | Marketing copy GenAI | Drafts campaign text | CMO Marketing | Public tool | Informs | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | **L** | Must not receive patient data (policy) |\n\n## Risks (extract)\n| ID | Risk (cause - event - impact) | Category | L | I | Inh. | Key controls (owner, evidence) | Res. | Decision | Action | KRI |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AI-01 | Because the model was trained mainly on adult data from other populations, it may miss abnormalities in paediatric patients, delaying diagnosis | Accuracy / fairness | 3 | 5 | 15 | Use restricted to adults ≥18 (configuration); radiologist reads all films (SOP audit) | 6 | Reduce | Local validation study on 2,000 films incl. age bands (Head Radiology, Q2) | Sensitivity by age band; missed-finding reports |\n| AI-02 | Because LLMs can generate plausible but wrong text, a drug dosage may be stated incorrectly and pass doctor review, causing patient harm | Accuracy (hallucination) | 3 | 5 | 15 | Medication section pulled verbatim from the pharmacy system, not generated; doctor attestation; 2% weekly audit sample | 6 | Reduce | Structured-field enforcement for all numeric data (CMIO, 60 days) | Error rate in audit sample (threshold 0.5%); edit-distance trend |\n| AI-03 | Because the agent reads clinical notes, hidden text in an uploaded external report could instruct it to submit altered claim data (indirect prompt injection) | Security / autonomy | 2 | 4 | 8 | Human approval before each submission; agent cannot change amounts; content sanitisation | 4 | Accept (CRO, to Mar 2027) | Red-team test quarterly | Blocked injection attempts; rejected submissions |\n| AI-04 | Because patients ask symptom questions, the bot may give medical advice, causing harm and regulatory exposure | Conduct / safety | 4 | 4 | 16 | Symptom keywords route to a nurse helpline; disclaimer; no diagnosis intents | 8 | Reduce | Expand escalation lexicon in 3 languages; weekly transcript review (Head PX) | Escalation rate; unsafe-response audit count |\n| AI-05 | Because the optimiser learns from historical rosters, it may perpetuate unfavourable night-shift allocation to junior or female nurses | Fairness | 3 | 3 | 9 | CNO approves rosters; monthly distribution report | 6 | Reduce | Add fairness constraint (max nights per person) | Night-shift share by cohort |\n\n## Board summary (quarter)\n- 6 systems (H 4, M 1, L 1); 2 added, 0 retired.\n- Top residual risks: AI-04 bot safety (8); AI-01 paediatric accuracy (6, use restricted); AI-02 dosage hallucination (6).\n- KRI breach: AI-02 audit error rate 0.9% in week 7 → root cause was a template change; fixed; monitoring weekly.\n- Risk accepted: AI-03 injection residual 4, accepted by the CRO until Mar 2027 subject to quarterly red-team testing.\n- DPDP readiness: notices and consent for AI-04 and AI-02 data flows to be updated before May 2027 (verify scope with the DPO)."}]}, {"id": "risk-appetite", "pack": "board", "title": "Risk Appetite Statement", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Draft a board risk appetite statement with qualitative positions and measurable limits.", "tryp": "Draft a risk appetite statement for a mid-size NBFC with a growing digital lending book.", "when": ["ERM framework set-up", "Annual board risk review", "Regulator or rating agency expectations"], "inputs": ["Organisation, strategy and sector", "Current key risk metrics", "Regulatory requirements"], "pairs": ["risk-assessment", "ai-risk-register", "board-paper", "scenario-planning"], "body": "# Risk Appetite Statement and Framework\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a risk appetite framework that actually changes decisions. Metrics are measurable and reported, thresholds trigger action, and it cascades into limits that management works within. Not a page of adjectives.\n\n## Get the vocabulary right (most frameworks fail here)\n- **Risk capacity:** the maximum risk the organisation can bear before breaching regulatory capital or liquidity minimums, covenants, solvency, or its licence to operate. A hard ceiling.\n- **Risk appetite:** the aggregate level and types of risk the board is *willing* to take to achieve strategy. Set inside capacity, with a buffer.\n- **Risk tolerance:** the acceptable variation around appetite for a specific metric; the amber zone that triggers attention.\n- **Risk limits:** quantitative allocations of appetite to businesses, products, counterparties and portfolios, used day to day.\n- **Risk profile:** the actual current risk position, measured against all of the above.\n\n(Definitions consistent with the FSB *Principles for an Effective Risk Appetite Framework*, 2013, and COSO ERM 2017.)\n\n## Design principles\n1. **Strategy first.** Appetite is the risk side of the strategy. Start from what the strategy needs you to take (growth in unsecured lending, entering a new market, deploying AI) and set appetite for that, not from a generic list.\n2. **Seek, accept, minimise, avoid.** State openly which risks the organisation *seeks* for reward (e.g. credit risk in chosen segments), which it accepts, which it minimises (operational), and which it has **zero appetite** for (e.g. bribery, wilful regulatory breach, harm to patient safety).\n3. **Measurable or it doesn't exist.** Every category has 1-3 metrics that are already, or will be, reported, with green / amber / red thresholds.\n4. **Forward-looking.** Include stress or scenario metrics (e.g. capital ratio under a severe-but-plausible stress), not only current-state ratios.\n5. **Cascade.** Board appetite → executive limits → business-unit limits. Limits must sum within appetite.\n6. **Breach governance.** Pre-agree who is told, how fast, what remediation is required, and when the board must decide.\n7. **Use test.** Evidence that appetite is used in strategy, the plan, pricing, product approval, capital allocation and incentives. If nobody can cite a decision it changed, it's decoration.\n\n## Method\n1. **Context:** strategy, business model, regulatory minimums (capital, liquidity, licence conditions), covenants, rating targets, stakeholder expectations.\n2. **Capacity:** quantify the binding constraints (e.g. CRAR minimum and internal buffer; liquidity coverage; net debt/EBITDA covenant; solvency ratio for insurers).\n3. **Overall statement (half a page):** purpose, what risks the organisation seeks and avoids, and a few headline metrics (earnings volatility, capital, liquidity, reputation).\n4. **Category statements:** for each material category, a qualitative appetite level (Averse / Minimal / Cautious / Open / Seeking), a one-paragraph position, and metrics:\n   - **Strategic:** concentration in one product or market; % of earnings from new businesses.\n   - **Credit:** NPA / delinquency by segment; single-name and sector concentration; expected loss.\n   - **Market and liquidity:** liquidity coverage; funding concentration; asset-liability gaps; FX open position.\n   - **Operational and resilience:** losses as % of revenue; critical-service downtime beyond impact tolerance.\n   - **Cyber:** critical vulnerabilities overdue; recovery-test success; major incidents.\n   - **AI and model:** high-tier systems without validation; fairness-KRI breaches; incidents (see `ai-risk-register`).\n   - **Conduct and customer:** complaints per 10,000 customers; mis-selling findings; vulnerable-customer outcomes.\n   - **Compliance:** regulatory breaches (zero appetite for wilful breaches); overdue regulatory actions.\n   - **ESG and climate:** emissions intensity vs pathway; exposure to high-transition-risk sectors; safety (LTIFR, zero fatalities ambition).\n   - **People:** regretted attrition in critical roles; key-person dependency.\n   - **Reputational:** usually captured through drivers (conduct, ESG, cyber) plus a qualitative statement.\n5. **Thresholds:** for each metric, set **appetite (green), tolerance or early warning (amber), and limit (red)** and confirm red sits inside capacity. Calibrate with history, peers, stress results and the plan. Thresholds the plan itself would breach are a strategy conversation, not a footnote.\n6. **Cascade:** allocate to business units or portfolios; show that the sum stays within appetite.\n7. **Escalation:**\n   - **Amber:** risk owner action plan within X days; report to the risk committee.\n   - **Red:** immediate notice to the CRO and risk committee chair; board informed by the next meeting or earlier; remediation or a formal decision to accept with a timebound exception.\n8. **Integration:** where appetite is used in the business (product approval, pricing, capital allocation, credit policy, AI deployment approvals, incentives).\n9. **Review:** annually with the plan, and on material change. The risk management committee recommends; the board approves.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Fewer metrics, better data.** 20-35 board-level metrics in total is common for complex groups; 10-20 for simpler firms. Hundreds means nobody reads them.\n- **Avoid thresholds set where you already are.** If every metric is green from day one, the thresholds were set to the risk profile, not to appetite.\n- **Zero appetite needs care.** \"Zero appetite for operational losses\" is impossible and gets ignored. Use zero only for conduct you can actually choose to avoid: fraud, bribery, wilful breach, knowingly unsafe products.\n- **Qualitative still matters** for reputation and culture, but anchor it to observable indicators.\n- **Appetite must survive a bad quarter.** If a breach is always met with \"temporary, accept\", the framework has no teeth. Track repeat exceptions.\n- **Link to incentives.** Remuneration and performance assessments should reflect risk outcomes (malus and clawback where applicable).\n\n## Sector variants\n- **Banks and NBFCs:** capital (CRAR/CET1) and liquidity (LCR/NSFR where applicable) dominate capacity. Credit concentration, interest-rate risk in the banking book, outsourcing and digital lending conduct. Align with RBI expectations for the entity's category and ICAAP. Verify current directions.\n- **Insurers:** solvency ratio vs the regulatory minimum; underwriting and reserving risk; catastrophe accumulation; claims conduct. Align with IRDAI corporate governance and risk-management requirements.\n- **Listed non-financial companies:** leverage and covenants; liquidity runway; customer and supplier concentration; commodity and FX; project risk; safety; cyber; ESG. SEBI LODR Reg 21 requires a risk management committee for the top 1,000 listed entities and a board-approved risk management policy. Verify current applicability and meeting requirements.\n- **Healthcare:** patient safety (zero appetite for avoidable harm), clinical governance, data protection.\n- **Tech and AI-heavy firms:** model risk, AI conduct, data protection, availability, third-party concentration (cloud and model providers).\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Risk Appetite Framework: [organisation], FY[..]\n### 1. Overall risk appetite statement\n...\n### 2. Capacity (binding constraints)\n| Constraint | Regulatory / contractual minimum | Internal buffer | Capacity used today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n### 3. Category appetite\n| Category | Level | Position (1-2 sentences) | Metric | Green (appetite) | Amber (tolerance) | Red (limit) | Current | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n### 4. Cascade to business limits (summary)\n### 5. Escalation and breach governance\n| Status | Who is told | By when | Required action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n### 6. Use test: where appetite is embedded\n### 7. Review and approval\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and texts this skill draws on, with dates and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, the deep dive for detailed criteria, and the sources before citing any law, standard or date.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Capacity, appetite, tolerance and limits are distinguished; red thresholds sit inside capacity with a buffer.\n- [ ] The overall statement links to strategy and states which risks are sought vs avoided.\n- [ ] Every category has measurable metrics with three thresholds and an owner.\n- [ ] At least one forward-looking (stress or scenario) metric.\n- [ ] Escalation rules are explicit; exceptions are time-bound.\n- [ ] Zero-appetite statements are used only where avoidance is genuinely possible.\n- [ ] The plan is checked against thresholds; conflicts are surfaced.\n- [ ] Regulatory specifics are flagged for verification.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Plain English; define technical terms on first use.\n- Use the user's currency and regulatory context.\n- Regulatory minimums and applicability change; recommend verification with the CRO, Company Secretary or counsel.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential data they are not permitted to share.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal, company-secretarial or audit advice. Regulatory references (Companies Act, SEBI LODR, SS-1, sector regulators) must be confirmed with your Company Secretary, counsel or assurance provider before use.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `risk-assessment`: project-level risks.\n- `ai-risk-register`: AI metrics.\n- `scenario-planning`: stress scenarios.\n- `board-paper`: approval paper for the framework.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (verify current versions)\n\n- **Financial Stability Board: Principles for an Effective Risk Appetite Framework** (Nov 2013): definitions of risk capacity, appetite, limits and profile; roles of the board, CEO, CRO, CFO and internal audit.\n- **COSO: Enterprise Risk Management, Integrating with Strategy and Performance** (2017): 5 components and 20 principles; appetite and tolerance in strategy setting.\n- **ISO 31000:2018**, Risk management: Guidelines.\n- **Companies Act, 2013:** s.134(3)(n) (statement on development and implementation of the risk management policy in the board's report); s.177(4)(vii) (audit committee evaluation of risk management systems); Schedule IV (independent directors to satisfy themselves that risk management systems are robust).\n- **SEBI LODR Regulations, 2015:** Reg 17(9) (board responsible for framing, implementing and monitoring the risk management plan); Reg 21 and Schedule II Part C-A (risk management committee: composition, meetings, role including framing the risk management policy and covering cyber and ESG risks). Applicability is the top 1,000 listed entities by market capitalisation. Verify the current text.\n- **RBI:** Guidance Note on Operational Risk Management and Operational Resilience (2024); ICAAP and capital-adequacy directions by entity category (scale-based regulation for NBFCs); outsourcing and IT governance directions. Verify the current master directions for your entity type.\n- **IRDAI:** corporate governance and risk-management requirements for insurers (master circular). Verify.\n- **Basel Committee:** *Principles for the Sound Management of Operational Risk* (revised 2021); *Principles for Operational Resilience* (2021)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional mid-size NBFC, ₹28,000 Cr AUM, growing digital unsecured lending)\n\n## 1. Overall statement\nWe take credit risk in our chosen segments (MSME secured lending, salaried personal loans) to earn risk-adjusted returns above our cost of equity. We operate with capital and liquidity buffers well above regulatory minimums, so we can withstand a severe-but-plausible stress without raising capital in distress. We have **no appetite** for wilful regulatory breaches, mis-selling, unfair recovery practices, fraud or bribery. We use AI and automation to improve speed and inclusion, but not at the expense of fair treatment or explainable decisions.\n\n## 2. Capacity\n| Constraint | Regulatory / contractual minimum | Internal buffer | Today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CRAR | 15% (verify for category) | Manage ≥ 19% | 22.4% |\n| Liquidity (30-day survival / LCR as applicable) | Per RBI framework (verify) | ≥ 120% of requirement | 165% |\n| Debt covenant (bank lines): Debt/Equity | ≤ 6.0x | Manage ≤ 4.5x | 3.9x |\n\n## 3. Category appetite (extract)\n| Category | Level | Position | Metric | Green | Amber | Red | Current | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Credit: digital unsecured | Open | Grow, but only in segments with proven loss rates | 90+ DPD (12-month-on-book cohort) | <3.0% | 3.0-3.5% | >3.5% | 2.7% | CRO |\n| Credit concentration | Cautious | Avoid single-name and sector exposure that a single shock could impair | Top-20 borrowers % of AUM | <8% | 8-10% | >10% | 6.1% | CRO |\n| Capital under stress | Minimal | Withstand a severe stress without breaching the buffer | Stressed CRAR (2008-style + 300 bps rate shock) | >17% | 16-17% | <16% | 18.9% | CFO |\n| Liquidity | Minimal | Survive 90 days of market closure | Survival horizon (days) | >90 | 60-90 | <60 | 120 | Treasurer |\n| Conduct | Averse | Fair treatment in sales and recovery | Complaints per 10k loans (RBI-reportable) | <4 | 4-6 | >6 | 3.2 | CCO |\n| Conduct: recovery | Zero (wilful) | No coercive recovery practices | Upheld recovery-conduct complaints | 0 | n/a | ≥1 triggers review | 0 | Head Collections |\n| AI and model | Cautious | Automate only explainable, tested decisions | High-tier models without current validation | 0 | 1 | ≥2 | 1 | CRO |\n| AI fairness | Cautious | Comparable approval outcomes across groups, after risk factors | Approval-rate ratio, lowest group vs highest (risk-adjusted) | ≥0.85 | 0.80-0.85 | <0.80 | 0.83 | Head Credit Analytics |\n| Cyber | Minimal | Protect customer data and critical services | Critical vulnerabilities open >30 days | 0 | 1-3 | >3 | 2 | CISO |\n| Operational resilience | Minimal | Loan origination and collections stay within impact tolerance | Unplanned downtime of critical services beyond tolerance (hrs/qtr) | 0 | ≤4 | >4 | 0 | COO |\n| Compliance | Zero (wilful) | Full compliance with RBI directions | Overdue regulatory actions | 0 | 1-2 (non-material) | >2 or any material | 1 | CCO |\n| ESG: climate | Cautious | Limit exposure to high-transition-risk sectors in MSME book | % of MSME book in high-transition-risk sectors | <12% | 12-15% | >15% | 9% | CRO |\n\n**Plan check:** the FY28 plan grows digital unsecured to 22% of AUM. The modelled 90+ DPD peaks at 3.3% in Q3, **in amber**. → Board discussion: accept, with tighter cut-offs in two sub-segments, or slow growth to 19%.\n\n## 5. Escalation\n| Status | Who is told | By when | Required action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Amber | Risk owner → CRO; risk management committee at next meeting | 5 working days | Action plan with dates; monitored monthly |\n| Red | CRO → RMC Chair and MD immediately; board at next meeting or a special meeting if material | 24 hours | Remediation plan, or a formal time-bound exception approved by the RMC (max 2 quarters), recorded and tracked |\n| Repeat amber (3 consecutive months) | Treated as red | — | — |\n\n## 6. Use test\n- Product approval: no new product without appetite-fit sign-off by the CRO.\n- Pricing: risk-based pricing floors linked to expected loss and the capital charge.\n- AI deployment: high-tier models need validation and fairness metrics within appetite before go-live.\n- Incentives: sales incentives subject to malus for upheld mis-selling; collections incentives exclude volume-only targets."}]}, {"id": "integrity-check", "pack": "board", "title": "Integrity Check", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Screen a plan, proposal or engagement for bribery, money-laundering, tax, health-product, competition and other legal and ethical red lines before it goes out.", "tryp": "Run an integrity check on this growth plan for a pharma client before it goes to the steering committee. It uses a local facilitator for a hospital tender and a doctors' summit abroad. [paste plan]", "when": ["Engagement acceptance and proposals", "Growth, sales, pricing, tax or market-entry recommendations", "When an agent, intermediary or success fee is involved", "Whenever something feels off"], "inputs": ["The plan, deck or engagement", "Countries, sector and any government touchpoints", "Payment flows and third parties"], "pairs": ["red-team-review", "risk-appetite", "risk-assessment", "board-question-bank"], "body": "# Integrity Check\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a structured screen that catches the advice that should never be given, before it reaches a client, while there is still time to change it. Most serious professional-services scandals did not start with a decision to break the law. They started with a growth target, a clever tactic, or a \"local partner\" nobody looked at closely, and no one asked the question this skill asks.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Screen the advice, not just the client.** Legal exposure often sits in what is recommended (a sales tactic, a tax structure, a payment route), not in who the client is.\n2. **Follow the money and the intermediaries.** Unusual fees, success fees, agents, \"facilitators\", subcontractors and payments routed through third parties are where bribery and laundering hide.\n3. **Ask who is harmed.** A tactic that works because a patient, borrower, consumer or vulnerable group is misled, pressured or put at risk fails, even if it is technically legal.\n4. **Substance over form.** Relabelling a payment as a \"consulting fee\", a gift as \"medical education\", or a scheme as \"tax planning\" changes nothing.\n5. **The newspaper and regulator test.** Would the plan survive being read out in court, in a regulator's order, or on the front page, in the client's own words?\n6. **Escalate early, in writing.** Raising a concern before work proceeds is cheap; raising it after delivery is not. Legal counsel involved early also helps preserve legal privilege.\n7. **Never tidy the record.** Once a problem is suspected, documents are preserved, not deleted or edited. Destroying records turns a problem into a crime.\n\n## Where Claude stops\nThis skill exists to keep advice lawful. Claude will **not** help to design, structure, conceal or disguise bribes or kickbacks; route payments to avoid detection or reporting thresholds; launder money or evade sanctions; create schemes whose main purpose is to evade tax; promote medicines or devices for unapproved uses or with misleading claims; fix prices or share competitors' confidential information; trade on or leak inside information; or destroy, alter or backdate records. If asked, Claude declines briefly, explains the risk in one line, and offers the lawful route to the same business goal (for example, compliant medical education, a documented agent due-diligence process, or legitimate tax planning with disclosure).\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- **What is being screened:** a proposal, an engagement, a deck, a strategy, a specific tactic, or a third party.\n- **Where it applies:** the countries involved, the client's sector (regulated sectors such as healthcare, financial services, defence and infrastructure carry higher risk) and whether government or state-owned entities are involved.\n- **The money flows:** who pays whom, for what, through whom, and how fees are structured (especially success fees and pass-through payments).\n- **Who it affects:** customers, patients, employees, communities, regulators, competitors.\n- **Who decides:** the engagement partner, the client's board, the compliance or legal function.\n\n## Method\n1. **Map the plan.** List each recommendation or tactic, each payment flow and each third party in a simple table.\n2. **Screen against the ten red-line areas** (detail and laws in `references/sources.md`):\n   1. **Bribery and corruption:** anything of value to a public official, or to anyone, to win or keep business improperly; facilitation payments; hospitality and gifts out of proportion; political or charitable contributions linked to decisions.\n   2. **Third parties and intermediaries:** agents, distributors, subcontractors, \"local partners\" or facilitators with unclear roles, government links, unusual fees, cash or offshore payment requests, or refusal to accept audit rights.\n   3. **Money laundering and sanctions:** unclear source of funds, complex structures without commercial reason, payments through unrelated parties, sanctioned countries, persons or goods, splitting amounts to stay below thresholds.\n   4. **Tax:** schemes with no real commercial substance, artificial use of tax havens, undisclosed income or assets, abuse of treaties, \"aggressive\" structures marketed as low-risk.\n   5. **Health products:** promotion of medicines or devices for unapproved uses or groups, misleading efficacy or safety claims, inducements to doctors or pharmacists, \"education\" or travel that is really a reward for prescribing, targeting high prescribers of addictive products.\n   6. **Competition:** agreeing or signalling prices, bids or markets with competitors; using a consultant as a channel for competitors' confidential data; abuse of a dominant position.\n   7. **Insider and confidential information:** use or sharing of unpublished price-sensitive information; use of information obtained from government or a former employer in breach of confidence.\n   8. **Consumer and vulnerable-group harm:** misleading claims, dark patterns, mis-selling, predatory pricing or lending, coercive collections, exploitation of addiction, children or people in financial distress.\n   9. **Conflicts of interest:** advising parties on both sides, personal or financial interests not disclosed, reviewing one's own work, advising a regulator and those it regulates.\n   10. **Records and obstruction:** pressure to delete, edit, backdate or not write things down; \"keep it off email\"; any action on records once an investigation or dispute is reasonably expected.\n3. **Record the evidence** for each flag: the exact wording, number or fact that triggered it. No evidence, no flag.\n4. **Assign a verdict to each issue:**\n   - **Stop:** likely unlawful, or serious harm; do not proceed in this form.\n   - **Escalate:** possible breach or significant harm; needs legal, compliance or ethics review before work continues.\n   - **Proceed with conditions:** acceptable with named controls (due diligence, contract clauses, disclosure, approvals, monitoring).\n   - **Clear:** no issue found on the facts given.\n5. **Offer the lawful alternative** that achieves the legitimate business goal, where one exists.\n6. **Name the reviewer:** legal counsel, compliance officer, tax adviser, medical-affairs or regulatory team, the audit committee, or the board.\n7. **Give an overall verdict** for the plan and the conditions to proceed.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Flag, don't accuse.** Use \"potential\", \"could be seen as\", \"needs review\". The skill identifies risk; it does not decide guilt.\n- **Grey areas still go up.** If reasonable people could disagree on whether something is lawful, the answer is escalation, not a confident \"fine\".\n- **Local custom is not a defence.** \"Everyone does it here\" is a red flag, not a reason.\n- **Legal is not the same as acceptable.** Flag serious harm even if no law is broken; label it clearly as an ethical or reputational issue.\n- **Weigh scale and intent signals.** One modest, recorded business meal differs from a pattern of gifts to decision-makers before a tender.\n- **Withdrawal is an option.** If the client insists on the problematic element, declining or exiting the engagement may be the right recommendation.\n- **Whistleblowing channels exist for a reason.** If wrongdoing seems to be under way, point to the organisation's vigil mechanism or speak-up channel and to legal advice.\n\n## Red flags\n- Success fees or commissions to a party whose service is unclear.\n- \"Don't put it in writing\", or requests to delete or edit documents.\n- A third party recommended by a government official or the client's buyer.\n- Growth plans that depend on the highest prescribers, heaviest users or most indebted customers of a harmful product.\n- Tax savings that are large compared with any real change in the business.\n- Competitor data \"from a contact\" or from a newly hired employee.\n- Urgency used to skip due diligence or approvals.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Engagement acceptance:** client, sector, country and politically exposed persons; source of fees; conflicts; whether the brief itself is legitimate.\n- **Growth or sales strategy:** tactics, incentives, targeting and claims; health products and financial services need extra care.\n- **Market entry or public-sector bids:** intermediaries, licences, government touchpoints, offset and local-content partners.\n- **Tax or structuring advice:** commercial substance, disclosure, GAAR or equivalent rules, reputational test.\n- **M&A and due diligence:** the target's past conduct, inherited liabilities, third-party networks.\n- **Board or audit committee:** use the output as an oversight checklist and ask management for evidence of controls.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Integrity Check: [plan / engagement], for [client / decision]\n**Overall verdict:** Stop / Escalate / Proceed with conditions / Clear\n**Countries and sector:** ...  **Reviewed by Claude on:** [information provided]; not a legal opinion\n\n| # | Item (tactic, payment or party) | Red-line area | Evidence that triggered the flag | Verdict | Lawful alternative or condition | Reviewer |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Conditions to proceed:** ...\n**Questions to answer before work continues:** ...\n**What is clear (keep):** ...\n**Records:** preserve all documents relating to the flagged items.\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: the laws, standards and guidance this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Every recommendation, payment flow and third party mapped.\n- [ ] All ten red-line areas considered; none skipped silently.\n- [ ] Every flag backed by specific evidence from the material.\n- [ ] Each issue has a verdict, a lawful alternative or condition, and a named reviewer.\n- [ ] Language is \"potential risk\", not an accusation; legal points marked for verification.\n- [ ] No help given to design, conceal or work around any red line.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** A \"clear\" result means no issue was found in the information provided; it is not clearance. Anti-bribery, money-laundering, tax, sanctions, competition and health-product rules differ by country and change often. If you suspect wrongdoing has already happened, preserve records and speak to legal counsel before anything else.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n- `risk-appetite`: Draft a board risk appetite statement with qualitative positions and measurable limits.\n- `risk-assessment`: Identify, score and plan responses to the risks of a project, decision or business.\n- `board-question-bank`: Generate the sharp, fair questions a non-executive director should ask on any paper or topic.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (laws, standards and guidance)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals. Laws below change often: verify the current text and enforcement position with qualified counsel before relying on any point.*\n\n## 1. Bribery and corruption\n- **India: Prevention of Corruption Act 1988, as amended in 2018.** Giving or promising an undue advantage is an offence (Section 8). **Section 9** makes a commercial organisation liable when a person associated with it bribes a public servant to obtain or retain business; the defence is \"adequate procedures\" in line with guidelines to be prescribed. *Verify:* as of late 2026, commentators report the central government has **not yet notified** those guidelines, so organisations rely on their own programmes, often modelled on the UK Bribery Act principles. Section 10 extends liability to directors and officers who consented or connived.\n- **United States: Foreign Corrupt Practices Act 1977 (FCPA).** Anti-bribery provisions (payments to foreign officials to obtain or retain business) and accounting provisions (accurate books and records, adequate internal controls) for issuers. *Verify:* enforcement was paused by executive order in February 2025 and resumed under narrower DOJ guidelines issued in June 2025 (priorities include cartels and transnational criminal organisations, harm to US competitiveness, national security and serious misconduct). The statute itself did not change; the limitation period means past conduct can still be pursued, and DOJ corporate enforcement and self-disclosure policies were revised in 2026.\n- **United Kingdom: Bribery Act 2010.** Offences of bribing, being bribed and bribing a foreign official; **Section 7** corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery by an associated person, with an \"adequate procedures\" defence. Ministry of Justice guidance sets six principles: proportionate procedures, top-level commitment, risk assessment, due diligence, communication and training, monitoring and review.\n- **International:** OECD Anti-Bribery Convention (1997); UN Convention against Corruption (UNCAC; ratified by India in 2011).\n- **Standards:** ISO 37001 (anti-bribery management systems); ISO 37301 (compliance management systems).\n\n## 2. Fraud (corporate failure to prevent)\n- **United Kingdom: Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023.** The **failure to prevent fraud** offence came into force on **1 September 2025** for \"large organisations\" (at least two of: turnover over £36m, assets over £18m, more than 250 employees; group-wide test). Liability arises when an associate commits a specified fraud intending to benefit the organisation; the defence is reasonable fraud prevention procedures; penalty is an unlimited fine.\n\n## 3. Money laundering and sanctions\n- **India: Prevention of Money-laundering Act 2002 (PMLA)** and its rules; obligations for reporting entities; Financial Intelligence Unit-India (FIU-IND).\n- **Global standard:** Financial Action Task Force (FATF) Recommendations, including customer due diligence, beneficial ownership and politically exposed persons.\n- **Sanctions and export controls:** UN Security Council sanctions; US OFAC and EU / UK sanctions regimes; India's SCOMET list for dual-use exports. Check the current lists before any transaction.\n\n## 4. Tax\n- **India: Income-tax Act 2025,** in force from **1 April 2026,** replacing the Income-tax Act 1961 (earlier years remain under the 1961 Act). Both include **General Anti-Avoidance Rules (GAAR),** which allow arrangements lacking commercial substance to be disregarded. *Verify* section numbers in the new Act.\n- **India:** Black Money (Undisclosed Foreign Income and Assets) and Imposition of Tax Act 2015; Prohibition of Benami Property Transactions Act 1988 (amended 2016).\n- **International:** OECD/G20 Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) actions, including mandatory disclosure rules for aggressive tax planning in several jurisdictions.\n- **Professional standards:** codes of ethics for accountants and tax practitioners (for example, the ICAI Code of Ethics and the IESBA Code) on tax planning and promoting schemes.\n\n## 5. Medicines and medical devices\n- **India: Uniform Code for Pharmaceutical Marketing Practices 2024 (UCPMP 2024),** Department of Pharmaceuticals: claims must be balanced and supported by approved product information; restrictions on gifts, travel and hospitality to healthcare professionals; rules for continuing medical education.\n- **India:** Drugs and Cosmetics Act 1940 and rules; Medical Devices Rules 2017; Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act 1954; National Medical Commission rules on professional conduct of doctors (gifts, travel, hospitality).\n- **United States:** Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (misbranding, including promotion for unapproved uses); Anti-Kickback Statute; False Claims Act.\n- **Concept:** \"off-label\" use is a prescriber's clinical decision; **promoting** a product for an unapproved use is the regulated act.\n\n## 6. Competition\n- **India: Competition Act 2002:** anti-competitive agreements, including cartels and bid-rigging (Section 3), and abuse of dominance (Section 4). Hub-and-spoke conduct, where a third party such as a consultant or distributor passes competitively sensitive information between competitors, can amount to a cartel.\n- Comparable rules: US Sherman Act; EU Articles 101 and 102 TFEU; UK Competition Act 1998.\n\n## 7. Insider and confidential information\n- **India: SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations 2015:** unpublished price-sensitive information (UPSI); consultants and advisers can be \"connected persons\"; structured digital database and trading-window obligations.\n- Breach of confidence and contractual confidentiality; Official Secrets Act 1923 for government information.\n- **India: Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023** and DPDP Rules 2025 for personal data.\n\n## 8. Consumers and vulnerable groups\n- **India: Consumer Protection Act 2019;** Central Consumer Protection Authority guidelines on misleading advertisements (2022) and dark patterns (2023).\n- **Financial services:** RBI fair practices codes for lenders and recovery agents; SEBI and IRDAI conduct rules on mis-selling.\n\n## 9. Conflicts of interest and whistleblowing\n- **India: Companies Act 2013:** related-party transactions (Section 188); vigil mechanism (Section 177(9)); auditor independence (Section 144).\n- **SEBI LODR Regulations 2015:** vigil mechanism (Regulation 22) and related-party rules.\n- **Standard:** ISO 37002 (whistleblowing management systems).\n\n## 10. Records and obstruction\n- **India: Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023:** causing disappearance of evidence (Section 238, formerly IPC Section 201).\n- **United States:** obstruction of justice and destruction of records in federal investigations (18 U.S.C. §§1512, 1519).\n- **Practice:** legal holds; preserve documents once litigation or investigation is reasonably anticipated; route sensitive reviews through counsel to preserve privilege.\n\n## Concepts used\n- Red flags for third parties and intermediaries (OECD, ISO 37001 and enforcement guidance).\n- \"Substance over form\" in tax and accounting.\n- The newspaper test and stakeholder-harm analysis in business ethics."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional Indian pharmaceutical company and fictional consulting team)\n\n**Situation:** A consulting team has drafted a growth plan for a mid-sized Indian pharmaceutical company. The brief is to grow sales of its prescription pain-relief brand (a Schedule H1 product with dependence risk) by 25% in 18 months, and to win a hospital-supply tender in a neighbouring country. The engagement partner runs the integrity check before the steering committee.\n\n**Plan as drafted (summarised):**\n1. Rank doctors by current prescription volume; focus field force on the top 10% of prescribers.\n2. Invite the top 200 prescribers to a \"knowledge summit\" in a resort abroad, all costs paid, with spouses.\n3. Promote the brand for post-operative pain in children, \"where competitors are absent\". The approved label covers adults only.\n4. Raise dose-strength mix: message that the higher strength \"works faster with no added risk\".\n5. For the tender, appoint a \"local facilitator\" recommended by the hospital authority's procurement head, paid a 6% success fee, invoiced through a company in a third country.\n6. Use competitor price lists provided by a newly hired sales manager from a rival.\n7. Partner's note on the draft: \"Keep items 2 and 5 out of the written deck; discuss verbally.\"\n\n## Integrity Check: Brand growth and tender plan, for steering committee\n**Overall verdict:** **Stop** in its current form. Five items need redesign; two can proceed with conditions.\n**Countries and sector:** India and neighbouring country; pharmaceuticals; public-sector hospital buyer. Not a legal opinion.\n\n| # | Item | Red-line area | Evidence that triggered the flag | Verdict | Lawful alternative or condition | Reviewer |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Target top 10% of prescribers | Health products; vulnerable groups | Growth depends on highest prescribers of a dependence-risk product | **Escalate** | Segment by unmet clinical need and appropriate use, not volume; add safety and responsible-use messaging; monitor for unusual prescribing | Medical affairs; compliance |\n| 2 | Resort \"knowledge summit\" with spouses | Bribery (doctors); UCPMP 2024 | All-expenses foreign travel and hospitality for top prescribers | **Stop** | Accredited continuing medical education in India, on clinical content, open selection, no leisure or spouse costs, within UCPMP limits | Compliance; legal |\n| 3 | Paediatric post-operative promotion | Health products (unapproved use) | Label covers adults only; plan targets children | **Stop** | Do not promote. If there is clinical evidence, pursue a label extension through the regulator | Regulatory affairs; legal |\n| 4 | \"No added risk\" claim for higher strength | Health products (misleading claim) | Claim not supported by approved product information | **Stop** | Use only claims in the approved prescribing information, balanced with safety data | Medical affairs |\n| 5 | Facilitator with 6% success fee via third country | Bribery (public officials); third parties; money laundering | Recommended by the buyer's procurement head; success fee; payment through an unrelated jurisdiction | **Stop** | Bid directly or through a distributor selected by documented due diligence, fixed fee for defined services, payment to a local account in the provider's name, audit rights and anti-bribery clauses | Legal; compliance; board audit committee |\n| 6 | Rival's price lists from new hire | Confidential information; competition | Data came from a former employer, not a public source | **Stop** | Quarantine and delete through legal; use public tender results and market research instead; brief the new hire on confidentiality | Legal |\n| 7 | \"Keep items 2 and 5 out of the deck\" | Records and obstruction | Instruction to avoid written record of high-risk items | **Escalate** (immediately) | Record all items and this review in writing; preserve drafts; route through counsel | General counsel; engagement risk partner |\n| 8 | Field-force training on the brand | — | Standard, approved-label content | **Clear** | Keep | — |\n| 9 | Pricing-tier review for hospital channel | Competition (low) | Uses public tender data only | **Proceed with conditions** | No contact with competitors on pricing; document data sources | Legal (light review) |\n\n**Conditions to proceed:** items 2-6 redesigned as above; item 1 reframed around appropriate use; written record of this review held by the engagement risk partner.\n\n**Questions to answer before work continues:**\n- Who introduced the facilitator, and has any payment or promise already been made?\n- Has the competitor data been shared beyond the new hire?\n- Does the 25% target still hold once items 2-5 are removed? If not, reset the target with the client rather than reintroducing the tactics.\n\n**What is clear (keep):** clinical-need segmentation logic, field-force training, hospital-channel pricing review using public data.\n\n**Records:** preserve all drafts, emails and notes relating to items 2, 5, 6 and 7.\n\n**Note on the target:** removing the flagged tactics reduces the modelled uplift. The team should present the lower, lawful growth case and explain why, rather than leaving the client to find the gap.\n\n*Have this reviewed by legal counsel before the steering committee.*"}]}, {"id": "esg-materiality", "pack": "board", "title": "ESG Materiality Assessment", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Identify and prioritise the sustainability topics that matter most, using double materiality.", "tryp": "Run a materiality assessment for an Indian auto-components manufacturer exporting to Europe.", "when": ["Sustainability strategy or report preparation", "Board ESG oversight", "Investor or customer ESG questionnaires"], "inputs": ["Company, sector, geographies, value chain", "Existing ESG data or reports", "Key stakeholders"], "pairs": ["brsr-readiness", "pestel-scan", "risk-appetite", "board-paper"], "body": "# ESG Double Materiality Assessment\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a defensible, documented list of the sustainability matters that genuinely matter to the business and to people and planet. It shows why each one matters, how strongly, over what horizon, and what the organisation will do and disclose about it. The process is built to survive an auditor's or investor's scrutiny.\n\n## Core concepts\n- **Impact materiality (inside-out):** the organisation's actual or potential, positive or negative impacts on people and the environment, across its own operations **and value chain**.\n- **Financial materiality (outside-in):** sustainability matters that could reasonably be expected to affect the organisation's cash flows, access to finance or cost of capital, over the short, medium or long term. This is the ISSB (IFRS S1) lens.\n- **Double materiality:** a matter is material if it is material on **either** lens (ESRS/CSRD approach; GRI covers the impact lens).\n- **IROs:** for each topic, identify specific **Impacts, Risks and Opportunities**. Assess IROs, not vague topic labels.\n- **Dependencies:** on natural resources, workers, communities and suppliers often link impact to financial risk. Water scarcity is a classic example.\n\nWhich lens leads depends on the reporting regime:\n- **ISSB / IFRS S1-S2:** financial materiality (investor-focused).\n- **ESRS / CSRD (EU):** double materiality, mandatory for in-scope companies. Verify current scope after the 2025-26 Omnibus simplifications.\n- **GRI:** impact materiality.\n- **BRSR (India):** principle-based disclosures, with a material responsible-business-conduct issues section (Section A). Double materiality is good practice.\n\n## Method\n1. **Scope and context.**\n   - Map the business model and the **value chain**: key upstream inputs (commodities, suppliers, geographies), own operations (sites, workforce), and downstream (products in use, end of life, customers).\n   - Note jurisdictions, export markets and customer requirements; examples include EU customers asking for product carbon data, and carbon border measures on steel, aluminium, cement and fertiliser imports into the EU.\n2. **Build the topic universe (long list, 30-60 sub-topics):**\n   - ISSB/SASB industry topics for the sector;\n   - GRI sector standards where available, plus GRI topic standards;\n   - the ESRS topical list (E1-E5, S1-S4, G1);\n   - BRSR's nine principles;\n   - peer reports; ESG-rating questionnaires; media, NGO and litigation scans; regulatory pipeline;\n   - internal risk register; incident and grievance data.\n3. **Identify IROs per topic**, located in the value chain.\n   - Example: \"Negative impact: water withdrawal in a water-stressed basin (own operations, Plant B).\"\n   - Example: \"Risk: production curtailment from water rationing.\"\n   - Example: \"Opportunity: water-recycling technology lowers cost.\"\n4. **Stakeholder engagement.**\n   - Identify affected stakeholders (workers, communities, value-chain workers, consumers) and users of reports (investors, lenders, customers).\n   - Engage through interviews, surveys and existing channels.\n   - Document who, how and what was heard. Representatives of vulnerable groups matter more than survey volume.\n5. **Score impact materiality** (see `references/deep-dive.md` for scales and `references/worked-example.md` for an extract):\n   - **Actual negative impacts:** severity = scale + scope + irremediability.\n   - **Potential negative impacts:** severity × likelihood. For human-rights impacts, **severity takes precedence over likelihood**, per the UN Guiding Principles.\n   - **Positive impacts:** scale + scope (+ likelihood).\n6. **Score financial materiality:** magnitude of financial effect (on revenue, costs, assets, capital access) × likelihood, per horizon. Short = ≤1-2 yrs, medium = to 5 yrs, long = 5+ yrs (state your definitions). Use quantitative proxies where possible, e.g. a carbon price × emissions, revenue at risk from customer requirements, or insurance cost.\n7. **Set thresholds** for each lens (e.g. ≥3 on a 5-point scale). Justify them, and test sensitivity: does a small change in threshold drop an obviously important topic?\n8. **Consolidate and validate:**\n   - aggregate sub-topics into material topics;\n   - run a sense check with management;\n   - review with the board or committee;\n   - document the rationale for each in or out decision (the **audit trail**).\n9. **Link to action:** for each material topic, record the policy, targets, KPIs, owner, capex or opex, and disclosure frameworks (BRSR principle / ISSB / ESRS / GRI). Integrate into the enterprise risk register and strategy.\n10. **Refresh:** fully every 2-3 years, and lightly every year or on significant change (an acquisition, a new market, a major incident, new regulation).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Value chain matters most.** For many companies the largest impacts sit upstream (raw materials, labour in the supply chain) or downstream (product use). A materiality assessment limited to own operations misses them.\n- **Severity over popularity.** A topic stakeholders rank low (e.g. migrant-worker conditions at third-tier suppliers) can be highly material because of severity. Don't let survey averages decide.\n- **Avoid the \"everything top-right\" matrix.** If 25 topics are material, prioritisation has failed. Distinguish material topics from **most-significant** ones, which get targets and board attention.\n- **Financial materiality needs numbers.** Even rough ranges (\"₹40-80 Cr EBITDA at risk by 2030 at ₹1,500-3,000/tCO2e\") beat adjectives.\n- **Climate is rarely immaterial** for industrial, financial, real-estate, agriculture and transport sectors. If you conclude it is, document why very carefully.\n- **Governance (G) topics** such as anti-corruption, data protection, AI and tax transparency are often material for every sector.\n\n## Sector hot spots (starting points, not conclusions)\n- **Auto components / engineering exporters:** product carbon footprint (customer requirements), energy and Scope 2, steel and aluminium embodied emissions, supplier labour, EHS.\n- **Banks and NBFCs:** financed emissions, climate risk in the loan book, financial inclusion, responsible lending, data protection, AI fairness.\n- **Pharma:** access and pricing, product quality and safety, effluent / API pollution, clinical-trial ethics, AMR.\n- **IT services:** talent and wellbeing, data privacy and cyber, energy in data centres, responsible AI, business ethics.\n- **FMCG / agri:** packaging and plastics (EPR), agricultural sourcing (deforestation, water, smallholder livelihoods), product health and nutrition.\n- **Cement / steel / chemicals:** GHG (and India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme intensity targets for covered sectors: verify), air quality, water, safety, community relations.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Double Materiality Assessment: [company], FY[..]\n**Scope:** entities, value chain boundaries, time horizons (S/M/L defined)\n**Method summary:** sources for long list; stakeholder engagement; scales; thresholds\n\n### IRO register (extract)\n| Topic | IRO (impact / risk / opportunity) | Value-chain location | Impact score (scale, scope, irremediability, likelihood) | Financial score (magnitude, likelihood, horizon) | Material? (I / F / both) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Material topics and the most significant ones\n| Material topic | Why material | Policy | Target / KPI | Owner | Disclosure mapping (BRSR / ISSB / ESRS / GRI) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### Stakeholder engagement evidence\n### Threshold and sensitivity notes\n### Board oversight and next review\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete worked example of this skill's output\n- `references/deep-dive.md`: detailed criteria, checklists, scales and patterns\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and texts this skill draws on, with dates and what to verify\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, the deep dive for detailed criteria, and the sources before citing any law, standard or date.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The long list is built from sector standards and evidence, not brainstorming alone.\n- [ ] IROs are specific and located in the value chain.\n- [ ] Impact scoring uses severity components; human-rights severity is prioritised.\n- [ ] Financial materiality is quantified where possible, with horizons defined.\n- [ ] Thresholds are stated and sensitivity-tested; every in/out decision has a rationale.\n- [ ] Stakeholder engagement is documented, including affected stakeholders.\n- [ ] Material topics are linked to targets, owners and disclosure frameworks.\n- [ ] Regulatory scope and dates are flagged for verification.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Plain English; define technical terms.\n- Reporting rules (ISSB adoption, CSRD/ESRS scope, BRSR, carbon-border measures) change; see `references/sources.md` and verify.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal, audit or assurance advice. SEBI, ISSB, CSRD/ESRS and carbon-border requirements change frequently; confirm applicability and dates with your Company Secretary, statutory auditor or assessment/assurance provider.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `brsr-readiness`: Indian disclosure readiness.\n- `pestel-scan`: horizon scanning.\n- `risk-appetite`: ESG metrics in appetite.\n- `board-paper`: approval of material topics and targets.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "deep-dive.md", "text": "# ESG materiality: scoring scales\n\n## Impact materiality scales (1-5)\n| Score | Scale (how grave) | Scope (how widespread) | Irremediability (how hard to reverse) | Likelihood (potential impacts) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Minimal | Very limited (few people, one small site) | Easily remediated, short term | Remote (<5%) |\n| 2 | Low | Limited | Remediable with effort | Unlikely |\n| 3 | Medium | Moderate (a region, a supplier tier) | Remediable over the long term | Possible |\n| 4 | High | Widespread | Difficult to remediate | Likely |\n| 5 | Very high (loss of life, irreversible ecosystem damage) | Global or systemic | Irreversible | Very likely / actual |\n\n- Severity = average (or max-weighted) of scale, scope and irremediability.\n- **Potential** negative impacts: severity × likelihood, but human-rights impacts with severity ≥4 are treated as material regardless of likelihood.\n\n## Financial materiality scale (1-5)\n| Score | Magnitude (illustrative; calibrate to the company) | Likelihood |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | <0.5% EBITDA or negligible effect on capital access | Remote |\n| 2 | 0.5-2% EBITDA | Unlikely |\n| 3 | 2-5% EBITDA, or a covenant / rating watch | Possible |\n| 4 | 5-10% EBITDA, or loss of a key customer or market | Likely |\n| 5 | >10% EBITDA, a licence to operate, or a major refinancing risk | Very likely |\n\nScore separately for short, medium and long term. A topic can be immaterial now and material by 2030; that is when strategy needs to act."}, {"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (verify current versions and applicability)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **IFRS S1** (General requirements) and **IFRS S2** (Climate), ISSB, June 2023; SASB Standards (industry topics), now maintained by the ISSB. Check India's adoption roadmap (ICAI / regulators).\n- **GRI 1, 2, 3 (2021)**: GRI 3 *Material Topics*; GRI sector standards (e.g. oil and gas, coal, agriculture, mining, financial services as published).\n- **ESRS 1** (General requirements, double materiality) and EFRAG Implementation Guidance IG 1 (Materiality assessment). CSRD scope and ESRS content were simplified by the EU Omnibus process (2025-26); verify which companies, and which years, are in scope.\n- **UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights** (2011): severity criteria; **OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises on Responsible Business Conduct** (2023 update) and OECD Due Diligence Guidance.\n- **TCFD** recommendations (2017; now incorporated into IFRS S2); **TNFD** recommendations (2023) for nature-related dependencies and impacts.\n- **India:** SEBI BRSR format and BRSR Core (see `brsr-readiness`); National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct (NGRBC, 2019); Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, 2023 and notified GHG emission-intensity targets for covered sectors (verify).\n- **EU CBAM** (Regulation (EU) 2023/956): definitive period from 1 Jan 2026, with simplifications adopted in 2025 (verify thresholds and obligations)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional Indian auto-components exporter, 40% revenue from EU OEMs)\n| Topic | IRO | Value chain | Impact score | Financial score | Material? | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Climate: GHG emissions | Negative impact: 210 ktCO2e Scope 1-2; Scope 3 dominated by purchased steel and aluminium | Own ops + upstream | 4 (scale 4, scope 4, irrem. 4) | M-term 4: EU OEMs require product carbon data and reduction pathways; CBAM costs may be passed through on steel inputs (verify scope) | Both | Customer contracts at risk without a credible pathway |\n| Energy | Opportunity: rooftop and open-access renewables cut Scope 2 and cost | Own ops | 3 | S-term 3: ~₹18 Cr/yr saving potential (est.) | Both | Cost and emissions co-benefit |\n| Supplier labour practices | Potential negative impact: forced overtime and unsafe conditions at tier-2 forging units | Upstream | Severity 4, likelihood 3 → material (human rights) | 2 | Impact | Severity precedence; customer audits rising |\n| Water | Negative impact: withdrawal in a water-stressed district (Plant C) | Own ops | 3 | 2 | Impact (borderline) | Monitor; set an intensity target |\n| Product quality and safety | Risk: recalls from defective parts | Downstream | 4 | 4 | Both | Already managed under IATF; link to disclosure |\n| Data protection and cyber | Risk: plant OT ransomware halts deliveries | Own ops | 2 | 4 | Financial | Just-in-time customers impose penalties |\n\n**Most significant:** GHG (with a target and capex plan), supplier labour (due-diligence programme), product quality, cyber/OT."}]}, {"id": "brsr-readiness", "pack": "board", "title": "BRSR Readiness Check", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Check readiness for India's Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report, including data gaps and assurance.", "tryp": "Check our BRSR readiness. We're a listed FMCG company; we did BRSR last year but data came from spreadsheets and assurance is coming.", "when": ["Preparing a BRSR filing", "Moving to reasonable assurance on BRSR Core", "Board or audit committee oversight of ESG disclosures"], "inputs": ["Company profile and listing status", "Last BRSR (if any) and data sources", "Assurance status and timelines"], "pairs": ["esg-materiality", "board-paper", "risk-assessment", "kpi-dashboard"], "body": "# BRSR and BRSR Core Readiness\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear view of whether the company can produce a complete, accurate and assessment- or assurance-ready BRSR on time. It shows which numbers would fail a provider's testing and why, who owns each fix, and what the board must oversee.\n\n> **Read first:** BRSR requirements are set by SEBI circulars and the LODR Master Circular, and they have changed repeatedly (2021, 2023, 2024-25). Everything below reflects research as of November 2026. **Confirm applicability, glide-path dates and the current format against the latest SEBI circulars with your Company Secretary and assurance provider before relying on it.**\n\n## Regulatory frame (summary; verify)\n- **BRSR:** mandatory for the **top 1,000 listed entities by market capitalisation** as part of the annual report (since FY 2022-23), structured around the **nine principles of the National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct (NGRBC)**.\n  - **Section A:** general disclosures.\n  - **Section B:** management and process.\n  - **Section C:** principle-wise performance, with **essential** (mandatory) and **leadership** (voluntary) indicators.\n- **BRSR Core** (SEBI circular of 12 July 2023, amended by the circular of 28 March 2025): a subset of KPIs under **nine ESG attributes**.\n  - Coverage was phased by market-cap rank: top 150 (FY 2023-24), top 250 (FY 2024-25), top 500 (FY 2025-26), top 1,000 (FY 2026-27).\n  - The 2025 amendment changed \"reasonable assurance\" to **\"assessment or assurance\"**. Assessment follows standards developed by the Industry Standards Forum in consultation with SEBI.\n  - Intensity ratios are computed on **revenue adjusted for purchasing power parity (PPP)**.\n  - Additional green-credit and similar disclosures were introduced; verify.\n- **Value-chain disclosures** (per the 2025 amendment, as reported): top 250 entities.\n  - Value-chain partners are those individually ≥2% of purchases or sales by value; disclosure covers partners making up 75% of purchases and of sales.\n  - Disclosure is **voluntary from FY 2025-26**, with assessment or assurance **voluntary from FY 2026-27**.\n  - Prior-year comparatives are optional in the first year.\n- **Provider independence:** the board must ensure the assessment or assurance provider has the necessary expertise. The provider and its associates must not provide non-audit, non-assurance consulting services to the entity or its group (as reported; verify the exact conditions).\n\n## The nine BRSR Core attributes (verify the current KPI list)\n1. GHG footprint (Scope 1, Scope 2; intensity)\n2. Water footprint (withdrawal, consumption, discharge; intensity)\n3. Energy footprint (consumption, renewable share; intensity)\n4. Embracing circularity: waste generated, recovered and disposed (by category; intensity)\n5. Employee well-being and safety (spend on well-being; safety incidents, LTIFR, fatalities)\n6. Gender diversity in business (wages paid to women; POSH complaints)\n7. Inclusive development (input material sourced from MSMEs and small producers and from within India; job creation in smaller towns)\n8. Fairness in engaging with customers and suppliers (cyber and data-breach instances; days of accounts payable)\n9. Openness of business (concentration of purchases and sales with trading houses, dealers and distributors; related-party share of purchases, sales, loans, advances and investments)\n\n## Method\n1. **Applicability and timeline:** market-cap rank (as at the relevant date), BRSR Core tier for the year, value-chain status, filing date (with the annual report), and the assessment or assurance provider's engagement timeline (start testing at least 4-6 months before year-end).\n2. **Indicator inventory:** list every indicator in Sections A, B and C plus BRSR Core KPIs. For each, record the **data owner, source system, calculation method / methodology reference, boundary (entities, sites), frequency and evidence**.\n3. **Data-trail test (the assurance lens):** for each Core KPI, can a provider trace from the reported number → calculation → source records → original evidence (meter readings, invoices, manifests, HR records, incident registers)? Rate each KPI:\n   - **Ready:** system-sourced, reconciled, controls evidenced.\n   - **Partial:** spreadsheet-based but traceable; reconciliations missing.\n   - **Not ready:** estimates, missing sites, undocumented methods.\n4. **Boundary and consistency checks:**\n   - Are all subsidiaries and sites in the BRSR boundary covered, and consistent with the financial-reporting boundary where required?\n   - Are restatements explained?\n   - Are units consistent (kWh vs GJ; kL vs m³)?\n5. **Methodology checks:**\n   - **GHG:** the GHG Protocol is the usual basis. Emission factors are documented and dated (e.g. India's CEA grid emission factor for Scope 2). Market vs location-based Scope 2 is stated.\n   - **Water:** withdrawal vs consumption vs discharge definitions; water-stressed area identification.\n   - **Waste:** hazardous categories aligned with the relevant rules; recovery vs disposal evidence (manifests, recycler certificates).\n   - **Safety:** LTIFR formula and denominator (per million person-hours worked); contractor coverage.\n   - **PPP revenue:** the source and year of the PPP conversion factor (e.g. the IMF / World Bank figure as specified).\n6. **Controls:** design minimum controls: maker-checker on data entry; monthly or quarterly reconciliations (e.g. fuel purchase invoices vs consumption logs); site sign-offs; a change log for methodologies; IT controls on the ESG data platform; internal-audit coverage.\n7. **Value chain (if in scope or chosen voluntarily):** identify partners above the 2% thresholds up to 75% coverage; design a data request; set data-quality expectations; plan supplier capacity building.\n8. **Governance:**\n   - Board or committee oversight (a CSR/ESG or risk committee).\n   - Approval of the BRSR.\n   - The **director responsible** for BRSR (Section B).\n   - Provider appointment and independence check.\n   - Management representation letters.\n9. **Gap closing plan:** prioritise Core KPIs rated Not ready or Partial; assign owners and dates; plan a dry run (Q3 data) with the provider to surface issues before year-end.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Assurance readiness is about evidence, not numbers.** A correct number with no trail fails; an approximate number with a documented, consistent method and evidence often passes, with qualification.\n- **Start with the 9 Core attributes,** then fill BRSR gaps. Core failures carry the most reputational risk.\n- **Sites are where data breaks.** Contractor hours, diesel in gensets, water from tankers and waste via informal recyclers are typical blind spots.\n- **Don't let the consultant own the numbers.** Management must own the data and methods; independence rules may also restrict who can help.\n- **Use BRSR as an operating tool.** Intensity KPIs belong in plant dashboards and the risk appetite, not only in the annual report.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## BRSR Readiness: [company], FY[..]\n**Applicability:** market-cap rank ...; BRSR Core tier ...; value chain: mandatory / voluntary / N/A; provider: ... (independence checked: Y/N)\n**Overall:** Ready / Ready with fixes / At risk - one line\n\n### BRSR Core KPI readiness\n| Attribute | KPI | Owner | Source | Method / factor | Boundary complete? | Evidence trail | Rating | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n### BRSR sections A / B / C gaps (summary by principle)\n### Controls to implement\n### Value-chain plan\n### Closing plan\n| Action | Owner | Due | Evidence of done |\n|---|---|---|---|\n### Board and committee oversight points\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and texts this skill draws on, with dates and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, the deep dive for detailed criteria, and the sources before citing any law, standard or date.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Applicability and dates are stated **and** flagged for verification against current SEBI circulars.\n- [ ] Every Core KPI is rated with a reason based on the evidence trail.\n- [ ] Methodology issues (GHG factors, PPP revenue, LTIFR basis, water definitions) are checked.\n- [ ] Boundary completeness is tested, including contractors and subsidiaries.\n- [ ] Provider independence and the board's role are addressed.\n- [ ] The closing plan has owners, dates and a dry run.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Plain English; define technical terms.\n- Always recommend confirming regulatory specifics with the Company Secretary, statutory auditor or the assessment/assurance provider.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential data they are not permitted to share.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal, audit or assurance advice. SEBI, ISSB, CSRD/ESRS and carbon-border requirements change frequently; confirm applicability and dates with your Company Secretary, statutory auditor or assessment/assurance provider.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `esg-materiality`: what matters beyond compliance.\n- `kpi-dashboard`: operational ESG dashboards.\n- `risk-assessment`: risks to the filing.\n- `board-paper`: BRSR approval paper.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (as researched November 2026; verify against SEBI's website)\n\n- **SEBI circular SEBI/HO/CFD/CFD-SEC-2/P/CIR/2023/122 (12 July 2023):** BRSR Core framework for assurance and ESG disclosures for the value chain; Annexure I (BRSR Core format), Annexure II (updated BRSR format). Original glide path of reasonable assurance: top 150 (FY 2023-24), top 250 (FY 2024-25), top 500 (FY 2025-26), top 1,000 (FY 2026-27).\n- **SEBI circular of 28 March 2025:** amended the BRSR Core framework (as reported):\n  - \"assessment or assurance\" in place of \"reasonable assurance\";\n  - assessment standards by the Industry Standards Forum in consultation with SEBI;\n  - value-chain disclosures voluntary for the top 250 from FY 2025-26, with assessment or assurance voluntary from FY 2026-27;\n  - the 2% / 75% value-chain thresholds;\n  - provider independence conditions.\n- **SEBI LODR Master Circular** (latest): consolidated BRSR requirements; Reg 34(2)(f) LODR (BRSR in the annual report for the top 1,000).\n- **National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct (NGRBC)**, Ministry of Corporate Affairs, 2019: the nine principles.\n- **GHG Protocol** Corporate Standard and Scope 2 Guidance; **Central Electricity Authority (CEA)** CO2 baseline database for the Indian power sector (grid emission factor; use the current version).\n- **ICAI Sustainability Reporting Standards Board** guidance / standards on sustainability assurance (e.g. SSAE 3000, SSAE 3410 for GHG; verify current numbering and status).\n- **Hazardous and Other Wastes (Management and Transboundary Movement) Rules, 2016;** Plastic Waste Management Rules (EPR); Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (POSH) Act, 2013; MSMED Act and Udyam registration.\n\n**Note:** secondary summaries (consultancies, ESG platforms) were used to cross-check the 2025 amendment. Read the primary SEBI circular before relying on any detail."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional listed FMCG company ranked ~#420, 6 plants, 2 subsidiaries)\n\n**Applicability (FY 2025-26, verify):** BRSR mandatory; BRSR Core in the top-500 tier, so assessment or assurance is required; value chain: not top 250 → N/A (voluntary). Provider: an affiliate of a large audit network. Independence check: the affiliate's consulting arm advised on the ESG platform in FY25 → **conflict to resolve** (change provider or confirm the conditions with counsel).\n\n**Overall:** At risk. 4 of 9 Core attributes have Partial or Not-ready KPIs. Resolvable in 5 months with focused fixes and a Q3 dry run.\n\n## Core KPI readiness (extract)\n| Attribute | KPI | Owner | Source | Method / factor | Boundary complete? | Evidence trail | Rating | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GHG | Scope 1 (tCO2e) | Head EHS | Plant spreadsheets | GHG Protocol; IPCC default factors (dated) | No: gensets at 2 depots missing | Fuel logs not reconciled to purchase invoices | **Partial** | Add depots; monthly reconciliation to ERP fuel purchases |\n| GHG | Scope 2 | Head EHS | Electricity bills | CEA grid factor (version not recorded) | Yes | Bills available | **Partial** | Record the factor version; state location-based approach |\n| GHG | Intensity per ₹ PPP revenue | CFO office | Finance + EHS | PPP factor source not documented | n/a | — | **Not ready** | Document PPP source/year as per SEBI format |\n| Water | Withdrawal and consumption (kL) | Plant heads | Meters at 4 plants; tanker estimates at 2 | Definitions inconsistent | Partially | Tanker receipts incomplete | **Not ready** | Install meters at the 2 plants (₹30 lakh); consistent definitions; tanker log |\n| Waste | Hazardous waste and recovery | EHS | Manifests | Per hazardous-waste rules categories | Yes | Manifests and recycler certificates on file | **Ready** | — |\n| Safety | LTIFR (per million hours), employees + contractors | HR / EHS | HRMS + contractor registers | Contractor hours estimated from headcount × 8 | Contractors approximated | Weak | **Partial** | Biometric attendance for contractors; actual hours |\n| Gender | Wages paid to females (% of total) | CHRO | Payroll | Gross wages | Yes | Payroll reports | **Ready** | — |\n| Inclusive development | % input material from MSMEs / small producers | CPO | ERP vendor master | MSME flag outdated for 30% of vendors | Yes | Udyam certificates missing | **Partial** | Refresh the vendor MSME status with Udyam numbers |\n| Fairness | Days of accounts payable | CFO | ERP | Formula per format | Yes | Trial balance | **Ready** | — |\n| Openness | Related-party share of purchases / sales | CS / CFO | RPT register | Per format | Yes | RPT register + audit committee approvals | **Ready** | — |\n\n## Closing plan (extract)\n| Action | Owner | Due | Evidence of done |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Resolve provider-independence conflict | CS + CFO | 15 Dec | Board/committee note; engagement letter |\n| Fuel and genset reconciliation for all sites, monthly | Head EHS | From Jan | Signed reconciliations |\n| Water meters at Plants E and F | COO | 31 Jan | Commissioning reports; Feb readings |\n| Contractor actual hours via biometric | CHRO | 28 Feb | System extract |\n| Methodology note (factors, PPP, definitions) approved | Head Sustainability | 31 Jan | Approved document, version-controlled |\n| Q3 dry run with the provider | CFO | 15 Feb | Provider management letter |\n| Board CSR/ESG committee review of draft BRSR | Committee Chair | May | Minutes |"}]}, {"id": "ai-opportunity-scan", "pack": "ai", "title": "AI Opportunity Scan", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Find where AI can create real value in a function or business, grounded in processes and pain points.", "tryp": "Scan our finance function (FP&A, AP, AR, close, treasury) for AI opportunities. 120 people, ERP, monthly close takes 9 days.", "when": ["Starting an AI strategy or roadmap", "Discovery workshops with a function", "Refreshing a stale use-case list"], "inputs": ["The business or function, key processes, volumes, systems", "Pain points and goals", "Constraints (data, regulation, budget)"], "pairs": ["ai-use-case-prioritizer", "jobs-to-be-done", "value-chain", "playbook-ai-roadmap"], "body": "# AI Opportunity Scan\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a long-list of AI use cases that a CFO would take seriously. Each one is tied to a real process step, has a value mechanism with a rough size, an honest data and risk check, and a view of whether it is a quick win, a build, or a platform capability.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Start from the work, not the model.** Map processes, volumes, decision points and pain. Technology choice comes last.\n2. **Value is a mechanism, not an adjective.** Every use case states *how* value appears: hours released × redeployment, faster cash collection, lower losses, higher conversion, avoided risk.\n3. **Distinguish three kinds of AI.** They have very different risk and change profiles:\n   - **Predictive:** forecast, classify, detect, score.\n   - **Generative:** draft, summarise, extract, translate, converse.\n   - **Agentic:** plan and execute multi-step tasks using tools and systems.\n4. **Data reality check early.** A brilliant idea with no labelled history or no system access is a data project first.\n5. **Adoption is the hidden denominator.** Most value is lost between \"the model works\" and \"people use it every day\".\n6. **Some processes should be fixed, not automated.** If a process is broken, AI scales the breakage.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- **Scope:** an enterprise, one function (Finance, HR, Ops, Sales, Service, Legal, IT), or one value stream (order-to-cash, procure-to-pay, claims, onboarding)?\n- **Strategic intent:** cost, growth, risk, customer experience, or talent? This changes what \"value\" means.\n- **Baseline facts:** headcount by activity, volumes, cycle times, error or rework rates, backlog, SLA breaches, cost per transaction.\n- **Systems and data:** core systems (ERP, CRM, HRMS, core banking, EHR), data platform maturity, document stores, API access.\n- **Constraints:** regulation (sector, DPDP/GDPR), works councils or unions, budget, appetite for customer-facing AI.\n- **Current AI activity:** existing pilots, vendor features already licensed and unused (common and cheap value).\n\n## Method\n1. **Map the value stream.** List the 8-15 main steps; for each: who does it, volume per month, time per item, error rate, systems used, and the decision made.\n2. **Find the AI signals at each step.**\n   - **Predictive:** a repeated judgement with historical outcomes, e.g. \"Will this invoice be disputed?\", \"Which claims are fraudulent?\", \"Which customers will churn?\"\n   - **Generative:** unstructured text, documents or conversations; first drafts; knowledge lookup across policies.\n   - **Agentic:** multi-step, rules-plus-judgement work across several systems, e.g. reconciliations, onboarding, pre-authorisation, vendor set-up, collections follow-up.\n   - **Automation without AI:** stable rules → workflow or RPA may be cheaper and safer.\n3. **Write each use case precisely:** \"For [user], AI [does what] in [process step], so that [measurable outcome].\" Avoid \"AI-powered insights\".\n4. **Size the value mechanism** (order of magnitude is enough at this stage). Use code for arithmetic.\n   - **Capacity:** volume × time saved per item × adoption % → hours; value only when redeployed or released.\n   - **Cash and working capital:** e.g. DSO reduction × daily revenue.\n   - **Loss reduction:** e.g. fraud loss × detection uplift.\n   - **Revenue:** conversion or retention uplift × base.\n   - **Risk:** expected loss avoided, or compliance cost avoided.\n5. **Agentic-readiness check** (for agent candidates). Score Yes / Partial / No on each:\n   - Is the process documented, with clear rules and exceptions?\n   - Are systems accessible via APIs (not screen-scraping only)?\n   - Are actions reversible, or can they be gated by human approval?\n   - Is there a measurable definition of a correct outcome?\n   - Is there an owner who will accept accountability for agent actions?\n   - Can every action be logged for audit?\n   Two or more \"No\" answers → start with recommend-only AI, or fix the process first.\n6. **Data and risk flags:** data availability and quality, personal or sensitive data, decision impact on people (feeds the AI risk tier), customer-facing or not.\n7. **Classify:**\n   - **Quick win:** off-the-shelf or a feature already licensed; under 3 months.\n   - **Build:** custom model, agent or integration; 3-9 months.\n   - **Platform capability:** needs shared foundations (data platform, retrieval layer, agent orchestration, evaluation tooling) that unlock several use cases.\n8. **Cluster into themes**, and note shared foundations. They usually decide sequencing.\n9. **Hand over to prioritisation** (`ai-use-case-prioritizer`) with the evidence.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Prefer \"AI + redesigned process\" over \"AI bolted on\".** The biggest gains come from removing steps, not speeding them up.\n- **Count only released or redeployed capacity as value.** \"Time saved\" with no plan for that time is a soft benefit.\n- **Treat vendor AI features you already pay for as the first quick wins.** They often go unused.\n- **Be careful with customer-facing GenAI early.** Start internal (agent-assist, drafting) where a human checks, then move outward.\n- **Look for \"decision bottlenecks\", not just \"effort hot spots\".** A two-day credit approval queue may be worth more than 20 clerks' typing time.\n\n## Red flags\n- A use-case list that reads like a vendor catalogue (\"chatbot\", \"copilot\", \"AI analytics\").\n- No volumes or baselines; value claimed as a percentage of nothing.\n- Every use case labelled \"high value, low effort\".\n- Agents proposed for processes with no API access, no rules and no owner.\n- No mention of personal data, decision impact or adoption.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Finance:** AP invoice capture and 3-way match exceptions, AR cash application and dispute prediction, intercompany reconciliation agents, close commentary drafting, FP&A driver forecasting and variance narratives.\n- **BFSI:** KYC document extraction, credit underwriting assist, collections prioritisation, fraud detection, claims triage, servicing agent-assist, regulatory reporting checks.\n- **Healthcare:** clinical documentation drafting, coding assistance, pre-authorisation agents, scheduling optimisation, imaging triage (regulated: check device rules).\n- **Manufacturing and supply chain:** demand forecasting, predictive maintenance, quality vision inspection, supplier risk monitoring, planning copilots.\n- **HR:** job-description and policy assistants, employee helpdesk; screening use is high-risk (check bias and law).\n- **GCC / shared services:** agent-led process ownership, knowledge copilots for global processes, automation of L1 support.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## AI Opportunity Scan: [scope]\n**Intent:** ...  |  **Baseline sources:** ...  |  **Systems:** ...\n\n| # | Process step | Use case (\"For..., AI..., so that...\") | Type (Pred / Gen / Agent / Non-AI automation) | Value mechanism and rough size | Data availability | People impact / risk flag | Agentic readiness | Class (Quick win / Build / Platform) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Themes and shared foundations:** ...\n**Quick wins (≤3 months):** ...\n**Fix-the-process-first candidates:** ...\n**Next step:** prioritise with value × feasibility × risk\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Every use case is tied to a named process step with a volume or baseline.\n- [ ] The value mechanism is stated and roughly sized, with arithmetic done properly.\n- [ ] Predictive, generative and agentic uses are distinguished.\n- [ ] Data availability and people-impact flags are recorded.\n- [ ] Agent candidates have passed (or failed) the readiness check.\n- [ ] At least one \"fix the process first\" or \"non-AI automation\" call where warranted.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-use-case-prioritizer`: Score and rank AI use cases on value, feasibility and risk, and pick the first wave.\n- `jobs-to-be-done`: Understand the real 'job' customers hire you for, and the outcomes they measure you on.\n- `value-chain`: Map where value and cost sit across the chain, and find where to compete, cut or integrate.\n- `playbook-ai-roadmap`: Build an AI roadmap for a business or function: opportunities, priorities, cases, governance and adoption.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources and further reading (verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **NIST AI Risk Management Framework 1.0** (2023): the MAP function frames context, intended purpose and impact early in use-case design.\n- **ISO/IEC 22989:2022:** AI concepts and terminology; useful for distinguishing system types.\n- **Process redesign before automation:** a long-established principle of business process reengineering and process innovation. Automating a broken process scales its defects.\n- **AI as cheap prediction:** the economic view that AI lowers the cost of prediction, so value comes from redesigning decisions and workflows around it, not from point automation.\n- **Workflows vs agents:** current engineering practice favours the simplest design that works. Use fixed workflows where steps are predictable, and agents only where flexible multi-step reasoning is needed, with well-defined tools and approval points.\n- **Agent security:** public guidance on LLM application risks (e.g. the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications, 2025, including \"excessive agency\"). Use it to flag agent candidates.\n- **Benchmarks:** external figures for touchless AP rates, DSO and close duration vary widely by industry. Use your own baseline first and treat external benchmarks as indicative only."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional Indian manufacturing group, Finance function, 140 people)\n\n**Intent:** reduce close time and cost-to-serve; improve cash.\n**Baseline:**\n- 9-day monthly close.\n- 210,000 supplier invoices a year with 22% touchless processing.\n- DSO of 68 days.\n- 14 legal entities on one ERP, with 2 acquired entities on a small-business accounting package.\n- Intercompany mismatches take ~600 hours a month.\n\n| # | Process step | Use case | Type | Value mechanism and rough size (est.) | Data | People / risk | Agentic readiness | Class |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | AP invoice capture | For AP clerks, AI extracts invoice data and matches to PO/GRN, routing only exceptions, so that touchless rises from 22% to 60% | Gen + rules | 210k × 38% newly touchless × 7 min = ~9,300 hrs/yr ≈ 5 FTE redeployable | 3 yrs of invoices in ERP + PDFs | Internal; low | n/a | Quick win (ERP feature already licensed, unused) |\n| 2 | AP exceptions | For AP leads, AI explains the likely reason for each exception and drafts the vendor query | Gen | 30% faster exception closure; fewer late-payment penalties | Exception history | Internal | n/a | Build |\n| 3 | AR cash application | For AR team, AI matches remittances (email/PDF) to open items | Gen + Pred | Unapplied cash down 40%; ~2 days DSO improvement × ₹11 Cr daily revenue ≈ ₹22 Cr cash released (one-off) | Bank files, remittance emails | Internal | n/a | Build |\n| 4 | Collections | For collectors, AI predicts invoices likely to go overdue and drafts tailored reminders | Pred + Gen | DSO -3 to -5 days (est.) | 3 yrs AR history | Customer-facing emails: human sends | n/a | Build |\n| 5 | Intercompany reconciliation | An agent pulls balances from both entities, matches, proposes adjustments under ₹1 lakh for approval, escalates the rest | **Agent** | ~600 hrs/month → ~150; close -1.5 days | ERP APIs yes; acquired entities via export only | Postings: approval required | Process docs Partial; APIs Partial (acquired entities); reversible Yes (approval gate); outcome measurable Yes; owner Yes; logging Yes → **proceed with ERP entities; acquired entities later** | Platform (agent orchestration) |\n| 6 | Close commentary | For FP&A, AI drafts variance commentary from trial balance and driver data | Gen | 3-4 days of analyst time per month; faster board pack | TB + drivers | Internal; numbers must be verified | n/a | Quick win |\n| 7 | Vendor master changes | Bank-detail change requests verified by AI against documents, with call-back for anomalies | Pred + Gen | Fraud-loss avoidance (one avoided incident can exceed ₹1 Cr) | Change history | Fraud risk: human approval mandatory | n/a | Build |\n| 8 | Travel and expense audit | AI flags out-of-policy claims | Pred | Audit coverage 5% → 100%; leakage ~0.8% of spend | T&E system | Employee-facing: fairness | n/a | Quick win (vendor feature) |\n| 9 | Month-end journal approvals | **Fix the process first:** 40% of manual journals are recurring and should be automated by rule | Non-AI | 2,000 journals/month | — | — | — | Process fix |\n\n**Themes and shared foundations:**\n- **Document AI layer:** serves use cases 1, 3 and 7.\n- **Agent orchestration with an approval workflow and audit log:** serves use case 5, and later the AP exceptions.\n- **Finance data model:** the trial balance plus drivers.\n\n**Quick wins:** 1, 6, 8. **Fix first:** 9.\n\n**Next:** prioritise. Use cases 3 and 5 likely lead on value, and 1 on speed."}]}, {"id": "ai-use-case-prioritizer", "pack": "ai", "title": "AI Use-Case Prioritiser", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Score and rank AI use cases on value, feasibility and risk, and pick the first wave.", "tryp": "Prioritise these 15 AI use cases for our insurance company: [paste list].", "when": ["After an AI opportunity scan", "Too many AI pilots and no focus", "Annual AI portfolio review"], "inputs": ["The use-case list with short descriptions", "Strategic priorities", "Constraints and risk appetite"], "pairs": ["ai-opportunity-scan", "ai-business-case", "data-readiness-check", "strategic-options"], "body": "# AI Use-Case Prioritiser\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a defensible, sequenced AI portfolio of wave 1, wave 2 and parked items. The scoring is transparent, risk is handled as a gate, and shared foundations are funded once. Anyone on the steering committee can see why something is first and why something else is not.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Weights before scores.** Agree criteria and weights with the sponsor before anyone scores. Otherwise scoring becomes advocacy.\n2. **Risk is a gate, not a discount.** A high-value use case with high risk is not \"medium priority\". It is \"only if governance is ready\". Treat prohibited or unacceptable uses as out, whatever the score.\n3. **Portfolio, not ranking.** Balance quick wins (credibility and funding) with strategic bets (differentiation) and foundations (enablers). A pure top-10 list usually ignores dependencies.\n4. **Feasibility means \"in this organisation, this year\".** That covers data that exists and is accessible, integration that is possible, and people who will use it.\n5. **Kill and park explicitly.** A portfolio that never stops anything has no priorities.\n6. **Re-rank on evidence.** Pilot results and data discoveries should move items up or down; plan quarterly reviews.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- How many candidates, and how well described (process step, value mechanism, data)? If thin, run `ai-opportunity-scan` first.\n- Strategic priorities for the next 12-24 months (cost, growth, risk, experience).\n- Capacity: budget, AI and data engineers, product owners, change capacity in the business.\n- Governance maturity: can the organisation safely run high-risk AI today?\n- Shared platforms available or planned (data platform, retrieval, agent orchestration, evaluation).\n\n## Method\n1. **Normalise the list.** One line per use case with process, user, value mechanism, rough value, data and risk tier. Merge duplicates and split mixed items.\n2. **Set criteria and weights.** Typical starting weights; adjust with the sponsor and record the reasons:\n   - **Value (35%):** financial (sized), strategic alignment, customer or employee impact.\n   - **Feasibility (30%):** data readiness, technical complexity, integration effort, vendor maturity.\n   - **Adoption (20%):** user pull, process change size, change capacity, a sponsor in the business.\n   - **Time to value (15%):** months to measurable benefit.\n3. **Define a 1-5 scale per criterion with anchors** so scorers agree. For example, Data readiness 5 = clean, labelled, accessible history ≥2 years; 1 = data doesn't exist.\n4. **Score with evidence.** One line per score. Use 2-3 scorers (business, tech, risk) and discuss big disagreements; they reveal unknowns.\n5. **Apply the risk gate.**\n   - **Unacceptable or prohibited:** exclude.\n   - **High-risk:** allowed in a wave only if the governance prerequisites are met (impact assessment, validation, human oversight, monitoring). Otherwise schedule governance first.\n   - **Limited or minimal:** standard controls.\n6. **Plot value vs feasibility** (2x2): **Quick wins** (high/high), **Strategic bets** (high value, lower feasibility: invest in enablers), **Fill-ins** (low value, high feasibility: do only if nearly free), **Park** (low/low).\n7. **Map dependencies and shared foundations.** Group use cases that share data, a platform or a team. Sometimes a medium-value case goes first because it builds the foundation for three others.\n8. **Build waves:**\n   - **Wave 1 (0-6 months):** 3-5 items including at least one quick win and the foundation needed by wave 2.\n   - **Wave 2 (6-12 months).**\n   - **Parked**, with the condition that would revive each one.\n9. **Sensitivity check:** would a reasonable change in weights change wave 1? If yes, discuss openly.\n10. **Define review triggers:** quarterly, plus after each pilot decision.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Watch the \"AI for its own sake\" item the CEO loves.** Score it honestly; if it ranks low, show why and offer a cheap test.\n- **Favour use cases with a committed business owner** over higher-scoring orphans. Adoption fails without ownership.\n- **One meaningful customer-facing use case** can be worth more for learning than five internal ones, if governance is ready. Make that trade-off explicit.\n- **Avoid pilot sprawl.** Cap concurrent pilots at what the organisation can actually evaluate and scale (often 3-6 for a mid-size firm).\n\n## Red flags\n- Scores without evidence; weights changed after seeing results.\n- High-risk items scored \"medium\" to sneak them in.\n- No foundations in wave 1, though wave 2 depends on them.\n- Wave 1 with 15 items.\n- Nothing parked or stopped.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Regulated (BFSI, health):** higher weight on risk-gate readiness and explainability. Credit, claims and clinical uses need validation capacity before scaling.\n- **GCC / shared services:** weight scalability across regions and process standardisation. Prioritise use cases deployable across many business units.\n- **Start-up / scale-up:** weight time-to-value and revenue impact more; accept higher technical risk.\n- **Public sector:** weight transparency, fairness and citizen impact; procurement timelines affect time-to-value.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## AI Use-Case Prioritisation: [scope], [date]\n**Criteria and weights (agreed with [sponsor]):** Value 35 / Feasibility 30 / Adoption 20 / Time 15\n\n| Use case | Value | Data | Tech | Integration | Adoption | Time | Weighted | Risk tier | Gate status | Quadrant | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Wave 1 (0-6 months):** item - why - prerequisite\n**Wave 2 (6-12 months):** ...\n**Shared foundations to fund now:** ...\n**Parked / stopped:** item - reason - revival condition\n**Sensitivity:** ...\n**Review cadence:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Weights stated before scoring, with scale anchors.\n- [ ] Every score has one line of evidence.\n- [ ] The risk gate is applied separately; high-risk items list governance prerequisites.\n- [ ] Dependencies and shared foundations are shown and sequenced.\n- [ ] Wave 1 is small enough to deliver; some items are parked or stopped with a reason.\n- [ ] Sensitivity to weights is checked.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-opportunity-scan`: Find where AI can create real value in a function or business, grounded in processes and pain points.\n- `ai-business-case`: Build an honest business case for an AI use case, including model, data, change and run costs.\n- `data-readiness-check`: Check whether the data for an AI or analytics use case is available, good enough, legal to use and accessible.\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources and further reading (verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA):** weighted-sum scoring with anchored scales, as described in public-sector appraisal guidance (e.g. UK HM Treasury *Green Book* on options appraisal).\n- **EU AI Act** (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689): Art. 5 prohibited practices; Annex III high-risk areas. As amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus, Annex III obligations apply from 2 Dec 2027 (verify). Use these to define the risk gate where in scope.\n- **NIST AI RMF 1.0** and **NIST AI 600-1 Generative AI Profile:** risk considerations to inform the gate.\n- **India:** RBI FREE-AI Committee report (Aug 2025) for regulated entities; MeitY India AI Governance Guidelines (Nov 2025); DPDP Act 2023 and DPDP Rules 2025 (most obligations from 13 May 2027).\n- **Portfolio horizons:** the widely used \"three horizons\" idea of balancing core, adjacent and transformational bets.\n- **Internal evidence:** your AI risk register and data-readiness results are the best inputs for gate and feasibility scores."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional general insurer, India, 14 candidate use cases)\n\n**Weights:** Value 35, Feasibility 30 (Data 12, Tech 9, Integration 9), Adoption 20, Time 15. Agreed with the COO and CRO on 3 Feb.\n\n| Use case | Value | Data | Tech | Integr. | Adoption | Time | Weighted (/5) | Risk tier | Gate | Quadrant | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Motor claims document triage (extract, classify, route) | 4 | 4 | 4 | 3 | 4 | 4 | **3.9** | Limited | Standard controls | Quick win | Head Claims Ops |\n| Servicing agent-assist (answers with policy citations) | 4 | 3 | 4 | 3 | 4 | 4 | **3.7** | Limited | Grounding + logging required | Quick win | Head Service |\n| Health claims fraud scoring | 5 | 4 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 | **3.6** | **High** (affects claim outcomes) | **Blocked until** validation function and fairness testing exist (est. Q3) | Strategic bet | CRO / Head Claims |\n| Motor own-damage claim auto-settlement (agent) | 5 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 2 | **3.0** | **High** | Blocked: needs agent controls plus human approval above ₹50k | Strategic bet | Head Claims |\n| Underwriting submission summariser (commercial lines) | 3 | 3 | 4 | 4 | 4 | 4 | **3.6** | Limited | Standard | Quick win | Head UW |\n| Renewal churn prediction | 3 | 4 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 3 | 3.2 | Limited | Standard | Fill-in (no committed owner) | — |\n| Marketing copy GenAI | 2 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 4 | 5 | 3.9 | Minimal | Policy only | Fill-in (low value, nearly free: do via enterprise tool) | CMO |\n| Voice bot for first notice of loss | 3 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2.4 | Limited | — | Park | — |\n| ... | | | | | | | | | | | |\n\n**Wave 1 (0-6 months):**\n1. Claims document triage: highest score; builds the **document AI layer** that the fraud and auto-settlement cases need.\n2. Servicing agent-assist: builds the **retrieval and grounding layer**; human in the loop.\n3. Underwriting summariser: reuses the retrieval layer.\n4. **Foundation: AI validation and fairness-testing capability** (second line). It is the prerequisite for the two high-risk bets.\n\n**Wave 2 (6-12 months):** health fraud scoring, once the governance gate is passed, then a pilot of auto-settlement for claims under ₹50k with human approval.\n\n**Parked:** FNOL voice bot (revive if vernacular speech-to-text accuracy exceeds 90% in testing). Churn prediction (revive when the Retention head commits as owner).\n\n**Sensitivity:** raising Value to 45% (cutting Adoption to 10%) moves fraud scoring into rank 1. It stays in wave 2 anyway because of the gate. Wave 1 is robust.\n\n**Review:** quarterly at the AI SteerCo, plus after each pilot decision."}]}, {"id": "ai-business-case", "pack": "ai", "title": "AI Business Case", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Build an honest business case for an AI use case, including model, data, change and run costs.", "tryp": "Build a business case to scale our GenAI contract-review assistant from 20 legal users to 400 across the group.", "when": ["Funding an AI initiative", "Pilot-to-scale decisions", "Challenging an over-optimistic AI proposal"], "inputs": ["Use case and users", "Pilot results if any", "Costs: licences/API, people, infrastructure", "Baseline process metrics"], "pairs": ["business-case", "ai-pilot-design", "sensitivity-analysis", "benefits-tracker"], "body": "# AI Business Case\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a case that survives a CFO and an investment committee. Benefits are traceable to a baseline and owned in budgets. AI-specific costs that grow with usage are visible. The downside is quantified, and funding is released in stages against evidence.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Baseline first.** Measure the current process (volume, time per item, cost per item, error or rework rate, cycle time) before claiming improvement. If there is no baseline, the first deliverable is one.\n2. **Benefit mechanisms, not percentages.** \"30% productivity\" means nothing until it becomes hours × adoption × what happens to those hours (released, redeployed to valued work, or absorbed).\n3. **Adoption and accuracy are ramps.** Model them by quarter. Real-world adoption rarely exceeds 60-80% in year one, and accuracy in production is usually below pilot accuracy.\n4. **The AI cost stack is wider than licences.** Include data preparation, integration, evaluation datasets, red-teaming, MLOps and monitoring, human review time, governance, and inference costs that scale with usage and context size.\n5. **Rework is a cost.** AI errors that humans must catch and fix reduce net benefit. Model it.\n6. **Stage the money.** Fund discovery, then pilot, then scale, each with kill criteria.\n7. **Ownership.** Each benefit has a business owner who accepts it in their budget.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The use case, its users, and the process step it changes. Is it assistive (human decides), or does the AI or agent act?\n- Pilot data available? Measured how, against what baseline, on what sample?\n- Company hurdle rate or WACC, evaluation horizon (3-5 years typical for AI given model churn), and the capex vs opex treatment the CFO expects.\n- Vendor vs build; pricing model (per seat, per token or usage, per transaction).\n- Who is the benefits owner, and what will happen to released capacity (hiring avoided, attrition not backfilled, redeployment to revenue work)?\n\n## Method\n1. **Baseline table:** volume per year, time per item, fully loaded cost per hour, error/rework rate, cycle time, cost per item, and quality or customer metrics. Note the source and period.\n2. **Benefit mechanisms** (one row each). Mark **hard** (cash, budget reduction) vs **soft** (capacity, quality):\n   - **Capacity:** volume × minutes saved × adoption % × share of tasks where AI applies ÷ 60 = hours. Value = hours × cost/hour **only for the portion released or redeployed to valued work** (state the plan).\n   - **Cycle-time and cash:** e.g. faster billing or collection → DSO days × daily revenue = one-off cash release (plus the financing benefit).\n   - **Loss avoided:** e.g. fraud, leakage, penalties × detection uplift × realisation rate.\n   - **Revenue:** conversion, retention or upsell uplift × base, with an attribution method (A/B test or control group).\n   - **Quality and risk:** error reduction, compliance improvement; quantify if possible, otherwise list as non-financial.\n3. **Ramps by quarter:** adoption (e.g. 20% → 45% → 65% → 75%) and production accuracy. Net benefit = gross benefit × adoption − rework cost.\n4. **Cost stack:**\n   - **One-off:** discovery, data preparation and labelling, integration, security review, evaluation sets, change management and training, contingency.\n   - **Run:** licences or seats; usage-based inference (requests per user per day × tokens per request × price, rising with adoption); hosting and vector or retrieval infrastructure; MLOps and monitoring; human review effort; model re-validation; support; governance overhead.\n   - **Exit or switching:** a provision if the model provider changes.\n5. **Financials:** cash-flow table by year; NPV at the hurdle rate; IRR; payback; break-even adoption. Compute with code.\n6. **Sensitivities:** adoption −30%; accuracy −10 points (higher rework); unit inference price ±50%; 6-month delay. Report NPV for each and the break-even value of the top driver.\n7. **Stage gates:**\n   - **Gate 0 (discovery):** baseline measured; data access confirmed.\n   - **Gate 1 (pilot):** defined metrics hit on a representative sample.\n   - **Gate 2 (scale):** adoption and quality thresholds sustained for N weeks; unit costs within the range.\n   - Kill criteria at each gate.\n8. **Risks and controls:** accuracy and hallucination, data leakage, bias, vendor lock-in, regulatory, and change resistance. Reference the AI risk tier.\n9. **Benefits register:** owner, metric, baseline, target, timing, and how it is banked (budget line reduced, hiring plan changed).\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Don't promise headcount reductions you can't deliver.** Most organisations realise AI value via avoided hiring, attrition and redeployment. Say which, and get HR's view.\n- **Watch the \"long tail\" of cases.** AI handles the easy 70% well. The remaining 30% may need more expert time than before. Model it.\n- **Inference costs can surprise.** Long contexts, agents that loop, and retrieval of large documents multiply token usage. Measure from the pilot and build in a price-change buffer.\n- **Prefer narrower scope with measured value** over enterprise-wide claims. A case that is 80% likely to deliver ₹8 Cr beats one that is 20% likely to deliver ₹40 Cr.\n- **Strategic value is real, but name it** (capability building, data assets, talent attraction). Don't hide it inside inflated hard benefits.\n\n## Red flags\n- No baseline; the benefit is a vendor's percentage.\n- 100% adoption from month one.\n- Costs limited to licences; no evaluation, monitoring or change costs.\n- Benefits owned by the programme, not by the business.\n- One-scenario financials; no break-even.\n- Full funding requested upfront for an unproven use case.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Agentic AI:** include orchestration, tool integration, approval workflow, action logging and rollback costs. Benefits come from end-to-end cycle time, not keystrokes. Phase autonomy (recommend → act with approval → autonomous for low-risk steps).\n- **Customer-facing GenAI:** include content safety, escalation staffing, complaint-handling and brand-risk controls. Measure CSAT and containment honestly (containment with poor CSAT is not a benefit).\n- **GCC delivery:** separate cost arbitrage already captured from the incremental AI benefit; avoid double counting.\n- **Regulated:** include validation, explainability and audit costs; allow longer time to scale.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## AI Business Case: [use case]\n**Ask:** ₹/$ ... released in [n] stages; this approval: Gate [x] for ...\n**Bottom line:** NPV ... @ ...%; IRR ...; payback ...; break-even adoption ...%\n\n### Baseline\n| Metric | Value | Source / period |\n### Benefit mechanisms\n| Benefit | Hard/soft | Formula | Year-3 value | Owner | How banked |\n### Ramps (by quarter): adoption, accuracy, rework\n### Costs\n| Item | One-off | Run (Y1/Y2/Y3) | Driver |\n### Cash flows and returns\n| | Y0 | Y1 | Y2 | Y3 | Y4 |\n### Sensitivities\n| Scenario | NPV |\n### Stage gates and kill criteria\n### Risks and controls (AI tier: ...)\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Baseline measured and sourced.\n- [ ] Each benefit has a formula, a hard/soft label, an owner and a banking method.\n- [ ] Adoption, accuracy and rework are ramped, not assumed perfect.\n- [ ] The cost stack includes usage-based inference, evaluation, monitoring, human review and change.\n- [ ] NPV, IRR, payback and break-even computed with code; sensitivities shown.\n- [ ] Stage gates with kill criteria; funding is staged.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `ai-pilot-design`: Design an AI pilot that proves value with clear success criteria, guardrails and a scale decision.\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n- `benefits-tracker`: Make sure promised benefits are defined, owned, measured and actually delivered.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources and further reading (verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Capital budgeting:** NPV, IRR and payback as taught in standard corporate-finance curricula. Use the company's own hurdle rate, horizon and capitalisation policies.\n- **Benefits realisation:** public-sector project-appraisal and benefits-management guidance (e.g. UK HM Treasury *Green Book* and related benefits-management guidance) and professional project-management bodies' practice standards.\n- **Evidence on GenAI productivity:** peer-reviewed and working-paper field studies (2023-2025) of GenAI use in customer support, consulting-style tasks and software development. They consistently find large but uneven gains: strongest on routine tasks and for less-experienced workers, with quality drops on tasks outside the model's competence. This supports modelling task mix, adoption ramps and rework rather than a single percentage.\n- **Inference cost drivers:** model providers' published per-token input and output prices and caching discounts. Prices change frequently, so use current rates and keep a ±50% sensitivity.\n- **NIST AI RMF 1.0** and **ISO/IEC 42001:2023:** governance activities whose cost should be included.\n- **Agentic systems:** agent loops multiply calls and tokens. Measure cost per completed task in the pilot, not cost per call."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional figures; recompute with real data)\n\n**Use case:** scale a GenAI contract-review assistant from 20 pilot users to 400 lawyers and contract managers across a group (India + UK).\n\n**Ask:** ₹14.2 Cr over 3 years, released in 3 stages. This approval is Gate 2 (scale to 150 users): ₹4.6 Cr.\n\n## Baseline (measured over 6 months, 2026)\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contracts reviewed / year | 52,000 | CLM system |\n| Avg review time | 95 min | Time study (n=380) |\n| Fully loaded cost / hour | ₹3,200 | HR / Finance |\n| External counsel spend on overflow reviews | ₹6.5 Cr / yr | AP |\n| Cycle time (request → signed) | 11 days | CLM |\n\n## Pilot result (20 users, 12 weeks, 2,900 contracts)\n- Time per review fell to 58 min (−39%) on standard templates, but **rose** for non-standard contracts (+8%, from extra checking).\n- Standard contracts are 72% of volume.\n- Clause-extraction accuracy was 94%. 3.5% of AI flags were false positives, adding ~4 min each to check.\n\n## Benefit mechanisms (Year 3, steady state)\n| Benefit | Hard/soft | Formula | Y3 value | Owner | How banked |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Overflow counsel spend avoided | **Hard** | 60% of overflow volume brought in-house × ₹6.5 Cr | ₹3.9 Cr | General Counsel | Legal budget cut from FY29 |\n| Hiring avoided (planned growth of 14 FTE for volume growth) | **Hard** | 10 FTE × ₹38 L | ₹3.8 Cr | GC + CHRO | Hiring plan revised |\n| Capacity released, redeployed to negotiation support | Soft | Hours released beyond the above | (not counted) | GC | — |\n| Cycle time -3 days → earlier revenue recognition on sales contracts | Hard (cash timing) | 3 days × ₹9 Cr daily contracted revenue | ₹27 Cr one-off cash timing (financing benefit ~₹0.3 Cr/yr at 10%) | CFO | Working-capital plan |\n\n## Ramps\n| Quarter | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Y2 | Y3 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Users | 150 | 250 | 350 | 400 | 400 | 400 |\n| Adoption (share of eligible reviews) | 35% | 50% | 60% | 65% | 72% | 75% |\n\n## Costs (₹ Cr)\n| Item | One-off | Y1 run | Y2 run | Y3 run | Driver |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Integration with CLM, SSO, DLP | 1.6 | | | | Vendor + internal IT |\n| Playbooks, clause library, evaluation set (1,000 annotated contracts) | 0.9 | | | | Legal ops |\n| Change, training, champions | 0.6 | 0.2 | 0.1 | 0.1 | |\n| Licences (400 seats) | | 1.9 | 2.1 | 2.1 | Per seat |\n| Usage-based inference (above seat allowance) | | 0.5 | 0.8 | 0.9 | ~22k tokens/review × volume |\n| Monitoring, evaluation reruns, re-validation | | 0.3 | 0.3 | 0.3 | 0.5 FTE + tooling |\n| Governance (AI risk review, DPIA, audit) | 0.2 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 | |\n| Contingency (15% of one-off) | 0.5 | | | | |\n\n## Returns (computed with code; illustrative)\n- NPV @ 12% over 5 years ≈ ₹11.4 Cr; IRR ≈ 41%; payback ≈ 2.1 years.\n- **Break-even adoption ≈ 38%** (steady state).\n\n| Scenario | NPV (₹ Cr) |\n|---|---|\n| Adoption −30% | 5.2 |\n| Accuracy −10 pts (rework doubles) | 7.9 |\n| Inference price +50% | 10.2 |\n| 6-month delay | 8.6 |\n| Counsel spend saving only 30% (not 60%) | 6.0 |\n\n## Stage gates\n- **Gate 2 (now → 150 users):** ₹4.6 Cr.\n- **Gate 3 (→ 400 users), month 6:** adoption ≥50% for 8 consecutive weeks; QA error rate ≤ baseline; inference cost ≤ ₹60 per review. **Kill/hold** if adoption is <35% or the error rate exceeds the baseline.\n- **Gate 4 (agentic redlining pilot):** separate case.\n\n## Risks and controls (AI tier: Limited; internal, human decides)\n- Confidential data leakage: enterprise tenancy, no training on our data (contract clause), DLP.\n- Hallucinated clause interpretations: answers cite clause text; lawyer sign-off is mandatory.\n- Vendor lock-in: export of playbooks and annotations; model-agnostic design for clause extraction."}]}, {"id": "data-readiness-check", "pack": "ai", "title": "Data Readiness Check", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Check whether the data for an AI or analytics use case is available, good enough, legal to use and accessible.", "tryp": "Check data readiness for a churn prediction model at a telecom: CRM, billing, network quality and call-centre data.", "when": ["Before an AI or analytics build", "When pilots keep stalling on data", "Data strategy and platform planning"], "inputs": ["The use case and the decision it supports", "Data sources and what you know about them"], "pairs": ["ai-use-case-prioritizer", "ai-pilot-design", "responsible-ai-review", "ai-business-case"], "body": "# Data Readiness Check\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** an honest answer to \"Can we actually do this with our data?\" before money is spent. Blockers are separated from nice-to-haves, the legal position is flagged, and you get the smallest dataset that lets a pilot start.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Fitness for purpose, not general quality.** Data good enough for a dashboard may be useless for a model, and the reverse. Judge it against the specific decision and the method.\n2. **Labels and outcomes are the hardest part.** Many predictive projects die because the outcome was never recorded consistently: what was fraud, why the customer left, whether the claim was valid.\n3. **Representativeness matters for fairness.** If some groups, regions or products are thin in history, the model will be weakest exactly where errors may hurt most.\n4. **GenAI has its own readiness.** Retrieval-based assistants need current, de-duplicated, access-controlled documents with clear ownership. A drive full of outdated versions produces confident wrong answers.\n5. **Legal usability is part of readiness.** Collected for one purpose ≠ usable for another. Purpose limitation, consent, retention and cross-border rules apply (India DPDP Act and Rules; GDPR where relevant).\n6. **Production, not just a one-off extract.** A pilot on a CSV export is fine. Scale needs a reliable pipeline with monitoring.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The use case and the decision it supports, the method (predictive model, GenAI with retrieval, agent, analytics), and the outcome to predict or the task to perform.\n- Candidate sources: systems, owners, formats (tables, documents, images, audio, logs).\n- Personal or sensitive data involved? Children's data? Data from other jurisdictions?\n- Where the AI will run (on-premise, own cloud, vendor service) and where the data would travel.\n\n## Method\n1. **List the data needed:** features or inputs, labels or outcomes (for predictive work), context documents (for GenAI), and feedback signals for monitoring.\n2. **For each source, assess:**\n   - **Availability and access:** exists? API or extract? Refresh frequency? Who approves access?\n   - **History and volume:** how far back; how many examples per class or segment (rare events need special attention).\n   - **Quality** on six dimensions: completeness (missing %), accuracy (sample verification against the source of truth), consistency (definitions across systems and time), timeliness (latency vs decision need), uniqueness (duplicates), validity (formats and ranges).\n   - **Labels:** how outcomes are recorded; consistency across teams and time; a sample re-label to estimate label noise.\n   - **Representativeness:** coverage by segment, region, product, channel and protected or proxy groups; known gaps.\n   - **Drift risk:** has the process, product or policy changed, making old data misleading?\n   - **Lineage and ownership:** a named data owner; documented transformations.\n3. **Unstructured / GenAI readiness** (if relevant):\n   - document inventory and duplicates or versions;\n   - currency (is the latest version identifiable?);\n   - access permissions, which must flow through to the assistant;\n   - format quality (scans vs text);\n   - language mix;\n   - sensitive content;\n   - an authoritative \"golden\" set for the top questions.\n4. **Legal and ethical usability:**\n   - lawful basis or consent for this purpose;\n   - notice given to individuals;\n   - sensitive attributes (and whether they are needed for fairness testing);\n   - retention limits;\n   - cross-border transfer;\n   - contractual restrictions on third-party data;\n   - IP or licence terms.\n   Flag for the DPO or counsel.\n5. **Security:** classification; whether data may be sent to an external model service; masking, tokenisation or pseudonymisation options; logging.\n6. **Rate each dimension** Ready / Partial / Not ready, with evidence. Give an overall rating.\n7. **Separate blockers from improvements:**\n   - A **blocker** prevents a valid pilot, e.g. no labels, no lawful basis, no access.\n   - An **improvement** raises performance or scalability.\n8. **Define the minimum viable dataset (MVD):** the smallest scope of sources, period and segments that allows a meaningful pilot. Note what it cannot tell you.\n9. **Remediation plan:** actions, owners, effort and dependencies; what to fix in source systems so the problem doesn't recur.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Don't let perfect data delay learning.** Start a scoped pilot on the MVD while fixing the root causes in parallel, if the blockers are cleared.\n- **Sample and verify.** Look at 50-200 real records with a domain expert; it reveals more than profiling statistics.\n- **Beware leakage.** Features recorded after the outcome (e.g. \"collections status\" when predicting default) make pilots look brilliant and production fail.\n- **Synthetic data helps testing, rarely ground truth.** Use it for privacy-safe development and edge cases, not as a substitute for real outcomes.\n- **Keep sensitive attributes for testing, carefully.** You may need them, under controls, to check fairness even if the model doesn't use them. Get a legal view.\n\n## Red flags\n- \"We have lots of data\" with no one able to say where the outcome field lives.\n- Label definitions that changed mid-history without a flag.\n- A knowledge base where nobody owns document currency.\n- Plans to send customer data to an external AI service without a data-processing assessment.\n- Pilot results on data that includes post-outcome information.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Credit, fraud, claims:** label definitions, reject inference (outcomes unknown for declined cases), class imbalance, regulatory explainability.\n- **Healthcare:** coding consistency, clinical documentation variability, consent, de-identification, device-regulation implications.\n- **Manufacturing and IoT:** sensor calibration, time alignment, missing intervals, maintenance logs as labels.\n- **HR:** small samples, historical bias, high sensitivity; usually high risk.\n- **GenAI knowledge assistants:** document hygiene, permission inheritance, golden question sets, multilingual content.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Data Readiness: [use case]\n**Decision / method:** ...  |  **Overall:** Ready / Partial / Not ready - one line\n\n| Source | Needed for | Access | History / volume | Quality (C/A/C/T/U/V) | Labels | Representativeness | Legal / privacy | Owner | Rating |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**GenAI content readiness (if relevant):** ...\n**Blockers:** ...\n**Improvements:** ...\n**Minimum viable dataset for pilot:** sources, period, segments, and what it cannot tell us\n**Remediation plan:** action - owner - effort - date\n**For DPO / counsel to confirm:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Data needs are derived from the specific decision and method.\n- [ ] Labels and outcomes are assessed explicitly, including noise.\n- [ ] Representativeness and leakage are checked.\n- [ ] Legal usability and security are addressed and flagged for review.\n- [ ] Blockers are separated from improvements; an MVD is defined.\n- [ ] The remediation plan has owners and fixes root causes.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-use-case-prioritizer`: Score and rank AI use cases on value, feasibility and risk, and pick the first wave.\n- `ai-pilot-design`: Design an AI pilot that proves value with clear success criteria, guardrails and a scale decision.\n- `responsible-ai-review`: Review an AI system before launch for fairness, transparency, privacy, safety, security and accountability.\n- `ai-business-case`: Build an honest business case for an AI use case, including model, data, change and run costs.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and laws; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **ISO 8000** (data quality) and **ISO/IEC 25012** (data quality model): quality dimensions and characteristics.\n- **ISO/IEC 5259 series** (data quality for analytics and machine learning; parts published 2024-2025).\n- **ISO/IEC 42001:2023**, Annex A data-related controls for AI systems.\n- **NIST AI RMF 1.0:** MAP and MEASURE functions on data representativeness and bias; **NIST SP 1270** (2022), *Towards a Standard for Identifying and Managing Bias in AI*.\n- **India: Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023** and **DPDP Rules, 2025** (notified 13 Nov 2025; most data-fiduciary obligations from 13 May 2027): notice, consent, purpose, retention, security safeguards, Significant Data Fiduciary duties.\n- **EU GDPR** (purpose limitation, data minimisation, DPIAs) where EU personal data is involved; **EU AI Act** Art. 10 data and data-governance requirements for high-risk systems (as amended; verify dates).\n- Common ML-practice concepts used here (target leakage, label noise, class imbalance, reject inference, data drift) are standard in data-science curricula."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional telecom operator, churn prediction for postpaid customers)\n\n**Decision:** which postpaid customers to target with retention offers each month.\n**Method:** supervised model predicting churn within 90 days.\n**Overall:** **Partial.** A pilot is feasible on a 14-month MVD. One blocker (consent wording for using network-quality data in marketing) must be cleared first.\n\n| Source | Needed for | Access | History / volume | Quality | Labels | Representativeness | Legal / privacy | Owner | Rating |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Billing | Tenure, ARPU, plan changes, payment delays | Nightly extract | 4 yrs; 6.2M accounts | Good; 0.4% missing ARPU | — | All circles | Contract necessity; marketing use per privacy notice: check | Billing IT | Ready |\n| CRM (disconnection reasons) | **Churn label** | API | 4 yrs | Reason codes changed in Mar 2025; 31% \"Other\" | Churn date reliable; **reason unreliable** | — | — | Customer Ops | Partial |\n| Network quality (dropped calls, data speeds by cell) | Experience features | Data lake, 2-day lag | 14 months only | Cell-to-customer mapping 85% complete | — | Rural circles under-covered | **Using network data for marketing targeting needs purpose review and possibly a notice update (DPDP)** | Network Analytics | Partial / **Blocker (legal)** |\n| Call-centre transcripts | Complaint themes | Vendor-hosted | 18 months; 3 vendors | Different taxonomies | — | Mostly urban, English/Hindi | Contains personal data; vendor transfer terms | Customer Service | Not ready (improvement) |\n| Retention offers history | Uplift modelling later | Spreadsheets | 2 yrs | Inconsistent | Offer accepted Y/N | — | — | Retention team | Not ready (improvement) |\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Leakage:** the \"port-out request\" flag occurs 3-7 days before churn and must be excluded from features.\n- **Label noise:** a re-label sample of 300 \"Other\" disconnections found 40% were actually price-related.\n- **Representativeness:** rural circles have half the network data coverage. Report model performance by circle, and don't use the model in rural circles until validated.\n\n**Blocker:** confirm with the DPO that network-quality data can be used for retention targeting. Options: a legitimate-use analysis under DPDP, or a privacy-notice update ahead of May 2027 obligations.\n\n**Minimum viable dataset:**\n- Billing and CRM churn dates (4 years).\n- Network features (last 14 months).\n- 18 urban circles only.\n- It cannot tell us churn reasons or offer effectiveness.\n\n**Remediation plan:**\n| Action | Owner | Effort | Date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DPO opinion on network data use | DPO | 2 weeks | 30 Nov |\n| Fix CRM reason codes (mandatory field, 12 codes) | Customer Ops | 4 weeks | Jan |\n| Standardise transcript taxonomy across vendors | Service + Procurement | 3 months | Q1 |\n| Log retention offers in CRM | Retention | 6 weeks | Feb |"}]}, {"id": "ai-pilot-design", "pack": "ai", "title": "AI Pilot Design", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Design an AI pilot that proves value with clear success criteria, guardrails and a scale decision.", "tryp": "Design a 10-week pilot for an AI assistant that drafts responses to customer complaints at a bank.", "when": ["Before any AI pilot or proof of concept", "Restarting pilots that never scale", "Getting approval for a pilot"], "inputs": ["The use case and expected value", "Users and process", "Data and tools", "Risk considerations"], "pairs": ["ai-business-case", "change-plan", "responsible-ai-review", "data-readiness-check"], "body": "# AI Pilot Design\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a pilot that ends in a clear, defensible decision. Success is defined before it starts, the measurement is believable, safety is protected throughout, and the scale-up cost is known. That ends \"pilot purgatory\".\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Decide the decision first.** Write down what result leads to scale, iterate or stop, before the pilot begins. Thresholds set afterwards are rationalisation.\n2. **Representative, not cherry-picked.** Pilot users, cases and data must resemble the scaled reality, including hard cases, languages and less-enthusiastic users.\n3. **Measure against a counterfactual.** Use a baseline period or, better, a control group doing the same work without AI.\n4. **Four metric groups, always:**\n   - **Value:** time, cost, revenue, cash.\n   - **Quality:** accuracy, error and rework, customer outcomes.\n   - **Adoption:** usage, satisfaction, intent to keep.\n   - **Safety:** harmful outputs, policy breaches, bias, data incidents.\n5. **Offline and online evaluation.** Test on a curated evaluation set before users see it, then in live use. GenAI especially needs task-specific test sets, not generic benchmarks.\n6. **Production-like guardrails from day one.** A pilot with real customers or decisions needs real controls.\n7. **Price the scale-up.** Integration, security, licences or inference at full volume, change, and governance. A pilot that cannot be afforded at scale has failed, even if accuracy is high.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Use case, users, process step and current baseline (or how to measure it in 2-4 weeks).\n- AI risk tier (affects guardrails and approvals): is anyone outside the organisation affected?\n- Data readiness (see `data-readiness-check`); the vendor or build approach.\n- Sponsor, business owner, and who decides at the end.\n- Constraints: timeline, budget, regulatory approvals, works-council or union consultation if roles change.\n\n## Method\n1. **Hypothesis:** \"If [users] use [AI capability] for [task], then [primary metric] improves by [x] within [time], without [guardrail metric] getting worse than [threshold].\"\n2. **Scope:** users (number, selection method), case types (include the hard 20%), channels, languages, duration (typically 6-12 weeks), exclusions.\n3. **Counterfactual:** control group (randomised or matched), or before/after with a stable baseline period. Note seasonality and other changes running at the same time.\n4. **Metrics and thresholds:**\n   | Group | Example metrics | Example threshold |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | Value | Handling time, cycle time, cost per case | ≥25% reduction |\n   | Quality | Error rate in QA sample, rework, accuracy on the evaluation set | No worse than baseline; ≥95% on critical fields |\n   | Adoption | Weekly active use, % of eligible tasks using AI, user satisfaction | ≥60% use by week 6 |\n   | Safety | Harmful or non-compliant outputs, data-loss events, bias gap | Zero critical; bias gap within tolerance |\n5. **Evaluation design:**\n   - **Offline:** curated test set (200-1,000 real, anonymised cases with expert answers); pass criteria before go-live.\n   - **Online:** human QA sample (e.g. 5-10% of outputs, stratified by difficulty); user feedback capture; automated checks (groundedness, PII leakage).\n   - **Sample size:** enough cases to detect the expected effect. Compute it, or use a simple rule: at least a few hundred cases per arm for proportions; more for small effects.\n6. **Guardrails:** human-in-the-loop points; data restrictions (what may be sent where); content filters; escalation paths; kill switch; logging; user disclosure where external people are involved.\n7. **Run-book:** roles (product owner, AI lead, risk partner, data owner), weekly review, an issue log, change control (no silent prompt or model changes mid-pilot without logging), communications to users.\n8. **Governance approvals:** risk tier sign-off, DPIA or privacy review if personal data is involved, security review, and works-council or HR consultation if relevant.\n9. **Decision rules** (agree now):\n   - **Scale:** all thresholds met for N consecutive weeks, and the scale-up cost is within the business case.\n   - **Iterate:** value met but quality or adoption is short, with fixable causes; define one more cycle with a time box.\n   - **Stop:** value below the minimum, or any critical safety breach not fixable by design.\n10. **Scale-up plan outline:** integration, security, licences or inference at full volume, support model, training, monitoring, and owner. Reuse `ai-business-case`.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Pick early users carefully but not only enthusiasts.** Include sceptics; their adoption predicts the scaled reality.\n- **Novelty fades.** Watch weeks 5-8, not weeks 1-2, for both usage and quality.\n- **Measure the cases AI makes harder.** Complex cases may take longer with AI because of verification. Segment the results.\n- **Freeze the configuration** for the measurement window. If you must change prompts or models, restart the clock for the affected metrics.\n- **A stop is a good outcome** if it is cheap and fast. Celebrate the learning so people keep reporting honestly.\n\n## Red flags\n- Success defined as \"users liked it\".\n- Pilot on curated easy cases; no hard cases or minority languages.\n- No baseline; before/after across a seasonal change.\n- Customer-facing GenAI with no logging or escalation.\n- No idea what full-scale inference or licence costs will be.\n- The pilot extended indefinitely with \"promising\" results.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Agentic pilots:** start in recommend-only or shadow mode (the agent proposes, a human executes), then act-with-approval. Measure proposal accuracy and the approval/override rate before any autonomy.\n- **Customer-facing:** begin with internal agent-assist; disclose AI use; measure complaint rates and escalation quality, not just containment.\n- **Regulated decisions** (credit, claims, hiring): involve the validation function from the start; human makes the final decision; test fairness by group.\n- **GCC / multi-region:** pilot in two regions with different languages or processes to test transferability.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## AI Pilot Design: [use case]\n**Hypothesis:** ...\n**Scope:** users ... | cases ... | duration ... | exclusions ...\n**Counterfactual:** control group / baseline ...\n\n| Group | Metric | Baseline | Threshold | How measured | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Evaluation:** offline set (size, pass criteria) | online QA sample | sample-size note\n**Guardrails:** ...\n**Run-book and roles:** ...\n**Approvals needed:** ...\n**Decision rules:** Scale if ... | Iterate if ... | Stop if ...\n**Scale-up requirements and cost estimate:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The hypothesis is falsifiable, with a guardrail metric.\n- [ ] The scope is representative, including hard cases.\n- [ ] A counterfactual is defined.\n- [ ] All four metric groups have thresholds.\n- [ ] Offline evaluation precedes live use; QA sampling is defined.\n- [ ] Guardrails and approvals match the risk tier.\n- [ ] Decision rules and scale-up costs are agreed upfront.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-business-case`: Build an honest business case for an AI use case, including model, data, change and run costs.\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n- `responsible-ai-review`: Review an AI system before launch for fairness, transparency, privacy, safety, security and accountability.\n- `data-readiness-check`: Check whether the data for an AI or analytics use case is available, good enough, legal to use and accessible.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught methods; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Experimental design:** randomised controlled trials, matched controls and difference-in-differences, as used in public policy and product experimentation; basic power and sample-size calculations for proportions and means.\n- **NIST AI RMF 1.0** (MEASURE and MANAGE) and **NIST AI 600-1 GenAI Profile:** test, evaluation, verification and validation (TEVV) expectations; GenAI risks such as confabulation and information security.\n- **ISO/IEC 42001:2023:** AI system lifecycle controls, including verification and validation before deployment.\n- **OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025):** risks to test in GenAI pilots (prompt injection, sensitive information disclosure, excessive agency, misinformation).\n- **India:** DPDP Act 2023 and Rules 2025 for personal data in pilots; RBI expectations on customer grievance handling, and the FREE-AI recommendations for regulated entities (verify any directions issued).\n- **EU AI Act** Art. 50 transparency for AI interacting with people, where in scope (verify dates after the 2026 Omnibus)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional private bank, complaint-response drafting)\n\n**Hypothesis:** if complaint handlers use an AI assistant that drafts responses grounded in the complaint, account history and approved policy texts, then **average handling time falls ≥30% within 10 weeks**, without the **QA compliance score** falling below baseline (88%) and with **zero** responses containing regulatory-language breaches.\n\n**Scope:**\n- 40 handlers across 2 centres (20 AI, 20 control, matched on tenure and QA score).\n- Complaint categories: cards, digital banking and charges. **Excludes** fraud and bereavement cases, which stay manual.\n- English and Hindi.\n- 10 weeks; configuration frozen from week 3.\n\n**Counterfactual:** a parallel control group, plus an 8-week baseline for both groups.\n\n| Group | Metric | Baseline | Threshold | How measured | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Value | Avg handling time | 46 min | ≤32 min (−30%) | Case system timestamps | Weekly |\n| Value | Complaints closed within 15 days | 81% | ≥88% | Case system | Weekly |\n| Quality | QA compliance score | 88% | ≥88% (no worse) | QA team, 10% stratified sample | Weekly |\n| Quality | Reopened complaints | 6.5% | ≤6.5% | Case system | Bi-weekly |\n| Adoption | % of eligible cases using the draft | — | ≥60% by week 6 | Tool logs | Weekly |\n| Adoption | Handler satisfaction (1-5) | — | ≥3.8 | Survey wk 4, 8 | Twice |\n| Safety | Responses with regulatory-language breaches | — | 0 critical | QA + automated phrase checks | Daily |\n| Safety | Personal data of another customer in a draft | — | 0 | Automated check + QA | Daily |\n\n**Evaluation:**\n- **Offline:** 600 historic complaints with approved final responses. Pass criteria before go-live:\n  - ≥90% factual consistency with account data;\n  - 100% of drafts cite a policy source;\n  - zero critical policy breaches in 200 adversarial cases.\n- **Online:** 10% QA sample, stratified by category and complexity.\n- **Sample size:** ~3,500 complaints per arm over 10 weeks is sufficient to detect a 10% change in the 15-day closure rate.\n\n**Guardrails:**\n- Drafts only; the handler edits and sends.\n- No drafting for excluded categories.\n- Retrieval restricted to approved policy texts.\n- Customer data stays in the bank's cloud tenancy, with no vendor training on it.\n- All drafts and edits are logged.\n- Kill switch with the Head of Complaints.\n\n**Approvals:** AI risk committee (tier: Limited, internal draft with human send); privacy review; security review; communication to the handlers' representative body.\n\n**Decision rules:**\n- **Scale** to 300 handlers if all thresholds are met in weeks 6-10 and projected run cost is ≤₹55 per complaint.\n- **Iterate** (one 6-week cycle) if handling time is met but QA is 85-88%.\n- **Stop** if handling time falls by <15%, or there is any repeated critical breach.\n\n**Scale-up requirements:**\n- Integration into the case system (currently a side panel).\n- Hindi accuracy improvements.\n- Support model.\n- Est. ₹2.4 Cr one-off and ₹1.1 Cr per year at 300 users (see the business case)."}]}, {"id": "agentic-workflow-design", "pack": "ai", "title": "Agentic Workflow Design", "time": "20 min", "summary": "Redesign a process for AI agents working with people: steps, tools, handoffs, controls and metrics.", "tryp": "Design an agentic workflow for intercompany reconciliation across 14 entities in two different ERP systems, month-end close.", "when": ["Redesigning processes with AI agents", "Moving from RPA to AI agents", "Assessing whether a process is ready for agents"], "inputs": ["The current process steps, systems, volumes, pain points", "Controls and compliance requirements", "Appetite for autonomy"], "pairs": ["ai-opportunity-scan", "responsible-ai-review", "operating-model", "raci-builder"], "body": "# Agentic Workflow Design\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a design that operations, risk and audit can sign off. It shows what the agent does, what it may touch, where a human decides, how it fails safely, how every action is traced, and how autonomy grows only as evidence accumulates.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Use the simplest thing that works.** Many steps are better as deterministic workflow or rules. Use an agent only where flexible reasoning across varied inputs is genuinely needed.\n2. **Autonomy is earned, step by step.** Classify each step's autonomy separately and raise it only on measured performance.\n3. **Least privilege, read before write.** Agents get only the tools and data scopes a step needs. Write actions are narrower than read actions and gated by value, type and reversibility.\n4. **Humans are accountable; agents are delegated.** Every agent action maps to a human owner, and control frameworks (segregation of duties, approval limits) still apply.\n5. **Everything is logged and replayable.** Inputs, retrieved context, reasoning summary, tool calls, outputs and approvals form an audit trail.\n6. **Design for failure.** Timeouts, loops, wrong tool calls, injected instructions in documents, partial completion. Define safe states and rollbacks.\n7. **Cost and latency are design parameters.** Agent loops multiply model calls. Cap steps, cache, and measure cost per completed case.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The process: steps, actors, systems, volumes, cycle time, exception rate, pain points; the documented rules and the undocumented ones.\n- Control environment: approval limits, segregation of duties, audit requirements, regulatory obligations.\n- System access: APIs available? Read and write scopes? Test environments?\n- Risk appetite for automation: which outcomes must never happen (e.g. paying the wrong party, sending wrong information to a customer)?\n- Owners: process owner, control owner, IT owner.\n\n## Method\n1. **Decompose** the process into steps with inputs, outputs, decisions, systems and exception paths. Fix obvious process waste first.\n2. **Classify each step:**\n   - **Deterministic automation:** stable rules, structured data.\n   - **AI-assisted:** extraction, classification or drafting, with a human or rule check.\n   - **Agentic:** multi-step reasoning, tool choice and varied inputs.\n   - **Human-only:** judgement with accountability, relationships, ethics, or high irreversible impact.\n3. **Set an autonomy level per step:**\n   - **L0** Human does it; the agent may observe (shadow).\n   - **L1** The agent recommends; the human executes.\n   - **L2** The agent executes after human approval (per action or batch).\n   - **L3** The agent executes within limits; a human reviews samples or exceptions afterwards.\n   - **L4** Fully autonomous. Rare in business processes; only for low-impact, reversible actions with strong monitoring.\n4. **Tools and permissions matrix:**\n   - each tool (system, API, document store, email, payment);\n   - read or write;\n   - scope (entities, amount limits, fields);\n   - credential handling (no shared human credentials);\n   - environment (sandbox first).\n5. **Handoffs:** what the agent passes to a human: summary, evidence, recommendation, confidence, and what it could not resolve. What the human passes back: decision and reason, captured for learning.\n6. **Exceptions and confidence:** triggers to stop and escalate, such as low confidence, missing data, conflicting sources, amount above the limit, new counterparty, suspected instruction injection, or an unrecognised document type. Define the safe state (pause, flag, no partial postings).\n7. **Controls:**\n   - segregation of duties (the agent cannot both create and approve);\n   - approval limits;\n   - dual control for payments or master-data changes;\n   - rate and volume limits;\n   - spend caps;\n   - kill switch;\n   - change control for prompts, tools and models.\n8. **Security:**\n   - prompt-injection defences: treat document and email content as data, not instructions; output validation; allow-listed tools;\n   - data-loss prevention;\n   - isolation of untrusted content.\n9. **Logging and audit:** immutable action log with case ID, inputs, context sources, tool calls, outputs, approver, timestamps and model version, retained per policy.\n10. **Metrics:**\n    - straight-through rate per step;\n    - accuracy of proposals;\n    - override / rejection rate;\n    - exception rate;\n    - cycle time;\n    - cost per completed case;\n    - control breaches;\n    - rollback count;\n    - user and customer impact.\n11. **Phased rollout:**\n    - **Phase 0** shadow (L0, compare to human outcomes);\n    - **Phase 1** recommend (L1);\n    - **Phase 2** act with approval (L2);\n    - **Phase 3** supervised autonomy (L3) for steps meeting thresholds over a sustained period, e.g. ≥98% proposal accuracy over 8 weeks with zero control breaches.\n    Define promotion and demotion criteria.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Batch approvals carefully.** Approving 200 agent actions at once with one click is often a rubber stamp. Use sampling with risk-based selection instead, and measure review quality.\n- **Don't automate around a missing control.** If the human process lacked a check, add it before the agent inherits the gap.\n- **Prefer structured tool outputs** (JSON with validation) over free text for anything that changes a system of record.\n- **Keep humans skilled.** If agents do 95% of the work, plan how people stay competent to handle the 5%, and to take over in an outage.\n- **Vendor agent platforms still need your controls.** Configuration, permissions and logs are your responsibility.\n\n## Red flags\n- An agent given a human's full credentials \"for the pilot\".\n- Write access to payment or master data without dual control.\n- No action-level log, or logs that don't capture the model version and context.\n- No defence against instructions hidden in emails, PDFs or web pages the agent reads.\n- Autonomy set by enthusiasm rather than measured accuracy.\n- Cost per case unknown.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Finance (reconciliation, close, AP exceptions):** postings via approved journals only; limits per entity and amount; reconciliation evidence attached; auditor-readable logs.\n- **Customer operations (onboarding, servicing):** customer communications at L1-L2 initially; identity verification remains a strong control; complaint and vulnerability detection routes to humans.\n- **Healthcare administration (pre-authorisation, coding):** clinical judgement stays human; payer submissions only after approval; protected health data handling.\n- **IT operations:** runbook automation with change-management integration; blast-radius limits; automatic rollback.\n- **Procurement:** vendor onboarding checks are good agent work; bank-detail changes always require independent verification.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Agentic Workflow: [process]\n**Goal and metrics:** ...  |  **Process owner:** ...  |  **Control owner:** ...\n\n| Step | Today (who, system) | Design: Rules / AI-assisted / Agent / Human | Autonomy (L0-L4) | Tools and access (R/W, scope) | Escalation triggers | Controls |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Handoff design:** ...\n**Security and injection defences:** ...\n**Logging and audit trail:** ...\n**Metrics and thresholds:** ...\n**Phased autonomy plan:** phase - criteria to promote - criteria to demote\n**Cost and latency controls:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Each step is justified as rules, AI-assisted, agent or human; the simplest viable option is chosen.\n- [ ] Autonomy is set per step, with promotion and demotion criteria.\n- [ ] Least-privilege tools matrix with read/write separation and limits.\n- [ ] Segregation of duties, approval limits and the kill switch are defined.\n- [ ] Injection and untrusted-content defences are included.\n- [ ] Action-level audit logging is specified.\n- [ ] Metrics include cost per case and override rates.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-opportunity-scan`: Find where AI can create real value in a function or business, grounded in processes and pain points.\n- `responsible-ai-review`: Review an AI system before launch for fairness, transparency, privacy, safety, security and accountability.\n- `operating-model`: Design how the organisation will work: structure, processes, people, technology, governance and locations.\n- `raci-builder`: Clarify who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each activity or decision.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and widely taught practices; verify current versions)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025)** and OWASP guidance on agentic AI threats: prompt injection (including indirect injection via documents), excessive agency, insecure output handling, sensitive information disclosure.\n- **NIST AI RMF 1.0** and **NIST AI 600-1 GenAI Profile:** governance, human oversight, monitoring.\n- **ISO/IEC 42001:2023:** AI management system controls; **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** information security, including access control and logging.\n- **Internal control frameworks:** the COSO Internal Control - Integrated Framework (2013): segregation of duties, authorisation limits, audit trails. For India, Companies Act 2013 s.134(5)(e) internal financial controls.\n- **Levels of automation:** widely used human-automation interaction scales (from \"human does all\" to \"fully autonomous\") adapted here as L0-L4.\n- **Engineering practice for agents:** prefer simple, composable workflows; give agents well-documented tools; add human checkpoints; test extensively in sandboxes. This is consistent with current published guidance from model developers and practitioners.\n- **EU AI Act** Art. 14 human oversight for high-risk systems (where in scope; verify dates as amended in 2026)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional group with 14 entities, intercompany reconciliation at month-end)\n\n**Goal:** cut intercompany reconciliation effort from ~600 to ≤150 hours a month and close 1.5 days faster, with zero unapproved postings.\n**Process owner:** Group Financial Controller. **Control owner:** Head of Internal Control.\n\n| Step | Today | Design | Autonomy | Tools and access | Escalation triggers | Controls |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Extract IC balances by counterparty | Analysts export from ERP | **Rules** (scheduled report) | n/a | ERP read: IC accounts only | Extract failure | Completeness check vs trial balance |\n| 2. Match transactions | Spreadsheet matching | **AI-assisted** (fuzzy match on amount, date, reference, FX) | L3 for exact matches; L1 for fuzzy | Read: both entities' ledgers | Match confidence <0.9 | Sample review 5% weekly |\n| 3. Investigate mismatches | Emails between entity accountants | **Agent:** gathers invoices, POs and emails; proposes a cause (timing, FX, missing invoice, wrong account) | L1 → L2 | Read: ERP, document store, shared mailbox (IC folder only) | Conflicting evidence; counterparty not responding in 2 days | Evidence attached to every proposal |\n| 4. Propose adjusting entries | Accountant drafts journal | **Agent** drafts the journal in the approved template | L2 (approval by entity controller) | Write: **draft** journals only; cannot post | Amount >₹1 lakh; new GL account; cross-currency >2% variance | Agent cannot post or approve; posting by human (segregation of duties) |\n| 5. Post entries | Accountant | **Human** | L0 | — | — | Standard journal approval limits |\n| 6. Sign-off and reporting | Controller | **Human**, with an agent-drafted summary | L1 | Read | — | Controller attestation |\n\n**Handoffs:** each proposal includes the mismatch, likely cause, evidence links, proposed entry, confidence and open questions. The controller's approve or reject decision and reason are captured to improve matching rules.\n\n**Security:**\n- Email and document content is treated as data. The agent cannot follow instructions found in documents.\n- Tool calls are allow-listed.\n- Outputs are validated against a schema (account codes, entity codes, amount limits).\n\n**Logging:** case ID, sources read, tool calls, proposal, model and prompt version, approver, timestamps. Kept for 8 years with finance records (per policy).\n\n**Metrics and thresholds:**\n- Proposal accuracy ≥95% (approved without edit).\n- Override rate trend.\n- Exceptions ≤15%.\n- Hours per close.\n- Cost per reconciled pair ≤₹120.\n- Zero unapproved postings.\n\n**Phased autonomy:**\n- **Phase 0 (1 close):** shadow mode; compare the agent's proposed causes with the accountants'.\n- **Phase 1 (2 closes):** L1 recommendations.\n- **Phase 2 (from close 4):** L2 drafting for amounts ≤₹1 lakh.\n- **Promote to L3 for exact matches** after 3 closes at ≥99% accuracy.\n- **Demote** on any control breach or accuracy below 90%.\n\n**Cost and latency:** max 12 tool calls per case; cache entity master data; run overnight in batches; budget alert at 120% of plan."}]}, {"id": "responsible-ai-review", "pack": "ai", "title": "Responsible AI Review", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Review an AI system before launch for fairness, transparency, privacy, safety, security and accountability.", "tryp": "Do a responsible AI review of our GenAI chatbot that will answer customer questions about insurance policies.", "when": ["Before launching or scaling an AI system", "After an AI incident or complaint", "Vendor AI due diligence"], "inputs": ["System description: purpose, users, data, model, outputs, decision impact", "Testing done so far", "Jurisdiction and sector"], "pairs": ["ai-risk-register", "ai-governance-review", "ai-pilot-design", "agentic-workflow-design"], "body": "# Responsible AI Review\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a clear, evidenced launch decision for one AI system, with the conditions that must be met and how the system will be watched once live. A risk committee, a regulator or an affected customer could read it and see that the right questions were asked and answered with evidence.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Start with people, not the model.** Who is affected, how, and how badly could it go wrong for them, including people who never touch the system (applicants, patients, citizens)?\n2. **Evidence over assurance.** Test results, samples and logs, not \"the vendor confirmed\".\n3. **Proportionality.** The depth of review scales with impact and autonomy. A drafting tool for internal emails needs a light check; a credit or claims decision needs the full review.\n4. **Fairness is measured, not asserted.** Define groups, metrics and tolerances; test; explain residual gaps.\n5. **Human oversight must be effective.** Reviewers need time, information, authority and training to override, and override rates must be monitored.\n6. **Contestability.** Affected people can find out a decision involved AI, get a meaningful explanation, and challenge it.\n7. **The review continues after launch.** Define monitoring, triggers for re-review, and an owner.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- **System description:** purpose; users; affected persons; inputs and data; model type (statistical, ML, LLM, agent; in-house or vendor); outputs; the decision it informs or makes; volume; jurisdictions.\n- **Risk tier** (from the AI risk register), or determine it now.\n- **Testing already done,** and what evidence exists.\n- **Legal context:** sector rules; DPDP or GDPR; EU AI Act category if in scope; employment, consumer or anti-discrimination law.\n\n## Review areas and the key questions\n1. **Purpose and context:** is AI appropriate here? What is the non-AI alternative? Benefits to affected people as well as to the organisation.\n2. **Affected people and impact:** which groups; the severity of potential harm (financial, access, health, liberty, dignity); vulnerable groups; scale.\n3. **Fairness:**\n   - Which groups to test: legally protected characteristics where lawful to process, or proxies such as region, language, age band or gender where available.\n   - Which metrics: selection-rate ratio, error-rate parity (false positive and false negative by group), calibration.\n   - Tolerance thresholds; the results; root causes of gaps; mitigations.\n4. **Transparency and explainability:**\n   - Do people know AI is involved?\n   - Can the organisation explain individual adverse outcomes in plain language (main reasons)?\n   - Is the documentation complete (model card or system card)?\n5. **Privacy and data protection:** lawful basis or consent; purpose limitation; minimisation; retention; data sent to third parties or other countries; DPIA where required or prudent.\n6. **Accuracy, robustness and reliability:**\n   - performance on a representative test set, by segment;\n   - edge cases;\n   - adversarial and stress tests;\n   - for GenAI: hallucination or groundedness rates on task-specific test sets, refusal behaviour, and consistency across languages.\n7. **Security:** prompt injection (direct and indirect), data leakage, model or data poisoning, access control, abuse monitoring, red-team results.\n8. **Human oversight:**\n   - Who reviews, at what point, with what information, and with authority to override.\n   - Review time and override rates.\n   - Training.\n   - Automation-bias safeguards.\n9. **Accountability:** named business owner and model owner; RACI; sign-offs; incident process.\n10. **Contestability and redress:** how affected people can complain or appeal; human review of appeals; timelines; feedback into the system.\n11. **Third parties:** vendor documentation, testing evidence, contract terms (data use, change notice, audit, incident notice).\n12. **Environmental and societal** (where material): compute footprint for large-scale use; effects on jobs and skills.\n13. **Monitoring plan:** KPIs and KRIs (performance by segment, drift, fairness metrics, complaints, overrides, incidents), frequency, thresholds, and re-review triggers (model change, new population, incident).\n\n## Method\n1. **Scope and tier** the system; set the review depth.\n2. **Gather evidence:** documentation, test reports, data descriptions, samples of outputs, logs and contracts. Interview the owner, developers, reviewers and frontline users.\n3. **Run or request tests** that are missing (fairness, groundedness, red-team). Specify what, on which data, and the pass criteria.\n4. **Rate each area:** Satisfactory / Needs improvement / Unacceptable, each with evidence.\n5. **Decide:**\n   - **Launch:** all satisfactory.\n   - **Launch with conditions:** time-bound fixes; extra monitoring; restricted scope.\n   - **Do not launch:** any unacceptable area on a high-impact system, or harm that cannot be mitigated.\n6. **Record:** sign-offs (business owner, risk, legal or privacy), conditions with owners and dates, and the next review date.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Explainability vs accuracy.** For decisions about individuals, prefer models whose main reasons can be explained. If a more complex model is used, show that its explanations are faithful and useful to the person affected.\n- **\"We don't collect protected attributes\" is not a fairness test.** Proxies can still discriminate. Test with lawful proxies or controlled samples.\n- **A disclaimer is not a control.** \"AI may make mistakes\" doesn't mitigate a harmful output in a high-stakes context.\n- **Restricted scope is a strong mitigation.** Launch to lower-risk segments or with stricter human review while evidence builds.\n- **Document the \"do nothing\" comparison.** Sometimes the AI is fairer or more consistent than the current human process. That matters, and it should be measured too.\n\n## Red flags\n- No identification of affected non-users.\n- Fairness \"not applicable\" for a system making decisions about people.\n- Accuracy reported only as an overall average.\n- Human reviewers approving in seconds; no override data.\n- No appeal route, or appeals reviewed by the same system.\n- Vendor claims with no test evidence.\n- No owner or no next-review date.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Credit and insurance:** adverse-action reasons; fairness across protected and proxy groups; regulatory model-risk expectations.\n- **Hiring and HR:** high risk in many jurisdictions; bias audits; candidate notice; human decision.\n- **Healthcare:** clinical safety, medical-device rules, clinician oversight, patient consent.\n- **Public sector:** transparency registers, citizen redress, equality duties.\n- **Customer-facing GenAI:** disclosure, grounding, escalation, vulnerable-customer handling, content safety, logging.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Responsible AI Review: [system], v[x], [date]\n**Decision:** Launch / Launch with conditions / Do not launch - one line\n**Tier:** ...  |  **Affected people:** ...  |  **Review depth:** ...\n\n| Area | Evidence reviewed | Finding | Rating | Required action | Before / after launch | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Fairness results:** groups, metrics, values, tolerance, gap explanation\n**Accuracy and GenAI evaluation:** test set, results by segment, groundedness / hallucination rate\n**Human oversight design and metrics:** ...\n**Monitoring plan and re-review triggers:** ...\n**Legal questions for counsel / DPO:** ...\n**Sign-offs:** business owner / risk / privacy / legal - date\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Affected people, including non-users, are identified, and harm severity assessed.\n- [ ] Fairness is tested with defined groups, metrics and tolerances, or the reason it's impossible is explained, with a mitigation.\n- [ ] Accuracy is reported by segment; GenAI groundedness tested on task-specific data.\n- [ ] Security includes injection and leakage testing.\n- [ ] Human oversight is shown to be effective (time, information, authority, override data).\n- [ ] Contestability and redress routes exist.\n- [ ] Clear decision, conditions with owners, monitoring and next review date.\n- [ ] Legal conclusions are flagged for counsel, not asserted.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Not legal advice on AI or data-protection law. This skill does not replace independent model validation, security testing (including red-teaming), or a formal data protection impact assessment.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-risk-register`: Build a risk register for AI use cases: classify, score, control and assign owners.\n- `ai-governance-review`: Assess how well an organisation governs AI: policy, roles, inventory, risk, controls, and board oversight.\n- `ai-pilot-design`: Design an AI pilot that proves value with clear success criteria, guardrails and a scale decision.\n- `agentic-workflow-design`: Redesign a process for AI agents working with people: steps, tools, handoffs, controls and metrics.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public standards and laws; as researched November 2026; verify)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **NIST AI RMF 1.0** (2023): trustworthiness characteristics (valid and reliable, safe, secure and resilient, accountable and transparent, explainable and interpretable, privacy-enhanced, fair with harmful bias managed). **NIST AI 600-1 GenAI Profile** (2024). **NIST SP 1270** (bias).\n- **ISO/IEC 42005:2025** (AI system impact assessment); **ISO/IEC 42001:2023** (AI management system); **ISO/IEC 23894:2023** (AI risk management); **ISO/IEC TR 24027** (bias in AI systems).\n- **OECD AI Principles** (2019, updated 2024); **UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI** (2021).\n- **EU AI Act** (Reg. 2024/1689, as amended by Reg. 2026/1744): Art. 9-15 requirements for high-risk systems (risk management, data, documentation, transparency, human oversight, accuracy and robustness); Art. 27 fundamental-rights impact assessment for certain deployers; Art. 50 transparency. Annex III obligations from 2 Dec 2027 (verify).\n- **India:** DPDP Act 2023 and Rules 2025 (most obligations from 13 May 2027; Significant Data Fiduciary algorithmic due diligence); MeitY India AI Governance Guidelines (Nov 2025); RBI FREE-AI report (Aug 2025) for regulated entities; sector regulators' consumer-protection and grievance rules.\n- **OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025):** security testing scope for GenAI.\n- **Fairness metrics** (selection-rate ratio, equalised odds, calibration) are standard in the algorithmic-fairness literature. Note that metrics can conflict, so choose them based on the harm in context and document why."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; fictional general insurer, GenAI policy-question assistant on web and messaging channels)\n\n**Decision:** **Launch with conditions**, restricted to existing policyholders' questions about motor and home policies. No claims-decision content. Hindi launch deferred.\n**Tier:** Limited (informational; customer-facing). **Affected people:** policyholders, including elderly and first-time digital users. **Review depth:** standard plus GenAI-specific testing.\n\n| Area | Evidence | Finding | Rating | Required action | When | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Purpose | Business case; contact-centre data | 38% of calls are policy-wording questions; 24x7 benefit | Satisfactory | — | — | Head Service | — |\n| Fairness | Test set by language and age-band proxy | Hindi answers less accurate (89% vs 97% English) | Needs improvement | Defer Hindi; improve retrieval and evaluation; retest | Before Hindi launch | AI lead | Feb |\n| Transparency | UI mock-ups | AI disclosure present; no \"talk to a human\" on the first screen | Needs improvement | Add a human-handover option on every screen | Before | Product | 2 wks |\n| Privacy | Data-flow map; vendor contract | Chat logs retained 3 yrs by default; vendor may use data to improve models | **Unacceptable** as is | Retention set to policy (e.g. 1 yr unless needed for complaints, aligned with DPDP Rules); contract amendment: no training on our data | Before | DPO / Legal | 3 wks |\n| Accuracy / groundedness | 800-question test set with answers from policy wordings | 96.1% correct; 2.4% partially correct; **1.5% wrong on add-on cover exclusions** | Needs improvement | Answers must cite the clause; refuse if no source is found; add 150 add-on questions to the test set; target ≥99% on exclusions | Before | AI lead | 3 wks |\n| Security | Red-team (300 prompts incl. indirect injection via uploaded PDFs) | 4 jailbreaks produced off-topic content; no data leakage | Needs improvement | Stricter input/output filters; disable PDF upload at launch | Before | Security | 2 wks |\n| Human oversight | Escalation design | Handover to agents available 8am-10pm only | Needs improvement | Out-of-hours: create a callback ticket; never \"no help available\" | Before | Head Service | 2 wks |\n| Contestability | Complaint process | Bot-related complaints not tagged | Needs improvement | Tag and review weekly | After | Complaints | 4 wks |\n| Third party | Vendor documentation | Model-change notice not contractual | Needs improvement | 30-day notice clause; regression test on each change | Before | Procurement | 3 wks |\n\n**Monitoring:** weekly 2% transcript QA; groundedness score; accuracy by language; complaints and escalations; jailbreak attempts. **Re-review** on model change, a new language, new products, or any serious incident.\n\n**Legal questions:** confirm disclosure and record-keeping obligations under insurance-regulator rules for digital channels; DPDP notice wording for chat (DPO).\n\n**Sign-offs:** Head Service (owner), CRO, DPO, General Counsel. Next review in 6 months."}]}, {"id": "meeting-brief", "pack": "leader", "title": "Meeting Brief", "time": "5 min", "summary": "A one-page brief before any important meeting: objective, people, positions, your asks and your lines.", "tryp": "Brief me for a 30-minute meeting with a prospective client's CFO who is evaluating us for a finance transformation.", "when": ["Before client, investor, board or senior internal meetings", "When you have 10 minutes to prepare", "Briefing a leader who is joining a meeting"], "inputs": ["Meeting purpose, attendees, time", "What you know about them and the history", "What you want out of it"], "pairs": ["audience-brief", "negotiation-prep", "stakeholder-map", "executive-email"], "body": "# Meeting Brief\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** you walk in knowing what you want, who wants what, what you'll say, what you'll be asked and how you'll answer, so the meeting produces a decision or commitment rather than a pleasant conversation.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Outcome first.** Define the specific outcome (a decision, commitment, information, or relationship step) and the minimum acceptable result. If you can't name it, you're not ready.\n2. **Know the room.** For each attendee: role, interests (what they really care about), position on this issue, influence, history, and what they need from you.\n3. **Three messages, not ten.** Leaders remember about three points. Choose them for this audience.\n4. **Pre-empt the hard questions.** List the 5-8 most likely and most dangerous questions; prepare short, honest answers with evidence.\n5. **Asks and offers are explicit.** What you will ask for, what you can give, and what you cannot.\n6. **Landmines mapped:** sensitive history, legal or regulatory constraints, confidential topics, personal dynamics.\n7. **Pre-wire.** Big decisions are rarely made in the meeting. Plan the conversations beforehand.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The meeting's purpose, date, length and format; who called it; the attendees.\n- Background: history, prior commitments, open issues, documents shared.\n- Your objectives and constraints (mandate, authority, what's not negotiable).\n- What's at stake for each side.\n\n## Method\n1. **Outcome:** the ideal result and the minimum acceptable result; how you'll know you got it.\n2. **Attendee map:** name and role | interests | likely position (supportive / neutral / opposed) | influence | what they need to hear | relationship notes.\n3. **Context in 5 lines:** why now, what's happened, what's changed.\n4. **Three key messages,** each with one proof point.\n5. **Questions and objections:** question → a short answer (2-3 sentences) → evidence → bridge back to the messages. Include the \"question you hope they don't ask\".\n6. **Asks and offers:** what you'll request; concessions you can make; red lines.\n7. **Landmines:** topics to avoid or handle carefully; legal or disclosure constraints (e.g. price-sensitive information, ongoing litigation, regulatory matters).\n8. **Facts at hand:** 5-10 numbers and dates you must know without notes.\n9. **Agenda and time plan:** a suggested flow; where to spend time; when to make the ask.\n10. **Pre-wiring plan:** whom to brief before, and on what.\n11. **Close and follow-up:** how to summarise agreements; owners and dates; a follow-up note within 24 hours.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **When the outcome is unrealistic for one meeting,** set a \"next step\" outcome (agreement to a working session, data access, a second meeting).\n- **Listen more in first meetings;** state less. Diagnose before prescribing.\n- **With regulators:** factual, complete, no spin; record commitments carefully; legal counsel where appropriate.\n- **With boards:** lead with the decision needed and the key risks; respect the governance process.\n- **Bad news:** say it early, own it, bring a plan.\n\n## Red flags\n- No defined outcome.\n- Unknown attendees' interests.\n- Ten messages; no answers to obvious hard questions.\n- Asks not stated; no red lines.\n- No follow-up plan.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Board or committee:** the decision, the options, the risks; the pre-read is concise; questions anticipated from each director's expertise.\n- **Investor:** strategy, performance, guidance consistency; disclosure-rule awareness (no selective price-sensitive information).\n- **Regulator:** facts, compliance status, remediation; legal review; a record of the meeting.\n- **Customer or partner:** their goals, value delivered, issues, the expansion or renewal ask.\n- **Negotiation:** combine with `negotiation-prep`.\n- **First meeting with a new stakeholder:** relationship and listening; learn their priorities.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Meeting Brief: [meeting], [date, duration]\n**Outcome wanted:** ... **Minimum acceptable:** ...\n**Context (5 lines):** ...\n\n| Attendee | Interests | Likely stance | Influence | Needs to hear |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Three messages:** 1. ... (proof) 2. ... 3. ...\n**Likely questions and answers:**\n| Question | Answer (2-3 sentences) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n\n**Asks / offers / red lines:** ...\n**Landmines:** ...\n**Facts at hand:** ...\n**Agenda and timing:** ...\n**Pre-wire:** ... **Follow-up:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] An outcome and minimum acceptable result defined.\n- [ ] Every attendee mapped with interests and stance.\n- [ ] Three messages with proof; hard questions answered honestly.\n- [ ] Asks, offers, red lines and landmines explicit.\n- [ ] Facts, agenda, pre-wire and follow-up plan; one page where possible.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Use judgement on interpersonal and negotiation situations. For employment, legal or HR matters, consult the appropriate professional.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `audience-brief`: Profile who you are persuading: what they care about, what they already believe, and what would change their mind.\n- `negotiation-prep`: Prepare a negotiation: interests, BATNA, ZOPA, trade-offs, opening position and concession plan.\n- `stakeholder-map`: Map who matters, where they stand, and the plan to move each of them.\n- `executive-email`: Write a short email or message that gets a senior person to read, decide and reply.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Stakeholder analysis:** interests vs positions; influence-interest mapping, as taught in management and negotiation curricula.\n- **Message discipline:** a few key messages with proof points; question anticipation and bridging, as used in executive communication training.\n- **Pre-wiring:** socialising proposals with key stakeholders before formal decision forums.\n- **Disclosure rules:** listed-company obligations on price-sensitive information and fair disclosure (e.g. SEBI LODR and PIT Regulations in India).\n- **Public procurement:** rules governing engagement with government buyers (state and central procurement rules)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; CEO of a mid-sized hospital chain meeting the state health secretary and team, 45 minutes)\n\n**Outcome wanted:** agreement in principle to a public-private partnership pilot. The chain would run critical-care units in 3 district hospitals, with a joint working group set up within 30 days.\n\n**Minimum acceptable:** the department nominates an officer to co-develop a proposal.\n\n**Context:**\n- District hospitals have critical-care bed shortages and staff gaps.\n- The state budget announced a PPP intent for specialty care.\n- Our chain runs 2 similar units in another state with outcome data.\n- A previous PPP in the state faced criticism over pricing for patients.\n\n| Attendee | Interests | Likely stance | Influence | Needs to hear |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Health Secretary | Visible improvement in outcomes; avoiding controversy; budget limits | Cautiously open | Decision-maker | Outcome evidence; patient-affordability safeguards; low fiscal risk |\n| Mission Director, health programme | Programme targets; staff deployment | Neutral | High (implementation) | How it fits existing schemes; staffing model |\n| Finance officer | Fiscal exposure; payment terms | Sceptical | Veto on cost | Cost per bed-day vs current; payment linked to outcomes |\n| District hospital superintendent | Workload; control of the hospital | Wary | Medium | A clear governance role; no displacement of existing staff |\n\n**Three messages:**\n1. Outcomes are proven. In our two partner units, critical-care mortality fell from 21% to 15% in 2 years (independently audited).\n2. Patients pay nothing. Care is free at the point of use under the state's insurance scheme, with rates at or below the scheme tariff.\n3. The risk is low and the exit clear. A 3-unit pilot, outcome-linked payments, quarterly public reporting, and a step-in right for the state.\n\n**Likely questions and answers:**\n\n| Question | Answer | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Won't patients be charged extra?\" | No. All services at these units are under the state scheme; there is no extra billing, and that is enforced by contract with penalties. | Draft contract clause; partner-state audit |\n| \"What happens to our staff?\" | Existing staff remain government employees; we add specialists and train local staff. 40% of our partner-unit nurses were trained from the local workforce. | Staffing plan |\n| \"Why should the state pay more per bed-day?\" | Cost per bed-day is ~8% higher, but cost per survivor is lower and length of stay shorter. Payments are linked to outcome targets. | Cost-outcome analysis |\n| Hope-they-don't-ask: \"Your other-state contract had a dispute\" | Yes, over delayed payments in year 1. It was resolved via the contract's dispute mechanism; we've since built in escrow and monthly reconciliations. | Resolution summary |\n\n**Asks:** a joint working group; data access for the 3 districts; nomination of a nodal officer.\n\n**Offers:** a no-cost feasibility study; training for 50 district nurses.\n\n**Red lines:** no open-ended unfunded mandates; payment-security mechanism required.\n\n**Landmines:**\n- Don't criticise the current district staff.\n- Avoid comparisons with the previously criticised PPP by name.\n- No commitments on the tender process (procurement rules apply).\n\n**Facts at hand:** district critical-care beds (current vs norm); occupancy; our outcome data; cost per bed-day; the pilot budget.\n\n**Agenda:** 5 min context; 15 min outcomes and model; 15 min concerns and Q&A; 10 min next steps and the ask.\n\n**Pre-wire:** brief the Mission Director's office on the staffing model, and share the cost analysis with the finance officer a week before.\n\n**Follow-up:** a note within 24 hours summarising agreements, owners and dates; the draft working-group terms of reference."}]}, {"id": "red-team-review", "pack": "leader", "title": "Red-Team Review", "time": "10 min", "summary": "Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.", "tryp": "Red-team this strategy: we plan to become the #1 AI consulting partner for mid-market manufacturers in India within 3 years. [paste plan]", "when": ["Before a board, investor or client presentation", "When everyone in the room agrees too fast", "Checking your own thinking"], "inputs": ["The plan, argument or document", "Context and audience"], "pairs": ["pre-mortem", "deck-quality-check", "board-question-bank", "hypothesis-tree"], "body": "# Red-Team Review\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** the hardest questions answered before the decision-makers ask them. A plan that has survived honest attack is stronger, and you'll know exactly where it's weakest.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Steelman first.** State the plan's strongest version and what it gets right. Critique of a straw man is useless.\n2. **Attack the logic chain.** Does the recommendation follow from the evidence? Where are the leaps?\n3. **Attack the evidence.** Is it sourced, recent, relevant and independent? Is any key number untraceable or optimistic?\n4. **Surface the assumptions.** List the beliefs that must be true; test the 3-5 that matter most against base rates.\n5. **Think like the opponent.** How will competitors, regulators, customers, employees, unions or activists respond?\n6. **Second-order effects:** cannibalisation, incentive distortions, precedent, the impact on other units.\n7. **Severity-ranked, with fixes.** Critique without a priority or a path forward is noise. Each issue gets a severity and a suggested fix or test.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- What's being reviewed (strategy, case, deck, plan); the decision and the decision-maker.\n- The stakes; who prepared it; the time before the decision.\n- Which perspectives to simulate (board, investor, regulator, competitor, customer, employees, media).\n\n## Method\n1. **Steelman:** summarise the plan's argument and its best points in 4-6 lines.\n2. **Logic review:** map claim → evidence → recommendation; identify gaps and non-sequiturs.\n3. **Evidence review:** grade the key facts and numbers (see `source-validation`).\n4. **Assumption test:** list the must-be-true assumptions; compare with base rates (typical cost overruns, adoption curves, win rates, synergy realisation); flag optimism.\n5. **Missing options:** what alternatives weren't considered (including doing nothing, staging, partnering)?\n6. **Number stress:** where would a 20-30% miss on a key driver break the case? (see `sensitivity-analysis`).\n7. **Execution realism:** capacity, capabilities, timelines vs comparable programmes; dependencies.\n8. **Stakeholder and competitor response:** simulate each perspective's strongest objection or move.\n9. **Legal, regulatory, ethical, reputational exposure.** For bribery, money-laundering, tax, health-product, competition or conflict concerns, run `integrity-check`.\n10. **Failure conditions:** \"This fails if…\" (pair with `pre-mortem`).\n11. **Severity ranking:**\n    - **Fatal:** must fix before the decision.\n    - **Major:** fix or mitigate, with disclosure.\n    - **Minor:** improve.\n12. **Questions to answer** before the decision, and suggested fixes or tests.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Be hard on the argument, respectful to the people.** The aim is a better decision, not winning.\n- **Don't drown the plan in minor points;** 3-5 serious issues beat 30 nitpicks.\n- **Distinguish \"unproven\" from \"wrong.\"** Unproven assumptions need a test or staging; wrong ones need a rethink.\n- **If the plan survives,** say so clearly. Red teams that always find a fatal flaw lose credibility.\n- **Independence matters.** Ideally the reviewer wasn't involved in building the plan.\n\n## Red flags\n- No steelman.\n- A list of opinions without evidence.\n- No severity ranking or fixes.\n- Ignoring the \"do nothing\" or staging options.\n- Avoiding the uncomfortable issue everyone knows about.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Investment case:** valuation assumptions, synergy realism, integration risk, downside protection.\n- **Strategy:** competitive response, capability gaps, resource conflicts.\n- **Board paper:** clarity of the decision ask, risk disclosure, options, governance compliance.\n- **Regulatory submission:** completeness, consistency, legal interpretation, precedent.\n- **AI or technology programme:** data readiness, model risk, adoption, vendor lock-in, responsible-AI issues.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Red-Team Review: [plan], for [decision / forum]\n**Steelman:** ...\n**Verdict:** survives with fixes / needs rework / not ready\n\n| # | Issue | Category (logic / evidence / assumption / option / execution / response / legal-reputational) | Severity | Why it matters | Fix or test |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**This plan fails if:** ...\n**Questions the team must answer before the decision:** ...\n**What the plan gets right (keep):** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] A steelman is stated fairly.\n- [ ] Logic, evidence, assumptions, options, numbers, execution and responses all examined.\n- [ ] Assumptions tested against base rates.\n- [ ] Issues severity-ranked with fixes or tests; no nitpick flood.\n- [ ] A clear verdict and the questions for decision-makers.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Use judgement on interpersonal and negotiation situations. For employment, legal or HR matters, consult the appropriate professional.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `pre-mortem`: Imagine the project has failed, explain why, and fix those causes now.\n- `deck-quality-check`: A senior-partner style review of a deck: storyline, logic, evidence, numbers and visual consistency.\n- `board-question-bank`: Generate the sharp, fair questions a non-executive director should ask on any paper or topic.\n- `hypothesis-tree`: Start from a likely answer, then list exactly what must be true and how you would prove or kill it.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Red teaming and structured challenge:** devil's advocacy, steelmanning, and alternative-analysis techniques used in strategy, military and intelligence analysis.\n- **Base rates and the outside view:** reference-class forecasting; typical cost overruns, schedule slips and synergy realisation in large projects and deals.\n- **Cognitive biases in decisions:** groupthink, confirmation bias, optimism bias, sunk-cost effects (behavioural decision research).\n- **Board oversight:** directors' duty of care and constructive challenge (Companies Act 2013, Section 166 in India; corporate governance codes).\n- **Competition law:** merger control thresholds (Competition Act 2002 in India; check the current deal-value thresholds)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; a proposal to acquire a regional dairy brand for ₹1,200 Cr to enter South India)\n\n**Steelman:**\n- The acquisition gives immediate access to a trusted regional brand, 9,000 village collection points and 40,000 retail outlets in a region where building organically would take 6-8 years.\n- The price of ~1.2x revenue is in line with past regional dairy deals.\n- The plan targets ₹180 Cr of annual synergies by year 3.\n\n**Verdict:** survives with fixes, but the synergy case and the farmer-relationship risk need rework before the board.\n\n| # | Issue | Category | Severity | Why it matters | Fix or test |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Synergies of ₹180 Cr (18% of the target's ~₹1,000 Cr revenue) are above typical realisation; 60% come from \"procurement price harmonisation\" with farmers | Assumption / response | **Fatal** | Cutting farmer prices risks supply loss to cooperatives and reputational damage; the value case depends on it | Rebuild synergies excluding farmer-price cuts; test the case at 50% synergy realisation; show IRR |\n| 2 | Volume growth of 14% a year assumed vs 7-9% category growth in the region | Assumption | Major | Requires share gains against strong cooperatives | Base-case growth at category growth; share gains as upside with named levers |\n| 3 | Integration of the cold chain and IT within 12 months | Execution | Major | Comparable integrations took 18-30 months; dual running costs not budgeted | 24-month plan; add ₹35 Cr dual-running cost |\n| 4 | Promoter family's role after the deal is unclear | Execution / response | Major | Brand trust and farmer relations are personal to the family | Retention and earn-out structure for 2 key family executives |\n| 5 | The competition-law filing threshold has not been assessed | Legal | Major | Deal-value thresholds may require approval; timeline risk | Legal opinion on combination filing |\n| 6 | No alternative considered (a JV, or building one state first) | Option | Minor-Major | Staging could cut risk at a lower price | Add a staged JV option to the paper with comparative value and risk |\n| 7 | Brand health data is 3 years old | Evidence | Minor | Brand strength may have shifted | A fresh brand study before signing |\n\n**This plan fails if:** farmers defect after price harmonisation; cooperatives respond with price and procurement wars; or integration delays erode service to retailers.\n\n**Questions before the decision:**\n- What is the deal IRR without farmer-price synergies?\n- What is our farmer-retention plan?\n- What happens if a large cooperative matches our procurement prices?\n\n**What's right (keep):** strategic logic of buying distribution and trust; the reasonable entry price; the strong retail-coverage data."}]}, {"id": "negotiation-prep", "pack": "leader", "title": "Negotiation Prep", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Prepare a negotiation: interests, BATNA, ZOPA, trade-offs, opening position and concession plan.", "tryp": "Prepare me for negotiating a 3-year renewal with our cloud vendor; they want a 18% increase, our budget allows 5%.", "when": ["Vendor, client or partner negotiations", "Contract renewals and pricing talks", "Internal negotiations for budget or people"], "inputs": ["What is being negotiated and the stakes", "Your goals, limits and alternatives", "What you know about the other side"], "pairs": ["meeting-brief", "stakeholder-map", "pricing-strategy", "red-team-review"], "body": "# Negotiation Prep\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** clarity on what you want, what they want, when to walk away, and which trades create value for both sides, so you negotiate from preparation rather than pressure.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Interests, not positions.** A position is what they ask for; an interest is why. Deals are built on interests.\n2. **BATNA is your power.** Your best alternative to a negotiated agreement sets your walk-away. Improve it before you negotiate; estimate theirs honestly.\n3. **Know your numbers:** reservation point (walk-away), target (ambitious but justifiable), opening (anchor, defensible).\n4. **Many issues, not one.** Price alone is a tug-of-war. Add issues (term, volume, payment, scope, service levels, exclusivity, risk-sharing) and trade on different valuations.\n5. **Objective criteria:** market benchmarks, precedents, cost data and expert standards make terms defensible and reduce ego.\n6. **Plan concessions:** make them small and decreasing, always in exchange, never unilateral.\n7. **Ethics and authority:** no misrepresentation; know your mandate and who signs.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- What's being negotiated; deadlines; history; relationship importance (one-off vs long-term).\n- Both parties: decision-makers, influencers, constraints, internal politics.\n- Information you have and need; what you can share.\n- Legal and regulatory constraints (competition law, procurement rules, employment law).\n\n## Method\n1. **Interests:** ours and theirs (business, personal, organisational); rank them.\n2. **BATNA:** ours (concrete and valued) and theirs (estimated); actions to strengthen ours and test theirs.\n3. **Numbers:** reservation point, target, opening anchor, with a justification for each.\n4. **ZOPA:** the overlap between our reservation point and theirs (estimated); if there's none, rethink or walk.\n5. **Issues and valuations:** issue | value to us (high / medium / low) | estimated value to them → trade the low-to-us / high-to-them items for the reverse.\n6. **Creative options:** contingent terms (earn-outs, performance pricing), phasing, risk-sharing, bundles.\n7. **Objective standards:** benchmarks and precedents for key terms.\n8. **Concession plan:** sequence; size; what to ask in return; \"nothing is agreed until everything is agreed\".\n9. **Team and roles:** lead, analyst, note-taker; authority limits; escalation path.\n10. **Process:** agenda, information exchange, first-offer strategy (anchor if well informed), how to handle deadlocks (break, reframe, escalate).\n11. **Relationship and culture:** communication style, face-saving, hierarchy, decision pace.\n12. **Walk-away rule:** a specific condition.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Anchor first** when you know the range well; let them go first when uncertain.\n- **Deadlines create pressure;** be careful about revealing yours.\n- **Share information strategically:** interests and constraints often help; reservation points rarely do.\n- **Long-term relationships:** fairness matters. A \"win\" that damages trust may cost more later.\n- **Multi-party negotiations:** map coalitions; sequence conversations.\n- **Emotion:** separate people from the problem; acknowledge concerns; take breaks.\n\n## Red flags\n- No BATNA, or a weak one not improved.\n- Single-issue price focus.\n- An opening with no justification.\n- Unplanned concessions; negotiating beyond authority.\n- Misrepresentation; competition-law-sensitive information exchanged.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Vendor or procurement:** total cost of ownership, service levels, exit terms, price indexation, liability caps.\n- **Sales / key account:** value quantification, multi-year terms, volume tiers, references.\n- **Partnership / JV:** governance, contributions, IP, exit and deadlock clauses.\n- **M&A:** valuation range, structure (cash / stock / earn-out), warranties and indemnities, conditions.\n- **Employment / compensation:** total package, role scope, growth, flexibility; market data.\n- **Settlement / dispute:** litigation cost and risk-weighted outcomes as the BATNA; confidentiality.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Negotiation Prep: [deal], with [counterparty]\n**Our interests (ranked):** ... **Their interests (estimated):** ...\n**BATNA:** ours ... (value) | theirs ... (estimated) | strengthen ours by ...\n**Numbers:** reservation __ | target __ | opening __ (justification)\n**ZOPA (estimated):** ...\n\n| Issue | Value to us | Value to them | Trade idea |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Creative options:** ...\n**Objective standards:** ...\n**Concession plan:** ...\n**Team and authority:** ...\n**Process and deadlock tactics:** ...\n**Walk-away rule:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Interests for both sides; BATNAs valued, with a strengthening plan.\n- [ ] Reservation, target and opening justified; ZOPA estimated.\n- [ ] Multiple issues with differential valuations and trades.\n- [ ] Objective standards; concession plan; authority defined.\n- [ ] Ethics and legal limits; a clear walk-away rule.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Use judgement on interpersonal and negotiation situations. For employment, legal or HR matters, consult the appropriate professional.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `meeting-brief`: A one-page brief before any important meeting: objective, people, positions, your asks and your lines.\n- `stakeholder-map`: Map who matters, where they stand, and the plan to move each of them.\n- `pricing-strategy`: Set or reset prices using value, cost and competition, with tiers and a test plan.\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Principled negotiation:** interests vs positions, BATNA, objective criteria, options for mutual gain, as taught in negotiation curricula.\n- **Distributive concepts:** reservation point, target, anchoring, the zone of possible agreement (ZOPA).\n- **Integrative bargaining:** trading across issues with different valuations; contingent agreements.\n- **Cross-cultural negotiation:** communication context, hierarchy and relationship norms.\n- **Legal limits:** competition law on information exchange (Competition Act 2002 in India); contract law; public procurement rules where the counterparty is government."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; a mid-sized retailer renegotiating a 3-year cloud and IT-managed-services contract, current spend ₹48 Cr a year)\n\n**Our interests (ranked):**\n1. Cut the run cost by ≥15%.\n2. Improve uptime during sale events.\n3. Flexibility to move workloads (avoid lock-in).\n4. Stable account team.\n\n**Their interests (estimated):**\n- Keep a marquee retail reference.\n- Multi-year revenue visibility.\n- Expand into data and analytics services.\n- Protect margin.\n\n**BATNA:**\n- **Ours:** a credible offer from an alternative provider at ₹41 Cr a year, plus a one-time migration cost of ₹9 Cr and 6 months of transition risk. Spread over 3 years, that is about ₹44 Cr a year all-in.\n- **Theirs:** losing ₹48 Cr a year of revenue and a reference customer; their pipeline to replace it is uncertain.\n- **Strengthen ours:** complete a migration-readiness assessment, and get the alternative's offer in writing.\n\n**Numbers (annual):**\n- **Reservation:** ₹44 Cr. Above this, the switch is cheaper.\n- **Target:** ₹40 Cr (−17%), justified by benchmark rates for comparable workloads and our volume growth.\n- **Opening:** ₹37.5 Cr, citing the benchmark range and committed growth.\n\n**ZOPA (estimated):** their likely floor is ~₹39-40 Cr given margin norms, so the zone is about ₹39-44 Cr.\n\n| Issue | Value to us | Value to them | Trade idea |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Annual price | High | High | Core |\n| Contract term (3 → 5 years) | Low-medium (if there's a price-review clause) | High | Give 5 years in exchange for price and an annual benchmarking right |\n| Sale-event uptime SLA (99.95%) with service credits | High | Medium (cost to them) | Ask; offer an advance capacity forecast |\n| Data-portability and exit-assistance clause | High | Low-medium | Must-have; low cost to them |\n| Analytics pilot (paid, ₹2 Cr) | Medium | High | Offer in exchange for the price target |\n| Case study / reference | Low | High | Give in exchange for SLA credits |\n| Payment terms (60 → 45 days) | Low | Medium | Concede late if needed |\n\n**Creative options:** volume-tiered pricing; a gain-share on savings from optimisation; a 2-year price lock with benchmarking in year 3.\n\n**Objective standards:** published rate cards for comparable services; an independent benchmarking study; our historical utilisation data.\n\n**Concession plan:**\n1. Term extension.\n2. Reference rights.\n3. Analytics pilot.\n4. Payment terms.\n\nEach is given only in exchange for movement on price, the SLA or the exit clause.\n\n**Team and authority:** CIO leads; CFO approves the final price (authority up to ₹42 Cr); procurement drafts; legal reviews the exit and liability terms.\n\n**Process:**\n- Share the benchmark and our growth plan early; don't reveal the alternative's exact price.\n- **On deadlock:** reframe to total value (SLA plus pilot), or escalate to the provider's regional head.\n\n**Walk-away rule:** if total annual cost exceeds ₹44 Cr, or the exit-assistance clause is refused, proceed with the alternative provider."}]}, {"id": "first-90-days", "pack": "leader", "title": "First 90 Days Plan", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Plan a new leadership role: diagnose, build relationships, secure early wins, and set direction.", "tryp": "I'm starting as Head of Data and AI at a mid-size insurer in 3 weeks. Build my 90-day plan.", "when": ["Starting a new role or job", "Taking over a team or function", "Joining a board"], "inputs": ["The role, organisation and mandate", "What you know about the situation", "Your boss or board's expectations"], "pairs": ["stakeholder-map", "meeting-brief", "operating-model", "implementation-roadmap"], "body": "# First 90 Days Plan\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a plan to learn fast, build the right relationships, secure early credibility and agree what success means, avoiding the common traps of acting too soon, or too late.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Diagnose the situation first.** A turnaround needs speed and tough calls; sustaining success needs restraint and learning. The same playbook fails in the wrong situation.\n2. **Agree the mandate with your boss:** expectations, success measures, resources, style, timing. Re-check at 30 and 60 days.\n3. **Learn systematically.** Structured listening across levels and functions, plus data. Know what you need to learn, from whom.\n4. **Early wins build credibility,** but choose ones that matter to the business and model the behaviours you want, not quick wins that signal the wrong things.\n5. **Relationships are the work:** the boss, peers, key team members, board or investors, customers, unions or works councils where relevant.\n6. **Team decisions are deliberate.** Assess by around 60-90 days; act decisively on clear cases; be fair and respectful.\n7. **Avoid the classic traps:** sticking with what worked before; coming in with \"the answer\"; neglecting horizontal relationships; trying to do too much.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Role, mandate, reporting lines, scope, resources.\n- Business situation: start-up / turnaround / realignment / sustaining success (possibly a mix by unit).\n- What's known: performance data, strategy documents, prior assessments, why the role is open.\n- Your transition type: internal promotion, external hire, cross-function, international.\n\n## Method\n1. **Situation diagnosis:** situation type by unit; implications for pace and style.\n2. **Expectations conversation with the boss:** the situation, expectations, style, resources, personal development. Write down the agreement.\n3. **Learning agenda:** key questions on strategy, performance, customers, operations, people, culture and politics; sources (data, documents, people).\n4. **Listening tour:** whom (team, peers, frontline, customers, board members, partners); 5 consistent questions (below); synthesise themes at days 30 and 60.\n5. **Stakeholder map:** influence and support; coalition-building plan.\n6. **Early wins (2-4):** visible, meaningful, achievable within 90 days, aligned with the longer agenda; behaviour-modelling.\n7. **Team assessment:** competence, judgement, energy, focus, relationships, trust; keep / develop / move / replace; timing; respectful process.\n8. **30-60-90 plan:**\n   - **Days 1-30:** learn, relationships, quick fixes.\n   - **Days 31-60:** a diagnosis shared, priorities, early-win delivery, team decisions forming.\n   - **Days 61-90:** strategy and plan agreed, team changes, operating cadence.\n9. **Operating cadence:** team meetings, reviews, communication rhythm.\n10. **Personal management:** energy, advice network, reflection; how to get feedback.\n11. **Check-ins:** with the boss at 30, 60 and 90 days; adjust the plan.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Turnaround:** move faster: stop the bleeding (cash, key customers, safety) in weeks, with a smaller listening tour.\n- **Sustaining success:** go slower; learn why it works before changing anything; protect what's working.\n- **Internal promotion:** reset relationships with former peers; signal the new role clearly.\n- **Outsider:** culture learning is critical; find \"cultural interpreters\"; respect history.\n- **Inherited commitments** (budgets, promises): understand them before reversing; be explicit about what changes.\n\n## Red flags\n- Prescribing before diagnosing.\n- No explicit expectations agreement with the boss.\n- Early wins that are cosmetic or contradict the desired culture.\n- Avoiding team decisions past 90-120 days.\n- Ignoring peers and informal influencers.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **CEO:** the board relationship, investor messaging, top-team composition, strategy refresh timing.\n- **CFO / CRO / CHRO:** controls and compliance health check early; key-risk review.\n- **Business-unit head:** P&L drivers, customer visits, frontline time.\n- **Post-acquisition leader:** integration plan, retention of key talent, culture bridging.\n- **Public-sector or regulated role:** statutory duties, stakeholder protocols, political environment.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## First 90 Days: [role], [organisation], start [date]\n**Situation diagnosis:** ... → implications for pace and style\n**Mandate and expectations (agreed with [boss]):** ...\n**Learning agenda:** key questions | sources\n**Listening tour:** whom | 5 core questions\n**Stakeholder map:** ...\n**Early wins:** 1. ... 2. ...\n**Team assessment approach and timing:** ...\n\n| Phase | Objectives | Key actions | Milestones |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Days 1-30 | | | |\n| 31-60 | | | |\n| 61-90 | | | |\n\n**Operating cadence:** ... **Pitfalls to avoid:** ... **Check-ins:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Situation diagnosed, with implications.\n- [ ] An expectations agreement with the boss, revisited at 30 and 60 days.\n- [ ] A learning agenda and listening tour with consistent questions.\n- [ ] Meaningful early wins; team assessment criteria and timing.\n- [ ] 30-60-90 milestones, a cadence and pitfalls.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Use judgement on interpersonal and negotiation situations. For employment, legal or HR matters, consult the appropriate professional.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `stakeholder-map`: Map who matters, where they stand, and the plan to move each of them.\n- `meeting-brief`: A one-page brief before any important meeting: objective, people, positions, your asks and your lines.\n- `operating-model`: Design how the organisation will work: structure, processes, people, technology, governance and locations.\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Leadership transitions:** situational diagnosis (start-up, turnaround, realignment, sustaining success), securing early wins, aligning expectations and building coalitions, as taught in executive-transition programmes.\n- **Stakeholder and coalition mapping:** influence and support analysis.\n- **Team assessment:** competence, judgement, energy, focus, relationships and trust as common evaluation dimensions.\n- **Organisational culture:** learning culture through listening, artefacts and behaviours.\n- **Employment and industrial-relations law:** consult HR and legal on team changes and union engagement (Industrial Relations Code 2020 in India, where applicable)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; an external hire as Chief Operating Officer of a ₹3,000 Cr consumer-durables maker; 3 plants, margins down 4 points in 2 years)\n\n**Situation diagnosis:**\n- **Operations:** a turnaround (cost, quality complaints up 60%, on-time delivery 78%).\n- **R&D and new products:** sustaining success (two strong launches).\n\n**Implications:**\n- Move fast on plant performance and quality.\n- Learn before touching the R&D processes.\n\n**Expectations agreed with the CEO:**\n- Restore 2 points of gross margin within 12 months.\n- On-time delivery above 92% in 9 months.\n- No change to the product roadmap.\n- Monthly one-to-ones.\n- Board update at day 90.\n- **Resources:** a budget for 2 senior hires.\n\n**Learning agenda:**\n- Where exactly is margin lost (materials, yield, freight, warranty)?\n- Why did quality fall (supplier change, a new line, skills)?\n- What do dealers say?\n- How do the plant heads and the union see the issues?\n\n**Listening tour (35 conversations):**\n- People: 3 plant heads, the quality head, procurement, the supply-chain team, 10 frontline supervisors, 2 union representatives, 6 dealers, 4 key suppliers, the CFO, the sales head, 2 board members.\n- **Five questions:**\n  1. What's working well?\n  2. What's getting in the way?\n  3. What would you change first?\n  4. What should I not change?\n  5. Who else should I talk to?\n\n**Early wins:**\n1. A daily quality-escalation huddle at all plants, with a 48-hour root-cause rule. It is visible, it models a fact-based culture, and it targets complaints.\n2. Clear the 6-week backlog of dealer warranty claims, which rebuilds dealer trust.\n3. Renegotiate the top 10 freight lanes (a quick ₹8-10 Cr annual saving).\n\n**Team assessment (by day 75):**\n- 3 plant heads, plus the heads of quality, procurement and supply chain.\n- **Criteria:** competence, judgement, ownership, collaboration.\n- Decisions communicated respectfully, with HR.\n\n| Phase | Objectives | Key actions | Milestones |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Days 1-30 | Learn; stabilise quality | Listening tour; plant walks; margin bridge with Finance; launch the quality huddles | Margin bridge done; huddles live at all plants |\n| 31-60 | Diagnose; first results | Share the diagnosis with the CEO and team; freight renegotiation; warranty backlog cleared; supplier quality audits | Diagnosis agreed; freight savings contracted |\n| 61-90 | Plan and team | 12-month operations plan (yield, procurement, network); team decisions; KPI cadence | Board update; plan approved; team changes announced |\n\n**Operating cadence:**\n- Weekly operations review (safety, quality, delivery, cost).\n- Monthly plant performance dialogues.\n- Quarterly town halls.\n\n**Pitfalls to avoid:**\n- Importing the previous employer's lean system wholesale.\n- Bypassing plant heads with the union.\n- Changing R&D processes.\n\n**Check-ins:** with the CEO at days 30, 60 and 90; a dealer pulse survey at day 75."}]}, {"id": "executive-email", "pack": "leader", "title": "Executive Email", "time": "5 min", "summary": "Write a short email or message that gets a senior person to read, decide and reply.", "tryp": "Write an email to our CEO asking her to approve hiring 6 people for the AI programme before the hiring freeze on 1 Dec.", "when": ["Any email to a busy senior person", "Escalations", "Requests needing a quick decision"], "inputs": ["What you need and from whom", "Key facts", "Deadline and tone"], "pairs": ["pyramid-structure", "slide-copy-editor", "executive-summary", "meeting-brief"], "body": "# Executive Email\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** messages a busy leader can act on from the preview pane: the point and the ask first, nothing they have to dig for, and nothing you'd regret if it were forwarded.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Purpose and action first.** Before writing, know the one thing the reader should do or know. If it's \"nothing\", reconsider sending.\n2. **The subject line is the summary:** \"Decision needed by Thu: approve ₹2.4 Cr for pilot extension\", not \"Update\".\n3. **Bottom line up front.** The first two lines carry the ask or conclusion and the deadline.\n4. **Short and scannable.** 3-5 short paragraphs or bullets; key numbers in bold sparingly; under ~200 words for most messages.\n5. **Decisions come with options and a recommendation,** plus the consequence of not deciding.\n6. **Explicit next steps:** who does what by when.\n7. **Assume it will be forwarded.** Tone, facts and sensitive information should survive being read by anyone.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The reader(s): role, what they care about, how they read (mobile? between meetings?), relationship.\n- The purpose: inform, request a decision, escalate, persuade, deliver bad news, thank, introduce.\n- Deadline and urgency; who else needs to be copied.\n- Sensitivity: confidential, legal, personnel, price-sensitive, regulatory.\n\n## Method\n1. **Purpose and action** in one sentence.\n2. **Subject:** [Action / FYI] + topic + deadline.\n3. **Opening (BLUF):** the ask or conclusion + why it matters + the deadline.\n4. **Supporting points (2-4):** context, key evidence and numbers, risks.\n5. **For decisions:** options (2-3) with a one-line trade-off each; the recommendation; the consequence of a delay.\n6. **Next steps:** owners and dates; what you'll do after their reply.\n7. **Tone:** direct, respectful, calm. No hedging piles (\"just\", \"I think maybe\"); no blame; apologise once if warranted.\n8. **Sensitive content:** move it to a call where appropriate; mark \"privileged and confidential\" only when legal counsel is involved and the label is warranted; avoid speculation in writing.\n9. **Edit:** cut 30%; one idea per paragraph; check numbers, names and dates; check attachments are referenced; the mobile preview test.\n10. **Recipients:** To (action) vs Cc (inform); avoid reply-all storms; Bcc only for legitimate privacy reasons.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Bad news:** say it plainly in the first line, with impact, cause (brief), the fix and the ask; don't bury it.\n- **Escalations:** factual, non-accusatory; what you've tried; the decision or help needed; the deadline.\n- **Persuasion:** lead with the reader's interest, not yours.\n- **Email vs call:** complex, emotional or contentious matters often need a call first, with email as the confirmation.\n- **Cultural context:** some audiences expect a brief courtesy opening; keep it to one line.\n\n## Red flags\n- Vague subject lines.\n- The ask in paragraph four.\n- Walls of text; multiple unrelated topics.\n- Decisions requested without options or deadlines.\n- Emotional tone; speculative statements about people or legal matters.\n- Wrong recipients.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Decision request:** an options table (2-3 rows) and a recommendation.\n- **Board or senior update:** 3 headlines, red / amber / green, decisions needed, risks.\n- **Meeting follow-up:** decisions made, actions with owners and dates, open issues.\n- **Introduction:** why the two should meet, what each gains, a suggested next step.\n- **Saying no:** appreciation, a clear no, the reason (brief), an alternative if possible.\n- **Messaging apps or short notes:** two lines: the point and the ask.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n**To:** ... **Cc:** ...\n**Subject:** [Action / FYI]: [topic], [deadline]\n\n[BLUF: ask / conclusion + why + deadline]\n\n[Supporting points, 2-4 short bullets or lines, with key numbers]\n\n[If decision: Options: A ... / B ... → Recommend A because ... If no decision by [date]: ...]\n\n**Next steps:** [owner, action, date]\n\n[Sign-off]\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] The subject carries the topic, ask and deadline.\n- [ ] The first two lines contain the point and the ask.\n- [ ] Short, scannable, numbers checked; under ~200 words unless necessary.\n- [ ] Options and a recommendation for decisions; explicit next steps.\n- [ ] Tone safe to forward; sensitive content handled; recipients right.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Use judgement on interpersonal and negotiation situations. For employment, legal or HR matters, consult the appropriate professional.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `pyramid-structure`: Structure any argument top-down: answer first, grouped support, evidence below.\n- `slide-copy-editor`: Tighten slide text: fewer words, clearer claims, consistent style, no jargon.\n- `executive-summary`: Write a one-page executive summary a busy leader can act on in two minutes.\n- `meeting-brief`: A one-page brief before any important meeting: objective, people, positions, your asks and your lines.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Bottom line up front (BLUF):** a communication convention from military and government writing, widely taught in business-writing courses.\n- **Plain-language principles:** short sentences, active voice, front-loaded meaning (plain-language guidelines used by governments).\n- **Answer-first structures:** see the `pyramid-structure` and `scqa-storyline` skills.\n- **Records and privilege:** emails are discoverable records; legal privilege depends on the involvement of counsel and the purpose, not on labels alone. Seek legal advice.\n- **Disclosure:** listed companies must avoid selective disclosure of price-sensitive information (SEBI PIT Regulations in India)."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked examples (illustrative)\n\n## 1. A decision request to the CFO\n\n**Before (draft):**\n- **Subject:** Pilot update\n- **Body:** a 420-word message, opening with the pilot history; the ask appears in paragraph 5.\n\n**After:**\n\n**Subject:** Decision by Thu 14 Nov: extend the AI contract-review pilot (₹2.4 Cr) or stop\n\nAnil, I need your approval by Thursday to extend the contract-review pilot by 6 months at ₹2.4 Cr. It cut review time by 39% with no rise in missed clauses, and the vendor's discounted rate expires on Friday.\n\n- **Results (12 weeks, 1,840 contracts):** review time 5.1 → 3.1 hours; error rate flat at 1.8%; lawyer satisfaction 4.2 / 5.\n- **Extension scope:** add procurement contracts; build the integration with our contract system.\n- **Risk:** vendor lock-in. The extension includes a data-export clause.\n\n**Options:**\n- **A.** Extend 6 months at ₹2.4 Cr (recommended).\n- **B.** Pause and re-tender. That loses the 20% discount and adds about 3 months.\n- **C.** Stop. The ~₹6 Cr a year of capacity benefit is not realised.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- With your approval, Procurement signs by Friday.\n- I'll report results monthly in the ops review.\n\nThanks, Priya\n\n*(about 150 words)*\n\n## 2. Bad news to the CEO\n\n**Subject:** Plant 2 line stoppage: 4-day delay to the festive shipment; recovery plan attached\n\nRakesh, Line 3 at Plant 2 is down after a gearbox failure. Festive shipments to North dealers will be about 4 days late, putting roughly ₹18 Cr of October revenue at risk.\n\n- **Cause:** a bearing failure. The spare was not in stock because of a supplier delay. Investigation underway.\n- **Recovery:** a replacement arrives Wednesday; overtime on Lines 1-2; 30% of volume diverted to Plant 1. Expected recovery is about ₹13 Cr of the ₹18 Cr within October.\n- **Dealer communication:** the sales team calls the top 50 dealers today.\n\n**No decision is needed now.** I'll update you Wednesday evening.\n\nIf you'd like to speak to the plant head directly, I'll set up a call.\n\n— Meera"}]}, {"id": "workshop-design", "pack": "leader", "title": "Workshop Design", "time": "15 min", "summary": "Design a workshop or offsite that produces decisions, not just discussion.", "tryp": "Design a one-day leadership offsite to agree our 3-year AI strategy priorities with 14 executives.", "when": ["Strategy offsites", "Discovery or design workshops", "Team alignment and kick-offs"], "inputs": ["Purpose and the outputs you need", "Participants and roles", "Time, place (in-person or virtual)"], "pairs": ["ai-opportunity-scan", "pre-mortem", "change-plan", "meeting-brief"], "body": "# Workshop Design\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a session that uses senior people's time to make real decisions, with everyone heard, the hard topics surfaced, and commitments that survive the next Monday.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Outcomes and decisions first.** Define the 2-4 outputs (decisions, prioritised lists, commitments) before choosing any activity.\n2. **Pre-work does the heavy lifting.** Facts, options and data are shared before; the room is for judgement and alignment.\n3. **Diverge → converge → decide → commit.** Open up ideas, narrow systematically, decide with an agreed rule, then commit owners and dates.\n4. **Decision rights are explicit.** Who decides: the CEO after input, consensus, majority, or consent (\"no strong objection\")? Agree this upfront.\n5. **Design for candour.** Silent individual work before discussion; small groups; anonymous inputs for sensitive topics; a facilitator who manages dominant voices.\n6. **Time is the scarcest resource.** Timed agenda; buffers; a parking lot; ruthless prioritisation.\n7. **Follow-through is designed in:** actions, owners, dates, a communication plan, and a check-in date.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Purpose and context: why now; the decisions needed; what's been tried.\n- Participants: who must be there (decision-makers, implementers, dissenters), number, dynamics, hierarchy.\n- Duration, format (in-person, hybrid), location.\n- Sensitivities: politics, conflicts, change fatigue, confidentiality.\n\n## Method\n1. **Outcomes:** decisions and outputs (e.g. \"agree top 3 priorities for next year with owners\").\n2. **Participants and roles:** sponsor, facilitator, participants, note-taker, subject experts.\n3. **Pre-work:** a fact base, options, a pre-survey (anonymous views on key questions), one-page pre-reads.\n4. **Agenda flow:**\n   - Opening: purpose, outcomes, decision rules, norms.\n   - Context: the shared fact base, with brief Q&A.\n   - Diverge: idea generation (silent brainstorm, small groups).\n   - Converge: clustering, criteria-based scoring, dot voting.\n   - Decide: per the decision rule.\n   - Commit: owners, dates, resources.\n   - Close: summary, communication, check-in.\n5. **Methods by purpose:**\n   - Ideas: silent writing, then sharing.\n   - Prioritisation: criteria scoring, impact-effort matrix.\n   - Risks: pre-mortem.\n   - Alignment: a \"where do we stand\" spectrum exercise.\n   - Conflict: structured debate with assigned positions.\n6. **Timings:** minutes per block; breaks; 10-15% buffer.\n7. **Facilitation:**\n   - Opening norms.\n   - Round-robins; time-boxed speaking.\n   - A devil's advocate role.\n   - Anonymous polling for sensitive topics.\n   - A parking lot.\n8. **Materials and set-up:** room layout, wall space, templates, polling tools, hybrid equipment.\n9. **Risks and contingencies:** what if the CEO dominates? A key person is absent? Time overruns? Conflict escalates?\n10. **Follow-through:** a written summary within 48 hours; an action log; the communication cascade; a 30-day check-in.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **If the decision is already made,** don't fake consultation. Run a \"how to implement\" session instead.\n- **Senior leaders speak last** on contentious topics, to avoid anchoring.\n- **Hybrid sessions:** remote participants first in discussions; a digital whiteboard for all; shorter blocks.\n- **Conflict is useful** when about ideas; intervene when it becomes personal.\n- **Energy management:** hard decisions before lunch; interactive blocks after.\n\n## Red flags\n- No defined outcomes; an agenda of presentations.\n- Pre-reads at the meeting.\n- Decision rule unclear until the end.\n- Dominant voices unchecked.\n- No owners or dates at the close; no follow-up.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Strategy offsite:** the fact base and strategic options pre-shared; choices and trade-offs; resource allocation.\n- **Annual planning:** priorities, targets, budget envelopes, dependencies.\n- **Problem-solving workshop:** problem statement, root causes, solution options, a pilot plan.\n- **Design sprint:** user problem, sketches, prototype, test plan.\n- **Retrospective / lessons learned:** blameless format; what worked, what didn't, what we'll change.\n- **Board-management retreat:** strategy, risk appetite, talent; independence norms.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Workshop Design: [name], [date, duration, format]\n**Outcomes / decisions:** 1. ... 2. ...\n**Participants and roles:** ...\n**Decision rule:** ...\n**Pre-work (due [date]):** ...\n\n| Time | Block | Purpose | Method | Output | Lead |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Facilitation notes and norms:** ...\n**Materials and set-up:** ...\n**Risks and contingencies:** ...\n**Follow-through:** summary by __; action log; cascade; check-in on __\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Outcomes and decisions defined; the decision rule agreed in advance.\n- [ ] Pre-work moves facts out of the room.\n- [ ] The flow diverges → converges → decides → commits, with methods matched to purpose.\n- [ ] Facilitation for candour and balance; timings with buffers.\n- [ ] Contingencies and follow-through with owners and dates.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Use judgement on interpersonal and negotiation situations. For employment, legal or HR matters, consult the appropriate professional.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-opportunity-scan`: Find where AI can create real value in a function or business, grounded in processes and pain points.\n- `pre-mortem`: Imagine the project has failed, explain why, and fix those causes now.\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n- `meeting-brief`: A one-page brief before any important meeting: objective, people, positions, your asks and your lines.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Facilitation practice:** diverge-converge structures, silent brainstorming, nominal group technique, dot voting, as taught in facilitation and design-thinking curricula.\n- **Group decision-making:** decision rules (consensus, consent, consultative, majority); avoiding groupthink and anchoring.\n- **Psychological safety:** creating conditions for candour in teams (organisational-behaviour research).\n- **Design sprints and retrospectives:** time-boxed, structured team methods from product development and agile practice.\n- **Pre-mortems:** see the `pre-mortem` skill."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; a 1.5-day strategy offsite for the 12-member leadership team of an NBFC; ₹18,000 Cr AUM)\n\n**Outcomes:**\n1. Agree the 3 strategic priorities for the next 3 years.\n2. Agree which 2 of 5 candidate initiatives to stop or defer.\n3. Assign owners and 100-day milestones.\n4. Agree the board messaging.\n\n**Participants:**\n- The CEO (sponsor and final decision-maker).\n- 11 leadership-team members.\n- An external facilitator.\n- A note-taker from the strategy office.\n- The CRO as the formal \"risk challenger\".\n\n**Decision rule:** the CEO decides after structured input. Priorities need no strong objection from the CRO or CFO on risk or capital grounds; objections are recorded.\n\n**Pre-work (due 1 week before):**\n- A 12-page fact base: performance, peers, customer trends, regulation, capital.\n- 5 initiative one-pagers (with value, cost, risk).\n- An anonymous pre-survey: top priorities, and what we should stop.\n\n| Time | Block | Purpose | Method | Output | Lead |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Day 1** 9:00-9:30 | Opening | Outcomes, decision rule, norms | Sponsor framing | Shared contract | CEO / facilitator |\n| 9:30-10:30 | Fact base | Shared reality | Pre-read Q&A only (no presenting); pre-survey results shown | Agreed facts; open questions | Strategy head |\n| 10:45-12:15 | Diverge | Future priorities | Silent individual writing (10 min), then 3 small groups | 15-20 candidate priorities | Facilitator |\n| 13:00-14:30 | Converge | Narrow down | Clustering; scoring on value, capability fit, risk, capital need | Top 6 | Facilitator |\n| 14:45-16:15 | Challenge | Stress-test the top 6 | Pre-mortem per priority; CRO challenge | Risks; refined list | CRO |\n| 16:30-17:30 | Initiative stop / defer | Free resources | Impact-effort plot of 5 initiatives; anonymous poll | Proposed 2 to stop | Facilitator |\n| **Day 2** 9:00-10:30 | Decide | Final 3 priorities and 2 stops | Positions stated (senior members last); CEO decides; objections recorded | Decisions | CEO |\n| 10:45-12:00 | Commit | Owners, 100-day milestones, resources | Template per priority | Commitments | Owners |\n| 12:00-12:30 | Close | Board messaging, communication cascade | Draft key messages | Communication plan | CEO |\n\n**Buffer:** 15 minutes built into each half-day.\n\n**Facilitation notes:**\n- Phones away except during breaks.\n- Round-robins on contentious items.\n- The CEO speaks last in the decide block.\n- A parking lot for operational issues.\n\n**Risks and contingencies:**\n- **The debate overruns:** drop the board-messaging block (do it in the following week).\n- **Conflict between Retail and SME heads over capital:** structured debate with criteria.\n- **A key member is absent:** their delegate gets a pre-briefing.\n\n**Follow-through:**\n- Written decisions within 48 hours.\n- An action log in the monthly leadership review.\n- Cascade to the top 100 managers within 2 weeks.\n- A 30-day check-in."}]}, {"id": "start-here", "pack": "playbooks", "title": "Start Here: Toolkit Guide", "time": "2 min", "summary": "Not sure which skill to use? Describe your situation and get the right skills in the right order.", "tryp": "I have a board meeting in 2 weeks to propose a new digital lending business. Which skills should I use and in what order?", "when": ["First time using the toolkit", "Unsure which skill fits your task", "Wanting a guided sequence for a bigger piece of work"], "inputs": ["What you are trying to achieve, for whom, and by when", "What you already have (data, drafts, notes)"], "pairs": ["playbook-strategy-review", "playbook-board-pack", "playbook-investment-case", "playbook-ai-roadmap"], "body": "# Start Here: Toolkit Guide\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** the shortest sensible path from your situation to a finished, decision-ready output, using the right skills in the right order, without having to learn all 84 first.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Start from the decision, not the tool.** Ask what decision or output is needed, for whom, by when. Then choose skills.\n2. **Fewest steps that do the job.** A one-off email needs one skill; a board-ready investment case needs a playbook. Don't over-process.\n3. **Sequence matters:** frame → evidence → analysis → story → delivery. Skipping framing is the most common cause of wasted work.\n4. **Each step produces something usable** that feeds the next; say what it is.\n5. **Be honest about limits.** Flag where legal, financial, regulatory, clinical or technical experts must review, and where facts must be verified.\n6. **Start immediately.** After recommending a path, offer to begin step 1 with what the user has already shared.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\nAsk (or infer from what's shared) up to five things:\n1. **The situation:** what's going on, in a sentence or two.\n2. **The decision or output needed:** a decision, a document, a deck, a plan, a meeting, a message.\n3. **The audience:** board, CEO, investors, regulator, team, customers.\n4. **The deadline** and the time available.\n5. **The material available:** data, documents, notes, prior work.\n\nIf the request is clear, skip the questions and recommend directly.\n\n## Method\n1. **Restate the need** in one line (decision, audience, deadline).\n2. **Choose the route:**\n   - **Single skill:** a narrow, well-defined task (e.g. an email, a chart choice, a SWOT).\n   - **Short chain (2-5 skills):** a moderate task (e.g. a market-sizing memo).\n   - **Playbook:** an end-to-end deliverable (a strategy review, board pack, investment case, AI roadmap, market entry).\n3. **Recommend steps:** skill → what it produces → why it's next. Mark optional steps.\n4. **Flag checks:** fact verification (`source-validation`), stress tests (`red-team-review`, `pre-mortem`), legal and ethical red lines (`integrity-check`), expert review.\n5. **Offer to start** step 1 now, and list what you need from the user for it.\n\n## The toolkit map (84 skills, 11 packs)\n- **Frame the problem:** `problem-statement`, `audience-brief`, `issue-tree`, `hypothesis-tree`, `mece-check`, `stakeholder-map`, `engagement-workplan`.\n- **Gather evidence:** `research-plan`, `source-validation`, `research-synthesis`, `interview-guide`, `interview-synthesis`, `survey-design`, `survey-analysis`.\n- **Understand the market:** `market-sizing`, `customer-segmentation`, `competitor-benchmark`, `five-forces`, `pestel-scan`, `value-chain`, `jobs-to-be-done`, `pricing-strategy`.\n- **Shape strategy:** `swot-tows`, `vrio-analysis`, `growth-options`, `strategic-options`, `portfolio-matrix`, `scenario-planning`, `build-buy-partner`.\n- **Do the numbers:** `business-case`, `financial-model-spec`, `unit-economics`, `sensitivity-analysis`, `break-even-payback`, `kpi-driver-tree`, `kpi-dashboard`, `cost-takeout`.\n- **Tell the story:** `scqa-storyline`, `pyramid-structure`, `executive-summary`, `insight-synthesis`, `action-titles`, `ghost-deck`, `chart-selection`, `exhibit-builder`, `slide-copy-editor`, `deck-quality-check`, `speaker-notes`, `decision-memo`.\n- **Deliver change:** `implementation-roadmap`, `operating-model`, `raci-builder`, `change-plan`, `risk-assessment`, `pre-mortem`, `steerco-update`, `benefits-tracker`.\n- **Boardroom and governance:** `board-paper`, `board-question-bank`, `ai-governance-review`, `ai-risk-register`, `risk-appetite`, `integrity-check`, `esg-materiality`, `brsr-readiness`.\n- **AI strategy:** `ai-opportunity-scan`, `ai-use-case-prioritizer`, `ai-business-case`, `data-readiness-check`, `ai-pilot-design`, `agentic-workflow-design`, `responsible-ai-review`.\n- **Leader's desk:** `meeting-brief`, `red-team-review`, `negotiation-prep`, `first-90-days`, `executive-email`, `workshop-design`.\n- **Playbooks:** `start-here`, `playbook-strategy-review`, `playbook-board-pack`, `playbook-investment-case`, `playbook-ai-roadmap`, `playbook-market-entry`.\n\n## Common routes\n| Situation | Route |\n|---|---|\n| \"I have a vague problem\" | `problem-statement` → `issue-tree` → `hypothesis-tree` → `engagement-workplan` |\n| \"Prepare a board paper\" | `playbook-board-pack` |\n| \"Should we invest in X?\" | `playbook-investment-case` |\n| \"Where should AI help us?\" | `playbook-ai-roadmap` |\n| \"Should we enter market Y?\" | `playbook-market-entry` |\n| \"Review our strategy\" | `playbook-strategy-review` |\n| \"Turn my analysis into a deck\" | `insight-synthesis` → `scqa-storyline` → `ghost-deck` → `action-titles` → `deck-quality-check` |\n| \"I need a decision from my boss by email\" | `executive-email` (optionally `decision-memo` first) |\n| \"Big meeting tomorrow\" | `meeting-brief` (+ `negotiation-prep` if negotiating) |\n| \"Is this number right?\" | `source-validation` |\n| \"Cut costs\" | `kpi-driver-tree` → `cost-takeout` → `implementation-roadmap` → `benefits-tracker` |\n| \"Launching a programme\" | `implementation-roadmap` → `raci-builder` → `change-plan` → `risk-assessment` → `steerco-update` |\n| \"New role\" | `first-90-days` → `stakeholder-map` |\n| \"Run an offsite\" | `workshop-design` → `pre-mortem` |\n| \"ESG / BRSR\" | `esg-materiality` → `brsr-readiness` |\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **When the user is in a hurry,** give the 1-2 skills that matter most, and start.\n- **When the stakes are high** (board, regulator, investment), always include verification and challenge steps.\n- **When material is thin,** start with `research-plan` or `interview-guide` rather than analysis.\n- **When the request fits no skill,** say so and help directly, using the toolkit's house rules (answer first, evidence, honesty about uncertainty).\n\n## Red flags\n- Recommending ten skills for a one-hour task.\n- Jumping to slides before the problem and evidence are clear.\n- No verification step for decision-critical numbers or legal points.\n- Recommending without offering to start.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Senior leader, little time:** a 3-step route; draft outputs fast; the user reviews.\n- **Consultant or analyst:** a full playbook with intermediate outputs for team review.\n- **Board member or reviewer:** `board-question-bank`, `red-team-review`, `source-validation` to scrutinise others' work.\n- **Learning the toolkit:** suggest one learning path (frame → story → numbers) with a practice task.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Your route: [need in one line]\n**Decision / output:** ... **Audience:** ... **Deadline:** ...\n\n| Step | Skill | What it produces | Why now |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `...` | ... | ... |\n\n**Checks before it goes out:** ...\n**Where expert review is needed:** ...\n**Shall I start step 1?** To do it, I need: ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Need restated (decision, audience, deadline).\n- [ ] The route is the shortest that does the job; correctly sequenced.\n- [ ] Every step names its output and its link to the next.\n- [ ] Verification and expert-review points flagged where stakes are high.\n- [ ] An offer to start step 1, with the inputs needed.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Playbooks chain several skills. Review each stage's output before relying on the next, since errors compound.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `playbook-strategy-review`: End-to-end strategy review: from the question to a recommended strategy and roadmap, in guided stages.\n- `playbook-board-pack`: Prepare a complete board submission: paper, summary, risks, resolution, and rehearsal of directors' questions.\n- `playbook-investment-case`: From idea to approval-ready investment case: sizing, economics, model, sensitivity, risks and the ask.\n- `playbook-ai-roadmap`: Build an AI roadmap for a business or function: opportunities, priorities, cases, governance and adoption.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Structured problem-solving:** the sequence of define → structure → analyse → synthesise → communicate → deliver, as taught in strategy and problem-solving curricula.\n- **Each skill's own sources file** lists the standards, laws and concepts it draws on.\n- **Limitations:** this toolkit supports thinking and drafting. It is not legal, financial, tax, medical or regulatory advice; verify facts and seek qualified review for high-stakes decisions."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked examples (illustrative)\n\n## 1. \"Our CEO wants to know if we should launch a subscription service for our appliance customers. Board meeting in 3 weeks.\"\n\n**Your route:** a go or no-go recommendation on an appliance-care subscription, for the CEO and then the board, in 3 weeks.\n\n| Step | Skill | What it produces | Why now |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `problem-statement` | A sharp question, success criteria, scope | Avoids solving the wrong problem |\n| 2 | `jobs-to-be-done` + `interview-guide` | Customer needs and an interview plan (10-12 customers) | Demand evidence is the biggest unknown |\n| 3 | `market-sizing` | Serviceable market and an adoption range | Sizes the prize |\n| 4 | `pricing-strategy` + `unit-economics` | Price tiers; contribution per subscriber; payback | Tests whether the economics work |\n| 5 | `business-case` + `sensitivity-analysis` | NPV, IRR and the drivers that break it | The investment decision |\n| 6 | `red-team-review` | Severity-ranked challenges | Before the CEO sees it |\n| 7 | `playbook-board-pack` | Board paper, resolution, director Q&A | The board submission |\n\n**Checks:** verify market figures with `source-validation`; Finance to review the model.\n\n**Expert review:** consumer-protection and auto-renewal rules need legal review.\n\n**Shall I start step 1?** Share what you know about the idea, your customer base size and any past attempts.\n\n## 2. \"I need to tell my boss the project is delayed.\"\n\n**Your route:** one skill, `executive-email`, using the bad-news variant: the delay and impact in the first line, the cause, the recovery plan, and any decision needed.\n\n**Shall I draft it?** Tell me the project, the new date, the reason, and what you're doing about it."}]}, {"id": "playbook-strategy-review", "pack": "playbooks", "title": "Playbook: Strategy Review", "time": "60-90 min", "summary": "End-to-end strategy review: from the question to a recommended strategy and roadmap, in guided stages.", "tryp": "Run a strategy review for our ₹1,200 Cr hospital chain: growth has stalled and national chains are entering our cities.", "when": ["Annual strategy refresh", "Business-unit strategy review", "New CEO or leader setting direction"], "inputs": ["The business and the strategic question", "Available data: financials, market, customers, competitors", "Timeline and audience for the final output"], "pairs": ["start-here", "strategic-options", "implementation-roadmap", "red-team-review"], "body": "# Playbook: Strategy Review\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a strategy that makes real choices, where to play and how to win, backed by facts and numbers, stress-tested, and translated into a plan people can execute, delivered stage by stage so leaders can steer along the way.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **A strategy is a set of choices,** including what not to do. A list of aspirations isn't a strategy.\n2. **Fact base before opinions.** Market, competition, customers and own economics, from validated sources.\n3. **Genuine options.** At least three distinct, feasible options, including a credible status quo, evaluated on agreed criteria.\n4. **Robust under uncertainty.** Test the recommendation across scenarios; define signposts and triggers.\n5. **Numbers make it real:** financial impact, investment, capability needs and risks.\n6. **Execution is part of strategy:** roadmap, operating model, KPIs and governance.\n7. **Stage gates.** Leadership reviews and steers at each stage; no \"big reveal\" at the end.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The trigger (performance, disruption, new leader, cycle); the scope (group, business unit); the horizon.\n- The decision-makers and governance (CEO, board, strategy committee).\n- Constraints: capital, regulation, ownership, non-negotiables.\n- Timeline and team; existing strategy documents and data.\n\n## Method (stages, skills and gates)\n| Stage | Purpose | Skills | Output | Gate |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Frame | Define the question, scope, criteria | `problem-statement`, `stakeholder-map`, `issue-tree`, `engagement-workplan` | Strategic question, success criteria, workplan | Sponsor agrees the question and criteria |\n| 2. Fact base | Shared reality | `research-plan`, `market-sizing`, `customer-segmentation`, `competitor-benchmark`, `five-forces`, `pestel-scan`, `source-validation` | Validated fact base | Leadership accepts the fact base |\n| 3. Diagnose | Position and capabilities | `value-chain`, `vrio-analysis`, `swot-tows`, `portfolio-matrix`, `kpi-driver-tree` | Diagnosis: where we win and lose, and why | Agreement on the diagnosis |\n| 4. Options | Generate and evaluate | `growth-options`, `strategic-options`, `scenario-planning`, `build-buy-partner` | 3-5 options scored on criteria and across scenarios | Shortlist of 2-3 |\n| 5. Choose and test | Recommendation | `business-case`, `sensitivity-analysis`, `red-team-review`, `pre-mortem` | Recommended strategy with numbers, risks and signposts | Leadership decision |\n| 6. Story | Communicate | `insight-synthesis`, `scqa-storyline`, `ghost-deck`, `action-titles`, `executive-summary`, `deck-quality-check` | Leadership or board deck | Board approval (via `playbook-board-pack`) |\n| 7. Execute | Translate to action | `implementation-roadmap`, `operating-model`, `raci-builder`, `change-plan`, `kpi-dashboard`, `benefits-tracker` | Roadmap, operating model, KPIs, governance | Programme launched |\n\n**For each stage:** state the inputs needed, produce the output, summarise the decisions needed at the gate, then proceed.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Time-pressed:** a compressed version of the fact base (the top 10 facts), 3 options, and a 1-day leadership workshop (`workshop-design`).\n- **Turnaround context:** prioritise cash, the core and cost (`cost-takeout`) before growth options.\n- **Disruption context:** heavier scenario work; option value; staged commitments.\n- **Group strategy:** portfolio roles and capital allocation; parenting advantage.\n- **Involve the people who will execute** in stages 3-5, or adoption suffers.\n\n## Red flags\n- A strategy that is a list of initiatives.\n- No fact base, or one that is unvalidated.\n- Options that are variants of one idea.\n- No numbers; no scenarios.\n- No owners, KPIs or roadmap.\n- A final big reveal with no stage gates.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Business-unit strategy:** within group constraints; synergies with other units.\n- **Functional strategy (IT, HR, operations):** link to business strategy; service levels; cost.\n- **Public-sector or not-for-profit:** mission impact as the criterion; stakeholders; funding.\n- **Family business:** family governance, succession, risk appetite.\n- **Digital or AI strategy:** combine with `playbook-ai-roadmap`.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Strategy Review: [organisation / unit], horizon [years]\n**Strategic question:** ... **Success criteria:** ...\n**Stage status:** 1 ✓ 2 ✓ 3 ◐ ...\n\n### Stage [n] output\n[the output from that stage's skill]\n**Decisions needed at this gate:** ...\n\n### Final: strategy on a page\n- Aspiration: ...\n- Where to play: ... | Where not to play: ...\n- How to win: ...\n- Must-win battles (3-5): ...\n- Capabilities and investment: ...\n- Financial impact: ...\n- Key risks and signposts: ...\n- Roadmap and KPIs: ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Clear choices, including what not to do.\n- [ ] A validated fact base; an agreed diagnosis.\n- [ ] 3+ distinct options, evaluated on criteria and scenarios.\n- [ ] A recommendation quantified, stress-tested, with signposts.\n- [ ] A roadmap, operating model, KPIs and governance; stage gates respected.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Playbooks chain several skills. Review each stage's output before relying on the next, since errors compound.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `start-here`: Not sure which skill to use? Describe your situation and get the right skills in the right order.\n- `strategic-options`: Compare 2-5 real options against agreed criteria, with trade-offs made explicit, and recommend one.\n- `implementation-roadmap`: Turn a recommendation into a phased roadmap with workstreams, milestones, dependencies and quick wins.\n- `red-team-review`: Stress-test a plan, argument or document like a tough, fair critic, before your audience does.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Strategy as choice:** where to play, how to win, capabilities and management systems, as taught in strategy curricula.\n- **Strategy process:** fact base → diagnosis → options → choice → execution, with stage gates and leadership involvement.\n- **Scenario planning and robustness:** see `scenario-planning`.\n- **Board role in strategy:** boards approve and monitor strategy (Companies Act 2013 and SEBI LODR in India for listed companies; corporate governance codes).\n- **Regulated sectors:** sector regulators' capital and risk requirements constrain options (e.g. RBI prudential norms for banks).\n- **Each stage's skills** carry their own sources."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; a regional private bank, ₹85,000 Cr balance sheet, 3-year strategy refresh in 10 weeks)\n\n**Strategic question:** how do we lift return on equity from 11% to 15% by FY29, while staying within our risk appetite and capital limits?\n\n**Success criteria:** ROE ≥15%; CET1 ≥13%; gross NPA ≤3%; customer satisfaction up.\n\n**Stage 2 fact base (top facts, validated):**\n- Our cost-to-income is 58% vs a peer median of 49%.\n- Retail deposits grew 9% vs 13% for peers.\n- MSME lending in our home states is growing ~16% a year; our share is 4%.\n- Digital channels handle 38% of transactions vs 70%+ for leading peers.\n- Gold-loan yields are attractive, but competition is intensifying.\n\n**Stage 3 diagnosis:**\n- We win in relationship-led MSME and affluent banking in 3 home states (VRIO: local relationships and credit judgement are valuable, rare and hard to imitate).\n- We lose on cost and digital deposits.\n- The unsecured retail book is sub-scale.\n\n**Stage 4 options:**\n\n| Option | Description | ROE FY29 (est.) | Capital need | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A. Status quo plus | Incremental efficiency | 12.0% | Low | Low |\n| B. MSME champion | Focus on MSME in home states plus adjacent states; a digital MSME platform; supply-chain finance | 15.3% | Medium | Medium (credit) |\n| C. National digital retail | Scale unsecured digital lending nationally | 14.1% | High | High (credit, competition) |\n| D. B + cost programme | B, plus a 7-point reduction in cost-to-income through branch rationalisation and automation | 16.0% | Medium | Medium |\n\n**Scenarios:** a rate-cut cycle, an MSME credit stress, and a digital-competition shock. D stays ≥14% in 2 of 3 scenarios. C falls to 10% in the credit-stress scenario.\n\n**Stage 5 recommendation:** Option D, MSME champion plus cost.\n- **Red-team:** the credit-quality assumptions for adjacent states are the weakest link.\n- **The fix:** a phased entry with strict underwriting gates.\n- **Signposts:** MSME stress indicators; deposit growth.\n\n**Strategy on a page:**\n- **Where to play:** MSME and affluent customers in 5 states.\n- **Where not to play:** national unsecured digital lending.\n- **How to win:** relationship plus a digital platform; supply-chain finance with anchor corporates.\n- **Must-win battles:** an MSME platform live in 12 months; deposit growth through transaction banking; cost-to-income ≤51% by FY29; credit quality.\n- **Roadmap and KPIs:** via `implementation-roadmap` and `kpi-dashboard`."}]}, {"id": "playbook-board-pack", "pack": "playbooks", "title": "Playbook: Board Pack", "time": "45-60 min", "summary": "Prepare a complete board submission: paper, summary, risks, resolution, and rehearsal of directors' questions.", "tryp": "Help me prepare the board pack for approval of our ₹300 Cr capex plan for a new plant.", "when": ["Any board or committee submission", "First time presenting to a board", "High-stakes approvals"], "inputs": ["The matter and the decision sought", "Analysis, numbers and supporting documents", "Board composition and meeting date"], "pairs": ["board-paper", "board-question-bank", "audience-brief", "risk-assessment"], "body": "# Playbook: Board Pack\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a board pack directors can read in 20 minutes and decide on confidently, with the hard questions already answered, the governance boxes ticked, and a resolution ready for the minutes.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **The decision is explicit:** approve, note, or guide, with the exact resolution wording.\n2. **Directors' duties shape the paper.** Give them what they need to exercise independent judgement and care: options, risks, assumptions, dissent, conflicts.\n3. **Short main paper, rich annexes.** About 4-8 pages plus annexes; an executive summary that stands alone.\n4. **Governance compliance is checked,** not assumed: approval thresholds, committee routing, related-party rules, disclosure obligations, conflicts.\n5. **Anticipate challenge.** Rehearse the 10-15 most likely director questions; answer them in the paper or the Q&A annex.\n6. **Honest risk and downside.** Boards punish surprises, not candour.\n7. **Pre-brief key directors** (committee chairs, the lead independent director) before the meeting.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The item: decision / approval / noting / discussion; the proposal and why now.\n- The board or committee, meeting date and paper deadline (often 7 days before).\n- Approval requirements: the delegation-of-authority matrix, statutory requirements, shareholder approval if needed.\n- Directors: backgrounds, known concerns, past positions; conflicts.\n- Material available: business case, analyses, legal opinions, committee recommendations.\n\n## Method (stages and skills)\n| Stage | Purpose | Skills | Output |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Frame the ask | The decision, the board's role, the resolution | `problem-statement`, `audience-brief` | Decision statement; draft resolution |\n| 2. Know the board | Directors' lenses and concerns | `stakeholder-map`, `audience-brief` | Director map |\n| 3. Substance | Options, financials, risks | `strategic-options`, `business-case`, `sensitivity-analysis`, `risk-assessment` (as needed) | Analytical annexes |\n| 4. The paper | Write the board paper | `board-paper`, `executive-summary`, `pyramid-structure` | Board paper and summary |\n| 5. Governance check | Compliance and routing | (checklist below) | Governance confirmation |\n| 6. Challenge | Questions and stress test | `board-question-bank`, `red-team-review`, `integrity-check` | Q&A annex; fixes |\n| 7. Quality | The final check | `deck-quality-check` (for any slides), `source-validation` | Clean pack |\n| 8. Meeting | Presentation and pre-briefs | `meeting-brief`, `speaker-notes` | Pre-brief plan; a 10-minute presentation |\n\n**Governance checklist (adapt to jurisdiction and constitution):**\n- Is the board or committee the right authority? Is committee review needed first (audit, risk, nomination and remuneration, CSR)?\n- Statutory or shareholder approval required?\n- Related-party transaction: audit committee approval; the interested directors recuse themselves.\n- Conflicts of interest declared.\n- Disclosure obligations (stock exchange, regulator) and timing.\n- Quorum and notice requirements.\n- Legal opinion obtained where needed.\n- Minutes: the draft resolution and the key discussion points to record.\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Noting vs decision:** don't hide decisions in noting items. Boards should knowingly approve.\n- **Dissent:** if management or a committee is divided, say so and give both views.\n- **Price-sensitive matters:** confidentiality, insider lists, disclosure timing.\n- **Late changes:** a supplementary paper with the changes highlighted; avoid tabling papers at the meeting.\n- **Repeat items:** reference prior decisions; show what's changed.\n\n## Red flags\n- A resolution missing or vague.\n- 40-page papers with no summary.\n- No options or downside case.\n- Governance routing not checked; related-party issues missed.\n- Directors surprised by a known issue.\n- Slides that repeat the paper.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Audit or risk committee:** control findings, risk metrics, remediation; independence.\n- **M&A or major capex:** valuation, financing, integration, downside protection; fairness or valuation opinions.\n- **Policy approvals (risk appetite, AI policy, ESG):** policy text, changes from the last version, impact.\n- **Regulated entities:** regulator-mandated approvals and reporting (e.g. RBI or IRDAI requirements).\n- **Unlisted or family companies:** shareholder agreements and reserved matters.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Board Pack: [item], [board / committee], [meeting date]\n**Decision sought:** approve / note / guide\n**Draft resolution:** \"RESOLVED THAT ...\"\n\n1. Board paper (4-8 pp): purpose, executive summary, background, options, recommendation, financials, risks, compliance, next steps\n2. Annexes: business case, sensitivity, risk register, legal opinion, ...\n3. Director Q&A annex: top questions with answers\n4. Governance checklist: ✓ items, with notes\n5. Presentation (≤10 min) and pre-brief plan\n6. Points to minute\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Decision and draft resolution explicit.\n- [ ] A standalone executive summary; options, financials and an honest downside.\n- [ ] Governance checklist complete: authority, committees, related-party, conflicts, disclosure.\n- [ ] Director questions anticipated and answered.\n- [ ] Pre-briefs planned; facts validated; pack within length.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Playbooks chain several skills. Review each stage's output before relying on the next, since errors compound.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `board-paper`: Write a board paper directors can read in 10 minutes: purpose, recommendation, analysis, risks and the resolution.\n- `board-question-bank`: Generate the sharp, fair questions a non-executive director should ask on any paper or topic.\n- `audience-brief`: Profile who you are persuading: what they care about, what they already believe, and what would change their mind.\n- `risk-assessment`: Identify, score and plan responses to the risks of a project, decision or business.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public laws, standards and widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **India (check the latest versions):**\n  - Companies Act 2013: Section 166 (directors' duties), Section 179 (board powers), Section 180 (borrowing limits requiring shareholder approval), Section 188 (related-party transactions).\n  - SEBI LODR Regulations: board and committee composition, related-party approvals, disclosure of material events (Regulation 30).\n  - SEBI PIT Regulations (insider lists, trading windows).\n  - Secretarial Standard SS-1 on board meetings (notice, agenda, quorum, minutes), issued by the company-secretaries' institute.\n- **Governance practice:** board-paper quality principles (decision clarity, options, risk); the role of committees; pre-briefing practice.\n- **Sector regulators:** for banks, insurers and NBFCs, RBI / IRDAI governance directions.\n- **Verify all statutory references** with the company secretary or legal counsel."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; a listed specialty-chemicals company seeking board approval for a ₹650 Cr greenfield plant, part-funded by ₹400 Cr of debt)\n\n**Decision sought:** approve the capex of ₹650 Cr and the borrowing of up to ₹400 Cr.\n\n**Draft resolution:** \"RESOLVED THAT the Board approves capital expenditure of up to ₹650 crore for the new plant at [site], as set out in the paper, and authorises borrowing of up to ₹400 crore on the terms outlined, subject to the borrowing limits approved by shareholders…\"\n\n**Director map:**\n- Chair (former banker): leverage and covenants.\n- Audit committee chair (CA): accounting, contingencies, controls.\n- Independent director (industry veteran): technology and demand.\n- Woman independent director (ESG background): environmental clearances and community issues.\n- Promoter directors: growth and timing.\n\n**Stage 3 annexes:** demand study; business case (IRR 17.8%, payback ~6 years); sensitivity (break-even utilisation 58%); risk register (clearances, technology scale-up, price cycles).\n\n**Governance check:**\n- Capex is above the CEO's delegated limit, so board approval is needed. ✓\n- Borrowing is within the shareholder-approved limit under the Companies Act. ✓\n- No related-party element. Technology licensing is from an unrelated party. ✓\n- Disclosure: the board decision is price-sensitive, so stock-exchange disclosure is needed within the prescribed timeline after the meeting; the insider list has been updated. ✓\n- Environmental clearance: applied for, awaited. Approval is made subject to it. ✓\n- The audit committee reviewed the funding plan. ✓\n\n**Director Q&A annex (excerpt):**\n- *\"What if prices fall back to the FY24 trough?\"* IRR falls to 11%, still above the cost of capital of 10.5%, but with thin headroom. The phasing option is described in section 6.\n- *\"Net debt to EBITDA peak?\"* 2.4x in FY28, against a covenant of 3.0x.\n- *\"What if the environmental clearance is delayed?\"* Each 6-month delay cuts IRR by ~1.2 points. No major orders are placed before clearance.\n\n**Red-team fixes made:** added a phased option (Phase 1 of ₹420 Cr); disclosed the customer-concentration risk (2 customers take 40% of planned volume).\n\n**Pre-briefs:** the audit committee chair (funding and controls); the Chair (leverage).\n\n**Presentation:** 8 minutes; 4 slides (the ask, the case, risks and mitigations, phasing).\n\n**Points to minute:** the conditions (clearance), the directors' questions on leverage and the answers given, and the phasing option considered."}]}, {"id": "playbook-investment-case", "pack": "playbooks", "title": "Playbook: Investment Case", "time": "45-60 min", "summary": "From idea to approval-ready investment case: sizing, economics, model, sensitivity, risks and the ask.", "tryp": "Build the investment case for launching a subscription maintenance service for our industrial pumps installed base.", "when": ["Capex or project approvals", "New product or service launches", "Transformation funding requests"], "inputs": ["The proposal", "Cost, price, volume data or estimates", "Hurdle rate and approval process"], "pairs": ["business-case", "sensitivity-analysis", "decision-memo", "unit-economics"], "body": "# Playbook: Investment Case\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** an investment case a CFO and board can trust: the right comparison, honest numbers, the drivers that make or break it, the risks and how they're managed, and an ask that's clear about what is being approved.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Compare against the real alternative.** Value is incremental to the \"do nothing\" (or the minimum viable) case, which is rarely flat.\n2. **Cash, not accounting.** Incremental cash flows, including working capital, tax, terminal or residual value and decommissioning.\n3. **Drivers before totals.** Unit economics and key drivers explain the value; totals follow.\n4. **Uncertainty is quantified:** sensitivity, scenarios, break-even and switching values, and the base rate for similar projects.\n5. **Stage where possible.** Gates and options reduce risk and the capital at risk.\n6. **The ask is explicit:** the amount, the phase, the conditions, the KPIs, the review dates.\n7. **Benefits are owned and tracked** after approval.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The proposal and the problem or opportunity it addresses; the decision-maker; the approval threshold.\n- The hurdle rate or cost of capital; the evaluation horizon; tax and accounting context.\n- Data available: costs, quotes, volumes, prices, comparable projects.\n- Constraints: capital budget, leverage limits, strategic fit.\n\n## Method (stages and skills)\n| Stage | Purpose | Skills | Output |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Frame | Decision, options, base case | `problem-statement`, `strategic-options` | Decision and option set, incl. do-nothing |\n| 2. Size | The prize or the problem | `market-sizing`, `customer-segmentation` (if revenue), `cost-takeout` (if savings) | Volume and value range |\n| 3. Economics | Drivers | `unit-economics`, `kpi-driver-tree`, `pricing-strategy` (if relevant) | Unit and driver model |\n| 4. Model | Financial structure | `financial-model-spec` | Model specification and assumptions log |\n| 5. Value | NPV, IRR, payback, funding | `business-case`, `break-even-payback` | Incremental cash flows and metrics |\n| 6. Uncertainty | Sensitivity and scenarios | `sensitivity-analysis`, `scenario-planning` | Tornado, switching values, scenarios |\n| 7. Risk and challenge | Risks; stress test | `risk-assessment`, `red-team-review`, `pre-mortem`, `integrity-check` | Risk register; fixes; staging |\n| 8. The ask | Communicate | `decision-memo` or `playbook-board-pack`, `executive-summary` | Decision memo or board paper |\n| 9. After approval | Track | `benefits-tracker`, `implementation-roadmap` | Benefits plan and KPIs |\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Optimism bias:** compare cost, schedule and benefit assumptions with base rates for similar projects; apply contingency.\n- **Strategic value beyond NPV** (option value, capability) is described explicitly and separately, not hidden in inflated cash flows.\n- **Sunk costs** are excluded; past spending is not a reason to continue.\n- **Financing vs investment decisions:** evaluate the project at the cost of capital first; financing structure second.\n- **Small cases:** a light version (stages 1, 3, 5, 6, 8) is enough below materiality thresholds.\n\n## Red flags\n- No do-nothing comparison.\n- Profit instead of cash; working capital ignored.\n- A hockey-stick ramp with no driver logic.\n- A single-point estimate; no break-even.\n- Risks listed without mitigation or staging.\n- An ask for the whole amount with no gates.\n- No benefit owner.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Capex / capacity:** utilisation ramp, price cycles, maintenance capex, technology risk.\n- **Technology / AI programme:** adoption curves, change costs, run costs, vendor risk (see `ai-business-case`).\n- **New product:** cannibalisation, marketing spend, payback per cohort.\n- **Acquisition:** standalone value plus synergies minus integration costs; price discipline; downside.\n- **Public sector:** social cost-benefit; affordability; value for money.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Investment Case: [proposal], ask ₹__ (phase __)\n**Recommendation:** ... **vs do-nothing:** ...\n\n| Metric | Base | Downside | Upside |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NPV @ __% | | | |\n| IRR | | | |\n| Payback | | | |\n\n**Value drivers and unit economics:** ...\n**Sensitivity:** top drivers; switching values; break-even\n**Risks and mitigations; staging and gates:** ...\n**Strategic value (non-financial):** ...\n**The ask:** amount, phase, conditions, KPIs, review dates\n**Benefits owner and tracking:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Incremental cash flows vs a realistic do-nothing case.\n- [ ] Driver-based economics; assumptions logged and sourced.\n- [ ] NPV, IRR and payback checked by calculation; scenarios and switching values.\n- [ ] Risks, base rates, staging and red-team fixes.\n- [ ] An explicit ask with conditions; a benefits owner and KPIs.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Playbooks chain several skills. Review each stage's output before relying on the next, since errors compound.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `sensitivity-analysis`: Find which assumptions really move the answer, and what has to be true for the decision to flip.\n- `decision-memo`: Write a crisp memo that frames a decision, the options, the recommendation and what is being asked.\n- `unit-economics`: Work out whether each customer, order or unit makes money, and which levers fix it.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (widely taught concepts and public standards)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Capital budgeting:** NPV, IRR, payback, incremental cash flows, working capital, terminal value, cost of capital, as taught in corporate-finance curricula.\n- **Uncertainty analysis:** sensitivity, switching values, scenarios, real options and staging.\n- **Optimism bias and reference-class forecasting:** base rates for cost overruns and benefit shortfalls in large projects.\n- **Public-sector appraisal:** social cost-benefit analysis and value-for-money principles in government appraisal guidance.\n- **Accounting context:** Ind AS 16 (property, plant and equipment) and Ind AS 36 (impairment) where relevant; verify with Finance."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; ₹120 Cr warehouse automation for a consumer-goods distributor; hurdle rate 12%; 7-year horizon)\n\n**Recommendation:** approve, with a gate. The base case is value-positive, but the case is highly sensitive to a delay in go-live, so a phased go-live and vendor delay penalties are conditions.\n\n**vs do-nothing:** without automation, volume growth needs ~₹24 Cr a year of extra labour and leased space by year 3 (the do-nothing is not flat).\n\n**Incremental cash flows (₹ Cr):**\n\n| Year | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cash flow | −120 | 18 | 32 | 36 | 36 | 36 | 36 | 36 |\n| Cumulative | −120 | −102 | −70 | −34 | 2 | 38 | 74 | 110 |\n\nBenefits are net of run costs, and include working-capital release from faster order processing.\n\n| Metric | Base | Downside (benefits −25%, capex +15%) | 1-year delay in benefits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NPV @ 12% | **+₹25.0 Cr** | −₹29.2 Cr | −₹5.0 Cr |\n| IRR | **17.8%** | 5.5% | 10.9% |\n| Payback | **~3.9 years** | ~5.7 years | ~4.9 years |\n\n**Value drivers:**\n- Labour productivity: picks per hour 110 → 260.\n- Error-rate reduction: 1.2% → 0.3%.\n- Avoided space.\n- Throughput for growth.\n\n**Sensitivity:**\n- **Switching value:** benefits can fall about **17%** before NPV turns negative.\n- A one-year delay alone makes NPV negative. **Schedule is the critical risk.**\n\n**Risks and mitigations:**\n- **Integration with the warehouse system and delay:** fixed-price contract with delay liquidated damages; a pilot zone first.\n- **Volume shortfall:** modular automation, sized for 3-year volume.\n- **Workforce transition:** redeploy 60% of affected staff to new sites; agree with the union early.\n\n**Staging:**\n- **Phase 1 (₹70 Cr):** 2 of 4 zones, with go-live by month 10.\n- **Gate:** productivity ≥220 picks per hour and system stability for 8 weeks.\n- **Phase 2 (₹50 Cr):** the remaining zones.\n\n**The ask:**\n- Approve ₹120 Cr in total, with Phase 2 release subject to the gate.\n- **Conditions:** delay penalties in the vendor contract; a workforce plan agreed before signing.\n- **KPIs:** picks per hour, error rate, cost per order.\n- Quarterly reviews.\n\n**Benefits owner:** Head of Supply Chain; tracked via `benefits-tracker`, with Finance validating."}]}, {"id": "playbook-ai-roadmap", "pack": "playbooks", "title": "Playbook: AI Roadmap", "time": "60-90 min", "summary": "Build an AI roadmap for a business or function: opportunities, priorities, cases, governance and adoption.", "tryp": "Build an AI roadmap for the operations function of a mid-size Indian logistics company.", "when": ["Enterprise or function AI strategy", "AI programme set-up", "Board-level AI agenda"], "inputs": ["Business/function, processes, systems, data", "Goals, budget, risk appetite", "Current AI activity and governance"], "pairs": ["ai-opportunity-scan", "ai-use-case-prioritizer", "ai-governance-review", "change-plan"], "body": "# Playbook: AI Roadmap\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** an AI plan tied to business value, not technology enthusiasm: a short list of use cases worth doing, proof they can work, the guardrails to do them safely, and a sequence the organisation can actually absorb.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Business value first.** Start from goals and value pools (revenue, cost, risk, experience), not from models or vendors.\n2. **Portfolio, not a wish list.** Quick wins, scalable bets and a few strategic plays, prioritised on value × feasibility × risk.\n3. **Data and process readiness decide feasibility.** Most failures are data, integration and adoption, not models.\n4. **Pilots are designed to decide:** a baseline, success metrics, a control group where possible, and scale or stop criteria.\n5. **Governance proportionate to risk.** Policy, inventory, risk tiering, human oversight, testing, monitoring, aligned to the applicable laws and standards.\n6. **Adoption is the value.** Workflow redesign, training, incentives and change management are budgeted.\n7. **Measured benefits.** KPIs and benefit tracking from the start; stop what doesn't deliver.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- Scope: enterprise or function; business goals; the current AI activity (pilots, tools, shadow use).\n- Data and technology landscape; talent; budget.\n- Risk appetite and regulatory context (sector rules, data protection, cross-border).\n- Sponsor and governance forum; timeline.\n\n## Method (stages and skills)\n| Stage | Purpose | Skills | Output |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Anchor | Goals, value pools, scope | `problem-statement`, `kpi-driver-tree`, `stakeholder-map` | Value pools and target outcomes |\n| 2. Scan | Generate use cases | `ai-opportunity-scan`, `value-chain`, `jobs-to-be-done` | Long list (30-60) |\n| 3. Prioritise | Shortlist | `ai-use-case-prioritizer`, `data-readiness-check` | 8-12 prioritised; 3-5 for pilots |\n| 4. Cases | Value and cost | `ai-business-case`, `sensitivity-analysis` | Business cases for the top use cases |\n| 5. Pilot | Prove it | `ai-pilot-design`, `agentic-workflow-design` (where justified) | Pilot designs with metrics and gates |\n| 6. Govern | Safe and compliant | `ai-governance-review`, `responsible-ai-review`, `ai-risk-register`, `risk-appetite` | Policy, controls, risk register |\n| 7. Organise and adopt | Operating model, people | `operating-model`, `change-plan`, `raci-builder` | AI operating model; change plan |\n| 8. Roadmap | Sequence | `implementation-roadmap`, `kpi-dashboard`, `benefits-tracker` | 12-24 month roadmap, KPIs |\n| 9. Communicate | Leadership and board | `scqa-storyline`, `executive-summary`, `playbook-board-pack` | Board paper or deck |\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Buy vs build:** buy for commodity capabilities; build or customise where data or process is a differentiator (`build-buy-partner`).\n- **Agentic workflows:** start with bounded, reversible tasks with human approval; expand autonomy with evidence.\n- **Shadow AI:** provide approved tools and guidance rather than only bans.\n- **High-risk uses** (credit, hiring, health, legal effects on people) need stronger testing, explainability, human review and legal sign-off.\n- **Measure end-to-end** (cycle time, quality, cost per case), not just model accuracy.\n\n## Red flags\n- A roadmap of technologies, not outcomes.\n- 40 pilots and no scale-ups.\n- No data-readiness assessment.\n- Governance as a policy document only (no inventory, testing or monitoring).\n- No change budget; benefits not tracked.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **Financial services:** model-risk management, fairness in credit, regulator expectations (e.g. RBI's framework for responsible AI in the financial sector), outsourcing rules.\n- **Healthcare:** clinical safety, consent, medical-device regulation where applicable.\n- **Public sector:** transparency, citizen rights, procurement rules.\n- **Manufacturing:** predictive maintenance, quality vision, safety; OT integration.\n- **Function-level (HR, finance, legal, customer service):** a process-by-process scan; workforce impact plans.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## AI Roadmap: [organisation / function], [horizon]\n**Business goals and value pools:** ...\n**Portfolio (prioritised):**\n| Use case | Value (₹/yr) | Feasibility | Risk tier | Data readiness | Wave |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Pilots (wave 1):** metrics, baseline, gates\n**Governance:** policy, inventory, risk tiering, oversight, testing, monitoring\n**Operating model and talent:** ...\n**Change and adoption:** ...\n\n| Quarter | Initiatives | Milestones | KPIs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Investment and benefits:** ...\n**Board asks:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Anchored in business goals and value pools.\n- [ ] Prioritised on value, feasibility, risk and data readiness.\n- [ ] Business cases and pilots with metrics and scale or stop gates.\n- [ ] Governance and responsible-AI controls proportionate to risk and law.\n- [ ] Adoption and change budgeted; a sequenced roadmap with KPIs and benefits tracking.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Playbooks chain several skills. Review each stage's output before relying on the next, since errors compound.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `ai-opportunity-scan`: Find where AI can create real value in a function or business, grounded in processes and pain points.\n- `ai-use-case-prioritizer`: Score and rank AI use cases on value, feasibility and risk, and pick the first wave.\n- `ai-governance-review`: Assess how well an organisation governs AI: policy, roles, inventory, risk, controls, and board oversight.\n- `change-plan`: Plan the people side of change: impact by group, messages, training, resistance and adoption measures.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public laws, standards and widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **AI management and risk standards:** ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management systems); ISO/IEC 23894 (AI risk management); NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0) and its generative-AI profile.\n- **Principles:** OECD AI Principles.\n- **Regulation (check current status):**\n  - EU AI Act (risk-based obligations; phased application).\n  - India: DPDP Act 2023 and the DPDP Rules; national AI governance guidelines; RBI's FREE-AI framework for the financial sector.\n  - Sector regulators' guidance on outsourcing, model risk and data.\n- **Application security for AI and LLM systems:** OWASP guidance on LLM application risks.\n- **Each stage's skills** (`ai-opportunity-scan`, `ai-governance-review`, etc.) list detailed sources.\n- **Verify** regulatory interpretations with legal and compliance."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; a general insurer, ₹14,000 Cr gross premium; 18-month AI roadmap)\n\n**Goals and value pools:**\n- Reduce the claims expense ratio by 1.5 points.\n- Cut fraud leakage (est. 6-8% of motor and health claims).\n- Improve retail renewal from 71% to 76%.\n- Lift underwriting productivity.\n\n**Long list:** 42 use cases across claims, underwriting, distribution, service, finance and HR.\n\n**Portfolio (top 8 of 12 prioritised):**\n\n| Use case | Value (₹ Cr/yr, est.) | Feasibility | Risk tier | Data readiness | Wave |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Motor-claim photo triage and estimate assist | 45-60 | High | Medium | Good | 1 |\n| Health-claim fraud scoring (assist investigators) | 60-90 | Medium | High (affects customers) | Medium | 1 (with human review) |\n| Service-agent copilot (policy Q&A, summaries) | 20-25 | High | Low-medium | Good | 1 |\n| Renewal-propensity and next-best-action | 30-40 | Medium | Medium | Medium | 2 |\n| Commercial-underwriting document extraction | 15-20 | High | Low | Good | 2 |\n| Health pre-authorisation agent (drafts decisions, with human approval) | 25-35 | Medium | High | Medium | 2-3 |\n| Regulatory-return preparation assistant | 5-8 | High | Low | Good | 2 |\n| Pricing optimisation | 50-80 | Low-medium | High (fairness, regulator) | Low | 3 (after data work) |\n\n**Wave-1 pilots:**\n- **Motor triage:** 3 regions; baseline cycle time of 9 days; target ≤6 days; estimate accuracy within ±10% for 80% of cases; scale if met for 8 weeks.\n- **Fraud scoring:** an investigator-assist tool only; no automated rejection. Metrics: hit rate, false positives, fairness checks by region and age band.\n- **Service copilot:** 200 agents; handle time −20%; quality audit scores no worse than baseline.\n\n**Governance:**\n- An AI policy approved by the board.\n- An inventory of all AI systems.\n- Risk tiering: high-risk uses need fairness testing, explainability, human review, a legal and compliance sign-off, and monitoring.\n- Alignment with the DPDP Act and the regulator's guidance on outsourcing and data.\n- A risk register (`ai-risk-register`) reviewed quarterly by the risk committee.\n\n**Operating model:**\n- A central AI centre of excellence: platform, standards, model risk.\n- Business-embedded product owners.\n- A model-risk function under the CRO.\n\n**Change:**\n- Training for 1,500 claims and service staff.\n- Revised investigator workflows.\n- Incentives updated (quality plus productivity).\n- Union and staff communication on role changes.\n\n| Quarter | Initiatives | Milestones | KPIs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Q1 | Governance, data foundations, 3 pilots | Policy approved; pilots live | Cycle time, handle time |\n| Q2 | Pilot results, scale decisions | 2 scale decisions | Claims-ratio impact |\n| Q3-Q4 | Scale wave 1; start wave 2 | Motor triage national | Fraud savings, renewal |\n| Q5-Q6 | Wave 2; pricing data work | Pre-auth agent pilot | Expense ratio −1.0 pt |\n\n**Investment:** ₹85 Cr over 18 months. Expected run-rate benefits of ₹150-210 Cr a year by month 24 (validated quarterly by Finance).\n\n**Board asks:**\n- Approve the AI policy and the governance structure.\n- Approve the investment, with wave gates."}]}, {"id": "playbook-market-entry", "pack": "playbooks", "title": "Playbook: Market Entry", "time": "60-90 min", "summary": "Decide whether, where and how to enter a new market, from attractiveness to entry mode and launch plan.", "tryp": "Should our Indian SaaS HR platform enter Saudi Arabia, and if so how?", "when": ["New country or region entry", "New customer segment entry", "Responding to a partner or acquisition opportunity abroad"], "inputs": ["Company and offering", "Target market(s)", "Resources, timelines and constraints"], "pairs": ["market-sizing", "build-buy-partner", "business-case", "pestel-scan"], "body": "# Playbook: Market Entry\n\n*moons2star Consultant's Toolkit · created by Dr. Munmun Das · [moons2star.in](https://moons2star.in)*\n\n**What this gives you:** a disciplined go or no-go, and the right where and how: markets ranked on facts, a proposition customers want, an entry mode matched to risk and capability, and a staged plan that limits the downside.\n\n## The senior standard\n1. **Attractiveness and ability to win, both.** A big market where you have no edge is a trap; a small one where you win can be a beachhead.\n2. **Screen broadly, then go deep.** Rank many candidates on a few criteria; deep-dive on 2-3.\n3. **Regulation and the local environment** often decide feasibility: licensing, foreign-ownership limits, tax, data localisation, labour rules.\n4. **Customers and competitors on the ground.** Interviews, field visits and local partners beat desk research alone.\n5. **The entry mode fits the risk, capability and control needed:** organic, partnership, licence or franchise, JV, acquisition.\n6. **Stage the commitment.** A test market or pilot city, with clear gates before scaling.\n7. **Exit criteria defined upfront:** what would make us stop.\n\n## Before you start: diagnose\n- The entry objective (growth, diversification, following customers, defending against competitors); the horizon; ambition.\n- Candidate markets or segments; existing presence; capabilities and assets that travel.\n- Constraints: capital, management bandwidth, risk appetite, regulatory limits.\n- Decision-makers and timeline.\n\n## Method (stages and skills)\n| Stage | Purpose | Skills | Output |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Frame | Objective, criteria, options | `problem-statement`, `issue-tree` | Entry question and criteria |\n| 2. Screen | Rank candidates | `market-sizing` (top-down), `pestel-scan` (light), scoring on attractiveness × ability to win | Ranked list; top 2-3 |\n| 3. Deep-dive | Market and environment | `market-sizing` (bottom-up), `pestel-scan`, `five-forces`, `research-plan`, `source-validation` | Sized market; regulatory map |\n| 4. Customers | Needs and segments | `customer-segmentation`, `jobs-to-be-done`, `interview-guide`, `survey-design` | Target segments and needs |\n| 5. Competition | Landscape and gaps | `competitor-benchmark`, `value-chain` | Competitive map; white space |\n| 6. Proposition | Offer and price | `pricing-strategy`, `swot-tows` | Value proposition and pricing |\n| 7. Entry mode | How to enter | `build-buy-partner`, `negotiation-prep` (if partner or acquisition) | Recommended mode |\n| 8. Case | Economics and staging | `unit-economics`, `business-case`, `sensitivity-analysis`, `break-even-payback` | Case with gates |\n| 9. Risk | Stress test | `risk-assessment`, `pre-mortem`, `red-team-review` | Risks; exit criteria |\n| 10. Launch | Plan | `implementation-roadmap`, `operating-model`, `kpi-dashboard` | Launch plan, KPIs, gates |\n| 11. Decide | Communicate | `decision-memo` or `playbook-board-pack` | Go / no-go paper |\n\n## Judgement calls\n- **Psychic distance** (language, culture, business practice) raises cost and risk. Partners can bridge it.\n- **Follow-the-customer entries** (serving existing clients abroad) are lower risk, but may limit local growth.\n- **Regulated sectors:** the licence timeline is often the critical path; build it into the plan.\n- **Acquisition vs organic:** speed and local capability vs integration risk and price.\n- **Pilot design:** choose a test market representative of the larger opportunity, with measurable gates.\n\n## Red flags\n- Choosing a market for its size alone.\n- Desk research only; no customer contact.\n- Regulatory feasibility checked late.\n- One entry mode considered.\n- A full-scale launch with no gates.\n- No exit criteria; sunk-cost escalation.\n\n## Variants by context\n- **International (cross-border):** foreign-investment rules, tax treaties, repatriation, currency, transfer pricing, data rules.\n- **New Indian state or city:** state-level licences, local language, distribution partners, state incentives.\n- **New segment (e.g. SME after enterprise):** product simplification, channel change, cost-to-serve.\n- **New category:** capability gaps, brand stretch, cannibalisation.\n- **Digital or platform entry:** network effects, customer-acquisition costs, local competitors.\n\n## Output format\n\n```markdown\n## Market Entry: [market / segment], go / no-go recommendation\n**Objective and criteria:** ...\n**Screen results:**\n| Market | Attractiveness | Ability to win | Regulatory feasibility | Rank |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n**Deep-dive (chosen market):** size; growth; regulation; competitors; customers\n**Proposition and pricing:** ...\n**Entry mode and rationale:** ...\n**Business case:** NPV / IRR / payback; break-even; sensitivities\n**Staging and gates:** ...\n**Risks and exit criteria:** ...\n**Launch plan and KPIs:** ...\n**Decision requested:** ...\n```\n\n## Reference files (read when needed)\n- `references/sources.md`: standards, laws and concepts this skill draws on, and what to verify\n- `references/worked-example.md`: a complete, realistic example from start to finish\n\nLoad the worked example when the user wants to see what good looks like, and check the sources before citing any standard, law or figure.\n\n## Quality check before you reply\n- [ ] Candidates screened on attractiveness and ability to win.\n- [ ] Regulatory feasibility assessed early.\n- [ ] Customer and competitor evidence from primary sources where possible.\n- [ ] Multiple entry modes compared; a staged case with sensitivities.\n- [ ] Risks, exit criteria, launch gates and KPIs defined.\n\n## House rules\n- Never invent facts, figures, quotes or sources. Label estimates \"(est.)\" and list assumptions openly.\n- If key inputs are missing, ask up to 3 short questions, or proceed with clearly stated assumptions if the user wants speed.\n- Answer first, then support. Plain English; define any jargon.\n- Use the user's currency, units, terminology and context (e.g. ₹ and lakh/crore for Indian users).\n- Do calculations with code or a spreadsheet when available, not mental arithmetic.\n- For legal, regulatory, tax or accounting specifics, flag them for verification against current official sources and qualified advisers.\n- Remind users not to paste confidential or personal data they are not permitted to share; suggest anonymising.\n\n## Limitations and disclaimer\n- **Decision support, not professional advice.** This skill provides a structured method and general information. It is not legal, financial, investment, tax, accounting, regulatory, medical or other professional advice. It does not replace qualified advisers or your organisation's own review and approval processes.\n- **AI can be wrong.** Claude can produce confident but incorrect facts, numbers, citations or legal references. Check anything you will rely on, sign or publish.\n- **Point-in-time content.** Frameworks, laws, thresholds and dates referenced reflect the author's research as of November 2026 and may since have changed. Confirm against current official sources.\n- **Only as good as the inputs.** Claude knows only what you share in the conversation; missing or inaccurate context leads to weaker output.\n- **Your data, your responsibility.** Do not share confidential, personal or price-sensitive information unless your organisation's policies and applicable law allow it.\n- **No warranty, no adviser relationship.** Provided \"as is\" under CC BY 4.0, without warranty of any kind. Using it creates no adviser-client relationship with the author, who accepts no liability for decisions made using it.\n- **Skill-specific:** Playbooks chain several skills. Review each stage's output before relying on the next, since errors compound.\n\n*Instruction to Claude:* when the output will support a board, legal, regulatory, financial or employment decision, end with one short line reminding the user to have it reviewed by the appropriate professional. Don't add longer disclaimers than that.\n\n## Pairs well with\n- `market-sizing`: Size a market top-down and bottom-up, reconcile the two, and show every assumption.\n- `build-buy-partner`: Decide whether to build a capability, acquire it, or partner, with a clear rationale.\n- `business-case`: Build a decision-ready business case: options, costs, benefits, NPV, risks and the ask.\n- `pestel-scan`: Scan political, economic, social, technological, environmental and legal shifts, and rank what really matters.\n\n---\n*Part of the **moons2star Consultant's Toolkit** by Dr. Munmun Das. https://github.com/mad0907/Claude-skills. Licensed CC BY 4.0: free to use and adapt with attribution to \"Dr. Munmun Das / moons2star\".*", "deep": true, "refs": [{"name": "sources.md", "text": "# Sources (public data, laws and widely taught concepts)\n\n*This toolkit references public standards, laws and widely taught concepts only. It names no companies, products or individuals.*\n\n- **Market selection:** screening on attractiveness and ability to win; weighted scoring; psychic and institutional distance, as taught in international-business curricula.\n- **Entry modes:** export, licensing or franchising, partnership, joint venture, acquisition, greenfield; staged internationalisation.\n- **Indian outbound investment rules:** the Overseas Investment Rules and Regulations under FEMA (2022) for Indian companies investing abroad; check the current RBI directions.\n- **Inbound rules (for entries into India):** the FDI policy and sectoral caps.\n- **Public data sources:** national statistics, business registries and licensing authorities, multilateral databases, trade statistics.\n- **Tax and transfer pricing:** double-taxation treaties; OECD transfer-pricing guidelines. Seek tax advice.\n- **Each stage's skills** list detailed sources."}, {"name": "worked-example.md", "text": "# Worked example (illustrative; an Indian B2B HR and payroll software company, ₹220 Cr revenue, seeking its first international market)\n\n**Objective:** a profitable international revenue stream reaching ₹60 Cr ARR within 5 years. It must reuse the product core and be within a ₹30 Cr peak investment.\n\n**Criteria:**\n- Attractiveness (40%): market size, growth, willingness to pay.\n- Ability to win (40%): product fit with local payroll rules, Indian-diaspora business networks, competitor strength.\n- Regulatory feasibility (20%): data residency, ease of doing business, payroll-law complexity.\n\n**Screen (scores 1-5):**\n\n| Market | Attractiveness | Ability to win | Regulatory feasibility | Weighted | Rank |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| UAE | 3.5 | 4.2 | 4.5 | **3.98** | 1 |\n| Saudi Arabia | 4.5 | 3.4 | 3.2 | **3.80** | 2 |\n| Indonesia | 4.2 | 2.6 | 3.0 | 3.32 | 3 |\n| Vietnam | 3.4 | 2.4 | 3.2 | 2.96 | 4= |\n| Kenya | 2.6 | 3.0 | 3.6 | 2.96 | 4= |\n\n**Deep-dive (UAE first, Saudi Arabia as wave 2):**\n- **Market:** ~9,000 companies with 50-1,000 employees in the target segments (est., from licensing-authority and chamber data; to be validated).\n- **Regulation:** wage-protection-system compliance is a must-have; local data-hosting expectations in some sectors.\n- **Customers:** 14 interviews. Pain points are end-of-service gratuity calculations, multi-nationality payroll and visa-linked HR documents.\n- **Competitors:** 3 regional players are strong in enterprise; the mid-market is underserved; global suites are expensive.\n\n**Proposition and pricing:**\n- Mid-market payroll plus HR with built-in local compliance and Arabic and English interfaces.\n- Priced 20-25% below global suites.\n- Per-employee-per-month tiers.\n\n**Entry mode:**\n- An organic subsidiary with a local reseller partnership for SME reach.\n- Acquisition was considered but rejected: targets are overpriced, and integration would absorb scarce management time.\n- A JV is not needed for a software business in the target free zones (verify the licensing route).\n\n**Business case (₹ Cr, 7 years; hurdle 14%):**\n\n| Year | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cash flow | −18 | −9 | 2 | 8 | 14 | 18 | 22 | 24 |\n| Cumulative | −18 | −27 | −25 | −17 | −3 | 15 | 37 | 61 |\n\n- **Base case:** NPV ₹18.3 Cr; IRR ~28%; payback ~4.2 years; peak funding ₹27 Cr (within the ₹30 Cr limit).\n- **Downside (inflows −30%):** NPV ₹5.0 Cr; IRR ~18%.\n- **Switching value:** inflows from year 2 can fall ~41% before NPV turns negative.\n\n**Staging and gates:**\n- **Gate 1 (month 12):** ≥25 paying customers, gross retention ≥90%, and compliance certification done. If met, add the sales team and the reseller network.\n- **Gate 2 (month 24):** ₹12 Cr ARR. If met, prepare Saudi Arabia entry.\n\n**Risks:**\n- Compliance errors in payroll (very high impact): local payroll experts plus pre-run checks.\n- Sales-cycle length.\n- Partner dependence.\n- Currency risk.\n\n**Exit criteria:** fewer than 15 customers at month 12, or a CAC payback of more than 30 months at month 18. Either triggers a stop-or-pivot review.\n\n**Launch plan:** a free-zone entity, a local payroll-compliance lead, 2 reseller partners, and 5 design-partner customers in the first 6 months.\n\n**KPIs:** ARR, logos, net revenue retention, CAC payback, compliance incidents.\n\n**Decision requested:** approve Phase 1 (₹18 Cr) and the gated release of up to ₹9 Cr more."}]}]};
